window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
  {"id":"hi-api-design-01","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"In a 45-minute mock system design interview for a food-delivery platform, the candidate spends 15 minutes writing exhaustive endpoint definitions with complete request and response JSON for every route, then rushes through storage and scaling in the time left. The interviewer's feedback is negative even though every endpoint was correct. What went wrong?","answer":"The candidate blew the time budget: API design deserves roughly five minutes and a reasonable sketch, because interviews are graded on architecture depth. Overspending on endpoints starved the part of the interview that actually matters.","explanation":"API design time budget in interviews (~5 minutes)"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-02","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"two-choice","question":"In a system design interview, the interviewer's scrutiny of your API design is roughly the same whether you are interviewing for a backend infrastructure role or a frontend/product-focused role.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"API design carries more weight for frontend/product-focused and junior candidates; most interviewers otherwise want a reasonable sketch so the time can go to the deeper architecture."},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-03","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A three-person startup is building a recipe-sharing site with a single web client and plain create/read/update/delete operations on recipes and comments. One engineer pushes to launch on GraphQL and gRPC to be future-proof. Which protocol fits this product, and why?","answer":"REST. It is the default for standard CRUD with one client: it maps directly onto HTTP semantics and database operations with no extra machinery. GraphQL and gRPC solve multi-client flexibility and internal performance problems this product does not have.","explanation":"REST as the default protocol for standard CRUD"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-04","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A social network serves both a mobile app and a web analytics dashboard from the same REST endpoints. The profile endpoint returns over 40 fields, so mobile users on slow connections download data only the dashboard ever displays, and the mobile team keeps filing requests for new stripped-down endpoints. What should the team change, and why?","answer":"Adopt GraphQL. Each client specifies exactly the fields it needs against one endpoint, so mobile fetches a few fields while the dashboard fetches everything — over-fetching disappears without multiplying bespoke endpoints.","explanation":"GraphQL eliminates over-fetching for divergent clients"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-05","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"An event-discovery mobile app renders its detail screen by calling four REST endpoints in sequence — event, then venue, then tickets, then reviews — so the screen paints slowly on cellular connections. The data model itself is fine; the problem is the number of round trips. Which API style addresses this, and how?","answer":"GraphQL. The client sends one query describing the whole nested shape (event with its venue, tickets, and reviews) and receives it in a single round trip, fixing the under-fetching waterfall.","explanation":"GraphQL eliminates under-fetching / request waterfalls"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-06","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A fintech startup redesigns its web dashboard every sprint. Under their REST API, nearly every redesign needs a backend ticket — a new endpoint here, extra fields there — and the frontend team is routinely blocked waiting for backend deploys even though the underlying entities never change. What API approach removes this cross-team dependency, and how?","answer":"GraphQL. The backend exposes a schema of types and relationships once, and each redesign is just a new client-side query shape — the frontend changes what it asks for without any new backend endpoints or deploys.","explanation":"GraphQL enables frontend iteration without backend endpoint changes"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-07","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A project-management SaaS migrated its task board to GraphQL. Loading a board with 200 tasks and their assignees now fires 201 database queries per request: one for the task list, then one per task to resolve its assignee. What is this problem called, and what is the standard fix?","answer":"The N+1 query problem: naive resolvers fetch each nested object individually. The fix is batching via a dataloader pattern, which collects the assignee lookups and resolves them in one query.","explanation":"GraphQL N+1 query problem and dataloader fix"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-08","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A hospital patient portal uses GraphQL. Nurses should see a patient's name and appointments but never the billing fields on the same Patient type, while billing staff need the reverse. In REST this would be separate protected endpoints. Where is this access control enforced in GraphQL?","answer":"At the field level, inside resolvers. GraphQL secures individual fields rather than whole endpoints, so a single Patient type can expose different fields to different roles.","explanation":"GraphQL field-level authorization vs REST endpoint-level"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-09","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing company's dispatch, pricing, and driver-location services exchange tens of thousands of internal calls per second using JSON over HTTP REST. Profiling shows serialization and connection overhead dominate latency. What should they switch these internal calls to, and why?","answer":"gRPC with Protocol Buffers. Binary serialization over HTTP/2 is significantly faster than JSON-over-HTTP for service-to-service traffic, which is exactly the internal, high-frequency workload gRPC is built for.","explanation":"gRPC for high-volume internal service-to-service calls"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-10","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A parcel-logistics firm has a Go tracking service calling a Java billing service over REST. Twice this quarter a renamed or re-typed JSON field slipped through review and broke production, because nothing verified the contract before deploy. What mechanism would catch these mismatches earlier?","answer":"Define the contract in Protocol Buffers (.proto files) and generate client and server code for both languages. Contract violations then fail at compile time in either codebase instead of surfacing in production.","explanation":"Protocol Buffers compile-time cross-language type safety"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-11","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A logistics company's internal dispatch service must continuously receive location pings from a vehicle-gateway service while simultaneously pushing route corrections back to it, all with low latency between two services the company controls. Plain REST request-response would force each side to poll the other. Which gRPC capability fits this, and why?","answer":"gRPC's bidirectional streaming: both services exchange typed messages continuously over a single multiplexed HTTP/2 connection, so neither side polls and every message rides an already-open stream.","explanation":"gRPC bidirectional streaming over HTTP/2"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-12","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"two-choice","question":"An RPC API models operations as named procedures such as cancelSubscription(userId), while REST models the same operation as a standard HTTP method applied to a resource, such as DELETE /subscriptions/{id}.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"A","explanation":"RPC is action-oriented (you call remote functions by name), whereas REST is resource-oriented (a fixed set of HTTP verbs applied to noun-named resources)."},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-13","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A smart-home IoT platform must expose an API to third-party integrators' web apps and also connect its own device-registry, automation, and notification microservices. An engineer proposes gRPC everywhere for consistency. What split does conventional guidance recommend, and why?","answer":"REST for the public-facing API and gRPC for internal service-to-service calls. External clients need universal compatibility and easy debugging, which REST gives them; internal traffic benefits from gRPC's speed and type safety. Forcing one protocol on both audiences sacrifices one of them.","explanation":"Practical split: public REST, internal gRPC"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-14","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A messaging app's clients poll GET /conversations/{id}/messages every two seconds. Servers are drowning in mostly-empty responses, and messages still arrive with visible lag. What should replace this design?","answer":"A server-push channel — WebSockets, or Server-Sent Events for one-way delivery — so new messages arrive the moment they exist. Request-response polling is the wrong primitive for real-time features like chat.","explanation":"WebSockets/SSE replace request-response for real-time features"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-15","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"One team at a sports-media company is building a live-score widget that only ever pushes updates from server to browser. Another team is building a collaborative whiteboard where every client constantly sends strokes and receives everyone else's. Both need real-time behavior. Which push technology fits each feature, and what is the distinguishing factor?","answer":"Server-Sent Events for the score widget: it is one-way server-to-client push over plain HTTP, the simplest fit when clients never send. WebSockets for the whiteboard, because it needs a persistent bidirectional channel where clients send as well as receive.","explanation":"SSE for one-way server push vs WebSockets for bidirectional communication"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-16","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"During an API review at a furniture e-commerce company, a new service ships endpoints named POST /createOrder, POST /cancelOrder, and GET /getOrders. The reviewer rejects the design as un-RESTful. What is the objection, and what should the endpoints look like?","answer":"REST models resources as plural nouns and expresses actions through HTTP methods, not verbs embedded in the URL. The endpoints should be POST /orders to create, GET /orders to list, and DELETE /orders/{id} (or a status-changing update) to cancel.","explanation":"Resources are plural nouns; actions live in HTTP methods, not verb URLs"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-17","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"An online-learning platform is designing two list endpoints. Comments only ever exist on a specific lesson and are only fetched in that lesson's context. Assignments must be filterable by any combination of course, student, and due date. How should each URL be structured, and why do they differ?","answer":"Nest comments under their parent — /lessons/{id}/comments — because the relationship is required for the request to be meaningful. Keep assignments flat with query parameters — /assignments?course_id=...&student_id=... — because each filter is optional and freely combinable.","explanation":"Nested paths for required relationships vs flat paths with query-param filters"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-18","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A peer-to-peer payments app sees occasional double-charges. Logs show that on flaky mobile networks the client times out waiting on POST /transfers, automatically retries, and both attempts eventually succeed server-side. What underlying API property is at fault, and what is the fix?","answer":"POST is not idempotent, so each retry creates a new transfer — the blind retry duplicates the money movement. The fix is an idempotency key so the server maps repeated attempts of the same logical operation to a single transfer.","explanation":"POST non-idempotency: retries create duplicates"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-19","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"two-choice","question":"An API gateway team wants to add automatic retries for any request that times out, without risking duplicated side effects. Which set of HTTP methods is safe to blanket-retry?","optionA":"GET, PUT, DELETE","optionB":"GET, POST, PATCH","correct":"A","explanation":"These are idempotent, so repeating them reaches the same final state, while POST (and in general PATCH) can create duplicates or compound changes when retried."},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-20","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A SaaS admin dashboard updates user emails by sending PUT /users/{id} with a body containing only the new email address. After the change ships, users' display names and notification preferences vanish. Why?","answer":"PUT replaces the entire resource, so every field omitted from the body is wiped. Either send the complete user object with PUT or switch to PATCH, which applies only the supplied fields.","explanation":"PUT replaces the whole resource; PATCH is a partial update"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-21","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A travel-booking API team is designing 'create a reservation at a hotel.' They must place three inputs somewhere: the hotel's ID, an optional flag to email a confirmation, and the guest list with payment details. Where does each belong, and why?","answer":"The hotel ID goes in the path because it is required identity — the request is meaningless without it. The confirmation flag goes in a query parameter as an optional behavior modifier. The guest list and payment details go in the request body, which is for complex, sensitive structured data.","explanation":"Data placement: path = required identity, query = optional modifier, body = complex payload"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-22","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"An analytics API returns HTTP 500 whenever a customer submits a malformed date filter. Customers' retry logic treats 500 as transient and hammers the endpoint, and on-call engineers get paged for what is really bad input. What should change?","answer":"Return 400 instead: malformed input is a client error (4xx), not a server failure (5xx). Correct classification tells clients not to retry and stops false alarms — the 4xx-versus-5xx split is the distinction that actually matters.","explanation":"4xx (client error) vs 5xx (server error) classification"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-23","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"two-choice","question":"A banking API's POST /accounts call succeeds and a new account record now exists. Which status code best describes the outcome?","optionA":"201 Created","optionB":"200 OK","correct":"A","explanation":"It explicitly signals that a new resource was created, which is more precise than the generic 200 success."},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-24","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A short-video app paginates its trending feed with ?offset=40&limit=20. Users scrolling during peak hours report seeing the same videos twice and never seeing others. The database itself is healthy. What is happening?","answer":"New videos inserted while users scroll shift every row's position, so successive offset windows overlap or skip records. Offset pagination is unstable under real-time inserts; cursor-based pagination anchored to a stable record pointer fixes it.","explanation":"Offset pagination instability under live inserts"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-25","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"An internal compliance tool lets auditors browse a frozen archive of 9,000 transactions and requires a 'jump to page N' control. The team implemented cursor-based pagination and now cannot build the page picker. What went wrong in the choice?","answer":"Cursors only advance relative to a known record, so random access to page N is awkward by design. For a static dataset needing page jumps, offset pagination is the right fit — its instability weakness does not apply when the data never changes.","explanation":"Cursor pagination cannot jump to an arbitrary page"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-26","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A news app is implementing cursor-based pagination for its article feed. A junior engineer asks what the server must actually return with each page, and how the client requests the page after it. Describe the request/response contract.","answer":"Each response returns the page of items plus an opaque next_cursor that encodes the position of the last item (for example its ID or timestamp). The client sends it back on the next request — ?cursor=abc123&limit=10 — and a missing or null next_cursor signals the end of the list.","explanation":"Cursor pagination mechanics: opaque next_cursor returned with each page"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-27","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A public weather-data API needs a breaking change to its forecast response format without stranding thousands of existing integrations. One engineer proposes /v2/forecasts in the URL; another proposes an Accept-Version request header. Which is the sensible default, and what is the trade-off?","answer":"URL versioning: /v2/ is explicit, trivially routed to separate code paths, and testable straight from a browser. Header versioning keeps URLs cleaner but is less discoverable and harder to exercise, so it is the exception rather than the default.","explanation":"URL versioning vs header versioning"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-28","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"In a fitness-class booking app, a logged-in user edits the reservation ID in the URL and successfully cancels a stranger's booking. The request carried a perfectly valid session token. Which security control is missing, and how does it differ from the one that passed?","answer":"Authorization is missing. Authentication — the valid token — only proves who the caller is; authorization checks whether that caller may act on that specific resource, here an ownership check that the booking belongs to the requester.","explanation":"Authentication (identity) vs authorization (permission)"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-29","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A music-streaming startup authenticates its consumer mobile app by issuing each listener a long-lived API key stored on the device. A security review flags the scheme. Why is this the wrong tool, and what fits instead?","answer":"API keys identify applications and servers — they suit server-to-server integrations and third-party developer access, not end users holding long-lived secrets on personal devices. Consumer sessions belong on JWTs or session tokens issued at login, which carry per-user identity and expire.","explanation":"API keys are for server-to-server/third-party access, not end users"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-30","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"A gaming platform's twelve microservices each validate every incoming request by calling one central session service that looks tokens up in a database. That service is now the platform's biggest bottleneck and single point of failure. What change removes the lookup entirely?","answer":"Switch to JWTs. The token itself carries signed user context, so any service holding the verification key validates requests locally by checking the signature — stateless authentication with no central database call.","explanation":"JWT statelessness removes central session lookups"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-31","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"An online marketplace has sellers who may edit only their own listings, support agents who may view all orders but change nothing, and admins who can do anything. Today authorization is a single is_admin flag plus scattered if-statements. What model should replace this, and what per-request check still remains?","answer":"Role-based access control: define roles (seller, support, admin), attach permissions to roles, and assign roles to users. Each request still needs a resource-level ownership check — a seller edits a listing only if they own it or are an admin.","explanation":"RBAC: role-scoped permissions plus resource-ownership checks"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-32","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"open","question":"During a limited sneaker drop, bots fire thousands of checkout requests per second and lock real customers out of an e-commerce site. The team wants browsing to stay generous while choking checkout abuse. What should they implement, and what should rejected requests receive?","answer":"Endpoint-specific rate limiting — a strict per-user cap on checkout attempts per minute layered on looser per-user and per-IP limits elsewhere — returning 429 Too Many Requests when a limit is exceeded.","explanation":"Endpoint-specific rate limiting with 429 responses"},
  {"id":"hi-api-design-33","topic":"core-concepts · api design","type":"two-choice","question":"Rate limiting is typically enforced centrally at an API gateway or middleware layer, rather than being re-implemented inside each individual endpoint handler.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"A","explanation":"A central gateway or middleware applies consistent limits across all endpoints and rejects excess traffic before it ever reaches application logic."},
  {"id":"hi-caching-01","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A SaaS analytics dashboard needs a p50 under 10ms for a workspace-settings lookup that currently takes 40ms from Postgres. One engineer proposes adding two read replicas; another proposes putting the lookup behind Redis. Which approach fits better, and why?","answer":"Redis. Replicas add capacity, but every query still pays the disk-backed query cost, so 40ms stays roughly 40ms; an in-memory read returns in about a millisecond, which is the only way to hit a sub-10ms target.","explanation":"Why cache: memory vs disk latency gap (~1ms Redis vs ~30-50ms database query)"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-02","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"An electronics e-commerce site's database sits at 85% CPU during evening peaks. Query logs show the same few thousand product-detail queries repeating constantly, and writes are a tiny fraction of traffic. What should the team change?","answer":"Put the product-detail reads behind a cache so repeated identical queries are served from memory instead of re-hitting the database. Read-heavy, repetitive access to slowly-changing data is exactly the workload caching exists to absorb.","explanation":"Caching to offload repetitive reads from an overloaded database"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-03","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing platform runs 12 API servers, each with its own local cache of driver profiles. The hit rate is poor, the same profiles are duplicated in 12 places, and two servers sometimes return different versions of the same driver. What structural change fixes all three symptoms?","answer":"Replace the per-server caches with one shared external cache like Redis. All servers read the same copy, so duplication disappears, any server's fetch warms the cache for everyone (raising the hit rate), and all servers see one consistent version.","explanation":"External shared cache (Redis/Memcached) shared across all app servers vs per-server local caches"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-04","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A mobile game serves its asset bundles from a single origin server in Virginia. Players in Australia wait around 300ms per asset request and complain about load times; the assets only change on releases. What should the team add, and what improvement should they expect?","answer":"Serve the assets through a CDN. Edge servers near the players cut round trips from roughly 250-300ms to 20-40ms, and rarely-changing static files are the ideal CDN payload.","explanation":"CDN edge caching to cut geographic latency for static media (~250-300ms origin vs ~20-40ms edge)"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-05","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A ticketing platform's anonymous 'upcoming events' JSON endpoint returns identical output for every visitor and accounts for 90% of traffic. An engineer objects that 'the CDN is only for images and JavaScript.' Are they right, and what should the team do?","answer":"They're wrong — a CDN can cache any public, non-personalized HTTP response, JSON included. Put the endpoint behind the CDN with a short TTL and most of that traffic never reaches the origin.","explanation":"CDNs can cache public, non-personalized API responses, not just static assets"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-06","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A smart-home app stores device configuration in the phone's local storage so screens render instantly. Support agents keep changing settings server-side, but some users see the old configuration for days. What is the inherent limitation here, and how should it be mitigated?","answer":"The backend cannot reach into client-side caches to invalidate them, so staleness is hard to control. Mitigate with expiry or version checks on the cached data (for example, a freshness check on app open), while accepting that client caches trade backend control for speed.","explanation":"Client-side caching trade-off: backend has weak control over invalidation and staleness"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-07","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A payments API checks a feature flag in Redis on every request, adding a ~1ms network round trip millions of times a day. The flags change maybe once a week. What should the team change, and why?","answer":"Cache the flags in-process with a periodic refresh. Local memory reads take microseconds because there is no network hop at all, and tiny, rarely-changing config data is the textbook use case for in-process caching.","explanation":"In-process caching for small rarely-changing data (feature flags, config) removes the network hop entirely"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-08","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A gaming company caches its global leaderboard in-process on each of its 20 game servers. Players comparing screens see different top-10 lists depending on which server they hit. What is going wrong?","answer":"Each server holds its own private copy refreshed at different moments — in-process caches are not shared across instances, so they diverge. Data that must look identical to everyone belongs in a shared external cache.","explanation":"In-process cache limitation: not shared across instances, causing divergent views"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-09","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"An online bookstore plans to bulk-load its entire 50-million-row catalog into Redis at startup 'so the cache is always warm.' Only about 2% of titles are viewed in any given week, and the load blows past the cluster's memory. What approach should they use instead?","answer":"Cache-aside lazy loading: populate a key only when a request actually misses. The cache then holds just the working set — the roughly 2% of titles actually being read — instead of the entire catalog.","explanation":"Cache-aside (lazy loading) keeps the cache lean by populating only on demand"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-10","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"two-choice","question":"In a cache-aside setup, a request that misses the cache is slightly slower than the same request would be in a system with no cache at all.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"A","explanation":"A miss pays for the failed cache lookup and then the full database read, so the miss path is strictly slower; the pattern only wins because hits vastly outnumber misses."},
  {"id":"hi-caching-11","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"two-choice","question":"A brokerage's positions page must never show a stale balance after a trade is recorded, and the firm can tolerate slightly slower writes. Which write strategy fits?","optionA":"Write-through: update the cache and the database synchronously on every write","optionB":"Write-behind: update the cache immediately and flush to the database asynchronously","correct":"A","explanation":"Completing both before acknowledging keeps the cache consistent with the database so reads are always fresh, whereas write-behind leaves a window of unflushed, potentially lost data."},
  {"id":"hi-caching-12","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery app writes order status to its cache and its database synchronously. During a database hiccup, some orders showed as 'confirmed' in the app but never existed in the database. What went wrong?","answer":"A dual-write partial failure: the order was written to the cache but the database write failed, and with no transaction spanning both systems nothing rolled the cache entry back. This inconsistency window is the classic weakness of synchronous dual writes; ordering the database write first (and invalidating rather than updating the cache) narrows it.","explanation":"Write-through dual-write failure: partial failure leaves cache and database inconsistent"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-13","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A fitness-wearable platform ingests hundreds of thousands of step-count updates per second and is crushing its database with tiny writes. Dashboards can lag by a minute without anyone caring. Which caching write strategy fits, and why?","answer":"Write-behind (write-back): accept writes into the cache and flush them to the database asynchronously in batches. It turns a flood of tiny writes into efficient batches, and the data's tolerance for lag makes the eventual consistency acceptable.","explanation":"Write-behind (write-back) for high write throughput when eventual consistency is acceptable"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-14","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"An ad-analytics service uses a write-back cache in front of its data warehouse. After a cache node crashed, the last several minutes of click data were simply gone. Why did this happen, and what is the lesson?","answer":"Write-behind buffers writes in cache memory before flushing, so a crash destroys everything not yet flushed. Use it only where losing a window of data is tolerable, or add replication or persistence to the cache tier.","explanation":"Write-behind risk: buffered unflushed writes are lost if the cache crashes"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-15","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"An architect at a recipe-sharing startup insists on wiring a read-through plugin into the cache tier so 'the application never talks to the database for reads.' What is the pushback?","answer":"Read-through requires specialized library or plugin support and buys almost nothing over plain cache-aside, which the app can implement in a few lines. Outside a CDN — the one canonical read-through system — there is rarely a reason to take on that complexity.","explanation":"Read-through is rarely worth proposing outside CDNs (library complexity vs cache-aside)"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-16","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A podcast app's cache is at capacity. Listening is dominated by whatever episodes came out this week, and the popular set shifts continuously as new shows drop. Which eviction policy should the team pick, and why?","answer":"LRU. It evicts whatever has not been touched recently, so the cache automatically tracks the shifting weekly working set — which is why it is the safe default for most workloads.","explanation":"LRU as the safe default eviction policy that adapts to shifting access patterns"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-17","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A video-streaming service's small set of blockbuster titles stays hot for months, but a nightly catalog-scanning job touches millions of cold titles and keeps flushing the blockbusters out of the LRU cache. What policy change helps, and why?","answer":"Switch to LFU (typically an approximate implementation). Frequency-based eviction protects the durably popular titles because a single scan touch cannot outrank months of accumulated hits, unlike recency-based LRU.","explanation":"LFU for durably popular items and resistance to one-off scan pollution"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-18","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A news aggregator picked FIFO eviction 'because it's simple.' Its most-read evergreen explainer articles — cached long ago and still heavily read — keep vanishing from the cache and hammering the database. What is wrong?","answer":"FIFO evicts strictly by insertion order and ignores usage, so the oldest entries get evicted even while they are the hottest. Recency- and frequency-aware policies like LRU and LFU exist precisely to avoid this.","explanation":"FIFO's flaw: evicts by insertion age and throws out still-hot items"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-19","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A weather app caches city forecasts in Redis with no expiry, evicting only under memory pressure. Users in low-traffic cities end up seeing day-old forecasts. What is the minimal fix?","answer":"Attach a TTL to each forecast entry so it expires after a bounded window and the next read fetches fresh data. TTL is the staleness bound; size-based policies like LRU manage memory, not freshness, so the two are combined.","explanation":"TTL bounds staleness and composes with size-based eviction policies"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-20","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A social app caches its homepage feed with a 60-second TTL. Monitoring shows database QPS spiking about 100x for a moment every minute — exactly when the entry expires — then dropping back. What is happening?","answer":"A cache stampede (thundering herd): when the popular entry expires, every concurrent request misses at once and each one independently hits the database to rebuild the same value.","explanation":"Cache stampede (thundering herd): synchronized misses on expiry flooding the database"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-21","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A live sports-scores site knows its match-page cache entry expires under heavy concurrency, and thousands of simultaneous misses pile onto the database each time. The team is debating mitigations. What is the most effective mechanism?","answer":"Request coalescing (single flight): let exactly one request rebuild the entry while all the others wait for its result. The database sees one rebuild instead of thousands, no matter how high the concurrency.","explanation":"Request coalescing (single flight) as the most effective stampede fix"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-22","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A job board deletes cache entries the moment a listing is edited and uses no TTLs. An engineer proposes a background job that 're-warms popular keys shortly before they expire' to smooth database load. Why doesn't the proposal fit?","answer":"There is no expiry to warm ahead of — with write-invalidation, entries die at unpredictable moments when writes happen, not on a schedule. Warming ahead of expiry only makes sense when entries expire on TTLs.","explanation":"Cache warming presupposes TTL-based expiry and is useless with write-invalidation"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-23","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"After every deploy, a flight-search site warms 10,000 route keys, all with exactly one-hour TTLs. Every hour on the hour, database load spikes hard. What should the team change?","answer":"Stagger the TTLs — add random jitter, or use probabilistic early refresh — so keys expire spread over time. Identical TTLs set at the same moment recreate a synchronized mass-miss spike every hour.","explanation":"Staggered/jittered TTLs or probabilistic early refresh to prevent synchronized mass expiry"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-24","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"On a professional networking app, users who upload a new profile photo still appear with the old one to everyone else for up to ten minutes. Profiles sit in a cache-aside layer with a 10-minute TTL. What change makes updates visible immediately while keeping the cache?","answer":"Delete the profile's cache key as part of the write path (invalidate on write). The next read misses and repopulates with the fresh value, so staleness no longer persists until the TTL runs out.","explanation":"Invalidate-on-write to make updates visible immediately under cache-aside"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-25","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A short-video app's product manager demands that like counts be perfectly up-to-date on every screen, which would force invalidating the counter cache on every single like. What should engineering push back with?","answer":"Like counts do not need strong consistency — a few seconds of staleness harms no one. Cache them with a short TTL and accept eventual consistency; per-like invalidation would destroy the cache's value to provide a guarantee the product doesn't need.","explanation":"Matching consistency strategy to data: accept eventual consistency where staleness is harmless"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-26","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A microblogging service's Redis cluster shows one node pinned at 100% CPU while the others sit idle. Nearly all traffic on that node targets a single celebrity's profile key. What is this failure mode?","answer":"A hot key: partitioning maps each key to exactly one node, so a single wildly popular key concentrates its entire load on one shard no matter how many nodes the cluster has.","explanation":"Hot key failure mode: one key concentrates load on a single cache shard"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-27","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A live-shopping platform's flash-sale item page drives millions of reads per second against one cache key, saturating the node that owns it. What mitigations should the team apply?","answer":"Replicate the hot key — store the same value under multiple copies that land on different nodes and spread reads across them — and/or keep an in-process copy on each app server so most reads never touch the cache tier at all. Rate limiting is the backstop if the spread-out load is still too much.","explanation":"Hot key mitigations: replicating the key across nodes, in-process fallback copies, and rate limiting"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-28","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A project-management SaaS decreed 'cache every endpoint.' Months later, cache memory has ballooned, the hit rate sits around 30%, and latency has barely improved. An audit shows they cache rarely-requested audit-log pages and task counters that change every second. What is the lesson?","answer":"Cache only data that is read often and changes rarely. Rarely-read entries burn memory without producing hits and volatile entries are stale on arrival, so both pollute the cache — exactly what a ~30% hit rate (healthy is roughly 80%+) is signaling.","explanation":"What not to cache: rarely-read or fast-changing data pollutes the cache and tanks hit rate (healthy ~80%+)"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-29","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A music-streaming service's Redis cluster failed, the full read load instantly landed on the database, and the database collapsed, turning a cache outage into a total outage. What should have been designed in beforehand?","answer":"A plan for losing the cache: circuit breakers and load shedding so the database only receives what it can survive, plus graceful degradation (and possibly a small in-process fallback cache) for the rest. A cache absorbing most traffic is an availability dependency, not just an optimization.","explanation":"Cache outage resilience: circuit breakers, load shedding, graceful degradation when the cache tier fails"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-30","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"In a mock system design interview, a candidate drops Redis boxes onto the diagram before discussing traffic volumes or requirements. The interviewer looks unimpressed. What feedback should the candidate get?","answer":"Establish the bottleneck first — quantify the read load or a specific query's latency — and then introduce caching as the targeted fix. Proposing a cache before demonstrating the need reads as pattern-matching rather than engineering judgment.","explanation":"Interview strategy: quantify the bottleneck before proposing a cache"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-31","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"In a system design interview, a candidate adds a cache-aside Redis layer with LRU eviction and a 5-minute TTL for a product catalog, then immediately moves on to the next component. The interviewer interjects: 'Anything else you want to say about that cache?' What is the interviewer fishing for?","answer":"The downsides: how updates are handled (invalidate on write, or explicitly accept TTL-bounded staleness) and what happens when the cache tier fails (circuit breakers so the database is not crushed by the fallback load). A cache proposal is incomplete until its consistency story and failure modes are addressed proactively.","explanation":"Interview strategy: a cache proposal is incomplete until staleness handling and failure modes are addressed"},
  {"id":"hi-caching-32","topic":"core-concepts · caching","type":"open","question":"A dating app expects 250,000 cache reads per second at peak and plans to serve them all from a single Redis node. Is the plan sound?","answer":"No — a single Redis node handles on the order of 100k+ operations per second, so 250k/sec calls for a cluster of roughly three or more nodes (or read replicas), with headroom for spikes.","explanation":"Capacity rule of thumb: a single Redis node handles on the order of 100k+ ops/sec"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-01","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"A database vendor pitches your team a globally distributed store, claiming it simultaneously delivers strong consistency, 100% availability, and full tolerance of network partitions. Your architect is skeptical. What is wrong with the vendor's claim?","answer":"The CAP theorem makes this impossible to guarantee: once a network partition occurs, each node must either refuse requests (sacrificing availability) or answer with possibly-stale data (sacrificing consistency). No distributed system can promise strong consistency and full availability through arbitrary partitions.","explanation":"CAP two-of-three impossibility"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-02","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"two-choice","question":"In the CAP theorem, what does the 'C' (consistency) refer to?","optionA":"Every read, on any node, returns the most recent successful write","optionB":"Constraints and invariants like foreign keys are never violated, as in ACID","correct":"A","explanation":"CAP consistency is about all nodes agreeing on the latest data, a different notion from ACID's consistency."},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-03","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"two-choice","question":"Under CAP, a system only counts as available if every response it returns contains the most recent data.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Availability only requires that every request to a non-failing node receives some response; the data in that response is allowed to be stale."},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-04","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"two-choice","question":"Which situation is a network partition in the CAP sense?","optionA":"Half the servers crash and stop responding entirely","optionB":"All servers stay healthy, but messages between two groups of them are lost or delayed","correct":"B","explanation":"A partition is a communication break between live nodes, forcing each side to act without knowing the other's state."},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-05","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"An architect's design doc for a multi-region order service reads: 'We will avoid the CAP trade-off entirely by choosing consistency and availability and dropping partition tolerance.' What is the flaw in this plan?","answer":"Partition tolerance is not something you opt out of: real networks will fail, so a multi-node system experiences partitions whether the doc acknowledges them or not. The only genuine decision is how to behave when a partition happens — favor consistency or favor availability.","explanation":"Partition tolerance is mandatory — 'CA' is not a real option"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-06","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"two-choice","question":"A distributed system must give up either consistency or availability at every moment, even while the network between its nodes is healthy.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"CAP forces the trade only while a partition is in progress; with a healthy network a system can serve up-to-date data and remain fully responsive at the same time."},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-07","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"A bank runs its account service in two regions. During a network split between them, customers routed to the secondary region get errors when checking balances, while the primary region works normally. An on-call engineer files this as an availability bug. What is actually going on?","answer":"The system is deliberately making the CP choice: rather than risk showing a balance that might be stale, the partitioned side refuses to answer. For money, erroring during the partition is correct behavior, not a bug.","explanation":"CP behavior during a partition: refuse rather than serve stale data"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-08","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"During a brief network partition between data centers, a messaging app keeps working everywhere, but users notice that friends' 'last seen' times and profile photos are a few minutes out of date. Once the partition heals, everything catches up. Which CAP stance did the app take, and was it reasonable?","answer":"It chose availability: keep responding through the partition and accept temporarily stale data that converges afterward. For social presence data that is the right call — a stale timestamp is harmless, downtime is not.","explanation":"AP behavior during a partition: serve stale data, converge later"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-09","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"A B2B analytics-dashboard team has been deadlocked for a week over whether their new reporting service needs strong consistency. What single question should they ask to settle the debate, and what answer does it give here?","answer":"Ask whether it would be genuinely damaging for users to briefly see stale data. A slightly out-of-date chart on an analytics dashboard harms no one, so they should pick availability with eventual consistency and move on.","explanation":"Litmus test: would brief staleness be catastrophic?"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-10","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"A distributed key-value store keeps five replicas of each record and requires acknowledgment from three nodes for every write and every read. A partition isolates two replicas from the other three. Reads and writes keep succeeding for clients who can reach the majority side, while clients stuck with the two-node side get errors. How does this design fit into the CAP trade-off?","answer":"It is a consistency-first (CP) design built on majority quorums: overlapping read and write quorums guarantee up-to-date data, so the three-node side keeps operating while the minority side must refuse rather than risk stale or divergent answers. CAP's availability sacrifice need not be total — it is paid only by the partition that cannot assemble a quorum.","explanation":"Majority quorums: only the minority side loses availability"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-11","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"A concert ticketing site replicated its seat map across regions for speed. During a network partition, two fans in different regions both purchased seat 12B for the same show, and both received confirmations. What went wrong?","answer":"Seat booking is a limited-resource transaction that demands strong consistency, but purchases were served from partitioned replicas that could not see each other's writes. The booking path must go through a strongly consistent store, even if that means rejecting some requests during partitions.","explanation":"Booking limited resources requires strong consistency"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-12","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"An online shop runs a flash sale with one unit of a rare sneaker left. Stock reads come from asynchronously updated replicas, and during the rush 14 customers successfully checked out the last pair. What should the team change, and why?","answer":"The checkout's decrement-and-check must be strongly consistent — it should atomically read and update one authoritative count. Eventually consistent stock numbers are fine for browsing pages, but never for the moment of purchase.","explanation":"Inventory checkout requires strong consistency (overselling)"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-13","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"A stock-trading app renders its order book from read replicas that can lag the primary by several seconds. Traders complain they placed orders against prices that no longer existed and got unexpected fills. Why is this design unacceptable in this domain?","answer":"Trading is a case where stale reads cause direct monetary harm, so price views and order placement need strong consistency. The extra latency and reduced availability of consistent reads is simply the price of correct trades.","explanation":"Financial/trading data requires strong consistency"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-14","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"An engineer proposes wrapping profile-photo updates on a social network in a two-phase commit across all regional replicas so everyone sees the new photo instantly. Upload latency would roughly triple. What should the team do instead?","answer":"Drop the 2PC — a profile photo lagging by seconds or minutes harms nobody, making this a textbook availability/eventual-consistency case. Expensive coordination should be reserved for data where staleness actually costs something.","explanation":"Social profile data tolerates staleness — don't over-engineer"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-15","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"A restaurant-review platform configured its European region to return errors whenever cross-region replication lags, reasoning that showing possibly-outdated opening hours would mislead users. European users now regularly hit error pages. Which stance fits this product better, and why?","answer":"Availability. Briefly stale hours or reviews are a minor annoyance, while an unusable site drives users away — a read-heavy content platform should serve the old data and let replicas converge.","explanation":"Content/review platforms should prefer availability over erroring"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-16","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"two-choice","question":"In a system design interview your design includes replication, and the interviewer asks how you will handle consistency. Which is the safer default answer?","optionA":"Eventual consistency everywhere, except strong consistency where the problem involves money, inventory, or booking limited resources","optionB":"Strong consistency everywhere, since fresher data can never be the wrong choice","correct":"A","explanation":"Blanket strong consistency buys needless latency and availability loss for flows that don't need it."},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-17","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"two-choice","question":"During a system design interview, when should you bring up the consistency-versus-availability decision?","optionA":"Early, while defining non-functional requirements, because it shapes the database and replication choices that follow","optionB":"At the end, once the architecture is drawn, as a closing remark","correct":"A","explanation":"The CAP stance drives pattern and technology selection, so it belongs up front."},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-18","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"A fintech's payment flow must debit a wallet balance in one database and append to a ledger in another, and a half-applied outcome is unacceptable. What mechanism keeps both stores in lockstep, and what does it cost?","answer":"A distributed transaction — classically two-phase commit — so both writes commit together or neither does. The cost is added latency and reduced availability, because the whole flow blocks whenever a participant cannot respond.","explanation":"Distributed transactions / two-phase commit for cross-node atomicity"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-19","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"A clinic-appointment startup handling a few thousand bookings per day needs a strict no-double-booking guarantee. One engineer wants an active-active multi-region database; another suggests a single primary Postgres instance with backups. Which fits better, and why?","answer":"The single primary. At this scale one authoritative database gives strong consistency trivially — every booking hits the same node — and sidesteps distributed coordination entirely. Active-active multi-region would introduce exactly the consistency problem they are trying to prevent.","explanation":"A single authoritative database sidesteps distributed consistency"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-20","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"A B2B invoicing SaaS moved its database to synchronous cross-region replication so every read anywhere reflects the latest write. Since the switch, p99 write latency has jumped from about 20ms to about 300ms. Why is this expected rather than a bug?","answer":"Strong consistency across regions means each write must be confirmed by remote nodes before it is acknowledged, putting wide-area round trips directly in the write path. The latency increase is the built-in price of the consistency they opted into, not a defect.","explanation":"Strong consistency's inherent latency cost"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-21","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"two-choice","question":"Your design's core requirement is strongly consistent booking transactions. Which technology pairing matches that priority?","optionA":"PostgreSQL, or Google Spanner for multi-region scale","optionB":"Cassandra, or a Redis cluster","correct":"A","explanation":"Traditional RDBMSs and Spanner are built for transactional strong consistency, while Cassandra and Redis clusters are tuned for availability."},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-22","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"A gaming company replicates its leaderboard asynchronously to every region so the game keeps running even if a region's link drops. Players occasionally see scores a few seconds behind. A new hire files the lag as a defect. Is it one?","answer":"No — asynchronous replication is the availability-first pattern working as designed: writes are acknowledged locally and propagate in the background, so brief replica lag is inherent. For a leaderboard, seconds-old scores are an acceptable trade for staying up.","explanation":"Async replication to replicas as the availability-first pattern"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-23","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"A marketplace needs its search index and cache to reflect catalog changes made in the primary database, but refuses to slow product writes with synchronous updates to those downstream systems. What pattern fits, and how does it work?","answer":"Change data capture: tail the primary database's change stream and push updates to the index and cache asynchronously. Writes stay fast and downstream systems converge moments later — an availability-oriented propagation pattern.","explanation":"Change data capture for asynchronous propagation"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-24","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"two-choice","question":"You are picking a datastore for an always-on social feed where brief staleness is fine but downtime is not. Which option matches that priority?","optionA":"Cassandra, or DynamoDB in its default multi-AZ eventually consistent mode","optionB":"A single-primary MySQL that requires synchronous commit confirmation before every acknowledgment","correct":"A","explanation":"These stores are engineered to keep accepting reads and writes through node and network failures, at the cost of temporary staleness."},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-25","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"An IoT fleet-management team needs strongly consistent reads for device-ownership records and rules out DynamoDB because 'it's an eventually consistent database.' Is that rejection justified?","answer":"No — DynamoDB is eventually consistent only by default; it also offers strongly consistent reads when you ask for them. The same product can sit on either side of the CAP trade depending on how it is configured.","explanation":"One technology can serve either CAP side via configuration (DynamoDB)"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-26","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery platform's architect declares the entire system must be CP because order placement cannot tolerate inconsistency — which would make restaurant-menu browsing return errors during partitions. What is the better framing?","answer":"CAP choices are made per feature, not per system: keep order placement and payment strongly consistent while menus, ratings, and ETAs prioritize availability. A single product can and should mix both stances.","explanation":"CAP stances are chosen per feature, not per system"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-27","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"In a dating app, two users swipe right on each other within the same second, but only one of them sees the match screen; the other finds out much later. Meanwhile, profile photos being minutes stale bothers no one. Where exactly does this app need consistency?","answer":"In match creation: both swipes must resolve against the same consistent record so the mutual match appears to both users atomically. Profile browsing can stay eventually consistent — it is the transactional interaction, not the content viewing, that needs the guarantee.","explanation":"Transactional interactions need consistency while browsing stays available"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-28","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"two-choice","question":"Among the consistency models, strong consistency is the cheapest to operate because it removes all ambiguity from reads.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"It is the most expensive: guaranteeing every read reflects the latest write forces coordination between nodes and adds latency, which is why it is reserved for cases like account balances that truly need it."},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-29","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"On a discussion forum, some readers see a reply saying 'Totally agree with this' before the comment it responds to appears in their view. No data is lost — the ordering is just wrong for a while. Which consistency model addresses exactly this, and what does it guarantee?","answer":"Causal consistency: it guarantees that causally related events — a comment and its reply — are observed in the same order by every user, while unrelated events may still arrive in any order.","explanation":"Causal consistency: related events seen in the same order"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-30","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"In a project-management tool, a user renames their workspace, sees the save confirmation, refreshes, and the old name is back; a minute later the new name finally sticks. Reads are served from lagging replicas. What guarantee should the team add for this flow?","answer":"Read-your-own-writes consistency: ensure a user's reads reflect at least their own recent updates, for example by pinning their post-write reads to the primary. Other users can briefly keep seeing the old name — it is the author's own view that must be immediate.","explanation":"Read-your-own-writes consistency"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-31","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"An IoT platform pushes a config change to a registry replicated across regions; for a couple of minutes different devices fetch different versions, then all replicas agree and every device settles on the new config. Which consistency model is this, and why is it acceptable here?","answer":"Eventual consistency — replicas may briefly diverge but are guaranteed to converge once updates stop flowing. For config that tolerates minutes of skew, much like a DNS record change, the gain in availability and simplicity is worth the temporary divergence.","explanation":"Eventual consistency: convergence over time"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-32","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"An online store's order-status page load-balances reads across replicas. A customer tracking their delivery sees the status reach 'Out for delivery', but on the next refresh it reverts to 'Preparing shipment', then later jumps forward again. Which consistency guarantee is missing here, and what does it promise?","answer":"Monotonic reads: once a user has observed a state, their later reads must never show an earlier one — time never appears to run backward. It is typically enforced by pinning a user's session to one replica, and note this is not read-your-own-writes, since the status was written by the fulfillment system, not the customer.","explanation":"Monotonic reads: successive reads never go backward"},
  {"id":"hi-cap-theorem-33","topic":"core-concepts · cap theorem","type":"open","question":"A video-streaming platform's metadata store spans three regions, and its network essentially never partitions. The team still faces a hard choice: acknowledge writes only after all regions confirm (slow), or acknowledge locally and replicate in the background (possibly stale reads elsewhere). Which extension of CAP names this trade-off?","answer":"PACELC: during a partition you trade availability against consistency, but else — in normal operation — you trade latency against consistency. The synchronous-versus-async replication decision is that 'else' branch, which plain CAP does not cover.","explanation":"PACELC: latency vs consistency even without partitions"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-01","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"An electronics e-commerce site caches product pages across 3 Memcached nodes, choosing a node with hash(productId) % 3. Ahead of a holiday sale they add a fourth node for extra capacity. Immediately the cache hit rate collapses and the primary database nearly tips over under the flood of reads. What is going wrong?","answer":"Changing N in hash(key) % N reassigned about three-quarters of all keys to different nodes, so most lookups missed simultaneously and the miss storm hammered the database. Consistent hashing would have confined the reshuffle to roughly the quarter of keys the new node takes over.","explanation":"Modulo hashing remaps most keys when a node is added (cache-miss storm diagnosis)"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-02","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A fintech company shards account ledgers across 10 database nodes using modulo hashing and plans to add an 11th node. An engineer estimates that about one-eleventh of the data will need to migrate. Is her estimate right, and under what scheme would it be?","answer":"No — under modulo hashing roughly 90% of keys change owners when N goes from 10 to 11, so almost everything migrates. Her ~1/N estimate is only true under consistent hashing, where just the keys in the new node's ring range move.","explanation":"Quantitative comparison: ~90% of keys move under modulo at N=10 vs ~1/N under consistent hashing"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-03","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"An IoT platform stores sensor readings on a hash ring. A new hire implements the read path as 'pick the node whose ring position is numerically closest to the key's hash, in either direction,' while the write path uses the standard rule. Reads now fail for a large share of keys even though nothing was deleted. What is the bug?","answer":"The ring rule is to walk clockwise from the key's position to the first node at or after it. 'Closest in either direction' picks the predecessor for keys that sit nearer to it, so reads go to a node that never stored the data.","explanation":"Ring lookup rule: walk clockwise to the first node at or after the key"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-04","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing service keeps trip histories in a consistent-hashed store with nodes at positions 0, 25, 50, and 75 on a 0-100 ring. They add a new node at position 90, and the DBA books a long maintenance window to rebalance the entire dataset. What data actually needs to move?","answer":"Only keys hashing into the range between 75 and 90 — they migrate from the node at position 0 (their former clockwise successor) to the new node. Every other key stays where it is, so a full rebalance is wasted effort.","explanation":"Adding a node moves only the predecessor-to-new-node range, taken from the successor"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-05","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A chat product keeps user session state on a consistent-hashed cache ring. One cache node crashes overnight, and the on-call engineer needs to know which surviving nodes now hold responsibility they didn't have before. What should you tell her?","answer":"Exactly one: the dead node's clockwise successor, which inherits the failed node's key range. Every other node's assignments are completely unchanged.","explanation":"Node removal or failure affects only the clockwise successor"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-06","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A multiplayer game stores live match state on a hash ring where each server occupies a single ring position. When one server dies, the next server on the ring inherits all of its load, doubles in traffic, and then dies too, cascading around the ring. The team understands why the neighbor absorbs everything — what design change stops the cascade?","answer":"Virtual nodes: give each server many positions scattered around the ring so a failed server's range is split among many different successors. Its load then spreads thinly across the fleet instead of doubling one neighbor.","explanation":"Virtual nodes prevent successor-overload cascades on failure"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-07","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"An SRE at a SaaS analytics vendor proposes adding virtual nodes to smooth out their cache ring. A manager vetoes it: 'There's no budget for more servers this quarter.' What is the manager misunderstanding?","answer":"Virtual nodes are not machines — they are extra hash positions (e.g., hashing 'node1-vn1', 'node1-vn2', ...) that all point back to the same physical server. The cost is a slightly larger routing table, not new hardware.","explanation":"Virtual nodes are logical ring positions, not additional hardware"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-08","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A logistics company's fleet-tracking store runs 6 identical nodes with 4 virtual nodes each, yet per-node data volume still varies by 40%. No individual key is unusually popular. What is the cheapest lever to even out the distribution?","answer":"Increase the number of virtual nodes per server. More positions per node makes each server's total slice of the ring statistically converge toward 1/N, so the imbalance shrinks.","explanation":"Virtual-node count is the tuning knob for load smoothness"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-09","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"An edtech startup launches a 3-node consistent-hashed cache, hashing each node to one ring position. From day one, one node holds 60% of all keys even though traffic per key is uniform. Why is the ring so lopsided?","answer":"Three random positions carve the circle into arcs of very unequal length, and each node owns everything in its arc — a structural imbalance baked in at placement time. It has nothing to do with traffic; virtual nodes are the fix.","explanation":"Few single-position nodes carve the ring into unequal arcs (structural imbalance)"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-10","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery app's restaurant cache is evenly balanced by key count, but the node holding one viral restaurant's menu sits at 100% CPU while the others idle. An engineer proposes switching hash functions to fix the skew. What is actually going on?","answer":"This is a hot key: one wildly popular key drives disproportionate traffic to whichever node owns it. A different hash function just relocates the problem — the key still lands on exactly one node — so they need replication or salting for that key instead.","explanation":"Hot spot recognition: popular-key workload skew, not bad hashing"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-11","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A social network sees one celebrity's profile overwhelming its cache node, so the team quadruples the virtual nodes per server. The overloaded node's traffic doesn't budge. Why didn't the change help?","answer":"Virtual nodes cure structural imbalance — uneven division of the key space — but this is workload imbalance: a single key's traffic. That key still hashes to exactly one owner no matter how many virtual nodes exist, so the fix is replicating or salting the hot key.","explanation":"Structural vs workload imbalance: virtual nodes cannot fix hot keys"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-12","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A live-sports app has one cache key — the championship game's scoreboard — read tens of thousands of times per second, dwarfing every other key, while writes to it are only a few per minute. Which hot-key mitigation fits this profile, and why?","answer":"Replicate that key to several nodes and load-balance reads across the copies. A read-heavy, write-light hot key is the textbook case for read replication, since keeping a handful of replicas in sync is cheap when writes are rare.","explanation":"Read replication for read-heavy hot keys"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-13","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A ticketing platform expects one superstar concert's availability key to take 100x normal read traffic at on-sale time. They plan to write the value under ten keys — 'show123-0' through 'show123-9' — with readers picking a random suffix. What is this technique and what does it buy them?","answer":"Key salting: the suffixes make each copy hash to a different ring position, scattering the read load across many nodes. The price is fanning out writes to all ten copies and making readers suffix-aware.","explanation":"Key salting with suffixes to scatter a hot key"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-14","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"An ad-tech platform's hot keys change hour by hour as campaigns launch and end, so any static ring layout is stale within a day. Pre-salting every key is deemed too expensive. What class of approach handles this moving target, and which major database works this way?","answer":"Adaptive rebalancing: continuously monitor per-range traffic and automatically shift hot key ranges off overloaded nodes as heat moves. DynamoDB manages partitions this way under the hood.","explanation":"Adaptive traffic-based rebalancing (DynamoDB-style)"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-15","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A healthcare-records platform consistent-hashes patient data and replicates each partition across three availability zones. A new SRE drafts an emergency runbook for mass-copying a failed node's data to its ring successor. Why is the runbook solving a non-problem?","answer":"With replication in place, a node failure is handled by promoting an existing replica to primary — typically via a consensus protocol like Raft — so no bulk data moves at failure time. Bulk transfer is reserved for planned membership changes like adding capacity, plus background re-replication to restore the replication factor.","explanation":"Replication turns failure handling into replica promotion, not data movement"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-16","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A gaming company builds an in-house consistent-hashed cache. In testing, lookups for a subset of keys throw a 'no owner found' error, and debugging shows every failing key hashes to a position greater than the highest node position on the ring. What did the implementers forget?","answer":"The ring wraps around: a key that hashes past the last node position belongs to the first node encountered continuing clockwise through zero, i.e., the lowest-positioned node. Their lookup treated the ring as a flat sorted list and fell off the end instead of wrapping.","explanation":"Ring wrap-around: keys hashing past the last node position belong to the first node"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-17","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"two-choice","question":"In a Cassandra-style ring store with replication factor 3, where do the three copies of a key live?","optionA":"On the key's owning node plus the next two distinct nodes walking clockwise around the ring","optionB":"On three nodes chosen randomly per key and recorded in a lookup table","correct":"A","explanation":"Consecutive-successor placement is deterministic, so any node can compute replica locations from ring state alone, and reads survive a failure by hitting the next replica."},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-18","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A travel-booking company adopts Redis Cluster to cache flight-search results. During setup an engineer asks where to configure the hash ring and the virtual-node count. What should the team tell him?","answer":"There is no ring to configure: Redis Cluster divides the key space into 16,384 fixed slots via CRC16(key) mod 16384 and assigns slot ranges to nodes. Rebalancing means explicitly reassigning slots between nodes — simpler to reason about than a ring, but it needs more coordination.","explanation":"Redis Cluster's fixed hash slots (16,384 via CRC16) versus a hash ring"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-19","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"two-choice","question":"In which system design interview prompt should you proactively explain hash rings, virtual nodes, and rebalancing in depth?","optionA":"Design a distributed cache from scratch","optionB":"Design an e-commerce checkout flow backed by managed cloud databases","correct":"A","explanation":"In infrastructure-from-scratch prompts (distributed cache, database, message broker), data placement IS the problem, so the mechanics are the substance of your answer; in product designs the platform already handles it."},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-20","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"two-choice","question":"In a product-design interview where you have chosen DynamoDB for storage, walking through hash ring mechanics unprompted is a reliable way to earn points.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"DynamoDB handles partitioning for you, so a one-line acknowledgment like 'consistent hashing distributes the data across partitions' is all that's needed. Go deep only if the interviewer explicitly asks; unprompted lectures burn time on a solved problem."},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-21","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"two-choice","question":"If a system's documentation says it uses consistent hashing, you can assume it implements a literal clockwise hash ring.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"The term is used loosely, and many such systems actually run variants like fixed hash-slot partitioning. The defining property is minimizing data movement when nodes join or leave, not the ring data structure itself."},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-22","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A collaborative document editor must route every websocket for a given document to the same backend server, because edits are merged in that server's memory. The fleet autoscales throughout the day: round-robin breaks affinity entirely, and modulo re-routes nearly every live session at each scale event. What routing scheme should they use?","answer":"Consistent hashing on the document ID. Each document sticks to one server, and a scale event only re-homes the small slice of documents adjacent to the added or removed server instead of disrupting every session.","explanation":"Stable request routing / session affinity via consistent hashing"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-23","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A video-streaming CDN must decide which edge server caches each video. Edge servers are added and drained many times a day as capacity is tuned regionally. Why is consistent hashing the standard mapping choice here?","answer":"Hashing the content ID onto a ring pins each video to a stable edge server, so caches stay warm, and any edge change only re-homes the videos in one slice of the ring. With modulo, every capacity tweak would effectively flush the entire edge cache.","explanation":"CDN edge-server content assignment that survives edge churn"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-24","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A podcast platform's single Postgres instance is saturated, and the team agrees to split its data across four machines. Before writing any migration code, what is the first design question they must answer, and what property should a good answer have?","answer":"Which machine owns which key — they need a deterministic key-to-shard mapping every client computes identically. The quality bar is how little data must move when the machine count changes later, which is exactly what consistent hashing optimizes.","explanation":"Sharding's core routing question: a deterministic, stable key-to-shard mapping"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-25","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A music-streaming engineer pushes back on adopting consistent hashing: 'When we add a server, every song ID gets hashed again, so everything moves anyway — how is this better?' What is the flaw in his reasoning?","answer":"Keys hash into a fixed space (say 0 to 2^32-1) that has nothing to do with the server count, so a key's ring position never changes when servers come and go. Only the ownership boundaries between nodes shift — that decoupling of key placement from N is the entire trick.","explanation":"Key hash positions are independent of node count (fixed hash space)"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-26","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A photo-sharing app shrinks its modulo-sharded cache fleet from 8 nodes to 6 to cut costs, expecting only the two removed nodes' keys — about 25% — to relocate. After the change, latency spikes across the entire app. What did they miscalculate?","answer":"Removing nodes changes N in hash(key) % N, which remaps most keys — here roughly 75%, not 25% — so far more of the cache went cold than just the removed nodes' share. Only consistent hashing limits scale-in movement to the removed nodes' own ranges.","explanation":"Modulo remapping on node removal (scale-in) is equally costly"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-27","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"two-choice","question":"Apache Cassandra and Amazon DynamoDB both rely on consistent hashing to place partitions across nodes.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"A","explanation":"Cassandra distributes data around its token ring using consistent hashing, and DynamoDB uses consistent hashing under the hood for partition placement, which is why both scale out with minimal data reshuffling."},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-28","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A streaming service adds a cache server to its ring every few weeks as its catalog grows. On the old design, where each server held one ring position, every capacity add crushed a single existing server, which had to ship its donated key range to the newcomer while still serving production traffic. After migrating to virtual nodes, what changes about the next capacity add?","answer":"The new server's many virtual positions land all over the ring, so it takes a small slice from many different servers instead of one big slice from a single neighbor. The migration work is spread across the whole fleet, and no single donor gets overwhelmed.","explanation":"With virtual nodes, an added node pulls small slices from many donors instead of one"},
  {"id":"hi-consistent-hashing-29","topic":"core-concepts · consistent hashing","type":"open","question":"A caching fleet mixes old servers with 32 GB of RAM and new ones with 128 GB. With the same number of virtual nodes assigned to every server, the big machines sit mostly empty while the old ones run out of memory. Without replacing hardware or abandoning consistent hashing, how do they make the big machines carry more of the data?","answer":"Assign the 128 GB servers proportionally more virtual nodes — about four times as many — so they own a correspondingly larger share of the ring. Virtual-node count doubles as a capacity weight: a server's expected fraction of keys is proportional to its number of ring positions.","explanation":"Weighted virtual nodes let heterogeneous servers own ring shares proportional to capacity"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-01","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A three-person startup runs its project-management SaaS on a mid-size cloud database instance nearing 70% CPU. The lead engineer proposes a six-week project to shard the database across four nodes before the next marketing push. What should the team do instead, and why?","answer":"Scale vertically first — move to a larger instance. It is an operationally trivial change, a modern single machine handles enormous load, and sharding's complexity is only justified once a big box genuinely cannot keep up.","explanation":"Vertical scaling as the default first move"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-02","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A brokerage app has responded to growth by repeatedly upgrading its single database server; it is now on the largest instance its cloud provider offers and usage is still climbing. Last month that instance's hardware failed and the entire product was down for an hour. What two limits of the team's scaling strategy has it just hit?","answer":"Vertical scaling has a hard ceiling — there is always a biggest machine — and it concentrates the whole system on a single point of failure. The team now needs horizontal scaling and redundancy, not another upgrade.","explanation":"Limits of vertical scaling: hardware ceiling and single point of failure"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-03","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing company moved surge-price computation from one large server to ten smaller workers. Now some workers sit idle while others fall behind, and every worker spends much of its time fetching trip data over the network from other machines. What two fundamental problem classes did the move introduce?","answer":"Work distribution and data distribution. Going horizontal forces you to route work evenly across nodes and to keep data near the compute that needs it — neither problem existed on the single machine.","explanation":"Horizontal scaling introduces work distribution and data distribution problems"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-04","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A multiplayer game keeps each live match's state in the server process's memory. To handle launch traffic, ops adds five more instances behind a round-robin load balancer, and players immediately start getting 'match not found' errors mid-game. What is going wrong?","answer":"The service is stateful, so naive horizontal scaling breaks it: each request round-robins to a random instance, but the match state lives in only one process's memory. Either externalize the state or route each match's traffic to the instance that owns it.","explanation":"Statelessness as a prerequisite for naive horizontal scaling"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-05","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A video platform's transcoding API sits behind a round-robin load balancer. Requests range from 10-second clips to 2-hour movies, and some servers are pegged while others idle. What should the team change about the load balancing, and why?","answer":"Switch from round robin to a load-aware strategy such as least-connections or utilization-based routing. Round robin assumes requests cost roughly the same; with wildly variable work sizes it piles heavy jobs onto unlucky servers.","explanation":"Load-aware balancing vs round robin for variable-cost work"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-06","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"An online-course platform sends enrollment confirmation emails synchronously inside the enrollment request handler. During a popular course launch, enrollments spike 50x, checkout latency balloons, and some emails are silently dropped when the mail provider throttles. What should the team change?","answer":"Put a queue between enrollment and email delivery. The queue absorbs the burst, workers drain it at the mail provider's sustainable rate, and the user-facing request no longer waits on — or fails because of — a slow side task.","explanation":"Queues to buffer bursts and decouple producers from workers"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-07","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A social feed service distributes fan-out work across workers by hashing the post author's ID. Whenever a celebrity with 40 million followers posts, one worker saturates while the rest idle, and feed delivery stalls. Why does the 'even' hash distribution fail here?","answer":"Hashing spreads keys evenly, not load — one skewed key can carry orders of magnitude more work than the others, creating a hot spot. Heavy keys must be split further, for example by partitioning the celebrity's fan-out into sub-tasks spread across many workers.","explanation":"Hot spots from skewed keys under hash distribution"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-08","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A home-goods marketplace's search service fans each query out to all 40 index shards and merges the results. Median latency is fine, but p99 is terrible — and it got worse after the team added shards to 'speed things up'. What is going on?","answer":"Scatter-gather latency is set by the slowest shard, so p99 tracks the worst straggler, and more shards raise the odds every query touches at least one slow node. It also amplifies failures: one bad shard degrades every single query.","explanation":"Scatter-gather tail latency and failure amplification"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-09","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A chat product stores messages partitioned by message ID. Opening a conversation now requires fetching from nearly every partition, and conversation loads are slow and fragile. What should change in how the data is distributed?","answer":"Co-locate related data: partition by conversation ID so all of a conversation's messages live together and a single node serves the read. The guiding rule of data distribution is designing so a typical request is answered by one node, not a cross-node fan-out.","explanation":"Data locality: co-locate related data so one node serves a request"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-10","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A B2B analytics service builds a large per-tenant in-memory cache to render dashboards. The service runs on eight nodes behind a round-robin balancer; cache hit rates are poor and every node ends up holding copies of the same tenants' data. What routing change fixes this, and why?","answer":"Route by tenant — sticky or consistent-hash routing — so a tenant's requests always land on the node already holding its cache. Affinity routing is the right choice when nodes hold valuable per-key state; spraying requests randomly destroys that locality.","explanation":"Affinity routing to exploit node-local caches"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-11","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A payments company replicates its ledger synchronously from Virginia to a replica in Frankfurt for durability. Since the change, every write takes an extra ~100ms, and during a Frankfurt network incident all writes started failing outright. Why is this the expected behavior of the chosen design?","answer":"Synchronous replication makes every write wait for the replica's acknowledgment, so writes inherit the cross-region round trip and the replica's availability. That is the trade: stronger durability and consistency in exchange for write latency and coupled failure.","explanation":"Synchronous replication's write-latency and availability cost"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-12","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"On a travel-booking site, a user updates their home airport, is redirected to their profile, and sees the old value; refreshing a few seconds later shows the new one. Reads are served from database replicas. What is happening, and what is a standard fix?","answer":"Asynchronous replication lag: the write hit the primary but the profile read hit a replica that had not applied it yet, violating read-your-writes. Route that user's reads to the primary briefly after a write (or pin the session) rather than abandoning replicas.","explanation":"Asynchronous replication lag and read-your-writes violations"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-13","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"An industrial-sensor platform acknowledges writes as soon as the primary database commits, replicating to a standby asynchronously. The primary's host died, the standby was promoted, and several seconds of acknowledged sensor readings are simply gone. Why?","answer":"With async replication the primary acknowledges before the standby has the data, so any writes not yet shipped are lost when you fail over. If acknowledged data must survive failover, those writes need synchronous replication, paid for in latency.","explanation":"Data loss on failover with asynchronous replication"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-14","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A news site is 95% reads and its single database is CPU-bound during traffic peaks; writes are a trickle. Half the team wants to shard now. What is the better first move, and why?","answer":"Add read replicas and send read traffic to them — read-heavy load scales nearly linearly with replicas and needs no rearchitecture. Sharding solves write-throughput and data-volume limits this site does not have, at far higher complexity.","explanation":"Read replicas before sharding for read-heavy workloads"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-15","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"During a sneaker drop, a store oversells: with one pair left, two buyers both saw stock '1', both passed the availability check, and both orders were accepted. The stock check and the decrement are separate queries. What class of bug is this, and what is the general remedy?","answer":"A race condition on shared state — concurrent read-modify-write with no concurrency control. The check and decrement must be made atomic: a conditional or atomic decrement, a transaction holding a lock, or equivalent serialization of the update.","explanation":"Race conditions on shared state require atomicity"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-16","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A note-taking app is adding conflict protection for saves. Two users editing the same note at the same moment is rare, but the team plans to grab a database row lock at the start of every editing session. Which concurrency approach fits better, and why?","answer":"Optimistic concurrency control: store a version on the note, write with a version check, and retry or surface a conflict on the rare mismatch. When contention is rare, paying lock overhead on every save — and holding locks for whole sessions — is pure waste.","explanation":"Optimistic concurrency control for low contention"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-17","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"An auction site is melting down in the final minute of a hot auction, when hundreds of bids per second target one item: nearly every write fails its version check, retries, and fails again. What should the team switch to, and why?","answer":"Pessimistic control — take a lock, or funnel bids for hot items through a single serialized writer or queue. Optimistic concurrency assumes conflicts are rare; under heavy contention, retry storms burn throughput on work that mostly gets thrown away.","explanation":"Pessimistic locking for high-contention hot keys"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-18","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A ticketing system takes one lock covering an event's entire seat map for every purchase. Big shows sell one seat at a time while thousands of buyers queue, even though almost none of them want the same seat. What is the design flaw?","answer":"Lock granularity is far too coarse: buyers of different seats do not actually conflict, but the event-wide lock serializes them anyway. Lock per seat so only genuine conflicts have to wait.","explanation":"Lock granularity"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-19","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A hotel-booking checkout locks the room-inventory row, then calls a third-party payment API that takes 2–5 seconds, then commits and releases. Under load, database connections pile up and unrelated bookings stall. What rule is being broken?","answer":"Hold locks as briefly as possible — and never across a slow external call. Reserve the room in a short transaction (for example a temporary hold with an expiry), make the payment call without holding the database lock, then finalize.","explanation":"Lock duration: never hold locks across slow external calls"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-20","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A banking backend processes a transfer by locking the sender's account row first, then the receiver's. When Alice sends money to Bob at the same instant Bob sends money to Alice, both transactions freeze until the database aborts one of them a few seconds later. What happened, and what convention prevents it?","answer":"A deadlock: each transaction holds one lock while waiting for the lock the other holds, so neither can ever proceed. Acquire locks in a single global order — for example, always lock the lower account ID first — so no cycle of transactions waiting on each other can form.","explanation":"Deadlock avoidance via consistent lock ordering"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-21","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A freight-dispatch system takes a Redis lock per truck while assigning loads. A worker crashed mid-assignment last week, and since then one truck has never been offered a load — its lock is still held by the dead process. What is the missing safeguard?","answer":"Distributed locks need a TTL/expiry, ideally with fencing to guard against the stale holder returning. A crashed process can never release its lock, so a lock with no timeout orphans the resource forever.","explanation":"Distributed locks need TTL/expiry"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-22","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"In an invoicing SaaS, a logged-in user at company A changes the numeric ID in an invoice URL and successfully downloads company B's invoice. Login is working perfectly. Which security control is missing?","answer":"Authorization. Authentication proved who the user is, but nothing checked whether that identity may access that specific resource — every data access needs a per-resource permission check, not just a valid session.","explanation":"Authentication vs authorization"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-23","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A fintech's twelve microservices each implement their own token validation and rate limiting; audits keep finding inconsistencies, and one internal service was discovered accepting expired tokens for months. What architectural change addresses the root cause?","answer":"Put an API gateway in front and centralize authentication, token validation, and rate limiting there. One enforcement point applied to every request beats twelve hand-rolled copies that drift and get forgotten.","explanation":"API gateway as central security enforcement point"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-24","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A smart-home vendor's devices report telemetry to the cloud over plain HTTP because 'the data is just temperatures' and TLS costs CPU on cheap chips. A security review rejects this. What is the core objection?","answer":"All traffic needs encryption in transit — plaintext lets anyone on the path read or tamper with the data, and 'harmless' telemetry still leaks occupancy patterns and permits spoofed or modified messages. TLS is table stakes, not optional per data type.","explanation":"Encryption in transit is table stakes"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-25","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A journaling app markets 'not even our employees can read your entries.' Engineering proposes enabling the database's disk-level encryption at rest and calling it done. Does that deliver the promise?","answer":"No. Encryption at rest protects against stolen disks, but the application — and thus employees or attackers with app or database access — still sees plaintext. The promise requires application-level or end-to-end encryption with keys the client controls, so servers only handle ciphertext.","explanation":"At-rest encryption vs application-level encryption with client-held keys"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-26","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A real-estate site's listing API is being hammered by scrapers pulling the whole catalog, degrading the database for real users. Each individual request is well-formed and authenticated. What control directly addresses this?","answer":"Rate limiting — cap requests per API key, IP, or user over a time window so no single caller can bulk-extract data or consume outsized capacity. It protects both the data behind exposed endpoints and the system's capacity.","explanation":"Rate limiting against abuse and scraping"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-27","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A dating app serves profiles at /profiles/48291, with IDs assigned sequentially. A researcher writes a loop and downloads every profile on the platform in an afternoon. Beyond missing rate limits, what design mistakes enabled the mass harvest?","answer":"Enumerable sequential IDs combined with missing per-resource authorization: anyone could guess every valid identifier and fetch it. Use unguessable IDs such as UUIDs and verify the requester is actually allowed to view each profile.","explanation":"Preventing resource enumeration: unguessable IDs plus access checks"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-28","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A podcast-hosting company's upload service degrades every few days and eventually crashes; each time, an engineer SSHes in afterward and discovers the host's disk had filled with temp files. Which monitoring layer is missing, and what does it watch?","answer":"Infrastructure monitoring — host-level metrics like CPU, memory, disk usage, and network. A basic disk-usage alert would have flagged the problem days before it became an outage.","explanation":"Infrastructure-level monitoring"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-29","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery company's support queue is full of 'the app is slow' complaints, but engineering can only shrug — nobody can say which endpoints are slow, how slow, or for what fraction of requests. What should they instrument first?","answer":"Service-level monitoring: per-endpoint request rate, error rate, and latency percentiles such as p95/p99. Those signals turn 'feels slow' into a measurable, alertable statement about how the service is behaving.","explanation":"Service-level monitoring: rate, errors, latency percentiles"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-30","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"After a Friday deploy, a streaming service's dashboards stayed green all weekend — CPU normal, latencies fine, error rates low — yet Monday reveals sign-ups flatlined at zero for 60 hours because the deploy broke the final sign-up step in the client. What monitoring gap does this expose?","answer":"Application-level monitoring: track business metrics like sign-ups per minute and alert on anomalies. Infrastructure and service metrics can be perfectly healthy while the product itself is broken.","explanation":"Application-level (business-metric) monitoring"},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-31","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"two-choice","question":"An e-commerce team can configure only one high-urgency page for its checkout service. Which alert should wake an engineer at 3am?","optionA":"Page when any host's CPU exceeds 80%","optionB":"Page when the checkout success rate drops below its normal baseline","correct":"B","explanation":"Alert on user-facing symptoms because they capture every failure mode that matters, while cause-level signals like CPU page noisily and miss failures that never touch CPU."},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-32","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"two-choice","question":"Opening a system design interview with a fully distributed, sharded, multi-region architecture demonstrates seniority.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Seniority shows in starting simple and adding distribution only when the load numbers demand it. A single well-tuned machine or database goes further than most candidates assume, and unjustified complexity reads as a red flag."},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-33","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"two-choice","question":"During a system design interview for a standard consumer app, you reach the security aspects of your design. How should you handle them?","optionA":"Proactively go deep: token formats, cipher suites, and key-rotation schedules","optionB":"Name where authentication, authorization, and encryption sit in the design, going deeper only if the interviewer probes or the product is security-critical","correct":"B","explanation":"Security is usually a demonstrate-awareness topic, and unprompted deep dives burn time the design needs elsewhere."},
  {"id":"hi-core-concepts-overview-34","topic":"core-concepts · core concepts overview","type":"open","question":"A candidate opens a system design interview with ten minutes of back-of-envelope math — QPS, storage, bandwidth — before drawing a single box, then later decides to shard the database without citing any numbers at all. What is the right way to use estimation in an interview?","answer":"Run the math at decision points, not as an upfront ritual: a quick calculation belongs exactly where it justifies a choice, such as showing write volume exceeds one database before proposing sharding. Upfront number dumps burn time nobody uses, while scaling choices made without numbers are unjustified.","explanation":"Capacity estimation at decision points, not upfront"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-01","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"In a mock system design interview for a food-delivery app, a candidate spends 18 of their 35 minutes producing a meticulous entity-relationship diagram: every table in third normal form, every column typed, every constraint labeled. The schema is technically flawless, but the interviewer cuts them off before they discuss scaling or caching. What did the candidate get wrong?","answer":"The bar for data modeling in an interview is a clear, functional schema aligned with requirements — not a complete normalization exercise. A good-enough model exists to enable the rest of the design discussion, and over-polishing it stole the time that discussion needed.","explanation":"Interview bar: good-enough schema over full normalization exercise"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-02","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"Asked to design a Ticketmaster-style booking system, a candidate races straight from functional requirements into drawing services and message queues. Ten minutes in, the interviewer interrupts: 'Wait — what are the main things this system actually stores?' The candidate has to backtrack awkwardly. Where in the interview should that question have been answered?","answer":"During requirements gathering, before any design work: data modeling shows up twice in the interview, first as naming the core entities (events, venues, tickets, bookings) up front, and only later as sketching the actual schema when the database is introduced. Naming entities early anchors the API and design discussion that follows.","explanation":"Identify core entities during requirements gathering, before schema design"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-03","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"Two co-founders are designing the backend for an event-ticketing platform: users, events, venues, orders, and refunds, with obvious relationships between them and no unusual scale requirements yet. One insists they should launch on a trendy NoSQL store 'to be modern.' Which choice fits, and why?","answer":"PostgreSQL or another relational database. The domain is ordinary related entities with consistency needs — exactly what SQL is built for — and you should only deviate from the relational default when a specific requirement clearly demands it, not for novelty.","explanation":"Default to relational/SQL unless requirements clearly demand otherwise"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-04","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"An engineer at a growing social app argues the team must migrate off Postgres immediately because they're projected to hit 30 million users, and 'relational databases don't scale.' The current database is handling load fine. How should a teammate push back?","answer":"That's a misconception — companies like Facebook and Airbnb run enormous systems on relational foundations. Scale comes from the architecture around the database (read replicas, sharding, caching, connection pooling), not from swapping the database category.","explanation":"SQL scales through architecture (replicas, sharding, caching), not by being abandoned"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-05","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A digital-wallet startup built peer-to-peer transfers on a simple store with no transaction support: the code decrements the sender's balance, then increments the receiver's. After a server crash mid-transfer, a user's money is debited but never credited anywhere. What is the fundamental fix?","answer":"The transfer must be atomic: both balance changes commit together or not at all. That means running it inside an ACID transaction on a database that supports them — the canonical reason financial operations belong on a transactional relational store.","explanation":"ACID transactions for correctness-critical multi-step operations"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-06","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A recipe platform stores each recipe as a deep tree — sections, steps, per-step ingredient variants, and equipment notes — and the app always loads the entire recipe at once. In their relational schema, rendering one recipe page requires joining six tables. Which alternative model fits this shape of data, and why?","answer":"A document database, embedding the whole recipe tree in one document. When deeply nested data is always read as a unit, embedding eliminates the join fan-out and makes each read a single document fetch.","explanation":"Document database fit: deeply nested data read as a single unit"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-07","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A marketplace's product catalog spans laptops (CPU, RAM, screen size), sneakers (size, colorway, material), and groceries (weight, expiry date, allergens). Their single SQL products table has grown to 60 columns, most of them NULL for any given row, and every new category adds more. What database model fits this data, and what is the telltale signal?","answer":"A document database, where each product is a document carrying only its own attributes. Records of the same logical type having vastly different structures is one of the genuine triggers for the document model — a sparse, ever-widening SQL table is the symptom.","explanation":"Document database fit: same-type records with wildly different structures"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-08","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce marketplace on MongoDB embeds the seller's store name inside every product document so listing pages render without lookups. A popular seller rebrands, and the migration script that rewrites their 40,000 product documents times out halfway, leaving search results showing two different store names. What trade-off did the team walk into?","answer":"Embedding duplicates the seller's name across every product, so reads are fast but a single logical update must touch thousands of documents. That update cost and the window of inconsistency are the price of document-style denormalization.","explanation":"Document embedding trade-off: duplicated data makes updates painful and inconsistent"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-09","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"two-choice","question":"In a system design interview, saying 'our schema might evolve over time' is usually a strong justification for choosing a document database over SQL.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Interview problems have deliberately narrow, scoped functional requirements, so genuinely evolving schemas almost never materialize. Leaning on schema flexibility usually signals reaching for a non-default database without a real reason."},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-10","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A B2B SaaS product validates a session token on every API request: given the token string, fetch the session record. It's millions of lookups per second, always by exact token, never any filtering or joining. What storage choice matches this workload?","answer":"A key-value store like Redis. The access pattern is a pure exact-key lookup at high volume — precisely what key-value stores are built for — and the lack of any richer query need means SQL's capabilities would be wasted overhead.","explanation":"Key-value store fit: exact-key lookups like sessions, caching, feature flags"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-11","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A news site's article pages are buckling under read traffic, and an engineer proposes migrating the entire content database from Postgres into Redis 'since Redis is faster.' What should the team do instead, and why?","answer":"Keep Postgres as the durable source of truth and put Redis in front as a cache for hot articles. Using both together is the standard pattern: you get fast reads for hot data without giving up durability and rich queries — a wholesale migration trades those away for nothing.","explanation":"SQL as source of truth with a key-value cache in front"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-12","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A gaming platform stores player profiles in a key-value store keyed by player_id, but the login flow needs to look players up by email — and there are no joins or secondary queries available. A new hire suggests scanning the whole keyspace on each login. What is the idiomatic key-value modeling approach instead?","answer":"Write the data under a second key: maintain an email-to-player mapping (or a duplicate record keyed by email) alongside the player_id record. In key-value stores you denormalize across keys, keeping one entry per access pattern, because there is no join or secondary query to fall back on.","explanation":"Key-value modeling duplicates data under multiple keys, one per access pattern"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-13","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"An IoT fleet platform ingests two million sensor readings per second — pure appends, never updated — and dashboards query them by device and time range. Their Postgres instance can't keep up with the write volume even after tuning. What class of database is purpose-built for this, and what makes it fit?","answer":"A wide-column store like Cassandra. These are designed for enormous append-heavy write throughput and time-series access, modeling data around device-plus-time query patterns — exactly this telemetry workload's shape.","explanation":"Wide-column database fit: massive write-heavy, append-only, time-series workloads"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-14","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"Designing a Twitter-style product in an interview, a candidate announces they'll use Neo4j 'because followers form a social graph.' The functional requirements are following users, posting, and viewing a feed. Why is this choice likely to hurt them?","answer":"Having relationships in the domain doesn't require a graph database — a follows junction table in SQL handles this pattern at far lower operational complexity. Even graph-heavy companies like Facebook and LinkedIn built their core relationship data on relational stores, so the exotic pick reads as showing off, not reasoning.","explanation":"Graph databases are rarely justified; SQL handles most relationship data"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-15","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A video-streaming service is designing its schema: a few million rows of video metadata, plus a projected petabyte-scale firehose of per-second view events. The team sketches both into one Postgres schema on one database. What requirement did they skip, and what should it have told them?","answer":"They never sized the data. Volume estimates should come before schema design, and here they'd show the event firehose can't live beside the modest metadata — the two belong in physically separate stores with schemas that reference each other, not one shared database.","explanation":"Data volume drives physical separation of stores and schemas"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-16","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A team building a project-management SaaS designs their 'ideal' schema in isolation, then defines the API afterward. The dashboard endpoint turns out to need a five-way join across tables that were organized by conceptual purity, and it's unusably slow. What should they have done differently from the start?","answer":"Enumerate the access patterns first — what queries each endpoint actually needs — and design the schema to serve them. Schema follows queries, not the reverse; a structure designed without knowing its reads is designed blind.","explanation":"Access patterns are the primary schema driver — schema follows queries"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-17","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing platform routes everything — driver payout balances and the 'your recent trips' activity feed — through one strongly consistent transactional store, and the feed's write volume is now throttling payment processing. How should they split their consistency requirements?","answer":"Match consistency to the data: payouts are financial and need strong ACID consistency, but a trips feed tolerates eventual consistency and can move to a separate, loosely coupled system. Applying the strictest guarantee to everything wastes the headroom the feed never needed.","explanation":"Consistency requirements differ per data type: strong for money, eventual for activity feeds"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-18","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"Two candidates designing a social feed both denormalize a like_count column onto the posts table. The first just announces the change and moves on. The second explains that feeds must load quickly and like counts can lag by a few seconds, so the read win is worth the consistency cost. Why does the second candidate score better with an identical schema?","answer":"Because they explicitly tied the decision to the system's requirements — read latency and an eventual-consistency tolerance — which demonstrates reasoning rather than a memorized pattern. In interviews, the justification connecting a schema choice to volume, access-pattern, and consistency requirements matters as much as the choice itself.","explanation":"Justify schema decisions aloud by tying them to requirement factors"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-19","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A fitness-studio SaaS used member email addresses as the primary key of its members table, with bookings and payments referencing that email. A member changes their email address, and the update now has to cascade through every referencing table or break history. What was the original design mistake?","answer":"Using business data as the primary key. Emails change because business facts change; a system-generated member_id would have stayed stable forever, which is exactly why surrogate keys are preferred over natural keys.","explanation":"System-generated surrogate primary keys instead of natural business keys"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-20","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A hobbyist forum lets users delete their accounts. Months later, thread pages start crashing: thousands of posts reference author IDs that no longer exist in the users table. What database-level protection was missing?","answer":"Foreign key constraints. A foreign key from posts.author_id to users would have refused the delete or forced explicit handling (cascade or reassignment), preventing orphaned posts that point at nonexistent users.","explanation":"Foreign keys enforce referential integrity and prevent orphaned records"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-21","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"An ad-tech company ingesting hundreds of thousands of impression rows per second finds that foreign key validation is a measurable share of write latency. What trade-off do some large-scale systems make in this situation, and what does it cost?","answer":"They drop database-level foreign key enforcement to reclaim write throughput and enforce referential integrity in application code instead. The cost is that correctness now depends on every write path getting it right — the database no longer guarantees it.","explanation":"Dropping FK enforcement at extreme scale in favor of application-level integrity"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-22","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"Modeling a blogging platform where each post has exactly one author but a user writes many posts, a junior engineer adds a post_ids array column to the users table and appends to it on every publish. Lookups and integrity checks quickly get awkward. What is the standard relational way to model this relationship?","answer":"Put a user_id foreign key column on the posts table — in a one-to-many relationship the foreign key lives on the 'many' side. An ID array on the 'one' side can't be enforced by the database, complicates queries, and turns every publish into a read-modify-write on the user row.","explanation":"One-to-many relationships put the foreign key on the many side"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-23","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A music app is modeling playlists: a song can appear in many playlists, and a playlist holds many songs. A junior engineer adds a playlist_id column to the songs table, and immediately a song can only belong to one playlist. What is the correct relational structure?","answer":"A junction table — playlist_songs with foreign keys to both playlists and songs. Many-to-many relationships can't be expressed with a column on either side; they need the linking table.","explanation":"Many-to-many relationships require a junction table"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-24","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"During a schema review at a CRM startup, someone notices a users table and a user_preferences table with a strict one-row-to-one-row relationship, always inserted, fetched, and deleted together. What does this pattern usually indicate?","answer":"That the split is unnecessary — a strict 1:1 relationship usually means the two tables should be merged into one. Genuine reasons to keep 1:1 splits (like isolating huge rarely-read columns) are the exception, and none apply here.","explanation":"One-to-one relationships are rare and usually signal tables should merge"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-25","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A survey platform has three services writing to the same accounts table, each with its own validation code. The table now contains null emails and duplicate usernames because one service's checks lagged behind the others. What layer should own these rules, and what's the trade?","answer":"The database, via NOT NULL, UNIQUE, and CHECK constraints. Enforcing correctness at the database level makes it impossible for any writer to sneak bad data in, at the cost of a little extra validation work per write — a trade worth taking here.","explanation":"Database-level constraints protect data quality at the cost of write overhead"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-26","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A job board's GET /companies/{id}/jobs endpoint has gotten slow as the jobs table grew to 50 million rows; the query plan shows a full table scan filtering on company_id. What is the first schema-level fix, and what general principle does it illustrate?","answer":"Add an index on jobs.company_id. The principle: indexes should map directly to your most important queries — read each endpoint's query and make sure an index serves it, rather than indexing speculatively.","explanation":"Choose indexes to directly serve the most important queries/API endpoints"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-27","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A photo-sharing app's profile page runs 'the 20 most recent photos by this user.' The photos table already has one index on user_id and another on created_at, yet the query still sorts millions of rows and is slow. What index actually serves this query?","answer":"A composite index on (user_id, created_at). It lets the database jump to the user's rows already ordered by time, satisfying the filter and the sort together — two separate single-column indexes can't do both at once.","explanation":"Composite indexes for combined filter-plus-sort access patterns"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-28","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A CRM stores the customer's company name on every one of that company's contact rows. After a client rebrands, a batch update misses some rows, and sales reps now see two different company names depending on which contact they open. What schema principle was violated?","answer":"Normalization — each fact should be stored exactly once. Because the company name was duplicated across rows, an update could reach some copies and miss others; a companies table referenced by foreign key makes that anomaly impossible.","explanation":"Normalization stores each fact once and prevents update anomalies"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-29","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"To make feed loads fast, a social app copied a like_count column onto the posts table instead of counting the likes table per request. Months later, the counts have drifted noticeably from the real like rows. What is the nature of this bug, and what discipline does the design demand?","answer":"This is the consistency cost of denormalization: the count is a duplicated derived fact, so every path that adds or removes a like must also update it — atomically or with periodic reconciliation. Miss one write path and the copies drift; that maintenance burden is the price paid for the fast read.","explanation":"Deliberate denormalization requires every write path to maintain the duplicated copies"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-30","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A hotel-booking startup with zero users yet is designing its first schema, and an engineer proposes duplicating hotel data into bookings, reviews, and search tables up front 'so we're ready for scale.' What is wrong with this plan?","answer":"It's premature denormalization. The rule is to start with a clean normalized model and denormalize only when a real, measured read pattern demands it — otherwise you take on permanent update-consistency burdens to solve a performance problem that doesn't exist.","explanation":"Start normalized; denormalize only when a demonstrated read pattern requires it"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-31","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A new engineer at an e-commerce company files a bug: the orders table copies product name and price at purchase time instead of joining to the products table, which they flag as a normalization violation. Why is the existing design actually correct?","answer":"Order rows are point-in-time snapshots: the customer bought that product at that price, and the record must not change when the catalog is renamed or repriced later. Snapshot-style data — orders, event logs, audit trails — is a legitimate exception where duplication is the requirement, not a flaw.","explanation":"Point-in-time snapshots (orders, audit logs) are legitimate denormalization"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-32","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A BI team maintains a reporting warehouse of pre-aggregated daily revenue tables — each row duplicates region names, product categories, and computed totals from the transactional system, all rebuilt by a nightly job. A backend engineer flags the duplication as bad schema design. Why doesn't the 'start normalized' rule condemn this system?","answer":"Analytics and reporting workloads are a recognized denormalization exception: the aggregated data changes infrequently, is rebuilt wholesale by a controlled pipeline, and read speed matters far more than update consistency. The anomaly risks that make duplication dangerous in a transactional schema barely apply here.","explanation":"Analytics and read-optimized systems are legitimate denormalization exceptions"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-33","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A SaaS dashboard's landing page needs a 'project summary' assembled from an expensive multi-table join, and the team is debating adding duplicated summary columns to the projects table to speed it up. What alternative keeps the schema clean, and why is it preferable?","answer":"Compute the summary and serve it from a cache like Redis, keeping the normalized tables as the source of truth. The cache delivers the denormalized read performance, while an invalidated or expired cache entry is far easier to correct than duplicated columns that have drifted.","explanation":"Serve denormalized views from a cache instead of denormalizing the schema"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-34","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A chat platform has outgrown one database and must shard its messages table. The overwhelmingly dominant query is 'load all messages in a conversation.' One engineer proposes sharding by a hash of message_id for perfectly even distribution. What's wrong with that, and what key fits?","answer":"Hashing message_id scatters each conversation across every shard, so the dominant query becomes a cross-shard scatter-gather. Shard by conversation_id instead: the shard key should follow the primary access pattern so related data lives together on one shard.","explanation":"Choose the shard key from the primary access pattern to co-locate related data"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-35","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A logistics-telemetry system sharded its GPS-ping data by month, reasoning that dashboards mostly query recent data. In production, the current month's shard is at 100% write capacity while eleven older shards sit idle. What went wrong?","answer":"Time-range sharding routes every current write to the newest partition, creating a hot shard — a classic anti-pattern for write-heavy workloads. It only suits archival or analytics data where writes aren't concentrated in the present.","explanation":"Time-range sharding creates a hot shard in write-heavy systems"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-36","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"open","question":"A social feed service sharded posts by the author's user_id. Building a user's home timeline now means querying the shards of all 500 accounts they follow and merging the results, and latency is terrible. What does this fan-out reveal about the design?","answer":"The shard key doesn't match this read pattern — routine cross-shard scatter-gather is the telltale symptom. The fix is designing so the hot query resolves within a shard, for example by precomputing each user's feed, rather than accepting cross-shard queries as normal.","explanation":"Frequent cross-shard queries are the symptom of a shard key mismatched to the read pattern"},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-37","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"two-choice","question":"Changing a shard key after launch is a routine migration, so it's reasonable to pick one quickly and revisit the choice once real traffic patterns emerge.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Resharding means redistributing essentially all your data and revisiting every query that depended on the old key, so a shard key is effectively permanent. That's exactly why it must be chosen against your primary access patterns up front."},
  {"id":"hi-data-modeling-38","topic":"core-concepts · data modeling","type":"two-choice","question":"During the high-level design portion of a system design interview, you're introducing the database. Which sequence is the stronger way to present it?","optionA":"Pick the database type (usually SQL), lay out each entity's columns to satisfy the functional requirements, mark primary and foreign keys, call out indexes for the key queries, and bring up denormalization or sharding only if the requirements demand them.","optionB":"Lead with the sharding key and the denormalizations you'll need at scale, then work backward to define the tables and columns that fit that partitioning scheme.","correct":"A","explanation":"Requirements drive the model; scaling machinery like sharding and denormalization is layered on only when justified, not assumed from the start."},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-01","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce site's order-history page takes 30 seconds to load. The query filters a 40-million-row orders table by customer email, and the query plan shows the database reading every page of the table from disk. What is going wrong, and what is the fix?","answer":"The query is doing a full table scan because there is no index on the email column, so the database must read every page to find matches. Adding a B-tree index on customer email gives it a direct path to only the pages holding the matching rows.","explanation":"Index purpose: avoiding full-table scans"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-02","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A junior engineer at a payments processor is puzzled: fetching a single 200-byte transaction by its reference number takes several seconds on an unindexed 100-million-row table. 'The row is tiny — why is this slow?' What explains the cost?","answer":"Rows sit unordered in heap files on disk, so without an index the database loads page after page into memory until it stumbles on the match. The cost is proportional to the number of disk pages scanned, not the size of the row returned.","explanation":"Heap files and page-based disk access cost"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-03","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A logistics company migrates its database servers from spinning disks to NVMe SSDs. An engineer proposes skipping index design on the new tables, reasoning that SSD random reads are effectively free. What is wrong with this reasoning?","answer":"Random access is still significantly slower than sequential access even on SSDs — the gap is smaller than on HDDs but it has not disappeared. Scans and scattered reads still cost real time at scale, so indexing remains essential.","explanation":"Random vs sequential access still differs on SSDs"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-04","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A project-management SaaS adds indexes on nearly every column of its 500 GB tasks table 'in case someone queries it.' Disk consumption roughly doubles and the ops team demands an explanation. What is going on?","answer":"Each index is a full separate data structure on disk that can consume nearly as much space as the data it indexes, so indexing every column multiplies storage. Only columns that queries actually filter or sort on should be indexed.","explanation":"Index storage overhead"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-05","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A ticketing platform's checkout inserts into an orders table that has accumulated six secondary indexes over time. Insert latency has crept up with each index added, and disk write volume per order is now several times the row size. What is the mechanism behind the slowdown?","answer":"Every insert must update not just the table but every index on it, so one logical write fans out into several physical disk writes. Six indexes mean roughly seven structures updated per checkout.","explanation":"Write amplification: each write updates every index"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-06","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"An IoT platform writes millions of sensor readings per hour into a raw audit table that analysts query about once a month. An engineer added five indexes to speed up those monthly queries, and ingestion throughput dropped sharply. What should the team change, and why?","answer":"Drop the indexes: on a write-heavy, rarely-read table, paying index maintenance on every one of millions of inserts to save one monthly query is a terrible trade. Let the monthly analysis run a sequential scan, or build a temporary index just before it.","explanation":"Skip indexing write-heavy, rarely-read tables"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-07","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A travel app developer adds an index to a 300-row reference table of airport codes and is annoyed that the query planner keeps ignoring it in favor of scanning the table. Is anything broken?","answer":"No — for a table that fits in a page or two, a sequential scan is cheaper than traversing an index structure, so the planner is correct to skip it. Indexes only pay off when they save meaningful numbers of page reads.","explanation":"Tiny tables: sequential scan beats an index"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-08","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A ten-year-old CRM has fourteen indexes on its contacts table, accreted by different teams. Nobody knows which are used, and the team fears both write overhead and 'memory pressure' from having so many. What is the right course of action?","answer":"Check the database's index usage statistics and drop every index no query uses — an unused index is pure write and storage cost. The memory fear is mostly overblown because modern buffer pool management handles caching well; the real waste is the maintenance work on every write.","explanation":"Auditing and dropping unused indexes"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-09","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A gaming studio's players table holds 100 million rows, yet an engineer inspecting the primary-key index finds the tree is only about four levels deep. Why is it so shallow?","answer":"B-tree nodes are sized to fill a disk page (typically ~8 KB) and therefore hold hundreds of keys each, giving enormous fan-out. With hundreds of children per node, a handful of levels is enough to cover hundreds of millions of rows.","explanation":"B-tree fan-out sized to disk pages"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-10","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A hotel-booking service grows its reservations table from 10 million to 100 million rows over a year. Lookups by reservation ID barely slow down at all. Why does the lookup cost stay nearly flat despite 10x growth?","answer":"A B-tree lookup costs only a few page reads — root, maybe an internal node, then a leaf — and depth grows logarithmically with a huge branching factor. A 10x data increase adds at most one level, so lookups stay at roughly 2-4 page reads.","explanation":"B-tree lookup depth stays flat as data grows"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-11","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A fitness app needs two query patterns: exact lookup of users by email, and listing a user's workouts between two dates. One engineer proposes a hash structure for the exact matches plus a separate specialized structure for the date ranges. What is the simpler standard answer, and why does it work?","answer":"Use B-tree indexes for both: because a B-tree keeps keys sorted, it answers exact-match lookups and range/ORDER BY queries equally well. One index type covering both patterns is precisely why B-trees are the default everywhere.","explanation":"B-trees serve both equality and range queries"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-12","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"After bulk-importing 50 million tracks in random order into a music-streaming catalog, an engineer schedules a nightly job to rebuild the index, fearing that random inserts have made the tree lopsided and query times unpredictable. Is the job needed?","answer":"No — B-trees self-balance: node splits and merges during inserts and deletes automatically keep all leaves at the same depth. Performance stays predictable under random write patterns without manual rebuilds.","explanation":"B-tree self-balancing under random inserts/deletes"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-13","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"two-choice","question":"In a system design interview you must pick an index type for a standard relational workload — mixed exact lookups, range queries, and sorting, with no extreme write rate — and you have no other information. Which is the safer default?","optionA":"A B-tree index","optionB":"An LSM-tree-based store","correct":"A","explanation":"It handles equality, ranges, and ordering with predictable performance, which is why it is the default in virtually every relational database and the safe bet when in doubt."},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-14","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A storage-engine reading group at a fintech startup notices that MongoDB's docs, like most database documentation, describe the index as a B+ tree rather than a plain B-tree. A member asks what practical difference the plus sign makes. What is it?","answer":"In a B+ tree, actual data lives only in the leaf nodes — internal nodes hold just routing keys, so each page fits far more keys and the tree stays shallower — and the leaves are chained together in sorted order. That linked leaf chain lets a range query walk sequentially from the first match instead of re-traversing the tree.","explanation":"B+ tree variant: data in linked leaves only"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-15","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A job-board engineer, chasing performance, runs CREATE INDEX on the users table's primary-key id column and again on its UNIQUE email column. Queries get no faster, but writes get measurably slower. Why?","answer":"Primary-key and unique constraints already create B-tree indexes automatically, so the new indexes are exact duplicates of ones that exist. They add write and storage overhead while providing zero new lookup capability.","explanation":"Primary key and unique constraints auto-create indexes"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-16","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"An observability startup ingests millions of metric points per second into Cassandra, and a new hire asks how any disk-backed store can absorb that write rate. What is the storage-engine answer?","answer":"Cassandra uses an LSM tree: writes land in an in-memory memtable (with a write-ahead log for durability) and are periodically flushed to disk as immutable, sorted SSTables. That converts random writes into fast sequential disk writes, which is what sustains the ingest rate.","explanation":"LSM write path: memtable, WAL, SSTable flush"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-17","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A chat service stores messages in an LSM-based database. Writes are blazing fast, but reads of older conversations are slower than expected, and latency worsens as data files accumulate on disk. What is the inherent cause?","answer":"Read amplification: a key may live in the memtable or in any of many SSTables, so a read must consult multiple structures newest-to-oldest before finding the value. Slower reads are the built-in price LSM trees pay for their write speed.","explanation":"LSM read amplification"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-18","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A social-feed team running RocksDB notices that most point lookups touch only one data file on disk even though dozens of SSTables exist. A teammate asks what stops the engine from reading every SSTable on each lookup. What is the mechanism?","answer":"Bloom filters: each SSTable carries one, and a negative answer guarantees the key is not in that file, letting the engine skip it without any disk read. Only the files whose filter says 'maybe present' are actually read.","explanation":"Bloom filters skip SSTables on reads"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-19","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A feature-flag service stores flag values in LevelDB, overwriting the same keys thousands of times a day. Disk usage keeps growing even though the number of distinct flags is constant, and reads slowly degrade. What background process addresses both symptoms, and how?","answer":"Compaction: it merges SSTables in the background, keeping only the newest version of each key and discarding overwritten and deleted entries. That reclaims the disk space and cuts the number of files a read must check.","explanation":"Compaction merges SSTables and reclaims space"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-20","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A video-streaming company is picking storage engines for two services: one ingests a firehose of playback events (huge write volume, occasional analytics reads); the other serves the title catalog (rare writes, constant reads). Which index structure fits each service, and why?","answer":"LSM tree for the event firehose — sequential memtable flushes are built for sustained write throughput. B-tree for the catalog — read-heavy point and range lookups are its strength, and the low write rate makes its per-write cost irrelevant.","explanation":"Choosing LSM vs B-tree by read/write workload"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-21","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A stock-trading app added a hash index on the executed_at timestamp of its trades table to speed up time-based queries. Exact-timestamp lookups fly, but the everyday query — all trades in the last hour — still scans the whole table. Why doesn't the index help?","answer":"Hashing destroys ordering, so a hash index can only answer exact-match lookups — it has no concept of 'greater than' or 'between.' The last-hour query needs a B-tree on the timestamp, which keeps keys sorted for range scans.","explanation":"Hash indexes cannot answer range queries"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-22","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"An e-learning platform validates session tokens on every request: pure exact lookup by token, dataset fits comfortably in memory, sub-millisecond target, and range queries will never be needed. What lookup structure fits best, and where is this pattern seen in practice?","answer":"An in-memory hash index — O(1) average exact-match lookups, which is exactly how Redis works under the hood. On-disk databases rarely bother with hash indexes because B-trees are nearly as fast and far more flexible, but in-memory exact-match-only lookups are the hash sweet spot.","explanation":"Hash index sweet spot: in-memory exact match (Redis)"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-23","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery app's 'restaurants within 2 miles' query is slow despite B-tree indexes on both the latitude and longitude columns. The query plan shows one index selecting an enormous set of rows before filtering. Why do the two indexes fail here?","answer":"Each B-tree narrows only one dimension: a latitude range matches a thin band wrapping the entire globe, so millions of irrelevant rows survive before the longitude filter. Two-dimensional proximity needs a purpose-built geospatial index, not two independent one-dimensional ones.","explanation":"Why separate lat/lng B-trees fail proximity queries"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-24","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A dating app wants a 'people near you' feature but the team must stay on plain Postgres with ordinary B-tree indexes — no new infrastructure. An engineer suggests storing a geohash string per user. How does that make proximity search work on a regular B-tree?","answer":"Geohash interleaves latitude and longitude bits into one string, so nearby points mostly share string prefixes — turning a 2D proximity search into a 1D prefix-range scan a standard B-tree can serve. The caveat is cell boundaries: two close points can straddle a boundary and share no prefix, so neighboring cells must be searched too.","explanation":"Geohash: 2D-to-1D encoding with prefix locality"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-25","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A navigation app indexes points of interest worldwide: Manhattan has thousands per square mile while the open ocean has nearly none. The team wants a spatial structure whose resolution adapts to that skew. Which approach fits, and how does it adapt?","answer":"A quadtree: it recursively splits space into four quadrants and subdivides a quadrant only when it exceeds its point capacity. Dense areas like Manhattan end up finely subdivided while empty ocean stays coarse — the resolution follows the data.","explanation":"Quadtree: density-adaptive recursive subdivision"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-26","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A real-estate platform must index property parcels — polygons, not points — and answer 'which parcels intersect the rectangle the user drew on the map.' Point-encoding schemes don't capture parcel shapes. Which index structure is built for this, and what well-known system uses it?","answer":"An R-tree: it organizes geometries into a hierarchy of minimum bounding rectangles (which may overlap) and prunes entire subtrees whose rectangles miss the query window. This is the structure behind PostGIS-style spatial indexes.","explanation":"R-tree: bounding rectangles for shapes/polygons"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-27","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A blogging platform's search runs WHERE body LIKE '%kubernetes%' and full-scans the posts table; adding a B-tree on the body column changed nothing. What index concept is missing, and what does it look like?","answer":"A B-tree on text only accelerates prefix matches, so a contains-a-word query can't use it. Full-text search needs an inverted index mapping each term to the list of documents containing it — the structure behind Elasticsearch/Lucene and Postgres full-text search.","explanation":"Inverted index for full-text search"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-28","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A marketplace keeps listings in Postgres as the source of truth and mirrors them into Elasticsearch for full-text search, syncing through change data capture. A PM files a bug: a seller renames a listing and the old title still appears in search results for a second or two. Must the team fix this?","answer":"No — an external search index fed by change data capture is eventually consistent by design, so it always lags the primary database slightly, and for search use cases that staleness is almost always acceptable. Flows that need read-your-writes (like the seller's own edit page) should read from the primary, not the search index.","explanation":"External search index sync: CDC lag is acceptable"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-29","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"An events platform constantly runs a query for events in a given city on a given date. The table has one single-column index on city and another on event_date, yet the query is still slow: each index alone matches hundreds of thousands of rows. What indexing change fixes this, and why?","answer":"Create one composite index on (city, event_date): its entries are sorted by both columns together, so the query seeks directly to the narrow slice matching that exact city-and-date combination. Two separate indexes each narrow only one dimension, leaving the database to intersect or filter huge intermediate row sets.","explanation":"Composite index beats separate single-column indexes"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-30","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing dispatch service has a composite index on (city, driver_status). A query filtering only on driver_status = 'available' across all cities ignores the index entirely. Why?","answer":"Composite indexes obey the leftmost-prefix rule: entries are sorted by city first, so rows for one status are scattered across every city and can't be reached with a single seek. It's a phone book sorted by last name — useless for finding everyone named 'Sarah.'","explanation":"Composite index leftmost-prefix rule"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-31","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A helpdesk SaaS constantly runs WHERE tenant_id = ? AND created_at > ? against its tickets table. The composite index (created_at, tenant_id) underperforms badly. How should the index be ordered, and why?","answer":"(tenant_id, created_at): put the equality column first and the range column last, so each tenant's rows sit contiguously sorted by time and the query becomes one narrow range scan. With the range column first, the tenant's matching entries are scattered across the entire date range.","explanation":"Composite index column order: equality before range"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-32","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"open","question":"A subscription-billing dashboard runs SELECT plan, renewed_at FROM subscriptions WHERE account_id = ? thousands of times a minute. Even with an index on account_id, the profiler shows a table fetch for every matching row. What change eliminates those fetches?","answer":"Use a covering index that also stores plan and renewed_at (as extra key columns or via an INCLUDE clause). The query is then answered entirely from the index — an index-only scan — without ever touching the table.","explanation":"Covering index enables index-only scans"},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-33","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"two-choice","question":"Since covering indexes let queries skip the table entirely, stuffing every column a query might ever touch into wide covering indexes is a free performance win.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Every extra column makes the index larger on disk and more expensive to maintain on every write, so covering indexes should be reserved for hot, well-defined queries where the read savings clearly outweigh the cost."},
  {"id":"hi-db-indexing-34","topic":"core-concepts · db indexing","type":"two-choice","question":"Deep index internals — LSM compaction, disk page layouts, B-tree node structure — carry equal weight in every system design interview regardless of the role.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Infrastructure-focused interviews probe index internals deeply, while product and full-stack interviews typically expect only a working sense of when and why to add indexes; staff-level candidates are held to mastery of the trade-offs that mid-level candidates are not."},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-01","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A SaaS dashboard's backend logs show 25ms of processing per API call, yet real-user monitoring reports 250ms+. The web client opens a brand-new HTTPS connection for every single API call, and each screen makes a dozen calls. Where is the missing time going, and what should the team change?","answer":"Every fresh connection pays DNS resolution, the TCP handshake, and TLS negotiation — multiple round trips before the first byte of the actual request. Reuse connections via keep-alive / HTTP/2 multiplexing so the setup cost is paid once and amortized across calls.","explanation":"Hidden lower-layer latency: per-request connection setup and connection reuse"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-02","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"two-choice","question":"As long as the network hardware is healthy, IP (layer 3) guarantees that packets arrive at the destination in the order they were sent.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"IP is best-effort: it routes individual packets with no delivery, ordering, or duplication guarantees. Reliability and ordering are added by TCP at the transport layer (layer 4)."},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-03","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A fast-paced multiplayer shooter streams player positions over TCP. On lossy Wi-Fi, players see the game freeze for a moment, then everyone 'teleports' as a burst of updates arrives at once. What is going wrong, and what should the team change?","answer":"TCP retransmits lost packets and refuses to deliver newer data until older data arrives, so the client stalls waiting for position updates that are already obsolete. Switch to UDP: a lost position packet should simply be dropped because the next packet supersedes it — low latency matters more than reliable delivery here.","explanation":"UDP for latency-sensitive, loss-tolerant traffic (TCP retransmission stalls real-time data)"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-04","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A brokerage's back-office pipeline passes trade confirmations between internal services, and losing even one confirmation is unacceptable. An engineer proposes moving the pipeline to UDP 'because it's faster.' Which transport fits, and why?","answer":"TCP. Its acknowledgment, retransmission, and ordering guarantees ensure every confirmation arrives intact and in sequence; UDP offers no delivery guarantee, so a dropped datagram silently loses a trade record. The small latency win is irrelevant when every message must arrive.","explanation":"TCP's delivery and ordering guarantees for correctness-critical messages"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-05","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"An IoT platform uploads large sensor archives from powerful gateways to ingestion servers over TCP. A new engineer warns that the fast gateways will overwhelm slower servers' buffers and congested network links, and proposes designing an application-level throttling protocol before launch. What does TCP already provide here?","answer":"TCP flow control has the receiver advertise how much buffer space it has left, so a sender can never overrun a slow receiver, and TCP congestion control automatically backs the send rate off when the network shows signs of congestion. Custom pacing is what you build when you choose UDP, which has neither.","explanation":"TCP flow control and congestion control: the transport layer already paces senders"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-06","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"two-choice","question":"A browser game's JavaScript can open a raw UDP socket to the game server to reduce latency.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Browsers expose no raw UDP sockets. The only UDP-like transport available to web pages is WebRTC (data channels), so any design sending UDP to browser clients needs a WebRTC approach or a TCP-based fallback."},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-07","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A news app's users on high-latency cellular links complain the first article load is slow. Traces show most of the time is spent before the request is even sent: a TCP handshake followed by a TLS handshake, each costing full round trips. What protocol change addresses this, and how does it help?","answer":"Adopt HTTP/3 (QUIC). QUIC runs over UDP and folds transport setup and TLS encryption into a combined handshake with fewer round trips, while still providing TCP-like reliability — directly cutting the connection-establishment latency that dominates on high-RTT links.","explanation":"QUIC/HTTP3 cuts connection-establishment round trips"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-08","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A customer-support tool keeps 'which ticket this agent currently has open' in the web server's process memory. It worked with one server, but after scaling to three servers behind a round-robin load balancer, agents randomly see the wrong ticket or none at all. What is going wrong?","answer":"HTTP is stateless and the load balancer sends consecutive requests to different servers, so state stored in one server's memory is invisible to the others. Move per-user state into shared storage (database/cache) that any server can read, rather than relying on which machine handled the last request.","explanation":"HTTP statelessness: server-local session state breaks behind a load balancer"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-09","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce site adds a one-click reorder link in emails that hits GET /reorder?orderId=123. Customers start reporting duplicate orders they swear they never clicked; logs show hits from email security scanners and link prefetchers. What is the design mistake?","answer":"GET is defined as safe and side-effect free, and crawlers, scanners, and prefetchers rely on that by freely following GET URLs. Placing an order changes state, so it must be a POST triggered by an explicit user action — never a side effect of fetching a URL.","explanation":"GET must be safe and side-effect free"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-10","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"two-choice","question":"A client sends PUT /profiles/42 with a complete replacement profile document, and the request times out with no response. The client can safely resend the exact same request without any idempotency key.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"A","explanation":"PUT is idempotent by definition: replaying the same full replacement leaves the resource in the same final state whether or not the first attempt actually landed. A POST creating a resource would need an idempotency key, since each delivery could create another one."},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-11","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A ticketing platform's API returns HTTP 200 with a body like {\"success\": false, \"reason\": \"sold out\"} for every failure. The ops dashboard shows a 100% success rate during an outage, and a partner's HTTP client caches error responses and never retries. What should change, and why?","answer":"Return real status codes: 4xx for client-side problems, 5xx for server faults, 2xx only for success. Status code classes are what generic infrastructure — monitors, caches, retry logic, client libraries — keys off; wrapping every failure in a 200 makes errors invisible to all of it.","explanation":"Meaningful status code classes vs 200-with-error-body"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-12","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A neobank's balance endpoint reads account_id from the request body and returns that account's balance. When a reviewer objects, an engineer replies that it is safe 'because all traffic is HTTPS.' What is wrong with this reasoning?","answer":"TLS only protects bytes in transit from third-party eavesdropping and tampering — it does nothing about a malicious client, who can put any account_id they want in a perfectly encrypted request. Authorization must be enforced server-side by deriving the account from the authenticated session, never trusted from request content.","explanation":"HTTPS encrypts transit but does not make client-supplied data trustworthy"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-13","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A billing team ships an API with POST /createInvoice, POST /markInvoicePaid, and POST /fetchInvoice. A reviewer calls it 'RPC pretending to be REST.' What is the reviewer's point, and how should the API look?","answer":"REST models nouns (resources) and lets HTTP verbs express the operation, rather than encoding operations in endpoint names. It should be POST /invoices to create, GET /invoices/{id} to read, and PATCH/PUT /invoices/{id} to change state — which also restores correct verb semantics like idempotent, cacheable reads.","explanation":"REST models resources with HTTP verbs, not RPC-style operation endpoints"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-14","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A social app's mobile feed screen makes five sequential REST calls and discards roughly 80% of the fields it downloads; users on cellular see slow loads. Multiple client teams keep requesting slightly different response shapes. What API approach targets this problem, and how?","answer":"GraphQL. The client declares exactly the fields it needs and gets them in a single query, eliminating under-fetching (many round trips to assemble one screen) and over-fetching (downloading fields it throws away) — exactly the situation of many clients wanting different slices of the same data.","explanation":"GraphQL solves over-fetching and under-fetching"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-15","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"An internal payroll service has exactly two consumers with stable, well-known data needs that haven't changed in a year. A new hire proposes rewriting its REST API in GraphQL 'for flexibility.' Which approach fits better here, and why?","answer":"Keep REST. GraphQL's payoff is client-driven flexibility, which is worthless when there are two known clients with fixed requirements — you'd be adding query-execution complexity and potential latency on the server for no benefit. Simple resource endpoints are the right default.","explanation":"When GraphQL is not justified: fixed requirements, few clients"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-16","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing platform's pricing service calls its geo service millions of times per minute over JSON/REST. Profiling shows significant CPU burned on JSON encoding/decoding and payloads far larger than the data they carry. What should the team switch to, and why?","answer":"gRPC. Protocol Buffers give compact binary serialization (a fraction of equivalent JSON size) over HTTP/2, yielding roughly an order of magnitude better throughput than JSON/REST — and both ends are internal services the team controls, which is exactly gRPC's sweet spot.","explanation":"gRPC for high-throughput internal service-to-service communication"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-17","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A startup launches its public developer API as gRPC-only. Third-party developers complain they cannot call it directly from browsers and that onboarding requires compiling proto definitions and special tooling. What should the startup change?","answer":"Expose a REST/JSON API for the public surface and keep gRPC for internal service-to-service traffic. gRPC's binary protocol has poor browser support and imposes tooling burden on external consumers, while REST works from any HTTP client with zero setup.","explanation":"gRPC is a poor fit for public, browser-facing APIs"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-18","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A charity-auction site needs every bidder's screen to reflect new bids within a second. Bidders submit bids only occasionally, via ordinary POST requests. The team is sizing a WebSocket cluster for this. Which push approach fits better, and why?","answer":"Server-Sent Events. The real-time flow is strictly server-to-client (bid updates out; bids already arrive via normal POSTs), so SSE delivers the push over plain HTTP with built-in reconnection and none of WebSockets' bidirectional, stateful connection overhead. WebSockets are only warranted for high-frequency two-way traffic.","explanation":"SSE vs WebSockets: one-directional server push needs only SSE"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-19","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A live-scores feature built on SSE works perfectly on localhost, but in production some users receive no updates for minutes and then a burst of them all at once. What is the likely cause?","answer":"An intermediary — a reverse proxy, load balancer, or corporate proxy — is buffering the streamed HTTP response and flushing it in chunks instead of forwarding each event immediately. SSE requires response buffering to be disabled along the entire path, and this is a classic SSE deployment gotcha.","explanation":"SSE pitfall: intermediary buffering defeats streaming"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-20","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A notification stream uses SSE, and many users are commuters whose connections drop in tunnels. The PM asks whether those users silently lose the notifications sent while they were disconnected. What does SSE offer here, and what must the server do to take advantage of it?","answer":"The browser's EventSource automatically reconnects and sends the ID of the last event it received (Last-Event-ID). If the server assigns IDs to events and can replay everything after a given ID, reconnecting clients catch up on what they missed — without server-side replay support, those events are simply lost.","explanation":"SSE reconnection: auto-reconnect with Last-Event-ID replay"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-21","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery app's order-tracking page must show status changes within a couple of seconds. The team rejects half-second polling as wasteful — thousands of clients would hammer the API for mostly-empty responses — but their hosting stack makes SSE and WebSockets awkward to deploy. What technique gives them near-real-time updates using only ordinary HTTP requests?","answer":"Long polling: the client sends a normal request and the server holds it open until a status change occurs (or a timeout passes), responds, and the client immediately re-requests. Updates arrive almost instantly, the mostly-empty responses disappear, and every intermediary sees nothing but plain HTTP request/response traffic.","explanation":"Long polling: near-real-time updates over plain HTTP by holding the request open"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-22","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"An ops dashboard displays fleet metrics that refresh once per minute. An engineer proposes WebSockets 'to make it real-time.' Is this the right call?","answer":"No — it is overengineering. There is no high-frequency bidirectional traffic, so simple periodic polling (or SSE at most) does the job. WebSockets would force the backend to maintain a fleet of long-lived stateful connections, which is significantly more expensive to operate at scale than stateless HTTP, for zero user-visible benefit at a 60-second refresh rate.","explanation":"WebSockets only for high-frequency bidirectional needs; stateful connections are costly"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-23","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"An enterprise chat app's WebSocket connections work flawlessly in development and for consumer users, but connections fail to establish for many corporate customers. What is the likely cause, and what should the team do?","answer":"Every hop in the path — corporate firewalls, proxies, load balancers — must support the WebSocket upgrade and tolerate long-lived connections, and one non-cooperating intermediary kills the connection. Corporate middleboxes frequently don't. Ship a fallback transport (SSE or long polling) and ensure their own infrastructure is WebSocket-aware end-to-end.","explanation":"WebSockets require end-to-end intermediary support"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-24","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A video-calling startup finds that a meaningful fraction of call attempts between two home users never establish a media connection, even though signaling completes fine. Both users are behind home routers. What is happening, and what infrastructure is needed?","answer":"Both peers are behind NAT, so neither can reach the other's private address directly. STUN servers let each peer discover its public IP/port and attempt hole punching; when NATs are too restrictive for that to work, a TURN relay server must carry the media between them. A production WebRTC service needs both — and must budget for TURN relay bandwidth.","explanation":"WebRTC NAT traversal: STUN hole punching, TURN relay fallback"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-25","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"two-choice","question":"Because WebRTC connects peers directly, a video-calling product can ship with no server-side components at all.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Peers cannot find each other on their own. WebRTC requires a signaling server to exchange connection metadata before the peer link exists, plus STUN servers for public-address discovery and TURN relays for when direct connection fails."},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-26","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A B2B analytics API runs comfortably on one server that is starting to hit CPU limits at peak. One engineer wants to move to eight small instances behind a new load balancer; another wants to simply upgrade to a much larger instance. Traffic is predictable and modest. What is the reasonable default, and what does the eight-instance plan drag in?","answer":"Scale vertically first: a bigger machine solves the CPU problem with zero architectural change. Going horizontal is what forces you to add a load balancer, health checks, and the discipline that no request depends on one server's local state — complexity worth paying only when a single machine genuinely can't keep up or redundancy requirements demand multiple servers.","explanation":"Prefer vertical scaling for simplicity; horizontal scaling is what forces load balancing"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-27","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A logistics company's internal microservices all call each other through a central dedicated load balancer, adding a network hop to every internal RPC, and tight latency budgets are being blown. All clients are the company's own services. What alternative should they consider, and why is it viable here?","answer":"Client-side load balancing: each service pulls the healthy-instance list from a service registry and picks a backend directly, eliminating the intermediary hop entirely (gRPC supports this natively). It is viable precisely because the clients are few, internal, and controlled — the situations where pushing server lists to clients works well.","explanation":"Client-side load balancing via service registry removes the extra hop internally"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-28","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"During a migration, an e-commerce team repoints their domain's DNS records at new servers and immediately shuts down the old ones. For hours afterward, a chunk of users get connection errors. What did they miss?","answer":"DNS responses are cached by resolvers and clients for the record's TTL, so many users kept resolving to the dead IPs until their caches expired. Lower the TTL well before the cutover and keep the old servers running until the old records have aged out everywhere.","explanation":"DNS TTL caching constrains how fast endpoint changes propagate"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-29","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A SaaS company fronts everything with a single load balancer in one data center, and an audit flags it as a single point of failure. The team asks how anyone runs load balancers without the load balancer itself being the weak link. What is the standard answer?","answer":"Deploy load balancers in multiple data centers or regions and publish all of them in DNS, rotating the returned addresses. Different clients naturally spread across the LBs, and if one dies, clients fail over to the remaining addresses — DNS is the layer that balances the balancers.","explanation":"DNS rotation across multiple load balancers for fault tolerance"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-30","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A trivia-game platform is adding a WebSocket tier for live matches and must choose between a layer 4 and a layer 7 load balancer in front of it. Which fits better, and why?","answer":"Layer 4. An L4 balancer routes on IP/port and effectively passes the TCP connection straight through, so the persistent WebSocket stays glued to one backend for its lifetime — the behavior WebSockets need. An L7 balancer would terminate the connection and proxy every frame just to inspect HTTP content that a long-lived upgraded connection no longer benefits from.","explanation":"L4 load balancing for WebSockets: TCP passthrough keeps connections pinned"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-31","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce site wants requests to /api/* routed to its API server pool, all other paths to the web-rendering pool, and any user carrying a beta cookie sent to a canary pool. Their current balancer routes purely on IP and port and cannot do any of this. What do they need, and what makes it capable of this?","answer":"A layer 7 load balancer. It terminates the client connection and parses the HTTP request, so it can route on URL path, headers, and cookies — none of which an L4 balancer can see, since L4 only looks at network-level information. The cost is extra CPU for request inspection, which is the standard trade for content-aware routing.","explanation":"L7 load balancing: content-based routing on URL, headers, cookies"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-32","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"One app server in a food-delivery backend deadlocks: the process still accepts TCP connections but never processes requests. The load balancer's health checks keep passing, so it keeps sending users there and they all get timeouts. What is wrong with the health check, and what is the fix?","answer":"The balancer is using a TCP health check, which only verifies that a connection can be opened — a hung process still passes. Switch to an HTTP (L7) health check that requires a 200 from a real endpoint, so servers that accept connections but can't actually serve get pulled from rotation.","explanation":"Health check depth: HTTP checks catch hung servers that still accept TCP"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-33","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A live-sports streaming service holds long-lived SSE connections. Under load it scales from four servers to six, but hours later the original four are still pegged while the two new ones sit nearly idle under round-robin balancing. Why, and what should change?","answer":"Existing long-lived connections never move, and round robin only splits new connections evenly — so the old servers keep their full backlog while new capacity fills at a crawl. Use least-connections, which routes each new stream to the server with the fewest active connections, evening out load quickly. Round robin is fine for short stateless requests, not persistent connections.","explanation":"Least-connections algorithm for long-lived connections"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-34","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A legacy quiz platform keeps each user's in-progress quiz state in the web server's memory and cannot be refactored before a big launch. The team asks whether the load balancer can at least keep each user landing on the same server for the duration of a quiz. What LB feature does this, and what are its sharp edges?","answer":"Session affinity: the balancer routes by a hash of the client IP or by a stickiness cookie, so all of a user's requests hit the same backend. It works, but a server crash or scale-in still wipes those users' state, and IP hashing dumps everyone behind a shared corporate NAT onto one server — it is a stopgap, with shared session storage as the real fix.","explanation":"Session affinity (sticky sessions): IP-hash or cookie routing pins a client to one server, with failure caveats"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-35","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A podcast app serves episode artwork — images that never change after upload — from a single origin in Virginia. Listeners in Australia wait noticeably long for artwork to load. What is the standard fix, and why does it work?","answer":"Put a CDN in front of the artwork. Immutable static assets are ideal cache material, so CDN edge servers near users serve them locally, replacing a trans-Pacific round trip with a nearby fetch. The origin only gets hit on cache misses.","explanation":"CDNs: edge caching of static content for global users"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-36","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A taxi-matching app runs all compute and its database in a single US region. Riders and drivers in Jakarta see roughly 250ms on every location update and matches feel sluggish — even though a Jakarta rider is only ever matched with a Jakarta driver. What architectural change fits this workload?","answer":"Partition regionally: run the matching compute and its data in a region near each city, since interactions are inherently local — a Jakarta rider never needs Miami's data. Requests are then served entirely by nearby services and a nearby database, eliminating cross-ocean round trips, and each region handles a far smaller user population.","explanation":"Regional partitioning: colocate compute and data for geographically local interactions"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-37","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A product manager asks the team to get London-to-New-York API round trips under 10ms 'by optimizing the code.' Why is this request impossible as stated, and what is the only real fix?","answer":"Light in fiber travels at roughly 200,000 km/s, so the ~5,600 km one-way distance costs on the order of 28ms before a single instruction runs — round trips are physically several times over the 10ms budget. No code change beats physics; the only fix is moving the endpoints closer together, i.e., serving those users from a nearby region.","explanation":"Speed-of-light propagation sets an irreducible latency floor"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-38","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A B2B reporting SaaS calls a third-party PDF-rendering API with no timeout configured. When the vendor's API hangs one afternoon, the SaaS's worker threads block indefinitely, the job queue backs up, and unrelated reports stall for everyone. What is the root mistake?","answer":"Network calls without timeouts turn a slow dependency into an unbounded resource leak: each hung call holds a worker forever. Every outbound call needs an explicit timeout so failures surface quickly, threads are released, and the caller can retry or degrade instead of silently seizing up.","explanation":"Always set explicit timeouts on network calls"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-39","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"After a two-minute outage, a fleet of 200,000 smart thermostats all retry their check-in at the same fixed 10-second interval. The backend comes up, absorbs one synchronized wave of 200k requests, and collapses again — repeatedly. What retry policy fixes this, and what does each piece contribute?","answer":"Exponential backoff with jitter. Backoff spaces retries progressively further apart (1s, 2s, 4s, 8s...) so pressure on the recovering service decays; jitter randomizes each device's retry moment so the fleet's requests spread into a smooth trickle instead of arriving as synchronized waves — the thundering herd that keeps re-killing the service.","explanation":"Exponential backoff with jitter prevents retry thundering herds"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-40","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A payments API's mobile clients retry the charge request when it times out — sometimes the original request actually succeeded and the customer gets billed twice. Retries can't be removed because networks are unreliable. What mechanism fixes this?","answer":"Idempotency keys. The client attaches a unique key per payment attempt; the server records processed keys and, on a retry with a known key, returns the original result instead of charging again. This makes an inherently non-idempotent operation safe to retry, which is a prerequisite for any retry-on-timeout policy.","explanation":"Idempotency keys make retries of non-idempotent operations safe"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-41","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"open","question":"A checkout service calls a recommendations service on every request. When recommendations goes down, every checkout waits out a 30-second timeout, checkout's thread pool exhausts, and soon the entire site is returning errors — a minor feature took down the business-critical path. What pattern prevents this, and how does it operate?","answer":"A circuit breaker. After a threshold of failures it opens and rejects calls to recommendations instantly — checkout fails fast, skips the widget, and stays healthy — while periodically letting a half-open probe through to test recovery before closing again. This contains the dependency failure instead of letting it cascade through exhausted resources.","explanation":"Circuit breakers fail fast and prevent cascading failures"},
  {"id":"hi-networking-essentials-42","topic":"core-concepts · networking essentials","type":"two-choice","question":"In a system design interview, the safest baseline is HTTPS with a REST API over TCP, and choices like UDP, WebSockets, gRPC, or GraphQL should each be explicitly justified by a specific requirement.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"A","explanation":"TCP + HTTP(S) + REST is the expected default that needs no defense, while each deviation trades something away (reliability, statelessness, browser support, simplicity) and should be introduced only when a concrete requirement, like real-time bidirectional traffic or extreme internal throughput, demands it."},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-01","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A junior engineer at a concert-ticketing startup designs the seat-inventory service straight out of a 2013 distributed-systems textbook: a 12-node cluster for 200 GB of data serving 2,000 queries per second. A senior reviewer rejects the design as wildly oversized. Why is the textbook steering the engineer wrong?","answer":"The book's numbers describe hardware a decade out of date. A modern single server offers hundreds of gigabytes of RAM and tens of thousands of queries per second, so 200 GB at 2k QPS is a one-box problem — capacity grew roughly an order of magnitude while the textbook stayed frozen.","explanation":"Outdated textbook hardware numbers cause systematic over-engineering"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-02","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"An ad-tech firm serves lookups against a 2 TB user-profile table. The architect assumes no single machine can hold 2 TB in RAM, so she designs a 16-node partitioned in-memory fleet with a custom routing layer. What is she getting wrong?","answer":"Memory-optimized cloud instances go to 4 TB and even 24 TB of RAM, so the entire table fits in memory on one machine. The partitioned fleet and router add failure modes to solve a problem that no longer exists.","explanation":"Modern single-server RAM reaches multiple terabytes (512 GiB standard, up to 24 TB)"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-03","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A smart-agriculture IoT platform accumulates about 30 TB of sensor readings per year. On day one the team proposes a horizontally sharded storage cluster because '30 TB will never fit on one node.' Is the premise sound?","answer":"No — single instances offer roughly 60 TB of local NVMe SSD or 336 TB of HDD, so a year or two of data fits on one box. Raw storage capacity alone almost never forces a distributed design anymore.","explanation":"Single-instance disk capacity (60 TB NVMe SSD / 336 TB HDD) means raw capacity rarely forces distribution"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-04","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A video-sharing app projects 2 PB of uploaded footage within three years. An infrastructure engineer starts speccing a self-managed distributed file cluster to hold it all. What should the team do instead, and why?","answer":"Put the blobs in object storage like S3, which serves petabytes routinely with high durability. Building and operating a custom storage cluster is enormous effort spent on an already-solved problem.","explanation":"Object storage (S3) handles petabyte scale for blobs — don't build custom storage clusters"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-05","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery backend is split into six microservices in the same availability zone, and placing an order crosses four internal service hops. A reviewer objects that each hop 'adds tens of milliseconds.' What do those hops actually cost on modern infrastructure?","answer":"Intra-AZ round trips run under a millisecond on 25 Gbps-class links, so four hops add a few milliseconds total. Hop count inside one AZ is rarely where latency problems live.","explanation":"Intra-datacenter networking is 25+ Gbps with sub-1 ms latency and is rarely the bottleneck"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-06","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"An ML team copies a 1 TB feature snapshot each night between two services in the same datacenter and books a 6-hour maintenance window for it, fearing the transfer will crawl. Roughly how long does the copy really take, and what does that imply?","answer":"At ~25 Gbps, 1 TB (8 terabits) moves in about five to six minutes. Intra-datacenter bandwidth is rarely the constraint, so the multi-hour window is pure superstition.","explanation":"Intra-datacenter bandwidth math: moving ~1 TB takes minutes, not hours"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-07","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A digital bank runs its primary database in one availability zone and its app servers in another, paying a barely noticeable 1-2 ms per query. For data-residency reasons the team now plans to move the database to another continent while leaving the app servers put, expecting a similar penalty. What are they missing?","answer":"Cross-region round trips cost 50-150 ms, not 1-2 ms, and a request issuing several sequential queries would stack up hundreds of milliseconds. Compute has to move with the data, or the new region needs its own replica.","explanation":"Cross-AZ latency (~1-2 ms) vs cross-region latency (~50-150 ms) gap"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-08","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A trading-analytics dashboard hosted in a Virginia region serves users in London, whose requests never complete in under about 80 ms no matter how aggressively the team profiles and optimizes the backend. What is going wrong?","answer":"Nothing in the code — roughly 80 ms is the physical floor for a transatlantic round trip through fiber. The only fix is geographic: serve those users from a European region or edge location.","explanation":"Speed-of-light latency floor (~80 ms NY-London) is fixed by geography, not code"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-09","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A mobile-gaming team budgets 20 ms for every Redis leaderboard read, and that assumption pushes them to build an elaborate request-coalescing layer to 'amortize the cache cost.' What is wrong with the budget?","answer":"A cache hit costs about 1 ms, so the estimate is off by 20x. The coalescing machinery is solving a latency problem that does not exist.","explanation":"Cache (Redis) single-operation latency is ~1 ms"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-10","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A social app projects 40,000 cache reads per second at peak. The infra team plans an 8-shard Redis cluster because 'no single instance can take that traffic.' Evaluate the plan.","answer":"One Redis node sustains well over 100k operations per second, so 40k fits on a single instance with headroom. Add a replica for availability if needed — sharding for throughput here is unjustified.","explanation":"Cache throughput: 100k+ operations/sec per node"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-11","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce site measures its hot product-catalog working set at about 300 GB and concludes it is 'far too big to cache,' sending every read to the database. What option are they overlooking?","answer":"Modern cache nodes hold up to about a terabyte in memory, so the entire 300 GB hot set fits in a single instance. 'Too big to cache' now means approaching a terabyte, not hundreds of gigabytes.","explanation":"Cache capacity: up to ~1 TB in memory on a single node"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-12","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A B2B analytics dashboard's Redis tier reports a 65% hit rate, 3 ms median read latency, and 85% memory utilization. The on-call engineer declares the cache healthy. What do these numbers actually indicate?","answer":"All three warning thresholds are breached: hit rate under ~80%, latency over ~1 ms, and memory over ~80%. The cache needs more capacity or a rethink of keys, TTLs, and eviction — it is not healthy.","explanation":"Cache scaling triggers: hit rate below 80%, latency above 1 ms, memory above 80%"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-13","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"During a week-long promotion, a flash-sale site's Redis shows an 82% hit rate and ~1 ms reads — both nominally fine — yet its eviction counter is climbing fast, and traces show the same product keys being evicted and re-loaded from the database every few seconds. Should the on-call engineer relax?","answer":"No — rapid eviction churn means the working set no longer fits in memory and the cache is thrashing, hammering the database with repeated refills. Churn is a scaling trigger in its own right; grow the cache (or fix keys and TTLs) before the headline hit-rate and latency numbers collapse too.","explanation":"Cache eviction churn signals an undersized cache even before hit rate and latency thresholds trip"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-14","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing startup forecasts 8,000 database queries per second at launch, and the CTO mandates a sharded multi-primary setup because 'a relational database can't survive that.' Which belief here is outdated?","answer":"A single modern Postgres instance handles tens of thousands of queries per second, so 8k QPS is comfortable single-node territory. Launch with one primary plus replicas and revisit only if traffic grows an order of magnitude.","explanation":"Single Postgres throughput: tens of thousands of QPS (~50k)"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-15","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A subscription-billing platform's relational database has grown to 1.5 TB, and leadership pushes a migration to a distributed NoSQL store purely because of the size. Is size a valid reason here?","answer":"No — single-node relational databases comfortably serve single-digit terabytes and can stretch to tens. 1.5 TB is not a storage problem, so the migration would trade a non-issue for a costly rewrite.","explanation":"Single-node relational storage: single-digit TB comfortable, tens of TB possible"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-16","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A chat product's team assumes every database query costs around 200 ms, so they put a cache in front of every table — including rarely touched user preferences. What flawed premise drives this design?","answer":"A well-indexed query against warm data returns in single-digit milliseconds, not 200. Blanket caching adds invalidation complexity to fix latency that was never there; cache only genuinely hot or expensive reads.","explanation":"Indexed/warm database read latency is single-digit milliseconds"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-17","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A logistics company's Postgres holds 5 TB and absorbs 3,000 writes per second, growing about 40% a year. The board asks engineering to name the point at which sharding becomes genuinely necessary. What is the right answer?","answer":"Shard when data approaches tens of terabytes or sustained writes exceed roughly 10,000 per second. On this trajectory that is years away — keep the schema shardable, but do not build it yet.","explanation":"Real sharding triggers: tens of TB of data or sustained writes above ~10k/sec"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-18","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A B2B SaaS database holds just 800 GB and takes 2,000 writes per second — far below any capacity or throughput threshold — but a new contract requires EU customers' records to be stored and served in Europe while US customers stay in a US region. The lead engineer argues 'we don't qualify for sharding yet.' What is he missing?","answer":"Geographic distribution is its own scaling trigger, independent of size and write rate. Residency rules and cross-region latency for local users force partitioning the data by region even though a single node could trivially carry the load.","explanation":"Geographic data distribution needs force partitioning even at modest size and write rates"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-19","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A news-feed service is 95% reads, and its single database is CPU-saturated from read traffic alone. An engineer proposes sharding as the fix. What should the team try first, and why?","answer":"Add read replicas and a cache — read-heavy load scales fine without splitting the data. Sharding exists for write-throughput and storage limits, and it drags in cross-shard queries and rebalancing this problem does not need.","explanation":"Read-heavy load scales with replicas and caching before sharding"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-20","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A smart-home platform must hold 1 million always-on WebSocket connections from devices. Working from an old 'about 5k connections per box' instinct, the team budgets 200 connection servers. What does modern hardware actually support?","answer":"A tuned modern server holds 100k+ concurrent connections, so on the order of 10-15 servers plus headroom suffices. The 5k figure is a relic of the C10K era.","explanation":"Application servers hold 100k+ concurrent connections per instance"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-21","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A photo-sharing API tier runs at 85% CPU during peak with response times drifting past the SLA, but each instance carries only 20,000 of its 100k-connection capacity. An engineer argues no scaling is needed since 'connections are fine.' What is the correct read?","answer":"CPU beyond ~70-80% and SLA-violating latency are the real scaling signals; app servers are almost always CPU-bound long before connection limits matter. Add instances now.","explanation":"Application servers are CPU-bound first: scale on >70-80% CPU or SLA-breaking latency"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-22","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"An online exam platform expects a 30,000-requests-per-second spike when results are published. A load test shows one app server sustains about 4,000 RPS. How should the team size the fleet, and why is this easy?","answer":"Divide: 30k / 4k is roughly 8 servers, so provision about 10 for headroom. Because the tier is stateless, capacity is just instances behind a load balancer — this one-line arithmetic is exactly the kind of estimation that should drive the decision.","explanation":"Stateless tier sizing arithmetic: total RPS divided by per-server RPS plus headroom"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-23","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A clickstream pipeline ingests 150,000 events per second, and the architect sizes a 20-broker Kafka cluster 'to handle the throughput.' What is mis-sized here?","answer":"A single modern broker pushes on the order of a million messages per second, so 150k is nowhere near a throughput problem. Broker count should be driven by replication, durability, and partitioning needs — a handful of brokers is plenty.","explanation":"Message queue throughput: on the order of 1M messages/sec per broker"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-24","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A payment processor must record an audit event for every transaction. Believing message queues add hundreds of milliseconds, the team builds a custom in-process buffer with homegrown retry logic rather than publishing to Kafka inside the request. What does the premise get wrong?","answer":"Modern queues deliver end-to-end in under ~5 ms, so publishing in the synchronous path adds a few milliseconds at most. The bespoke buffer re-implements, worse, what the queue already provides.","explanation":"Queue end-to-end latency under ~5 ms makes queues viable in the synchronous hot path"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-25","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"An analytics team wants the last 30 days of raw events replayable for backfills. Assuming 'queues can't hold data,' they build an immediate ETL hop from Kafka to S3 and replay from S3 with custom tooling. What simpler design do modern brokers enable?","answer":"Kafka brokers store tens of terabytes, so just retain 30 days in the topic and replay by rewinding consumer offsets. The log itself is the replay buffer — no side pipeline required.","explanation":"Queue retention of tens of TB enables replay and backfill directly from the log"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-26","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"An event-streaming platform's hottest Kafka broker is sustaining about 850,000 messages per second, and the consumer-lag graph has climbed all day without ever draining back to zero. The on-call engineer sees no errors and declares the cluster fine. Which scaling signals is she ignoring?","answer":"Both classic queue triggers are firing: sustained throughput near ~800k msgs/sec is brushing a broker's ~1M ceiling, and lag that grows without recovering means consumers permanently cannot keep up. Add brokers and partitions and scale out consumers now — 'no errors yet' is not headroom.","explanation":"Queue scaling triggers: throughput near ~800k msgs/sec per broker and consumer lag that grows without draining"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-27","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"In a mock system-design interview, a candidate opens with ten minutes of arithmetic — daily active users, storage growth, bandwidth — then designs the entire system without referencing a single computed number. The feedback is negative even though the math was correct. Why?","answer":"Estimation earns credit only when it drives a decision — whether to shard, whether the cache fits in memory, how many servers to run. Upfront ritual math that never touches the design demonstrates memorization, not judgment.","explanation":"Estimate at decision points in the interview, not as an upfront ritual"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-28","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"two-choice","question":"True or false: proposing to shard a 500 GB relational database in a system design interview signals strong scalability instincts.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"It signals calibration to decade-old hardware. Single nodes comfortably serve terabytes, so sharding at 500 GB buys cross-shard complexity and rebalancing pain for zero benefit."},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-29","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A travel-booking team sets a 1-second SLA for its search page, reasoning that the path — load balancer, app server, cache check, one indexed database query — 'probably costs 500 ms.' What is a realistic modern budget for that path, and why does the inflated estimate matter?","answer":"Sub-millisecond internal hops, a ~1 ms cache check, and a single-digit-millisecond indexed query put the whole path in the tens of milliseconds. Overestimating by 10x produces bloated SLAs and mitigation layers built for slowness that is imaginary.","explanation":"Interview mistake: overestimating latency inflates budgets and spawns unnecessary layers"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-30","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A fitness app ingests 4,000 workout-record writes per second. A proposal introduces Cassandra for storage with Kafka buffering in front, because 'relational databases can't absorb this write load.' Diagnose the proposal.","answer":"4k writes per second is less than half the ~10k writes/sec threshold where sharding even enters the conversation, and far below the ~50k transactions per second a single Postgres node sustains. The NoSQL-plus-buffer stack is over-engineering for a write volume that is not high, paid for in daily operational complexity.","explanation":"Interview mistake: over-engineering the write path with NoSQL/buffers at modest write rates"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-31","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"open","question":"A seed-stage startup CTO, aiming to save money, plans a self-managed cluster of eight small instances with sharding middleware instead of one large memory-optimized machine that costs slightly more per month. What is the flaw in the cost analysis?","answer":"The dominant cost is engineering time spent building and operating distributed complexity, not the hardware delta. One big simple box is almost always cheaper in total until you genuinely exceed what a single machine can do.","explanation":"Cost reality: one big machine plus simplicity beats distributed complexity; engineer time dominates hardware cost"},
  {"id":"hi-numbers-to-know-32","topic":"core-concepts · numbers to know","type":"two-choice","question":"For a single data read, which statement better describes modern storage-tier latencies?","optionA":"RAM, SSD, and cross-region network reads each step up roughly 1,000x in latency (nanoseconds, then microseconds, then ~100 milliseconds)","optionB":"RAM, SSD, and cross-region network reads each step up roughly 10x in latency, so tier choice barely matters at scale","correct":"A","explanation":"The gaps are orders of magnitude, which is why keeping hot data in memory dominates performance design."},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-01","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A podcast-hosting startup's single Postgres server is straining under a 900 GB episodes table. An engineer announces they have 'sharded the database' by splitting the table into twelve monthly pieces — all still living on the same server. Has the system actually been sharded, and why does the distinction matter?","answer":"No — splitting a table inside one database instance is partitioning, not sharding. Sharding means distributing the data across multiple machines, each with its own CPU, memory, and storage; the partitioned table still shares a single machine's capacity ceiling.","explanation":"partitioning vs sharding distinction"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-02","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A news site's articles table stores small, frequently-read metadata columns (title, author, publish date) alongside a multi-megabyte body_html column that is rarely read. Listing pages query only the metadata, yet those scans are slow because every row drags the huge column along. Which style of partitioning fits here, and what does it do?","answer":"Vertical partitioning — split by columns, keeping the hot metadata columns in one partition and the bulky, rarely-read body in another. Horizontal partitioning splits rows and would not stop metadata scans from carrying the giant column.","explanation":"horizontal vs vertical partitioning"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-03","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"An accounting SaaS has a 1.8 TB invoices table on one database server. Traffic and CPU are modest, but index rebuilds lock the table for hours and month-end reports scan the whole thing. The team is pricing out eight new servers to shard across. What should they try first, and why?","answer":"Partition the table (for example by month) on the existing server. The pain is table size and maintenance, not machine capacity — partitioning shrinks indexes and localizes maintenance and scans without taking on the operational complexity of a distributed system.","explanation":"partitioning before sharding for single-machine table pain"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-04","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A ticketing platform partitioned its orders table by month, but during big on-sales the single database server still maxes out on writes. An engineer argues 'we already split the data, there's nothing left to split.' What is the engineer missing?","answer":"Partitioning only reorganizes data on one machine, so every write still competes for that machine's CPU, memory, and I/O. Sharding across multiple machines gives each data subset its own hardware and connection pool, so write throughput scales with the number of shards.","explanation":"sharding scales throughput via independent machines"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-05","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A smart-meter IoT platform stores readings in Amazon Aurora and is on track to accumulate roughly 400 TiB within eighteen months. Queries perform fine today, so the team plans no changes. What is wrong with that plan?","answer":"Aurora has a hard storage ceiling — a cluster volume maxes out in the low hundreds of TiB (~128-256 TiB depending on engine and version) — so they will hit a wall well before 400 TiB regardless of query performance. A single database's storage limit is by itself a sharding trigger, and they need to plan the split before growth forces it.","explanation":"single-database storage ceiling as a sharding trigger"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-06","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A recipe website serves about 40,000 reads per second but only 80 writes per second, and its 200 GB dataset fits comfortably on one server. Pages are slowing because the primary is saturated with read traffic, so the team schedules a quarter-long project to shard across 16 machines. What simpler fix are they skipping?","answer":"Read replicas — copies of the database that absorb read traffic — solve a pure read-throughput bottleneck without sharding's complexity. Sharding is warranted when storage or write volume exceeds one machine, or when reads outgrow what a replica fleet can serve; none of that is true here.","explanation":"read replicas before sharding for read-heavy load"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-07","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A banking app shards its accounts table by account_type, which has exactly three values: checking, savings, and credit. Scaling stalls at three increasingly overloaded shards. Which property of a good shard key was violated?","answer":"High cardinality. A key with three possible values caps the system at three shards forever, so capacity can never scale out further. A key with millions of unique values, like account_id, is required.","explanation":"shard key high cardinality"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-08","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery company shards its orders by country code. 85% of orders come from one country, and that shard is many times larger and slower than all the others combined. What went wrong?","answer":"The shard key's values are heavily skewed, so it fails the even-distribution requirement — most data and traffic pile onto one shard. Hashing a high-cardinality, evenly spread key like customer_id or order_id would balance the load.","explanation":"shard key even distribution"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-09","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A project-management SaaS hash-shards its tasks table by task_id across 32 shards. Every board view loads 'all tasks in project X,' which now fans out to all 32 shards on each page load. What should they have keyed on, and why?","answer":"project_id (the unit the app actually queries by), because a shard key should align with the dominant query so common reads hit exactly one shard. Keying by task_id scatters a project's tasks everywhere and turns the most frequent query into a cross-shard operation.","explanation":"shard key alignment with query patterns"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-10","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A logistics platform shards shipment-event records into ranges by event date. The shard holding the current week runs at 95% CPU while shards holding past months sit nearly idle. What is going on?","answer":"A time-based hot spot: with a time-ordered shard key, every new write lands on the newest shard, so one shard absorbs the entire write load while older shards only serve occasional historical reads. Hash-sharding on something like shipment_id would spread the writes.","explanation":"time-based write hot spot from time-ordered keys"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-11","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A B2B inventory tool's most common query fetches contiguous blocks of SKU IDs, such as SKUs 200,000 through 210,000. Under hash-based sharding, each of these queries fans out to all 16 shards. Which strategy would serve them from one shard, and why?","answer":"Range-based sharding — it stores contiguous key values together, so a range scan lands on a single shard. Hashing deliberately scatters neighboring IDs across shards, which destroys range locality.","explanation":"range-based sharding enables single-shard range scans"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-12","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"An HR platform assigns each corporate client a contiguous block of employee IDs, and every client only ever queries its own employees. Which sharding strategy exploits this workload especially well, and how?","answer":"Range-based sharding: map each tenant's ID range to a shard so every client's queries stay on one shard. Multi-tenant systems with per-tenant key ranges are the classic strong fit for range sharding.","explanation":"range-based sharding for multi-tenant ID ranges"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-13","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A photo-sharing startup expects millions of users with roughly uniform per-user traffic, and nearly every query is 'fetch this user's photos.' The team wants load spread evenly without extra routing infrastructure. Which strategy fits, and why is it the usual starting point?","answer":"Hash-based sharding on user_id — the hash function scrambles keys so users distribute evenly, and every user-scoped query still hits one shard. It is the default, most common strategy, and the one an interviewer assumes unless you say otherwise.","explanation":"hash-based sharding as the even-distribution default"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-14","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A game studio routes player data with hash(player_id) % 8. To add capacity they move to ten shards, and the migration plan shows nearly every player record has to move to a different shard. Why?","answer":"Changing the modulus from 8 to 10 changes the routing result for almost every key, so naive hash-mod placement remaps nearly the whole dataset any time the shard count changes. Massive data movement on every resize is the core weakness of modulo routing.","explanation":"hash-mod resharding remaps nearly all keys"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-15","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A chat service expects to grow from 16 to 32 to 64 shards over three years and cannot afford to reshuffle its entire dataset at each step. What routing technique should they adopt, and what does it buy them?","answer":"Consistent hashing — when a shard is added or removed, only the keys adjacent to it on the hash ring move, a small fraction of the data. That makes incremental shard additions cheap instead of the near-total remap that modulo routing causes.","explanation":"consistent hashing minimizes resharding data movement"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-16","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A live-streaming platform wants a few mega-streamers pinned to dedicated high-end hardware while millions of ordinary channels sit on commodity shards, with the ability to move any specific channel at will. Which sharding strategy supports this, and how?","answer":"Directory-based sharding — a lookup table maps each key to its shard, so any individual key can be placed on or migrated to any shard on demand. Formula-based strategies (hash or range) cannot give one specific key special placement.","explanation":"directory-based sharding's per-key placement flexibility"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-17","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"After an ad-serving platform switched to directory-based sharding, every request got a few milliseconds slower, and one afternoon the entire system went down even though all data shards were healthy. What two costs of this strategy did they hit?","answer":"Every request must first ask the directory which shard holds the key, adding lookup latency, and the directory itself becomes a critical dependency — a single point of failure that takes the whole system down when it is unavailable. This is why directory-based sharding is rarely the answer unless its flexibility is truly needed.","explanation":"directory-based sharding's lookup latency and SPOF"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-18","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A social network hash-shards by user_id, and row counts per shard are almost perfectly even. Yet one shard's latency spikes every time a particular pop star posts, while all other shards stay calm. What is this problem?","answer":"The celebrity problem: one key generates orders of magnitude more traffic than a normal key — every profile view, like, and message for that account hits the same shard. Hashing balances where keys live, not how much load each key generates.","explanation":"celebrity problem hot spot"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-19","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"An SRE at an online marketplace suspects a shard hot spot, but the cluster-wide aggregate dashboards all look normal. What should they instrument to confirm or rule it out?","answer":"Per-shard metrics: query latency, CPU usage, and request volume broken out by shard. A hot spot shows up as one shard consistently running hotter than its peers — a signal that cluster-wide averages smooth away.","explanation":"hot spot detection via per-shard metrics"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-20","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A short-video app has three viral creators whose accounts repeatedly overwhelm whatever shard they hash onto, degrading service for every ordinary user co-located with them. What targeted fix should the team apply?","answer":"Isolate the hot keys: move those creator accounts onto dedicated shards that serve only high-traffic accounts, typically via a directory-style mapping that overrides normal hash placement. Ordinary users then stop sharing hardware with celebrity traffic.","explanation":"isolating hot keys on dedicated shards"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-21","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"During a championship match, all bets for that one event in a sports-betting app hash to a single shard, which buckles under the write rate. Provisioning dedicated hardware for every big event is not practical. How can the shard key itself be changed to fix this?","answer":"Use a compound shard key — for example hash(event_id + time bucket) — so one event's records spread across many shards instead of all landing on one. The trade-off is that reading everything for that event now touches multiple shards.","explanation":"compound shard keys to spread a hot key"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-22","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"two-choice","question":"A team wants their datastore to notice an oversized or overheated chunk of data and rebalance it across shards without a human initiating the move. Which behavior matches that requirement?","optionA":"MongoDB's balancer, which splits and migrates chunks automatically","optionB":"Vitess online resharding, which an operator must drive","correct":"A","explanation":"MongoDB rebalances range-based chunks on its own in the background, whereas Vitess supports online resharding but requires an operator to initiate it."},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-23","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A job board shards postings by employer_id across 64 shards. The homepage's 'newest jobs across all companies' widget now issues 64 queries, waits for every shard to respond, and merges the results; p99 latency has ballooned. What underlying cost is at play?","answer":"Scatter-gather: any query not scoped to the shard key must fan out to every shard, paying 64x the network calls and waiting on the slowest shard before it can merge and return. Cross-shard queries multiply cost roughly by the shard count.","explanation":"scatter-gather cost of cross-shard queries"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-24","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A music-streaming app's 'Global Top 100' chart is computed by querying all shards on every page view, even though the chart only needs to be roughly current. What is the cheap fix?","answer":"Cache or precompute the aggregated result — for example a background job refreshing it on a short TTL like five minutes. One expensive fan-out then serves thousands of requests, and the staleness is fine because the chart does not need real-time accuracy.","explanation":"caching cross-shard aggregates"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-25","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce order-history page shows each order alongside its seller's display name. Orders and sellers live on different shards, so every page load makes an extra cross-shard fetch per order. Storage is cheap and seller names rarely change. What should the team do?","answer":"Denormalize: copy the seller's display name onto the order's shard at write time so the page reads everything from one shard. It duplicates data and complicates updates, but that is the accepted price for keeping a hot read path single-shard.","explanation":"denormalization for single-shard reads"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-26","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A healthcare SaaS compliance team runs a 'total patient records across all shards' report twice a month. An engineer proposes building denormalized counters and a streaming pipeline so the report returns instantly. What is the pragmatic call?","answer":"Skip the machinery and let the report scatter-gather slowly. Accepting the cross-shard hit is fine for infrequent, non-latency-sensitive queries — twice-a-month reporting does not justify permanent extra infrastructure.","explanation":"accepting slow rare cross-shard queries"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-27","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"In a design review for a ride-hailing system, the core matching flow — 'find open ride requests near this driver' — queries every shard and merges results on each request, and it is the single most frequent operation in the system. What should the reviewer conclude?","answer":"A hot-path operation that fans out to all shards is a design smell: the shard key does not match the dominant access pattern. Reshard so the common query is single-shard — here by geographic region or city — instead of shipping scatter-gather on the critical path.","explanation":"hot-path cross-shard query as design smell"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-28","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A payments company implements two-phase commit for transfers that span wallet shards. In production, throughput is poor, and when the coordinator crashed mid-commit, several shards sat holding locks with transactions frozen. Why is this outcome expected?","answer":"2PC guarantees atomicity but is slow and fragile by design: participants prepare and then block waiting for the coordinator's decision, so a coordinator or shard failure mid-protocol can wedge the whole system. This is exactly why most production systems avoid 2PC.","explanation":"2PC fragility and blocking"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-29","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A neobank shards account balances by user_id but shards ledger entries separately by entry_id, so recording any deposit requires a transaction spanning two shards. What redesign eliminates the distributed transaction entirely?","answer":"Co-locate everything the transaction touches: shard the ledger entries by user_id too, so a user's balance and entries live on the same shard and every deposit becomes an ordinary single-shard transaction. Designing data placement to avoid cross-shard transactions is the best consistency strategy.","explanation":"co-locating transactional data on one shard"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-30","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"On a freelance marketplace, releasing escrow debits the client's wallet on shard 3 and credits the freelancer's wallet on shard 7 — genuinely two shards — and the team has ruled out two-phase commit. How should the operation be structured to stay correct?","answer":"As a saga: a sequence of independent local steps, each with a compensating action — debit the client, then credit the freelancer, and if the credit fails, run the compensation that refunds the client. Correctness comes from undoing completed steps, not from a distributed atomic commit.","explanation":"sagas with compensating actions"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-31","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A fitness social app denormalizes follower counts across shards for fast profile reads. QA files a bug: two views of the same profile a few seconds apart showed counts differing by 2, and a fix using distributed transactions is proposed. What is the right response?","answer":"Accept the eventual consistency. A denormalized vanity counter being out of sync for a few seconds is harmless, and paying the cost and fragility of distributed transactions to fix it is a bad trade. Reserve strong consistency for data that actually requires it.","explanation":"accepting eventual consistency for denormalized counters"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-32","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"two-choice","question":"If nearly every new feature in a sharded system ends up needing a distributed transaction, the natural conclusion is that the workload is inherently distributed and the team should invest in a robust two-phase commit implementation.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Constantly needing cross-shard transactions is a symptom of choosing the wrong shard key or shard boundaries. Most applications can be redesigned, usually by re-keying so related data is co-located, until nearly all transactions are single-shard."},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-33","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A travel-booking startup chose DynamoDB, and an engineer budgets a full sprint to implement consistent hashing, shard routing, and partition rebalancing on top of it. What is the misconception?","answer":"Modern distributed databases handle sharding internally — DynamoDB already hashes the partition key to route items and automatically splits partitions as they grow. The team's real job is choosing a good partition key, not building sharding machinery.","explanation":"managed databases shard automatically"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-34","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A team migrating to Cassandra writes a design doc for an application-level consistent-hash ring that will decide which node stores each row, plus custom rebalancing scripts for when nodes join the cluster. What have they misunderstood about Cassandra?","answer":"Cassandra already does this internally: its partitioner (Murmur3) consistent-hashes each row's partition key onto a token ring, and virtual nodes spread token ranges so data redistributes automatically when nodes join or leave. Their job is picking a good partition key, not building placement or rebalancing logic.","explanation":"Cassandra's built-in consistent hashing with vnodes"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-35","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"two-choice","question":"A company is committed to PostgreSQL's SQL semantics but has outgrown one machine and does not want to hand-build shard routing, cross-shard query handling, or resharding. Which approach fits better?","optionA":"Put an open-source sharding layer like Vitess or Citus in front of the database","optionB":"Have each application service implement its own shard-routing logic","correct":"A","explanation":"These proven layers handle query routing, cross-shard operations, and resharding for Postgres/MySQL, instead of duplicating fragile routing logic across every service."},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-36","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"A global retail platform needs full SQL semantics and transactions but has far outgrown any single database machine, and its three-person ops team refuses to operate and reshard a self-managed sharding layer. Among managed cloud offerings, what class of database dissolves the problem, and how?","answer":"A managed distributed SQL database such as Google Cloud Spanner, which shards, replicates, and rebalances data across machines transparently while preserving SQL and transactions. The team designs the schema and keys; the platform owns partitioning, routing, and growth.","explanation":"managed distributed SQL as a transparent-sharding option"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-37","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"open","question":"In a system design interview for a URL shortener, the candidate estimates about 100 GB of total data and 500 writes per second, then immediately opens the design with a 32-shard database layout. What is the mistake?","answer":"Premature sharding — the single most common sharding mistake in interviews. That load fits comfortably on one well-tuned database, and sharding should only be proposed after the numbers show a single database cannot handle the storage or throughput.","explanation":"premature sharding without proven bottleneck"},
  {"id":"hi-sharding-38","topic":"core-concepts · sharding","type":"two-choice","question":"In an interview, a candidate has just shown with numbers that a single database cannot handle the write load. Which is the stronger way to continue?","optionA":"Propose a shard key based on the app's access patterns, state the distribution strategy, call out the cross-shard trade-offs and how to mitigate them, then address growth (e.g., start with 64 shards plus consistent hashing)","optionB":"Define all three sharding strategies with their pros and cons and let the interviewer choose one","correct":"A","explanation":"Walking through the reasoning demonstrates judgment about trade-offs, while reciting definitions demonstrates only memorization."},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-01","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"two-choice","question":"Contention and load are the same problem: a service handling 50,000 requests per second necessarily has a serious contention problem.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Contention means many concurrent operations targeting the same resource (one seat, one row, one counter). Massive traffic spread across millions of independent resources can have zero contention, while a tiny site selling one concert ticket can have severe contention."},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-02","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"An online merch store runs a flash sale for 100 limited hoodies. Checkout code does SELECT stock FROM items, checks stock > 0 in application code, then runs UPDATE items SET stock = stock - 1. After the sale, stock reads -37 and there are 137 paid orders. What went wrong?","answer":"A check-then-act race: between one request's read and its write, other requests read the same stale stock and also passed the check, so all of them decremented. The read, the check, and the write happen as separate steps, and under concurrency they interleave — the check must be made atomic with the write.","explanation":"Check-then-act race condition from separate read, check, and write"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-03","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A fintech's gift-card service occasionally lets a card holding $20 make two simultaneous $20 purchases, leaving the balance at -$20. The team wants the smallest fix — no new locks, no new infrastructure. What should they change?","answer":"Use a conditional write: a single UPDATE cards SET balance = balance - 20 WHERE id = ? AND balance >= 20, then check rows-affected — zero rows means decline the purchase. The database applies the check and the decrement atomically, so concurrent requests can no longer both pass a stale check.","explanation":"Conditional (atomic) write with the check in the WHERE clause"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-04","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A clinic-scheduling SaaS fixed double-booked slots with UPDATE slots SET status='booked' WHERE id=? AND status='open'. A new rule now requires verifying, before booking, that the patient has no outstanding balance and no same-day appointment (both in other tables), and writing to an appointments table plus an audit table. Why is the conditional write no longer sufficient by itself?","answer":"A conditional write only protects logic that fits inside one statement's predicate on the row being written. Checks spanning other tables and writes spanning multiple rows need a transaction with explicit locking (or serializable isolation) so the entire multi-step decision is atomic.","explanation":"Conditional writes only cover single-statement logic"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-05","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"An API gateway enforces 100 requests/minute per key using Redis: it GETs the counter, compares it to 100 in Node, then SETs counter+1. During bursts, clients consistently get 130-140 requests through. What should the team change?","answer":"Replace the GET/compare/SET sequence with Redis's atomic INCR and check the returned value against the limit. The GET-then-SET pair is the same check-then-act race as a SQL read-then-update — concurrent requests all read the same stale count — and a single atomic operation in the store closes the gap.","explanation":"Atomic operations in a KV store (Redis INCR) replace read-modify-write"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-06","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery platform sometimes assigns one courier to two orders placed in the same second. The team wants the second concurrent transaction to block until the first commits, then see the courier's updated status before deciding. Which database mechanism does exactly this, and how?","answer":"Pessimistic locking with SELECT ... FOR UPDATE inside the transaction: it takes an exclusive lock on the courier's row at read time, so the second transaction waits at its own SELECT, and when the first commits it reads the fresh committed state and sees the courier is already taken.","explanation":"Pessimistic locking mechanics via SELECT ... FOR UPDATE"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-07","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A ticketing site locks the seat row with SELECT ... FOR UPDATE, then calls the payment processor (2-8 seconds) inside the same transaction before committing. During a big on-sale, the database becomes unresponsive and the connection pool exhausts. What is the design mistake?","answer":"Holding a row lock — and the database connection carrying it — across a slow external call. Locks should live for milliseconds, not payment round-trips; every waiting buyer stacks up behind the lock while connections drain. Take payment outside the transaction and represent the hold as short-lived data (a reservation with an expiry) instead.","explanation":"Never hold row locks and connections across slow external calls"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-08","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"In a game's player-to-player marketplace, when two players click Buy on the same rare item, the loser's request queues behind the winner's row lock and only errors seconds later, wasting a connection the whole time. Product wants the second buyer told 'someone beat you to it' instantly. What locking option fits?","answer":"SELECT ... FOR UPDATE NOWAIT: if the row is already locked, the query errors immediately instead of waiting, so the app can instantly return 'this item is being purchased' without tying up a connection in the lock queue.","explanation":"FOR UPDATE NOWAIT to fail fast instead of queueing on a lock"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-09","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A marketing SaaS stores outgoing email jobs in a table, and 20 workers each SELECT the oldest pending row FOR UPDATE. Nineteen workers sit blocked behind whichever one got there first, so the fleet processes jobs almost serially. How should the query change?","answer":"Add SKIP LOCKED: each worker skips rows locked by others and claims the next unlocked job, so the 20 workers spread across 20 different rows instead of convoying on one. This is the standard pattern for using a relational table as a concurrent job queue.","explanation":"FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED for database-backed job queues"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-10","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A P2P payments app locks both account rows when processing a transfer. When Alice pays Bob at the same moment Bob pays Alice, both transactions hang until the database kills one with a deadlock error. What is happening, and what is the standard prevention?","answer":"Each transaction locked its first account and is waiting for the other's — a circular wait, i.e., a deadlock, caused by acquiring locks in opposite orders. Prevent it by always acquiring locks in one global order (e.g., sort the two account IDs and lock the lower one first), which makes circular waits impossible.","explanation":"Deadlocks from opposite lock order; prevent with consistent ordering"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-11","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A B2B analytics platform sees occasional API requests hang for minutes because they are waiting on a row lock held by a stuck nightly batch job. The team cannot guarantee every lock holder will always be fast. What safety net should the request path add?","answer":"Configure a lock (or statement) timeout of a few seconds so waiters fail fast with an error instead of hanging indefinitely. A bounded wait converts a silent multi-minute stall into a quick, visible, retryable failure.","explanation":"Lock/statement timeouts as a bounded-wait safety net"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-12","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"In a headless CMS, two editors open the same article and whoever saves last silently wipes out the other's changes. Concurrent edits to the same article are rare. Which mechanism fits, and how does it work?","answer":"Optimistic concurrency control: store a version number on the article, read it along with the content, and save with UPDATE ... WHERE id = ? AND version = <version you read>, incrementing the version. Zero rows affected means someone saved first, so you reject or merge instead of silently overwriting — and because conflicts are rare, no locks are held while users edit.","explanation":"Optimistic concurrency control with a version column"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-13","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A note-syncing app uses version-checked writes and, on any version conflict, immediately re-reads and retries in an uncapped tight loop. During a sync storm, devices spin at full CPU and hammer the backend with retry traffic. How should the retry logic be designed?","answer":"Cap the retries at a few attempts, wait between attempts with exponential backoff plus jitter, and surface a conflict error once the budget is spent. Unbounded immediate retries turn contention into a self-amplifying retry storm.","explanation":"OCC retries must be bounded with backoff and jitter"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-14","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A sneaker-drop platform uses version-column optimistic writes on each shoe's stock row. During drops, about 5,000 buyers hit one row at once; each request retries dozens of times, database CPU is pegged, and only a trickle of purchases succeed. Why is this approach failing here, and what fits better?","answer":"OCC bets that conflicts are rare; with thousands of writers on one row, nearly every attempt fails its version check, so the system burns capacity on doomed retries instead of progress. For a single hot resource, serialize the writes instead — pessimistic locking, an atomic conditional decrement, or a queue in front of the row.","explanation":"High contention breaks OCC; hot resources need pessimistic or serialized writes"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-15","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A smart-home platform updates a device's setting with 'write only if the current value still equals what I read.' A stale controller read brightness=50; meanwhile another service set it to 80 and back to 50. The stale controller's write then passes the check even though its decision was based on an outdated picture. What is this failure called, and what fixes it?","answer":"The ABA problem: the value returned to its original state, so an equality check on the value cannot see that changes happened in between. Compare a monotonically increasing version number (or generation counter) instead of the value — versions never repeat, so any intervening write makes the stale writer's check fail.","explanation":"The ABA problem: value comparison misses intervening changes"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-16","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A hotel-booking team eliminates double-bookings by switching the database to SERIALIZABLE isolation. In production, users now intermittently see raw 'could not serialize access' errors during checkout. What did the team forget?","answer":"Serializable isolation works by detecting conflicting transactions and aborting one of them — those aborts are the mechanism, not a bug. The application must catch serialization failures and transparently retry the transaction a bounded number of times instead of leaking the error to users.","explanation":"SERIALIZABLE isolation aborts conflicting transactions; app must retry them"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-17","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A campsite-reservation site wrapped its 'read remaining sites, check > 0, decrement' logic in BEGIN ... COMMIT on Postgres at the default isolation level, expecting the transaction to stop double-bookings. Overbooking continues. Why didn't the transaction fix it?","answer":"A transaction gives atomicity of its own statements, not exclusion of concurrent readers: at read committed, two transactions can both read the same count, both pass the check, and both commit a decrement. Stopping the race requires a conditional write, SELECT FOR UPDATE, or serializable isolation — BEGIN/COMMIT alone changes nothing.","explanation":"A plain transaction at read committed does not prevent the race"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-18","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A hospital roster app enforces 'at least one doctor on call per shift.' Doctors Aisha and Ben are both on call tonight and both request to drop the shift at the same moment. Each transaction counts on-call doctors (sees 2), concludes one may leave, and updates only its own row; both commit, leaving zero doctors on call — despite each running in its own transaction and even having version checks on the row it wrote. What is this anomaly, and what actually prevents it?","answer":"Write skew: the two transactions read overlapping data but wrote different rows, so row locks and version checks on the written rows never conflict. Prevent it by running the transactions at SERIALIZABLE isolation (the database detects the dangerous interleaving and aborts one) or by explicitly locking the rows the check reads — e.g., SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on all of tonight's on-call rows — so the two checks serialize.","explanation":"Write skew: disjoint writes evade row locks; serialize or lock what you read"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-19","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A movie-ticketing app must hold a selected seat for up to 10 minutes while the user enters payment details across several web requests. An engineer proposes keeping a SELECT ... FOR UPDATE lock on the seat row for the whole checkout. What is the right design instead?","answer":"Represent the hold as data: atomically claim the seat by setting status='held' with an expires_at timestamp, and let expired holds become reclaimable. Database locks cannot span user think-time across multiple requests — doing so would pin connections and locks for minutes and collapse the system.","explanation":"Model long-held reservations as data with an expiry, not as locks"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-20","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A video platform's transcoding workers are spread across several services; upload state lives in S3 and a third-party media SaaS, with no shared database, and two workers occasionally transcode the same upload simultaneously, doubling cost. The team has so far solved every contention problem with database row locks. Why doesn't that work here, and what does?","answer":"There is no row to lock or conditionally update — the contended resource spans systems that no single database owns, which is exactly the case for a distributed lock (e.g., Redis SET NX with a TTL, or ZooKeeper): each worker must acquire the per-upload lock before transcoding. Reserve this tool for cross-system coordination; when the state does live in one database, its native locking is simpler and safer.","explanation":"Distributed locks fit cross-system resources no single database owns"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-21","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A logistics platform's report scheduler takes a Redis lock (SET key NX, no expiry) so only one node runs reports. After a node crashed mid-run, no reports were produced for three days until someone deleted the key manually. What was missing?","answer":"A TTL on the lock (SET key NX PX <ttl>). A distributed lock must expire on its own so that a holder that crashes without releasing it doesn't leave the resource locked forever; the TTL is the liveness guarantee.","explanation":"Distributed locks need a TTL so crashed holders self-release"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-22","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A cloud-storage service guards index rebuilds with a Redis lock carrying a 30-second TTL. One worker hit a long GC pause, its lock expired, a second worker acquired the lock and began writing — then the first worker woke up and kept writing too, corrupting the index. What is the standard fix beyond TTLs?","answer":"Fencing tokens: the lock service issues a monotonically increasing number with every grant, each write carries its token, and the storage layer rejects any write with a token older than the highest it has seen. The zombie's writes then fail even though it still believes it holds the lock.","explanation":"Fencing tokens protect against zombie lock holders after TTL expiry"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-23","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A florist marketplace keeps all inventory in one Postgres database. To stop overselling bouquets, an engineer proposes deploying a ZooKeeper cluster and requiring every service to acquire a distributed lock before touching stock. What is the better call, and why?","answer":"Use the database's own contention tools — an atomic conditional UPDATE or a row lock — because all the contended state lives in one Postgres. Databases are purpose-built for exactly this problem; adding an external coordination system introduces new failure modes to re-solve something the database already solves.","explanation":"Databases are built for contention — don't bolt on external coordination for single-DB state"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-24","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A warehouse system splits its data so stock lives in one database and orders in another, and every sale must decrement stock and insert an order atomically. The team starts implementing two-phase commit between the two databases. What risk are they taking on, and what alternatives should they weigh?","answer":"2PC is a blocking protocol: if the coordinator dies after the prepare phase, participants sit holding locks in limbo, and availability degrades with every participant added. Prefer keeping the contended invariant in a single database, or accept eventual consistency with a saga/outbox flow and compensation instead of distributed atomic commit.","explanation":"Two-phase commit is blocking and fragile; prefer single-DB or saga alternatives"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-25","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"two-choice","question":"A food-ordering app must guarantee each promo code is redeemed at most once, and all data lives in one Postgres database. Which should the team reach for first?","optionA":"A single atomic UPDATE codes SET redeemed = true WHERE code = ? AND redeemed = false, checking rows affected","optionB":"Running every redemption transaction at SERIALIZABLE isolation","correct":"A","explanation":"Always start with the simplest mechanism that enforces the invariant; serializable isolation is a blunt, database-wide tool that adds abort-and-retry overhead for no extra benefit here."},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-26","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"two-choice","question":"In a system design interview, which requirement should make you immediately plan for contention handling?","optionA":"Users can bookmark any article to their private reading list","optionB":"Exactly one support agent may claim each incoming ticket","correct":"B","explanation":"'exactly one' of a shared resource with many simultaneous claimants is the classic contention signal, like limited inventory or auction items; private per-user writes have no competing writers and no contention."},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-27","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A social app engineer notices like counts occasionally lag behind reality and proposes wrapping every like in a Redis distributed lock to make counting 'correct'. Likes arrive at thousands per second. Is the lock warranted?","answer":"No — likes are commutative increments with no invariant to protect: there is no limited supply and nothing to oversell, so ordering does not matter. Plain atomic increments (and tolerance for briefly stale counts) are correct and fast, while a lock would only strangle throughput.","explanation":"Don't overcomplicate: commutative operations without a scarcity invariant need no locking"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-28","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"A video platform stores each video's view count in a single row. For viral videos, thousands of increments per second all serialize on that one row's lock and database latency spikes. Exact real-time counts are not required. What is a standard mitigation?","answer":"Shard the counter: split it into N rows (say 16), have each increment pick one shard at random, and sum the shards on read — optionally batching increments in memory and flushing periodically. This spreads one hot row's writes across many rows, trading a slightly stale total for restored throughput.","explanation":"Hot-row mitigation with sharded counters"},
  {"id":"hi-dealing-with-contention-29","topic":"patterns · dealing with contention","type":"open","question":"An art-auction site sees heavy lock contention on popular lots as concurrent bids race each other. An engineer proposes dropping row locks entirely: publish every bid to a per-lot queue partition consumed by a single worker that applies bids strictly in order. What does this design buy, and what does it cost?","answer":"It buys serialization without locks: one writer per lot applies bids in a strict order, so there is no lock contention and no wasted retries even at peak. The cost is asynchrony — bids are acknowledged before they are applied, latency rises, and bidders must be notified of win/lose outcomes out-of-band instead of in the response.","explanation":"Queue-based serialization: a single ordered writer per resource instead of locks"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-01","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce marketplace is adding seller-uploaded product demo videos averaging 200MB. One engineer proposes a video_bytes BLOB column in the existing MySQL orders database \"so everything stays in one place\"; another wants an object store with just a reference in MySQL. Which approach is right, and what heuristic decides it?","answer":"The object store. Anything above roughly 10MB that you never need to query with SQL belongs in blob storage, with only a pointer and metadata in the database. 200MB videos are pure bytes — the database gains nothing from holding them.","explanation":"The ~10MB rule of thumb for choosing object storage over the database"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-02","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A B2B invoicing SaaS has stored customer PDF invoices as bytea columns in Postgres for two years. Nightly backups that once took 20 minutes now take 6 hours, read replicas lag by minutes during business hours, and unrelated queries on the invoices table have slowed. What is going wrong?","answer":"The blobs are bloating the database: every backup and every replication stream must ship gigabytes of PDF bytes, and the fat rows degrade query performance. Moving the PDFs to object storage and keeping only metadata rows fixes all three symptoms at once.","explanation":"Symptoms of storing large binaries inside a relational database"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-03","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A messaging app added 1GB video attachments, uploaded via client to API server to S3. Every evening the API servers' network interfaces saturate, and even tiny requests like marking messages read start timing out fleet-wide. What is going wrong?","answer":"The app servers are proxying every attachment byte, so file traffic consumes the same NICs and connections as the rest of the API — the servers became a bandwidth bottleneck. The fix is presigned URLs so clients upload directly to S3 and file bytes never touch the API fleet.","explanation":"Server-as-proxy antipattern creating a bandwidth bottleneck"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-04","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing company's driver-verification service accepts license scans and dashboard videos. Each request handler reads the entire uploaded file into memory before writing it to storage. During Monday-morning onboarding surges the service OOM-kills repeatedly even though CPU usage is low. What should the team change, and why?","answer":"Stop holding file bytes in the service at all: issue presigned URLs so drivers upload directly to blob storage. Buffering N concurrent large files in RAM guarantees memory exhaustion under load — the crash is a direct consequence of proxying.","explanation":"Memory exhaustion from buffering large uploads in app servers"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-05","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A gaming platform lets players share replay files. To keep upload latency acceptable, the team quadrupled their app-server fleet, yet CPU utilization sits near 10% and the monthly compute bill has tripled. What does this pattern of symptoms tell you?","answer":"They are paying compute prices to do a data-transfer job. The fleet scaled for bandwidth, not processing — object storage already handles ingest natively and cheaply, so direct client-to-storage uploads would let them shrink the fleet back down.","explanation":"Cost inefficiency of scaling compute just to move bytes"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-06","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"An IoT camera startup saved uploaded clips to the attached disk of its single application VM. The disk failed and three months of customer footage is unrecoverable. Which property of managed object storage would have prevented this, and roughly how strong is it?","answer":"Durability. Object stores replicate every object across multiple devices and availability zones, advertising around 99.999999999% (11 nines) durability, so a single disk or host failure loses nothing. A lone VM disk is a single point of data loss.","explanation":"Object-store durability versus local server disk"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-07","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A fintech app must let mobile users upload KYC selfies straight into a private bucket, but the security team forbids embedding any storage credentials in the mobile binary. How can clients get permission to write to the bucket, and what makes the mechanism safe?","answer":"The backend generates a presigned URL: a cryptographically signed link scoped to one object key, one operation, and a short time window. The client uses the URL directly with no credentials on the device, and the signature cannot be altered to reach other keys or operations.","explanation":"Presigned URLs grant temporary scoped access without shipping credentials"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-08","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A ticketing platform \"adopted presigned URLs\": the client sends the seat-map image to the API server, which attaches a presigned URL and forwards the bytes to storage. Upload load on the API servers has not dropped at all. What did the team get wrong?","answer":"They kept the server in the data path. The whole point is that the backend only issues the URL and records metadata, and the client itself PUTs the file to storage using that URL — the bytes must never transit the API server.","explanation":"In the direct upload flow the client itself transfers the bytes"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-09","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A telehealth portal keeps lab-result PDFs in a private bucket. Today the API server downloads each PDF from the bucket and streams it to the patient's browser, and downloads are slow under load. The bucket must stay private. What should they change?","answer":"Have the API authorize the request, then return a presigned GET URL so the browser fetches the PDF directly from storage. The bucket stays private — the time-limited signature is the access grant — and the server stops relaying bytes.","explanation":"Direct downloads via presigned GET from a private bucket"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-10","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A legal-tech file-sharing tool generates presigned download links that expire after 7 days for convenience. A link to a confidential deposition leaked into a public Slack channel and remained fetchable by anyone for days. What should the team change, and why?","answer":"Cut the expiry to minutes and mint a fresh URL on each authorized request. Expiration is the only control left once a signed URL escapes — a short window turns a leak into a near-miss instead of a week-long open door.","explanation":"Short expiration windows limit the blast radius of leaked URLs"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-11","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A cloud-backup product uploads 10GB archives with S3 multipart upload. Logs show every part uploading successfully with an ETag returned, yet the final object never appears in the bucket. Which step is the client missing?","answer":"CompleteMultipartUpload. Parts sit unassembled under the upload ID until the client sends the completion call with the part-number-to-ETag manifest; only then does storage stitch them into the visible object.","explanation":"Multipart upload requires an explicit complete call with the part manifest"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-12","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A drone-mapping SaaS has field crews upload 4GB imagery bundles over flaky rural LTE. Today a connection drop at 99% restarts the transfer from byte zero, and crews sometimes retry for hours. What should the team change, and why does it work?","answer":"Switch to chunked multipart uploads and track which parts completed. After a drop, the client asks which parts storage already has and re-uploads only the missing ones, so a 99% failure costs one chunk, not 4GB.","explanation":"Resumable uploads re-send only the missing parts after failure"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-13","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A music-mastering web app uploads 2GB WAV files as one long PUT. Users on other continents report uploads using only a small fraction of their measured bandwidth, though nothing is throttled. How can the app speed these uploads up without any network changes?","answer":"Split the file into parts and upload several concurrently. A single connection on a high-latency path can't fill the pipe; parallel part uploads multiply effective throughput and multipart APIs accept parts in any order.","explanation":"Parallel part uploads raise throughput on high-latency links"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-14","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A job board lets applicants record video resumes on their phones. If the app is killed mid-upload, reopening it shows the progress bar back at 0% even though most chunks reached storage, and users often start over. What piece of state management is missing?","answer":"Persistence of per-chunk completion. The client (or backend) should record which parts finished — or query the storage service's list-parts API — so on relaunch it can render true progress and resume from the last completed chunk.","explanation":"Persisted per-chunk state enables accurate progress and cross-session resume"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-15","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A cloud-drive product supports resuming interrupted uploads, but only within one browser session. When a user retries yesterday's failed 8GB upload from a different laptop, the backend cannot tell it is the same file as the half-finished attempt it already has parts for, so it starts from zero. What should the client compute and send, and what does it enable?","answer":"A fingerprint: a hash of the file's content (or of its chunks) computed before uploading. Presenting it lets the server match the new attempt to the stored partial upload and resume it, and if the fingerprint matches an already-complete object it can skip the upload entirely — deduplication.","explanation":"File fingerprinting identifies the same file across sessions for resume and dedup"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-16","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"two-choice","question":"A single S3 PUT request can upload an object of any size, so multipart upload is purely a throughput optimization.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"A single PUT is capped at 5GB, so multipart upload is mandatory for anything larger (objects up to 5TB) and is recommended for files over roughly 100MB for speed and resumability."},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-17","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A log-archiving service uploads 100GB bundles to S3 with multipart upload and, to make retries cheap, an engineer sets the chunk size to 1MB. Uploads start erroring once the part number passes 10,000, and even smaller test files are rejected at the completion step. Which two part-level limits is the design hitting, and what chunk size fixes it?","answer":"A multipart upload allows at most 10,000 parts, and every part except the last must be at least 5MB — 1MB chunks break both rules. Chunks must be at least 5MB and big enough to keep the part count under 10,000, so at least ~10MB for a 100GB file.","explanation":"S3 multipart part constraints: 5MB minimum part size and 10,000-part cap drive chunk sizing"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-18","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A social feed's support queue shows two recurring bugs: posts whose database row exists but whose video 404s in the bucket, and orphan videos in the bucket with no database row. What class of problem is this, and what does it stem from?","answer":"State synchronization between the metadata store and the blob store. The two systems share no transaction, so a crash between 'write DB row' and 'upload blob' (in either order) leaves them disagreeing — you need a completion signal plus periodic reconciliation.","explanation":"State drift between metadata database and blob storage"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-19","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"An online-lecture platform flips a video's status to 'ready' when the uploader's browser calls /uploadComplete after its direct upload. Some lectures stay stuck in 'uploading' forever, and a pen-tester managed to mark a nonexistent file as ready. What is the weakness in this design?","answer":"It trusts the client. Browsers close before confirming (stuck files) and a malicious client can confirm without uploading (fake files). Completion must be verified server-side — check the object exists in storage, or better, consume storage event notifications.","explanation":"Client-side completion confirmation is unreliable and untrustworthy"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-20","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A print-on-demand shop needs artwork marked available in its database the moment the file lands in the bucket, even if the customer closes the tab a millisecond after upload finishes. What mechanism gives the backend a dependable completion signal?","answer":"Bucket event notifications: storage emits an ObjectCreated event (S3 to SQS/Lambda, GCS to Pub/Sub) that a backend consumer uses to flip the database status. The storage system itself reports completion, so no client cooperation is needed.","explanation":"Storage event notifications as the reliable completion signal"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-21","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A real-estate site inserts a photo's database row at the moment it issues the presigned upload URL. Listings now show broken image tiles for photos still mid-upload or abandoned entirely. What should change in how the metadata row is handled?","answer":"Create the row with a pending/uploading status and only flip it to complete once the upload is confirmed. Queries that render listings should filter to completed files, so half-done or abandoned uploads never surface to viewers.","explanation":"Pending status lifecycle keeps half-uploaded files invisible"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-22","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A design-collaboration SaaS notices its storage bill creeping upward. An audit finds thousands of weeks-old incomplete multipart uploads and matching 'pending' metadata rows left by users who closed their laptops mid-upload. What should the team put in place?","answer":"Cleanup on both sides: a bucket lifecycle rule that aborts incomplete multipart uploads after a few days (their invisible parts are still billed), and a background sweeper that deletes or expires stale pending metadata rows.","explanation":"Garbage-collecting abandoned uploads via lifecycle rules and metadata sweeps"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-23","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"two-choice","question":"Your team is porting a direct-upload design from AWS to Azure. Which Azure mechanism plays the role of an S3 presigned URL?","optionA":"A shared access signature (SAS) URL","optionB":"An Event Grid subscription","correct":"A","explanation":"SAS tokens are Azure's time-limited, scoped-access URLs for Blob Storage; Event Grid is the notification service, the analog of S3 event notifications, not of presigned access."},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-24","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"In a system design interview for a food-delivery platform, the interviewer adds: \"restaurants also upload menu photos and hygiene-certificate PDFs.\" The candidate starts sketching how those files fit into the restaurants table. What should this requirement have triggered instead?","answer":"The large-blob pattern: files go to object storage via presigned direct upload with a CDN for serving, and the database keeps only metadata and keys. Any 'users upload files' requirement is the standing cue to reach for this pattern, not for table columns.","explanation":"Recognizing the interview cue: any user-file requirement triggers this pattern"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-25","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A smart-home platform ingests 2KB JSON telemetry readings every few seconds per device. An engineer proposes issuing a presigned URL for each reading so devices write directly to S3, \"for scalability like the big file systems do.\" Why is this the wrong tool here?","answer":"The pattern exists for large blobs; 2KB structured readings that need querying belong in a database or stream. Per-reading presigned URLs add a signing round-trip and per-object overhead while removing the ability to query — complexity with zero benefit.","explanation":"When not to use: small structured payloads belong in the database"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-26","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A podcast-hosting service caps episodes at 500MB, checked in the web form. A user scripted a request to the presigned URL endpoint and uploaded an 80GB file, spiking the storage bill. Given the server never sees the bytes, where must the limit be enforced?","answer":"In the signed upload policy itself: for example an S3 presigned POST with a content-length-range condition (and expected content-type), so storage rejects any upload outside the limits. Once the URL is issued the server is out of the data path, so only conditions baked into the signature can be enforced.","explanation":"Enforcing size and content-type limits inside the signed upload policy"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-27","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A free-tier note-taking app finds one account requesting thousands of presigned upload URLs per minute and filling the bucket with junk objects, each individually within the size limit. Where is the right choke point to stop this, and how?","answer":"The issuance endpoint — it's the last step the backend controls. Rate-limit presigned-URL requests per user and enforce storage quotas before signing, refusing new URLs once an account exceeds its allowance.","explanation":"Rate limits and quotas on the presigned-URL issuance endpoint"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-28","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A recruiting platform lets candidates upload resumes that recruiters then download. Security asks: since files go straight from candidate to bucket, how do we ensure a recruiter never downloads malware? What flow should the team propose?","answer":"Quarantine by default: mark each upload unavailable until an asynchronous virus/content scan — triggered by the storage upload event — passes, then flip the status to downloadable. Files that fail are deleted or flagged, never served.","explanation":"Quarantine and async scanning before uploads become downloadable"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-29","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A project-management tool renders each project's attachment list by calling the bucket's ListObjects API on page load. Listings are slow, and the team can't filter by uploader or sort by upload date. What is the correct division of labor they're missing?","answer":"Metadata belongs in the database: a row per file with owner, name, size, type, status, and the blob key, indexed for listing, search, and permission checks. The bucket stores only bytes under opaque keys and is never queried to answer product questions.","explanation":"Metadata belongs in the database; the bucket holds only bytes"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-30","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A 3D-printing model marketplace serves 1GB model bundles directly from a bucket in us-east-1. Customers in Australia report downloads several times slower than US customers, and origin egress fees are climbing. What should the team add, and why does it fix both problems?","answer":"A CDN in front of the bucket. Popular files get cached at edge locations near users, so Australians download from a nearby edge instead of crossing the Pacific, and cache hits stop billing origin egress.","explanation":"CDN edge caching for fast global downloads"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-31","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"A fitness-subscription app moved its paid workout videos behind a CDN for speed, but now anyone who obtains a video URL can stream premium content without an account. The bucket itself is locked down to the CDN. How should access control work now?","answer":"Use signed CDN URLs (or signed cookies): the backend authenticates the subscriber, then issues a short-lived signed URL the CDN validates before serving. Authorization moves to the edge, and unsigned or expired requests are refused.","explanation":"Signed CDN URLs keep private content protected at the edge"},
  {"id":"hi-large-blobs-32","topic":"patterns · large blobs","type":"open","question":"An online cooking-class site serves 2GB lesson videos from blob storage via a CDN. When a student jumps to minute 40, the player stalls because it is downloading the file from the beginning. Which HTTP mechanism should the player use, and what does it do?","answer":"Range requests: the player sends a Range header to fetch only the byte window it needs, and blob stores and CDNs serve the partial content. That makes seeking instant and also enables resuming interrupted downloads.","explanation":"HTTP range requests enable seeking and partial downloads"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-01","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A SaaS analytics dashboard lets customers export a year of data as a PDF. The export endpoint does all the work inside the request handler and takes about two minutes, while the load balancer in front of the app has a 60-second idle timeout. Every export now ends in a 504 in the browser, even though server logs show the export code finishing successfully a minute later. What is going wrong?","answer":"The work outlasts the load balancer's timeout, so the connection is cut before the handler returns — the request fails even though the work succeeds. Minutes-long work cannot be held inside a request; it must become an async job: enqueue, return a job ID immediately, and finish on background workers.","explanation":"LB/gateway timeout ceiling on synchronous requests"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-02","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce platform runs merchant catalog imports synchronously on the same web servers that serve product pages. Whenever a few large imports are running, ordinary shoppers see slow loads and timeouts on pages that normally return in 50ms. What is happening?","answer":"The long imports pin web-server threads and connections for minutes, starving the shared pool that fast requests depend on — a handful of heavy requests degrades everyone. Heavy work belongs on a separate worker fleet fed by a queue so the web tier only ever does quick request handling.","explanation":"Web-tier thread/connection exhaustion from slow synchronous work"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-03","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A fintech app generates year-end tax packets through a synchronous endpoint. When generation runs long, the request times out, users click Generate again, and some customers end up with three duplicate packets emailed to them. What design flaw does the duplication reveal?","answer":"Timeouts plus user retries restart the same expensive work from scratch on every attempt, and synchronous processing gives no handle to deduplicate on. Accept the request once as a tracked job with an ID; a retry then maps to the existing job instead of spawning new work.","explanation":"Timeout-driven client retries duplicating expensive work"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-04","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A creator video platform is designing its post-upload flow. Transcoding takes 10-30 minutes, and the team is debating what the 'start processing' API call should actually do before it responds to the client. What should that endpoint do, and what should it return?","answer":"Validate the request, persist a job record, push a message onto the queue, and return the job ID — all within milliseconds. The transcoding itself happens later on workers pulling from the queue, and the client tracks it through the job ID.","explanation":"Two-phase design: validate, enqueue, return job ID immediately"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-05","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A ticketing company moved bulk refunds for cancelled events behind a message queue. Refunds now process reliably, but support agents cannot answer 'did my refund batch finish?' — the only trace of a job is its queue message, which vanishes once a worker consumes it. What component is missing?","answer":"A job status record in a database: created at submission and updated by workers through pending, running, completed, or failed, with a pointer to results, exposed via a status endpoint. The queue is transport, not a system of record.","explanation":"Job status table as the system of record for job state"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-06","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery app's data-export feature first enqueues the job message and then inserts the job row into the database. Occasionally a worker grabs the message so quickly that its 'mark as running' update finds no row, and users who poll right after submitting get a 404. What should the team change?","answer":"Reverse the order: commit the job record first, then enqueue the message. A message must never exist before the row it references, or workers and pollers race against a record that isn't there yet.","explanation":"Persist the job record before enqueueing to avoid the message-before-row race"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-07","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A gaming studio computes end-of-season reward bundles for millions of players. Under the old synchronous design they had to triple the entire web fleet at each season boundary even though normal API traffic stayed flat. Now the computation runs behind a queue with dedicated workers. How should their scaling approach change?","answer":"Scale the worker fleet on queue backlog, independently of the web tier — API servers stay sized for request traffic while workers grow and shrink with job volume. Decoupling the two tiers' scaling is a core gain of the pattern.","explanation":"Independent scaling of the web tier and the worker fleet"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-08","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"An IoT platform compiles customer firmware images. Previously, a compiler bug that OOM-killed the process would take an API server down with it. Since moving compilation onto queue-fed workers, the same crash kills only one worker, the message reappears, and another worker finishes the build — the API never blips. Which benefit of the async pattern is this demonstrating?","answer":"Fault isolation: worker failures are contained to the worker fleet, and the job survives in the queue for redelivery, so user-facing availability is untouched by processing crashes.","explanation":"Fault isolation between workers and the user-facing API"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-09","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A social app resizes uploaded photos through a queue and a worker pool sized for average load. During a festival, uploads spike to ten times normal; nothing errors and no uploads are lost — processing simply lags by a few minutes until the spike passes. What role is the queue playing here?","answer":"It is a buffer decoupling arrival rate from processing rate: bursts accumulate as backlog instead of overloading anything, and workers drain it at their own pace. Absorbing spikes without dropping work is one of the pattern's core gains.","explanation":"The queue as a burst-absorbing buffer decoupling arrival from processing rate"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-10","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A workspace chat product lets admins bulk-invite users; the API enqueues the job and instantly shows a success toast. An admin invites 500 people and moves on — but the job later dies on a bad SSO config, and nobody notices for a week. What did the team overlook?","answer":"An async acceptance means 'accepted', not 'done' — the system became eventually consistent, so completion and failure must be surfaced explicitly through status UI, notifications, or alerts. Returning success at enqueue time and never closing the loop hides failures.","explanation":"Async acceptance means eventual consistency — completion and failure must be surfaced"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-11","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A startup adopted a 'queue everything' rule, so even a profile-name update — a single 20ms database write — now goes through a queue, a worker, and a client-side polling loop before the UI can display the new name. What is wrong with this architecture?","answer":"Async is the wrong tool for sub-second work: synchronous handling is simpler, returns the result immediately, and provides natural backpressure. Queues should be reserved for work too long to hold a request open — queueing everything adds latency and infrastructure for nothing.","explanation":"Heuristic: keep sub-second work synchronous instead of reflexively queueing"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-12","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A three-person team building an image-thumbnail pipeline is debating between Kafka and a managed queue like SQS for dispatching jobs to workers. They need per-message retries, a dead-letter path for bad messages, and minimal operational burden; they do not need replay or strict ordering. Which fits better here, and why?","answer":"SQS: it is fully managed and provides at-least-once delivery, visibility timeouts, receive-count tracking, and native dead-letter queues out of the box. Kafka earns its operational cost only when you need very high throughput, partition ordering, or replayable history — none of which this team needs.","explanation":"Choosing a managed queue (SQS) vs Kafka for job dispatch"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-13","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A media company runs two background workloads: film-length video renders taking 3-4 hours each, and thumbnail jobs taking about two seconds that arrive in unpredictable bursts. They must pick compute for each. What should they choose, and why?","answer":"Containers for the renders — serverless functions like Lambda cap execution around 15 minutes, and long steady work suits a persistent fleet. Lambda fits the thumbnails: short tasks with spiky arrival get instant scale-out with no idle servers.","explanation":"Worker compute choice: containers for long steady work vs serverless for short bursty work"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-14","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing company is designing a GDPR 'download my data' feature that gathers records from a dozen services and takes several minutes. A junior engineer proposes keeping it synchronous and simply raising the gateway timeout to ten minutes. Why is that the wrong call?","answer":"Minutes-long work is the textbook trigger for the async job pattern — raising the timeout just relocates the failure cliff while still pinning connections and server resources for minutes, and it does nothing for crashes or client retries. Accept it as a job with an ID and process it on workers.","explanation":"Raising timeouts is not a fix — minutes-long work is the design-time signal for async"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-15","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce order system generates invoices on workers reading from SQS. During a deploy, a worker was killed halfway through a job, yet the invoice appeared a minute later with no human intervention and no resubmission. What mechanism made that happen?","answer":"The visibility timeout: an in-flight SQS message is hidden, not deleted, and when the dead worker never acknowledged it, the message became visible again and another worker processed it. That redelivery loop is the pattern's built-in crash recovery.","explanation":"Visibility timeout redelivery as built-in crash recovery"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-16","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A data platform's ETL jobs take about ten minutes each, and the team left the queue's visibility timeout at its 30-second default. Nearly every job is now processed by several workers at once, and results get written multiple times. What is going wrong?","answer":"Each message reappears after 30 seconds while the first worker is still mid-job, so other workers pick up what looks like a stalled message. Set the visibility timeout beyond worst-case job duration, or heartbeat to extend it, so redelivery only happens on genuine failures.","explanation":"Visibility timeout must exceed job duration or live jobs get redelivered"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-17","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A logistics company's workers pull route-optimization jobs from SQS. Worried about duplicate processing, a developer changed the workers to delete each message from the queue the moment it is received, before starting the multi-minute computation. Last week a spot instance was reclaimed mid-computation and that job simply vanished — never completed, never retried. Why did the queue's crash recovery not kick in?","answer":"Deleting the message on receipt throws away the queue's safety net: redelivery only works while the message still exists, so the change traded at-least-once for at-most-once and a mid-job crash now loses the job permanently. Delete the message only after the work is finished and the result recorded — the visibility timeout already hides it from other workers while the job runs, and idempotency absorbs the rare redelivery.","explanation":"Delete the queue message only after the work is done — ack-on-receipt loses jobs on crash"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-18","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A cloud backup service runs multi-hour archive jobs that copy millions of files per customer. When a worker dies at hour three, the queue redelivers the message and the next worker starts the whole archive over from file one — the largest accounts have never completed a backup because something always fails before the end. What should the workers persist, and why does it change the outcome?","answer":"Durable checkpoints of progress — for example, the last file or batch completed, recorded in the job record as the job runs — so a redelivered job resumes from the checkpoint instead of from zero. Without checkpoints the cost of a retry grows with job length and the longest jobs can fail forever; with them, each attempt only risks the work done since the last checkpoint.","explanation":"Checkpointing long jobs so a retry resumes instead of restarting from zero"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-19","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A newsletter platform's send-workers call a third-party email API. When that API has a brief outage, the workers retry every failed call immediately in a tight loop, and the provider responds by rate-limiting the platform even harder, prolonging the outage. How should the retry behavior change?","answer":"Retry with exponential backoff plus jitter — increasingly spaced attempts give the struggling dependency room to recover, and the jitter staggers the fleet so retries don't land in synchronized waves. Tight-loop immediate retries turn a blip into a self-inflicted, extended outage.","explanation":"Exponential backoff with jitter for transient dependency failures"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-20","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"An IoT ingestion pipeline hits a malformed device message that crashes the worker parsing it. The queue redelivers it, the next worker crashes too, and the cycle repeats endlessly, burning worker capacity. What should the team add?","answer":"A dead-letter queue wired to a max receive count: after N failed deliveries, the poison message is automatically moved aside instead of cycling forever. Alert on DLQ depth and inspect the parked messages offline.","explanation":"Poison messages handled by max receive count plus a dead-letter queue"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-21","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"two-choice","question":"Messages that land in a dead-letter queue are automatically retried until they eventually succeed.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"A dead-letter queue is a parking lot for messages that already exhausted their retries; they sit there for humans to inspect, fix the underlying cause, and deliberately replay. Nothing consumes a DLQ automatically."},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-22","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A payroll product runs payout jobs from a queue the vendor documents as 'guaranteed at-least-once delivery.' Rarely, an employee is paid twice for the same run even though the job was only ever submitted once. Why does this happen, and what is the fix?","answer":"At-least-once means occasional duplicate delivery by design — after a crash or timeout, the same message can reach two workers. The handler must be idempotent: record an idempotency key such as the payout ID transactionally, and skip work that has already been performed.","explanation":"At-least-once delivery requires idempotent handlers"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-23","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A ticketing platform saw duplicate confirmation emails, so workers now read the job row and skip processing if status is already 'running.' Duplicates still slip through when two workers receive the same message within milliseconds of each other. Why does the check fail, and what fixes it?","answer":"It is a check-then-act race: both workers read 'pending' before either writes 'running.' Claim the job atomically — a conditional update like SET status='running' WHERE id=? AND status='pending' — and only proceed if your update actually changed the row.","explanation":"Atomic job claim via conditional update — naive check-then-act still races"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-24","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A B2B SaaS company gets complaints that data exports finish hours late on certain days, yet its dashboards show worker CPU and memory perfectly healthy the whole time. What metric is the team missing, and what should it drive?","answer":"Queue depth and the age of the oldest message — backlog is the real signal that consumers are not keeping up, and worker autoscaling should key off it. Healthy per-worker CPU says nothing about how long jobs wait in line.","explanation":"Queue depth and oldest-message age as the scaling signal, not worker CPU"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-25","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"During a stadium on-sale, a ticketing system's job queue grows without bound even with the worker fleet at its maximum size, and jobs run hours after submission. Scaling workers further is not an option. What should the team do at the front of the system?","answer":"Apply admission control: rate-limit or reject new job submissions so the backlog stops growing past what workers can drain. When consumers are maxed out, backpressure must be pushed to producers — an unbounded queue just converts overload into unbounded latency.","explanation":"Admission control when the worker fleet cannot scale further"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-26","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A photo app pushes both avatar thumbnails (about 100ms each) and full video renders (30+ minutes each) through one shared queue and worker pool. During render backlogs, users complain that a newly uploaded avatar takes an hour to appear. What is going wrong?","answer":"Head-of-line blocking: the fast jobs sit behind long ones because every worker is tied up with renders. Split the job classes into separate queues with their own worker pools so cheap, latency-sensitive work never waits behind heavy work.","explanation":"Head-of-line blocking when mixed workloads share one queue"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-27","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"two-choice","question":"A marketplace runs urgent background jobs (payment receipts) and bulk jobs (weekly seller analytics) through its async system. Which design keeps the urgent jobs consistently fast?","optionA":"One shared queue and worker pool, with a priority field on each message","optionB":"Separate queues per job class, each with its own dedicated worker pool","correct":"B","explanation":"Plain queues like SQS will not reorder on a priority field, and even where priorities exist, a shared pool lets bulk jobs occupy every worker; reserved capacity per class is what actually protects urgent latency."},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-28","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A marketing platform models a 2-million-recipient email campaign as a single queue message that one worker processes end to end. Campaigns take hours because only one worker can ever work on one, and a crash near the end triggers a redelivery that re-sends to almost everyone. How should the job be structured instead?","answer":"Fan the campaign out into many small tasks — for example, one message per batch of recipients — so the whole worker fleet processes it in parallel and a failed task retries only its own small slice. A parent job record aggregates completion across the child tasks so the campaign still reads as one job.","explanation":"Splitting one huge bulk job into many small tasks for parallelism and small retry blast radius"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-29","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"An ML platform's pipeline runs extract, transform, train, then report, implemented by each worker enqueueing the next step's message when it finishes. Failures mid-pipeline leave runs silently stuck, and nobody can tell how far a given run progressed. What should replace this design?","answer":"A workflow orchestrator such as Step Functions, Airflow, or Temporal that owns the multi-step state: it tracks each run's progress, retries individual steps, and handles branches and failure paths. Hand-rolled chain-enqueueing has no central state, so visibility and recovery simply do not exist.","explanation":"Workflow orchestrators for multi-step job dependencies instead of ad hoc chaining"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-30","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A browser-based video editor starts exports as background jobs and renders a progress bar by polling the status endpoint every 500ms from every open tab. The status service has become one of the platform's biggest sources of load. How should progress reach clients instead?","answer":"Poll at a modest interval — a few seconds is plenty for a progress bar — or push updates over WebSockets or SSE so clients receive progress without hammering the API. Sub-second polling at scale is self-inflicted load.","explanation":"Delivering progress to clients: sane polling cadence vs push over WebSockets/SSE"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-31","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"two-choice","question":"Moving long-running work behind a queue reduces overall system complexity, because the web tier becomes simpler.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"It increases complexity: you add a queue, a worker fleet, status tracking, retry machinery, and monitoring, and you take on eventual consistency. That cost is paid deliberately in exchange for responsiveness, fault isolation, and independent scaling."},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-32","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A CRM's bulk-import endpoint enqueues whatever CSV a user uploads and returns a job ID instantly. Malformed files then fail an hour later deep inside a worker, and users learn nothing at upload time. What should move, and to where?","answer":"Validation belongs in the synchronous accept phase: check format, size, and permissions — anything cheap — before enqueueing, so obviously bad requests fail immediately in front of the user. Only the genuinely long work should pay the async cost.","explanation":"Cheap validation belongs in the synchronous accept phase"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-33","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A reporting service's workers generate PDFs, save them to the worker's local disk, and store the file path in the job record. Downloads fail whenever the request routes to a different machine, or after the worker that ran the job is recycled. What should change?","answer":"Workers must write results to shared durable storage — blob storage like S3 — and put that URL in the job record. Worker-local disk is ephemeral and invisible to the rest of the fleet, so results stored there are effectively lost.","explanation":"Job results go to shared durable storage referenced by the job record, not worker-local disk"},
  {"id":"hi-long-running-tasks-34","topic":"patterns · long running tasks","type":"open","question":"A Node.js side project keeps pending export jobs in an in-memory array inside the API process, with a background loop draining it. Every deploy or crash silently loses whatever jobs were waiting. What is the structural fix?","answer":"Pending jobs must live in a durable external queue — SQS, RabbitMQ, or persistent Redis — that survives process restarts. The queue is the system of record for accepted-but-unfinished work, so it cannot share the application process's lifetime.","explanation":"Pending jobs must live in a durable external queue, not in-process memory"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-01","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"A concert-ticketing site handles checkout in one HTTP request handler: charge the card, then reserve the seat, then email the ticket, one call after another. During a peak on-sale, a routine deploy restarts the servers, and support is flooded with customers who were charged but hold no seat. What is the fundamental design flaw?","answer":"The process's only record of progress lived in the in-flight request, so a crash between steps silently abandoned the workflow mid-way with no way to resume or undo. Multi-step processes need their state persisted outside the request handler so a crash can be detected and recovered.","explanation":"Naive in-request sequential service calls lose all progress on a crash, leaving inconsistent state"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-02","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery startup's order service has grown into nested try/catch blocks, retry counters, and status flags tangled together with business rules like 'if the restaurant rejects, offer a substitute.' New engineers cannot tell recovery code from product logic, and every business change breaks some retry path. What does the multi-step process pattern identify as the root problem?","answer":"System-level concerns (crashes, retries, timeouts) are interwoven with business-level logic, which is exactly what makes these systems brittle. The fix is to push reliability into a workflow layer or durable execution engine so the business code reads as a clean sequence of steps.","explanation":"Interweaving system-level concerns (crashes, retries) with business logic makes workflows brittle"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-03","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"An HR SaaS runs employee onboarding as rows in an 'onboarding' table with a status column; a cron worker scans for stalled rows every minute and fires the next step. It worked at 4 steps, but at 12 steps the transitions are scattered across a dozen handlers and rows keep getting stuck in states nobody wrote code for. What approach is this, and why is it hitting a wall?","answer":"This is the single-server primitive: a database-as-state-machine with a polling recovery worker. It is fine for simple flows, but the checkpointing, transitions, and retry logic are all hand-rolled and scattered, so every added step multiplies edge cases — the point where a workflow engine that centralizes state and recovery pays off.","explanation":"Single-server primitive: database status-column state machine plus polling worker, and how it sprawls"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-04","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"A print-on-demand shop outgrows its cron-scanned status table: each completed step now enqueues a message for the next stage (render, then print, then ship), and stateless workers consume from the queues. Throughput is much better, but when a customer asks 'where is my order?', engineers have to inspect three queues and three per-stage tables to answer. What did the queues buy them, and what gap remains?","answer":"Queues added durable handoff and horizontally scalable workers — an unacknowledged message survives a worker crash and is redelivered — but no single component holds the end-to-end workflow state, so per-order progress, cross-step timeouts, and compensation logic stay scattered. That missing explicit central state is exactly what an orchestrator or workflow engine adds on top.","explanation":"Queue-based step handoff: durable delivery and scalable workers, but end-to-end workflow state stays implicit"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-05","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"A travel site books a trip across three separate services: flight, hotel, then rental car. The hotel service goes down after the flight is already booked, stranding customers with half a trip. Distributed transactions across the three vendors are impossible. What pattern addresses this, and how does it work?","answer":"The saga pattern: model the booking as a chain of local transactions, each with a compensating action (cancel the flight, release the hotel) that runs in reverse order when a later step fails. Instead of atomicity, you get guaranteed forward completion or guaranteed semantic undo.","explanation":"Saga pattern: chain of local transactions each paired with a compensating transaction"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-06","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"A fintech wallet uses a saga: step one credits the recipient's balance, a later step fails, and the compensation debits it back. But in the gap between credit and compensation, some recipients saw the money and spent it. Which property of sagas does this incident expose?","answer":"Sagas have no isolation — each local transaction commits immediately, so intermediate states are visible to concurrent readers, and compensation is a new transaction rather than a rollback. Money-like resources need pending/hold states so nothing acts on funds until the saga completes.","explanation":"Sagas lack isolation: intermediate committed states are visible and compensation is not a true rollback"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-07","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"When a match ends on a gaming platform, three things must happen: update player ratings, grant loot, and post to the activity feed. The team has the match service publish a single MatchCompleted event to a broker, and each of the three services subscribes and reacts on its own. What coordination style is this, and what is its main advantage?","answer":"Event-driven choreography: there is no coordinator, each service independently reacts to events. Its advantage is loose coupling — a fourth consumer can be added later without touching the match service or any existing subscriber.","explanation":"Event-driven choreography: services react to broker events with no central coordinator (loose coupling)"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-08","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"A social network runs content publishing as a chain of events across nine choreographed services. When on-call is asked 'where is post X stuck?', the only way to answer is grepping nine services' logs, and the true end-to-end flow exists only in senior engineers' heads. What is the core weakness being exposed?","answer":"In choreography the workflow is implicit — no single component owns the definition or the current state, so answering 'where is this instance?' and reasoning about the flow requires reconstructing it from scattered services. An orchestrator (or workflow engine) gives one authoritative place that knows the flow and each instance's position in it.","explanation":"Choreography pitfall: workflow definition and state are implicit and scattered, so tracing and changing the flow is hard"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-09","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"An IoT company rolls firmware updates through canary, health-check, staged, and full-fleet phases. One camp wants services reacting to each other's events; the other wants a coordinator service that drives each phase and records where every rollout stands. Compliance requires an instant, auditable answer to 'what stage is rollout Y in, and who advanced it?' Which approach fits better, and why?","answer":"Orchestration. A central coordinator makes the flow explicit and keeps authoritative per-instance state, so observability and audit trails come for free — exactly what choreography makes painful.","explanation":"Orchestration benefit: a central coordinator holds explicit workflow state, giving observability and auditability"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-10","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing service built an orchestrator that walks each trip through matching, driver notification, and pickup confirmation, keeping each trip's progress in the orchestrator's memory. The orchestrator host dies mid-afternoon and hundreds of in-flight trips freeze permanently. What must change for the design to survive this?","answer":"Every state transition must be persisted to durable storage before moving on, so any other worker can read the last recorded step and resume the workflow. This is precisely the guarantee durable execution engines provide by journaling each step to an event history.","explanation":"Orchestrator crash recovery requires persisting every state transition so another worker can resume"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-11","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"An engineer adopting a durable execution engine for an e-commerce checkout objects: 'After a crash it re-runs my workflow function from the top — won't the customer get charged a second time?' Is the concern valid, and why or why not?","answer":"No. On replay the engine re-executes the workflow function but intercepts each completed activity and returns its recorded result from the event history instead of calling it again, so the charge activity runs once and its stored result is reused. Only steps that never completed actually execute.","explanation":"Durable execution replay: completed activity results are read from event history, not re-executed"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-12","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"A subscription-billing workflow function reads the current wall-clock time and makes a direct HTTP call to a tax-rate API inline in the workflow body. After a worker restart, resumed workflows start failing with non-determinism errors. Why, and what is the rule being violated?","answer":"Replay requires the workflow function to make identical decisions every run, but wall-clock reads and direct network calls return different values on replay than they did originally. All side effects and nondeterministic operations must be moved into activities, whose results get recorded in the history and replayed verbatim.","explanation":"Workflow code must be deterministic; side effects and nondeterministic calls belong in activities"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-13","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"An insurance platform's payout activity calls the bank's transfer API. Occasionally the call times out after the bank has actually processed it, the workflow engine retries the activity, and the claimant is paid twice. What should the team change?","answer":"Activities run at-least-once under retries, so they must be idempotent: send a stable idempotency key (e.g., the claim ID) with the transfer so the bank deduplicates repeat attempts. The retry then becomes harmless instead of a double payout.","explanation":"Activities execute at-least-once, so they must be idempotent (idempotency keys on external calls)"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-14","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"A B2B analytics dashboard's report pipeline has five steps, each wrapping its own hand-written retry loop with hardcoded sleeps; policies are inconsistent, and one flaky downstream API triggers synchronized retry storms. The team is moving to a workflow engine — what specifically does the engine replace here?","answer":"The engine replaces all the hand-rolled loops with declarative per-activity retry policies — max attempts, exponential backoff, and timeouts configured rather than coded. Retry behavior becomes uniform, tunable, and out of the business logic.","explanation":"Workflow engines provide declarative per-step retry policies with exponential backoff and timeouts"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-15","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"An edtech product must wait 30 days after signup to end a trial, send a reminder, wait 3 more days, then downgrade the account. An engineer proposes a service that holds each user's flow on a thread sleeping for 30 days. What is wrong with this, and what does the pattern use instead?","answer":"In-memory sleeps die with the process and pin resources for weeks. Durable timers persist the wake-up time in the engine's store, so the workflow consumes nothing while waiting and fires correctly even across crashes and deploys — this is how workflows span days or months.","explanation":"Durable timers survive restarts and let workflows sleep for days or months without holding resources"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-16","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"An AWS-hosted startup is choosing a workflow system for order processing. Their flow has deep branching and loops they want to express and unit-test in their own programming language, and they already operate a Kubernetes cluster; the alternative on the table is a fully managed service where the flow is defined as a declarative JSON state machine. Which of the two approaches fits better here, and why?","answer":"A code-first durable execution engine like Temporal: complex branching and loop-heavy logic is far more natural and testable as real code than as a JSON state machine, and they can run the cluster themselves. Step Functions wins when the flow is simple and the team wants zero infrastructure, trading expressiveness for a managed service.","explanation":"Temporal-style code-first durable execution vs AWS Step Functions declarative managed state machines"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-17","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"A video platform picked Apache Airflow to run its per-upload pipeline — transcode, thumbnail, then notify — triggered thousands of times a day by individual user uploads. Runs queue behind the scheduler and per-video latency is terrible. Why is this tool a misfit?","answer":"Airflow is built for scheduled, batch-oriented data DAGs, not for high-volume workflows triggered per user event, so its scheduler becomes the bottleneck. Event-triggered transactional flows belong on a durable execution engine designed for many concurrent low-latency workflow instances.","explanation":"Airflow targets scheduled batch data DAGs, not high-volume event-triggered transactional workflows"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-18","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"In an interview you are designing a notification system: try a push notification first, wait five minutes for a read receipt, fall back to SMS, and finally to email. A candidate proposes chaining three queues with cron jobs sweeping for un-acked messages. What is the sharper way to frame this requirement?","answer":"This try-wait-fallback sequence is a multi-step process in disguise: model each notification as a durable workflow with steps, durable timers for the wait windows, and per-channel retries. That framing replaces the fragile queue-plus-cron scaffolding with one auditable state machine per notification.","explanation":"Recognizing workflow-shaped interview problems: multi-channel fallbacks, payments, fulfillment, human steps"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-19","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"A startup ships an AI coding agent: each run has an LLM plan the work, then execute a chain of tool calls — clone the repo, edit files, run tests, open a pull request — often taking 20 minutes with retries on flaky model calls. The whole loop lives in one HTTP request handler, and users lose entire runs whenever a pod recycles. Framed as a system design problem, what shape does this have and what should back it?","answer":"An agent run is a multi-step process: a long-lived, stateful sequence of failure-prone steps, so it belongs on a durable workflow — each model call and tool invocation as a retryable activity with progress checkpointed between steps — not in a request handler. This is why AI agent pipelines have become a prime spot where interviewers expect the workflow pattern.","explanation":"AI agent pipelines are workflow-shaped: long-running, stateful, failure-prone model and tool steps suit durable execution"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-20","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce team wants to introduce a workflow engine to handle converting a gift card into store credit — debiting one row and crediting another, both in the same Postgres database. Is the workflow engine justified here?","answer":"No — this is a single atomic operation in one database, so a plain ACID transaction gives all-or-nothing semantics with none of the machinery. Workflow patterns earn their complexity only when steps span services or systems that cannot share a transaction.","explanation":"When NOT to use: a single-database atomic operation should be an ACID transaction, not a workflow"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-21","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"An ad-tech engineer, excited after adopting a workflow engine for billing, proposes routing every real-time bid request — which must return in under 100 milliseconds — through the same engine as a three-step workflow. Why is this a bad idea?","answer":"Durable execution persists state and schedules tasks at every step, adding storage round-trips and queueing overhead that a sub-100ms synchronous path cannot absorb. Reserve workflow engines for long-running or background processes and keep hot request paths as direct calls.","explanation":"When NOT to use: latency-critical synchronous request paths cannot afford durable-execution overhead"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-22","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"A logistics company adds a customs-declaration step to its delivery workflow and deploys. Deliveries started before the deploy — some mid-flight for days — immediately begin failing with replay errors on the workflow engine. What happened, and what is the standard remedy?","answer":"Replaying old event histories against the changed code diverges from what was recorded, tripping non-determinism detection. The remedy is workflow versioning: gate the new step behind a version check (or register the change as a new workflow version) so old instances replay the original logic while new instances take the new path.","explanation":"Workflow versioning: deploying changed code breaks replay of in-flight histories; version the definitions"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-23","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"Months after versioning its refund workflow, a marketplace team wants to delete the crufty v1 code path entirely, but some v1 refund workflows are still in flight and could replay at any time. What are their options for retiring it safely?","answer":"Either drain: keep v1 code until every v1 instance has completed, then delete; or explicitly migrate the in-flight instances' state into the v2 definition. Deleting code that live histories still need to replay will break those workflows.","explanation":"Retiring old workflow code: drain in-flight instances or explicitly migrate their state first"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-24","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"A healthcare platform's imaging workflow passes 50MB scan files directly as activity inputs and outputs between its analysis steps. The workflow engine's history storage is ballooning and replays are getting slow. What should the team change?","answer":"Store the scans in object storage and pass only references (URLs/keys) through workflow state, since every activity input and output is journaled into the event history. Workflow state should carry small pointers and metadata, never large payloads.","explanation":"Keep workflow state small by passing blob-storage references instead of large payloads"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-25","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"A music-streaming service models each subscription as one long-lived workflow that loops forever: sleep until renewal, charge, handle failures, repeat monthly. After a couple of years, these workflows' event histories are enormous and replays crawl. What is the standard fix?","answer":"Periodically restart the workflow with a continue-as-new style operation: complete the current run and start a fresh one that carries forward only compact current state, resetting the event history. Perpetually looping workflows must truncate history or replay cost grows without bound.","explanation":"Unbounded looping workflows need continue-as-new style history truncation"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-26","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"A lending platform's loan workflow must stop after document collection and wait — possibly for days — for a human underwriter's decision, but escalate to a supervisor if nothing arrives within 72 hours. An engineer suggests an activity that polls the approvals table in a loop. What is the better model?","answer":"Have the workflow durably wait on an external signal (sent when the underwriter clicks approve) raced against a 72-hour durable timer that triggers escalation. The workflow consumes no compute while blocked, survives restarts, and this is the pattern's standard shape for webhooks and human-in-the-loop steps.","explanation":"External events and human-in-the-loop: durable waits on signals with timeout timers"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-27","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"A crypto exchange's PM demands a hard guarantee that each withdrawal-confirmation email is sent exactly once, and an engineer replies that their workflow engine 'already guarantees exactly-once execution.' Is the engineer right, and what is the honest answer?","answer":"No — no engine can guarantee exactly-once external side effects, because an activity can succeed right as its acknowledgment is lost, forcing a retry. The honest guarantee is at-least-once execution plus deduplication at the receiving side (an idempotency key per withdrawal the email provider honors), yielding effectively-once outcomes.","explanation":"Exactly-once external side effects are impossible; at-least-once plus receiver-side dedup gives effectively-once"},
  {"id":"hi-multi-step-processes-28","topic":"patterns · multi step processes","type":"open","question":"In a mock system design interview about order fulfillment, a candidate cleanly diagrams payment, inventory, shipping, and email as a happy-path sequence and declares the design done. The interviewer looks unimpressed. According to this pattern's interview guidance, what is the candidate missing?","answer":"The senior signal is proactively walking the failure modes — a crash between charge and reserve, a retried step double-firing, a stuck workflow — and showing concretely how state persists and recovery works. Interviewers probe here deliberately because these processes dominate real teams' oncall, so a happy-path-only answer reads as junior.","explanation":"Interview signal: proactively articulate failure modes and recovery, since these workflows dominate production oncall"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-01","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"In a mock system design interview for a link-shortening service, a candidate spends 25 of the 35 minutes deriving from first principles that they need an API server, a load balancer, and a database, then runs out of time before touching the actually hard parts like custom aliases and extreme read skew. Their design was correct the whole way. What should the candidate do differently next time?","answer":"Recognize the problem instantly as a standard DB-backed CRUD pattern, sketch that baseline in a couple of minutes, and spend the recovered time on the novel, difficult requirements. Pattern recognition is the point: interviewers read fast identification of known structures as seniority, and time spent re-deriving them as a junior signal.","explanation":"Pattern recognition saves interview time and signals maturity"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-02","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"Two candidates in separate interviews propose the identical queue-plus-worker design for a receipt-scanning pipeline. When the interviewer asks 'what breaks here?', the first immediately lists duplicate processing from redelivery, poison messages crashing workers, and backlog growth during spikes — with a mitigation for each — while the second stares at their own diagram and starts guessing. Both drew the same boxes, so what does the first candidate have that the second doesn't?","answer":"Real command of the pattern, which comes bundled with its characteristic failure modes and standard mitigations: duplicates imply idempotency, poison messages imply a dead-letter queue, backlog implies queue-depth autoscaling. Recognizing a pattern is valuable precisely because it lets you predict how the system fails before it does, and interviewers probe for exactly that.","explanation":"Knowing a pattern means knowing its failure modes in advance"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-03","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"Two engineers are starting a recipe-sharing app: users create, edit, browse, and delete recipes. One proposes launching with a dozen microservices, Kafka between them, and separate read/write stores 'so we never have to rewrite it.' The other proposes a single service in front of one relational database. Which approach fits, and what does the simple version actually look like?","answer":"The single service wins: this is a textbook DB-backed CRUD workload. The baseline is clients hitting a load balancer, a horizontally scalable tier of stateless app servers, and one primary database — that shape carries most products much further than people expect, and the microservice version buys nothing here but operational pain.","explanation":"DB-backed CRUD service is the default baseline architecture"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-04","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"A SaaS analytics dashboard keeps each logged-in user's session object in the app server's memory. The team scales from one server to four behind a load balancer, and users immediately start getting randomly logged out or seeing half-loaded dashboards as they click around. What is going wrong?","answer":"The app servers are stateful, so a request routed to a different instance than the one holding the session finds nothing. Move session and request state into a shared store (the database or Redis) so every server can handle any request — statelessness is the property that makes the load-balanced tier scalable at all.","explanation":"Stateless app servers enable safe horizontal scaling"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-05","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce site's product database is pegged at high CPU, and profiling shows the same few hundred trending product pages are being fetched thousands of times per second, almost never changing between fetches. An engineer opens a design doc proposing to shard the database across eight nodes. What is the better first move, and why?","answer":"Put a cache in front of the database for these hot, rarely-changing reads — it removes most of the load with a one-component change. Sharding is a heavyweight, mostly irreversible escalation; the CRUD baseline is supposed to be extended incrementally, and repeated identical reads are exactly what caches exist for.","explanation":"First fix for CRUD read hotspots is a cache, not a rearchitecture"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-06","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"A secondhand-goods marketplace stores listings in Postgres. Users now want to type 'blue mid centry sofa' and get relevant results despite typos and word order. The team has been layering on LIKE '%...%' queries and trigram hacks, and search latency keeps climbing as listings grow. What architectural change should they make?","answer":"Stand up a dedicated search index (an inverted-index system like Elasticsearch) alongside Postgres and route search traffic to it. Full-text, fuzzy, relevance-ranked queries are a different access pattern than the primary database is built for — the standard CRUD extension is a purpose-built index next to the source-of-truth DB, not ever-cleverer SQL.","explanation":"Add a dedicated search index when queries outgrow primary-DB lookups"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-07","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"A ticketing site writes each new event to Postgres and then, in the same request handler, makes a second call to index it in Elasticsearch. Occasionally the Elasticsearch call fails after the Postgres commit succeeds, and those events are simply unfindable in search until someone notices. What should the team change?","answer":"Stop dual-writing from application code and derive the index from the database instead — change data capture or a queue fed from committed writes, with the indexer able to retry and catch up. Two independent writes with no transaction spanning them will always drift; making the index a downstream consumer of the source of truth makes convergence automatic.","explanation":"Sync a secondary index from committed data, not dual writes"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-08","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"A photo-book service generates print-ready PDFs, which takes about two minutes per book. Orders are processed inside the web request, so the load balancer kills the connection at its 30-second timeout and customers see errors even though rendering eventually finishes. What is the right structural fix?","answer":"Split acceptance from execution: the web server validates the order, drops a job onto a queue, and responds immediately; a separate pool of workers pulls jobs and renders the PDFs. Work that takes minutes has no business living inside a synchronous request — the queue-plus-worker-pool shape is the standard pattern for it.","explanation":"Queue plus worker pool decouples acceptance from execution"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-09","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"A podcast-hosting platform moved episode transcription into a background worker pool, and the endpoint now returns 202 with an empty body. Support tickets pour in: creators upload an episode, see nothing, and re-upload the same file three or four times 'because it didn't work.' The workers are actually processing everything fine. What is missing?","answer":"Job status tracking. Submission should return a job ID backed by a status record the client can poll (or be notified about), so users see queued/processing/done instead of silence. Going async without observable status trades a timeout problem for a trust problem.","explanation":"Async jobs need a job ID and queryable status"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-10","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"An IoT company batches device telemetry into processing jobs. Each job spends most of its time waiting on network calls to a third-party enrichment API. During evening peaks the queue backlog grows for hours, yet the worker autoscaler never adds instances because it triggers on CPU utilization, which sits at 15%. What should the team change?","answer":"Scale the worker pool on queue depth or backlog age instead of CPU. For I/O-bound jobs, host metrics say nothing about demand — the queue itself is the demand signal, and a growing backlog is the direct evidence that the pool is undersized.","explanation":"Autoscale workers on queue backlog, not CPU"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-11","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"A fintech app emails monthly statements via a queue and worker pool. A few customers each month receive the same statement email two or three times. Logs show a worker crashed (or was rebalanced) after sending the email but before acknowledging the message, and the queue handed the message to another worker. Why is this happening, and what is the fix?","answer":"The queue is doing its job: at-least-once delivery redelivers anything unacknowledged, so duplicates are guaranteed eventually. The fix is idempotent processing — record a per-statement send under a dedupe key and have workers check it before sending, so redelivery becomes a no-op rather than a second email.","explanation":"At-least-once delivery requires idempotent workers"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-12","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"A gaming studio's replay-processing pipeline ground to a halt overnight. One corrupted replay file crashes any worker that touches it; the queue then requeues it, the next worker picks it up and crashes too, and the cluster spends the night in a crash-requeue loop while valid replays pile up behind it. What mechanism is missing?","answer":"A dead-letter queue. After N failed attempts, the message should be shunted to a quarantine queue for later inspection instead of being redelivered forever. Without a DLQ, a single poison message can consume the whole pool and starve every healthy job.","explanation":"Dead-letter queues quarantine poison messages"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-13","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"During a redesign 'for scale,' a team routes their signup form's username-availability check — a 40ms indexed database lookup — through a message queue and worker pool. Users now watch a spinner for a second or more, responses occasionally arrive after the user has already typed a different name, and load spikes now silently pile up in the queue instead of failing fast. Was the change right?","answer":"No — this is the classic over-application of the async pattern. A 40ms check belongs in the synchronous request path, where the user gets an instant answer, the code stays simple, and overload produces natural back-pressure instead of an invisible backlog. Queues earn their complexity for long-running work, not fast lookups.","explanation":"Don't push short fast operations behind a queue"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-14","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"A streaming service wants each homepage row personalized with a heavy ML model that scores how likely the viewer is to watch a title. Running the model against all 20 million titles per page load is orders of magnitude over the latency budget, but the product team refuses to give up model quality. What architecture solves this?","answer":"A two-stage design: a fast, cheap first stage (simple heuristics, an ANN/vector lookup, or lightweight filters) narrows 20 million titles to a few hundred candidates, and the expensive model reranks only those. Precision where it counts, applied to a set small enough to afford — that split is the whole pattern.","explanation":"Two-stage architecture: cheap candidate generation, expensive reranking"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-15","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"A job-search site runs keyword retrieval followed by an ML reranker. To 'improve result quality,' an engineer bumps the candidate set passed to the reranker from 500 to 50,000 postings. Ranking latency explodes and quality barely moves. What did the engineer misunderstand about the pattern?","answer":"The division of labor. The reranker's per-item cost is exactly why the candidate set must stay small — its latency budget caps how many items stage two can score, so the candidate count is a fixed budget, not a quality dial. If quality is lacking, the fix lives in the first stage, not in shoveling a corpus-sized input into the expensive stage.","explanation":"Stage-two budget caps candidate count; quality comes from stage-one recall"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-16","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce search team has spent two quarters upgrading their reranking model, yet shoppers searching 'couch' still never see products listed as 'sofa.' Offline evaluation says the reranker orders its inputs nearly perfectly. Why isn't the reranker fixing this, and where is the real problem?","answer":"The reranker can only reorder what retrieval hands it — the 'sofa' products are being dropped by the keyword-matching first stage, so no amount of reranking skill can surface them. The fix is stage-one recall: add synonym expansion or embedding-based retrieval so relevant items make it into the candidate set at all.","explanation":"Items filtered out in stage one are unrecoverable downstream"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-17","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"A digital wallet stores only each user's current balance, overwriting it on every transaction. A customer disputes their balance, and support has no way to show how the number came to be — the history simply doesn't exist. Regulators are now asking for exactly that history. What storage model should the team move to?","answer":"Event sourcing: persist an append-only log of immutable events (credits, debits, reversals) as the source of truth, and compute the balance by folding over the log. The current-state-only model destroys history by design; the event log makes every balance explainable and auditable.","explanation":"Event sourcing: append-only event log as source of truth"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-18","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"A competitive gaming platform has recorded every match event (kills, objectives, surrenders) in an append-only log since launch. Product now wants a brand-new 'clutch rating' leaderboard — a statistic nobody thought to compute or store — and wants it populated for all three years of past matches, not just future ones. Can they do it, and how?","answer":"Yes, and it's a headline benefit of event sourcing: write the new projection logic and replay the historical event log through it to backfill three years of clutch ratings. Because events, not derived tables, are the source of truth, new read models can be built retroactively at any time.","explanation":"Replaying the event log builds new projections retroactively"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-19","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"An event-sourced shopping-cart service rebuilds each cart's state by replaying its full event stream on load. For long-lived accounts with hundreds of thousands of events, opening the cart now takes multiple seconds, and the replay time only grows. What is the standard mitigation?","answer":"Snapshots: periodically persist the computed state, then rehydrate by loading the latest snapshot and replaying only the events after it. Replay cost scaling with total history is the known tax of event sourcing, and snapshotting is the standard way to pay it down.","explanation":"Event replay cost grows with history; snapshots fix rehydration"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-20","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"In a ride-hailing backend, the trip service's 'trip completed' handler has grown to call the receipt emailer, the driver-rating prompt, the loyalty-points service, and the analytics pipeline in sequence. Every new post-trip feature means editing and redeploying the trip service, and a failure in any callee delays trip completion itself. How should this be restructured?","answer":"Have the trip service publish a trip-completed event to a bus and make each feature an independent consumer. Producers stop knowing who cares: new features subscribe without any change to the trip service, and one consumer's failure no longer drags down trip completion — that decoupling is the core payoff of event-driven architecture.","explanation":"Event-driven decoupling: add consumers without touching producers"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-21","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"A price-intelligence company crawls retail sites in jobs that each run six to eight hours on cheap preemptible VMs. Whenever a VM is reclaimed mid-crawl, the job is requeued and the replacement worker starts the entire crawl from page one, so some jobs effectively never finish. What is the right fix?","answer":"Make the job durable by checkpointing progress externally — persist the URL frontier and completed set in a shared store as the crawl proceeds, so a replacement worker resumes from the checkpoint rather than from zero. Any job whose runtime rivals the machine's failure interval must record progress outside the worker, or it will restart forever.","explanation":"Durable checkpointing lets long jobs resume instead of restart"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-22","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery app matches orders to nearby couriers by computing the distance from the restaurant to every one of its 2 million active couriers on each request — a full-table scan with a distance formula in the WHERE clause. Matching latency is now measured in seconds. What should replace this query?","answer":"A geospatial index (geohash, quadtree, or a PostGIS-style spatial index) that divides the map into regions, so the query examines only couriers in cells near the restaurant and prunes the rest of the planet outright. Distance-filtered full scans can't use a normal index; spatial partitioning is precisely the structure this lookup needs.","explanation":"Geospatial indexes partition space to prune irrelevant regions"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-23","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"two-choice","question":"A retail chain's store-locator must answer 'stores within 5 miles of the user' across roughly 300,000 locations. The entire backend already runs on Postgres. Which approach fits better?","optionA":"Enable PostGIS on the existing Postgres database and add a spatial index","optionB":"Stand up a dedicated Elasticsearch cluster to handle the geo queries","correct":"A","explanation":"Mainstream databases already ship capable geospatial support, and extending the system you operate beats adopting a whole new cluster for one query shape at this scale."},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-24","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"A boutique gym chain with 800 locations builds a 'find my nearest gym' feature. An engineer designs a dedicated microservice with Redis geospatial sets and a quadtree fallback, arguing nearest-neighbor search demands purpose-built indexing. Is the design justified?","answer":"No — at 800 rows, computing the distance to every gym per query is microseconds of work, faster and vastly simpler than operating a purpose-built index. Geospatial indexes earn their overhead in the hundreds of thousands to millions of items; below that, just scan.","explanation":"Below ~thousands of items, a full scan beats a geospatial index"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-25","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"two-choice","question":"True or false: a proximity-search service must be optimized for queries that span the whole globe at once, since a user could ask for results anywhere.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Users overwhelmingly search near their own location, so queries touch a small neighborhood of the map. That locality is exactly why dividing space into regions works: each query hits a handful of nearby cells, and globe-spanning queries are a non-goal."},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-26","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"A podcast platform is designing its creator pipeline: creators upload multi-gigabyte audio files, the platform transcodes them for minutes, creators watch a live progress bar, and on completion a publish sequence runs — transcript generation, subscriber notifications, feed updates — that must survive partial failures. The tech lead wants to design this as one bespoke system from scratch. What is the better way to frame the design?","answer":"Decompose it into the standard patterns it's made of: direct-to-storage upload for the large files, a queue-and-worker stage for transcoding, a realtime-update channel for the progress bar, and a multi-step workflow for the publish sequence. Real systems are compositions of known patterns wired together — recognizing that turns a daunting bespoke design into assembling solved pieces.","explanation":"Real designs compose multiple known patterns"},
  {"id":"hi-patterns-overview-27","topic":"patterns · patterns overview","type":"open","question":"Asked to design a personal to-do list app in an interview, a candidate opens with sharded Cassandra, a Kafka event bus, and separate read and write services, explaining they want to 'show depth from the start.' The stated requirements are a few thousand users and basic list operations. How will a strong interviewer read this, and what should the candidate have done?","answer":"As a red flag, not depth — complexity that the requirements don't justify signals the candidate can't calibrate. The right move is to start with the simple pattern the problem calls for, then escalate deliberately if and when the interviewer pushes scale, narrating what would trigger each addition.","explanation":"Escalate complexity only when requirements demand it"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-01","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A recipe-sharing site wants new comments to appear under a recipe the moment they are posted. A junior engineer asks why the API server can't simply 'send an HTTP response' to every browser currently viewing the recipe whenever a comment lands. What is the flaw in that plan?","answer":"HTTP is strictly client-initiated request/response — the server has no way to reach a browser that hasn't asked. Real-time delivery requires either clients repeatedly asking (polling) or a connection deliberately held open (long polling, SSE, WebSockets) that the server can write into.","explanation":"HTTP request-response cannot server-initiate updates"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-02","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"In a mock interview for a messaging app, a candidate picks WebSockets and moves on. The interviewer asks: 'Alice is connected to server 1 and Bob to server 7 — walk me through how Alice's message reaches Bob's phone.' The candidate freezes. What half of the real-time problem did they skip?","answer":"The second hop. Choosing WebSockets only solves client-to-server delivery; they never addressed how an update travels from its source to the specific server holding the recipient's connection — which needs pub/sub or consistent-hash routing.","explanation":"Two-hop decomposition of real-time delivery"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-03","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A studio's fast-paced multiplayer shooter streams player positions 30 times per second over a TCP-based channel. Players on lossy Wi-Fi report the game freezing briefly, then everyone 'teleporting' as a burst of updates catches up. What transport property is causing this?","answer":"TCP's ordered, reliable delivery: one lost packet blocks everything behind it until retransmission, then delivers a burst of stale positions. For data where fresh beats complete, UDP is the right layer-4 choice — drop the lost update and keep moving.","explanation":"TCP vs UDP for latency-sensitive streams"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-04","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A mobile trading app polls a quotes endpoint every 2 seconds, and profiling shows each poll opens a brand-new HTTPS connection. Per-poll latency is several times the server's 40 ms processing time, and battery drain is high. What is the app paying for on every request?","answer":"Full connection setup on each poll — DNS resolution, the TCP handshake, and the TLS handshake add multiple round trips before any data flows. Reuse persistent connections (keep-alive) or switch to a held-open streaming approach.","explanation":"Connection establishment cost (DNS/TCP/TLS handshakes)"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-05","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A concert-ticketing ops dashboard polls seat-inventory every 30 seconds. Ops complains the numbers are 'up to 30 seconds stale, usually around 15.' Engineers confirm the backend answers in 50 ms. Why is the staleness in exactly that band?","answer":"Polling freshness is bounded by the interval, not backend speed: worst case is one full interval, average is about half. The only fixes are a shorter interval or a push-based mechanism.","explanation":"Polling latency bounded by the polling interval"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-06","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"An internal SaaS admin panel used by about 200 employees shows background-job statuses, and updates arriving within a few seconds are perfectly acceptable. An engineer proposes building a WebSocket gateway tier backed by Redis pub/sub. What should the team do instead, and why?","answer":"Poll every couple of seconds. The latency tolerance is seconds and the scale is tiny, so stateful persistent-connection infrastructure is pure complexity tax — polling is stateless, trivial to operate, and fully meets the requirement.","explanation":"Right-sizing: polling as the baseline choice"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-07","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce platform's order-tracking page polls every second. With 2 million shoppers watching packages, 99.9% of responses say 'no change' and the server fleet is buckling. What is the structural problem with this design?","answer":"Polling cost scales with users times frequency regardless of how often anything actually changes, so nearly all capacity is spent producing empty responses. Move to long polling or SSE so server work scales with actual events instead.","explanation":"Polling load scales with users x frequency, not events"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-08","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"An auction site wants bidders to learn they've been outbid within about a second. Outbids on any single item are sporadic, and the stack sits behind an aging corporate proxy that only reliably handles ordinary HTTP request/response. Which delivery approach fits, and how does it work?","answer":"Long polling: the client sends a normal request, the server parks it until an outbid happens or a timeout fires, responds, and the client immediately re-requests. It's near-instant for sporadic events and rides plain HTTP, so the legacy proxy never notices anything unusual.","explanation":"Long polling mechanism"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-09","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A team implements long polling and holds each request open for 5 minutes to 'reduce request churn.' In production, clients sporadically receive connection resets around the 60-second mark. What did they miss?","answer":"Intermediaries — load balancers and proxies — enforce idle timeouts, commonly 30-60 seconds, and kill requests held longer. Cap the hold time safely below the shortest timeout in the path and let the client re-poll.","explanation":"Long polling vs intermediary idle timeouts"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-10","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A cricket live-scores app uses long polling. During overs with several ball-by-ball updates per second, clients fall behind and request volume explodes. Why does long polling specifically break down here?","answer":"Each delivered message costs an entire request/response cycle, so at high frequency long polling degrades into rapid-fire polling with all its overhead. High-rate streams belong on SSE or WebSockets, which push many events over one held connection.","explanation":"Long polling degrades at high update frequency"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-11","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"An AI writing tool streams generated drafts to the browser word-by-word, while users only send input through occasional form submissions. The team is debating a WebSocket build-out. What is the better-fitting protocol, and why?","answer":"Server-Sent Events: one long-lived HTTP response streaming events server-to-client is exactly the token-streaming shape. Traffic is essentially one-directional, so WebSockets' full-duplex machinery and stateful infrastructure buy nothing here.","explanation":"SSE fit for one-way server-to-client streaming"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-12","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A news site's election liveblog runs on SSE. Mobile readers on trains drop the connection often; the browser reconnects on its own, but posts published during the gap never appear. Which built-in SSE capability did the team leave unused?","answer":"Event IDs with Last-Event-ID resume. If the server stamps each event with an ID, EventSource automatically sends the last one it received on reconnect, and the server can replay everything after it — no missed posts.","explanation":"SSE auto-reconnect with Last-Event-ID"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-13","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"While evaluating protocols for a customer-support chat widget, a tech lead vetoes SSE: 'Customers have to send messages too, and SSE is one-way, so it can't work.' Is the veto sound?","answer":"No. SSE's one-way limit only covers the server-to-client stream; customer messages go up as ordinary HTTP POSTs. SSE-down plus HTTP-up is a legitimate, simpler design — WebSockets only earn their keep if upstream traffic is very frequent.","explanation":"SSE one-way limit doesn't block client sends"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-14","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A fitness platform streams live class stats over SSE. It works perfectly against a dev laptop, but in production events arrive in delayed clumps every ten seconds or so instead of flowing continuously. What is the likely culprit?","answer":"An intermediary — reverse proxy or CDN — is buffering the streamed response and flushing it in chunks. Disable response buffering on the streaming path and send periodic heartbeat comments so idle detection doesn't kill the connection.","explanation":"Intermediary buffering breaks SSE streams"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-15","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"An analytics SaaS opens a separate SSE stream for every dashboard widget. Customers with 8-widget dashboards served over HTTP/1.1 report that images stop loading and other API calls hang. What limit did the team hit, and what are the fixes?","answer":"Browsers cap roughly six concurrent HTTP/1.1 connections per host, and the SSE streams exhausted the pool, starving everything else. Multiplex all widgets over a single stream, or serve the site over HTTP/2 where streams share one connection.","explanation":"HTTP/1.1 per-host connection limit vs HTTP/2"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-16","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing company moves its rider traffic behind a new layer-7 load balancer. Ordinary API calls and pages work fine, but the realtime WebSocket channel now fails instantly at connect time. What is the most likely cause?","answer":"The load balancer isn't handling the HTTP Upgrade handshake. A WebSocket starts life as an HTTP request that gets upgraded into a persistent full-duplex TCP connection, and every hop in the path must support and forward that upgrade.","explanation":"WebSocket HTTP Upgrade handshake and infra support"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-17","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A collaborative whiteboard broadcasts each user's pen strokes to everyone in the room while simultaneously receiving theirs — dozens of small messages per second in both directions per user. Which client-server protocol fits, and what property makes it the fit?","answer":"WebSockets. A persistent full-duplex connection with minimal per-message framing is built precisely for high-frequency two-way traffic; polling variants would burn a request per message and SSE only covers the downstream half.","explanation":"WebSockets for high-frequency bidirectional traffic"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-18","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A mobile game's chat runs on a WebSocket tier autoscaled on CPU exactly like the rest of the stateless fleet. Scale-in events keep booting thousands of players mid-conversation. Why doesn't this tier behave like the others?","answer":"WebSocket servers are stateful: each instance physically holds live connections, so terminating one severs every user on it. The tier needs connection draining, conservative scale-in, and reconnect handling — it cannot be treated as interchangeable stateless capacity.","explanation":"WebSocket statefulness vs autoscaling/deploys"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-19","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A B2B SaaS embedded its WebSocket handling inside the main monolith. Now every deploy of any feature — billing, search, anything — drops all realtime connections and triggers reconnect churn. What architectural change limits the blast radius?","answer":"Split out a thin connection/gateway tier whose only job is terminating sockets and relaying messages to stateless backend services. Business logic then deploys freely without ever touching live connections, confining statefulness to a small, rarely-changed layer.","explanation":"Thin connection gateway tier isolates statefulness"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-20","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A chat service adds three WebSocket servers to its fleet during a traffic surge. Hours later the new machines sit nearly idle while the original servers stay saturated, even though the load balancer confirms it is spreading incoming connections evenly. Why hasn't the load evened out, and what fixes it?","answer":"Persistent connections are balanced only once — at connect time — and existing sockets never migrate, so pre-surge load stays pinned to the old servers no matter how new connects are distributed. Use least-connections balancing and actively rebalance by capping connection lifetimes or asking some clients on hot servers to reconnect.","explanation":"Long-lived connections don't rebalance after scale-out"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-21","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A tutoring startup built 1:1 video by capturing frames and relaying them through its API servers over WebSockets. Video is laggy and server bandwidth bills are exploding. What should they use instead, and what changes?","answer":"WebRTC. Media flows peer-to-peer over UDP directly between the two participants, so the extra relay hop's latency disappears and the servers shrink to lightweight signaling plus STUN/TURN — the bandwidth bill largely evaporates.","explanation":"WebRTC peer-to-peer media transport"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-22","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"The same startup's WebRTC calls connect flawlessly between two laptops on the office LAN but fail for most customer pairs on home and mobile networks. What part of a WebRTC deployment is missing?","answer":"NAT traversal infrastructure. Peers behind home/mobile NATs can't address each other directly: they need STUN to discover their public address/port mapping and TURN as a relayed fallback when a direct path can't be punched.","explanation":"STUN/TURN and NAT traversal"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-23","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"An IoT fleet-management product maintains an always-on WebSocket subsystem — heartbeats, reconnect logic, sticky routing — to display sensor readings that devices report once per hour. The subsystem generates a steady stream of on-call pages. What should the team change?","answer":"Remove the realtime plumbing entirely. Data that changes hourly with no sub-second requirement is perfectly served by polling or refresh-on-load; persistent connections here are all operational cost and no product value.","explanation":"Recognizing when real-time infra is unnecessary"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-24","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"An e-learning platform adds a class leaderboard that clients poll every 5 seconds. An engineer insists they must also add a pub/sub bus 'so the servers receive score updates.' What is he missing about how a polling design works server-side?","answer":"With client pull there is no push problem to solve: any stateless server answers a poll by reading current standings from the database. No server needs updates routed to it — that operational simplicity is exactly what polling buys you.","explanation":"Second hop under polling: stateless DB reads"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-25","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A collaborative slide-deck editor keeps each open deck's live state — operation log, cursors, presence — in memory on one server, but users connect from anywhere. How should the system decide which server owns deck #4831 so all of its connections and updates converge on the same machine?","answer":"Consistent-hash the deck ID onto the server ring: any node can deterministically compute the owner and route connections or updates there, and a coordination service like ZooKeeper or etcd tracks ring membership as servers join and leave.","explanation":"Consistent hashing to route to stateful session owners"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-26","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A document-editing platform assigns each document's connections to an owning server by computing hash(docId) mod N across its N connection servers. When a tenth server is added to relieve load, nearly every open document abruptly maps to a different owner, dropping sessions platform-wide and forcing mass state migration. What routing change confines the damage of future capacity changes?","answer":"Replace mod-N with consistent hashing: servers and keys sit on a hash ring, so adding or removing a node remaps only the roughly 1/N of documents adjacent to it instead of almost all of them. Capacity changes then disturb a sliver of sessions rather than the whole platform.","explanation":"Consistent hashing minimizes remapping on ring changes"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-27","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"two-choice","question":"A team is choosing the second-hop architecture for pushing updates. For which workload is a ring of stateful servers with consistent hashing a better fit than a pub/sub broker?","optionA":"A collaborative 3D-modeling tool where each active project holds heavy in-memory session state that every participant's updates must flow through","optionB":"A price-alert service fanning independent notifications out to whichever servers hold subscribers' connections","correct":"A","explanation":"Consistent hashing co-locates a session's state and its connections on one deterministic owner, while stateless fan-out of independent messages is exactly what pub/sub is for."},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-28","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"In a chat app, Alice's message lands on whichever API server happened to take her POST, but Bob's WebSocket lives on a different machine and the sender has no idea which one. Without building and maintaining a connection registry, how can the message find Bob's server?","answer":"Put a pub/sub broker in the middle: each connection server subscribes to a channel per connected user, and the sender just publishes to Bob's channel. The broker delivers to whichever server currently holds him — publishers never need to know the connection topology.","explanation":"Pub/sub decouples publishers from connection servers"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-29","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A social app's single Redis pub/sub node was fine at launch, but as connection servers and channels multiplied it is now pegging CPU and network. What does this reveal about the pub/sub second hop, and what is the remedy?","answer":"The broker is a centralized chokepoint — decoupling publishers from subscribers concentrates all routing traffic in one place that must be scaled deliberately. Shard channels across a Redis cluster or partition topics across multiple brokers.","explanation":"Pub/sub broker as central scaling bottleneck"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-30","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"two-choice","question":"For a product delivering updates over WebSockets fed by Redis pub/sub, the realtime pipeline by itself guarantees that every client eventually receives every message.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Redis pub/sub is fire-and-forget at-most-once, so anything published while a subscriber or client is disconnected is simply gone; the push path must be treated as a best-effort optimization with a durable store as the source of truth for catch-up."},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-31","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A fintech platform's WebSocket gateway node crashes and 400,000 clients all retry the instant they detect the drop, knocking over the remaining nodes — whose recovery triggers the identical stampede again. What client behavior breaks this loop?","answer":"Reconnect with exponential backoff plus jitter, so retries spread out over time instead of arriving as a synchronized wave. Pair it with server-side connection draining on planned restarts so mass disconnects rarely happen at once.","explanation":"Reconnect storms: exponential backoff with jitter"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-32","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"Commuters using a team-messaging app lose connectivity in tunnels. On reconnect the WebSocket resumes fine, but messages sent during the outage are simply absent until users force-close the app. What is the standard design fix?","answer":"Give every message a per-conversation sequence ID and have the client report the last ID it saw on reconnect, back-filling the gap from the database. The socket is a delivery optimization; the durable store is the source of truth.","explanation":"Cursor-based catch-up from durable source of truth"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-33","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing dispatch service holds WebSockets to drivers. Drivers passing through dead zones drop off the network, yet the server's connection count climbs well past the number of active drivers and dispatches go to phantom sockets. Why doesn't the server notice, and what fixes it?","answer":"A peer that vanishes without closing leaves a half-open TCP connection that looks alive to the server indefinitely. Add application-level heartbeats — ping/pong with a timeout — and reap any connection that misses them.","explanation":"Heartbeats to detect half-open dead connections"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-34","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"On a live-streaming platform, a creator with 5 million followers goes live. The notification system publishes one broker message per follower channel and the pub/sub cluster melts. How should the fan-out be restructured?","answer":"Use one channel per creator: each connection server subscribes to it once and locally fans the event out to its own connected followers. Broker traffic then scales with the number of servers — a few hundred — instead of the number of followers.","explanation":"Celebrity fan-out via per-server subscriptions"},
  {"id":"hi-realtime-updates-35","topic":"patterns · realtime updates","type":"open","question":"A sports-betting app pushes odds changes from several backend servers. Some users briefly see odds move to 3.5 and then 'back up' to 3.2, while others see the opposite order. Server clocks are NTP-synced. What is the fix?","answer":"Mint a monotonic sequence number per market from a single authority (a database counter or single partition owner) and have clients apply updates in sequence order, discarding stale ones. Synchronized wall clocks still drift by milliseconds and can never provide a total order across servers.","explanation":"Single-authority sequence numbers for ordering"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-01","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A news reader app's team is planning capacity for next year and assumes they need to beef up write throughput because 'articles are the product.' Metrics show each published article is written once but fetched about 40,000 times, and article publishes are a few hundred per day. Where should the scaling investment actually go, and why?","answer":"Into the read path. The workload is extremely read-heavy (tens of thousands of reads per write), so replicas and caching on reads will move the needle; write capacity is nowhere near the bottleneck.","explanation":"Identifying read-heavy workloads via read-to-write ratio"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-02","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A meal-kit startup runs everything on one large Postgres box. As traffic grows, engineers keep rewriting queries and tweaking config, but CPU sits at 100%, the buffer cache is thrashing, and disk IOPS are maxed even though every query is already well-indexed. Why won't further query tuning fix this?","answer":"They've hit the machine's physical limits — a single node has finite CPU cycles, RAM, and disk I/O. Once well-tuned queries saturate the hardware, the only options are bigger hardware or spreading load across machines; no amount of SQL polish creates more IOPS.","explanation":"Physical ceilings of a single database node"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-03","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A project-management SaaS has one slow screen. An engineer proposes a design doc adding Redis, three read replicas, and a routing layer. The database is a modest instance serving 150 QPS, and the slow screen's main query has no index and scans a 30M-row table. What should the team do instead, and why?","answer":"Add the index (and generally optimize within the database) first. The escalation ladder starts with in-database fixes because they solve most problems at this scale for near-zero operational cost; replicas and caches add infrastructure, consistency issues, and ops burden that 150 QPS doesn't justify.","explanation":"Escalation order: optimize in-database before adding infrastructure"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-04","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce marketplace looks up orders by customer email on the support dashboard. The lookup took 20ms at launch, 400ms at 5M orders, and now times out at 50M orders — the slowdown tracks table size almost linearly. What is going wrong?","answer":"The query is doing a full table scan because there's no index on the email column, so cost grows linearly with row count. An index turns the lookup into a tree traversal that stays fast (roughly logarithmic) as the table grows.","explanation":"Missing index causes full table scans that degrade with data growth"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-05","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A team chat app loads a channel with: WHERE channel_id = ? ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 50. The messages table has separate single-column indexes on channel_id and on created_at, yet the query still sorts millions of rows for big channels. What should the team change, and why?","answer":"Create one composite index on (channel_id, created_at). It lets the database jump to the channel's rows already ordered by time and read just 50 entries; two separate indexes can't deliver filtered results pre-sorted, forcing a sort step.","explanation":"Composite index matching the query's filter-plus-sort shape"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-06","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"An IoT platform's ops team, chasing faster dashboard queries, added nine indexes to the sensor-readings table. Dashboards did get quicker, but device data ingestion throughput dropped 40% and write latency spiked. What is going wrong?","answer":"Every insert now has to update all nine index structures in addition to the table, so writes pay the cost of the read optimization. They should keep only the indexes real queries use — indexing is a read-for-write trade, not free.","explanation":"Indexes impose a per-write maintenance cost"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-07","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing company's trip-history endpoint is fast in staging but slow in production. One engineer insists the query 'obviously uses the index' and wants to start provisioning replicas. What is the cheap first step the team should take before touching infrastructure?","answer":"Run EXPLAIN (the query plan) against production-scale data. It shows definitively whether the index is used or the planner is scanning; production data distribution often flips plans, and guessing wastes money on infrastructure that may not address the real cause.","explanation":"Use the query planner (EXPLAIN) to diagnose, not guess"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-08","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A fintech firm's internal reporting database is straining under analyst read load. The team has a hardware budget, no bandwidth for application changes this quarter, and the working set is 200GB on a machine with 64GB of RAM. What is the pragmatic near-term move, and what is its limitation?","answer":"Scale vertically — move to a machine with enough RAM to hold the working set (plus faster NVMe storage), which serves most reads from memory with zero code changes. The limitation: there's a ceiling on how big one machine gets, cost grows steeply, and it remains a single point of failure, so it buys time rather than ending the problem.","explanation":"Vertical scaling: simplest lever, but a ceiling and a single point of failure"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-09","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A photo-sharing app displays a like count under every photo by running COUNT(*) over the likes table at render time. Viral photos with millions of likes now time out, and feeds full of popular photos are slow. What should the team change, and what does it cost them?","answer":"Store a precomputed like_count on the photo row (incremented when likes are written) instead of counting at read time — reads become a single cheap column fetch. The cost is write-path complexity and keeping the counter consistent with the source-of-truth likes table.","explanation":"Denormalization: precomputed aggregates instead of read-time computation"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-10","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A microblogging app renders its home feed with a query that joins posts to users to profiles to media — five tables per page load — and join cost now dominates read latency even though every join key is indexed. The schema is fully normalized and writes are a tiny fraction of traffic. What schema change would cut the read cost, and what new burden does it create?","answer":"Denormalize: copy the fields the feed actually renders (author username, avatar URL, media thumbnail) directly onto the post rows so a feed read is a single-table fetch with no joins. The burden shifts to writes — when a user renames themselves or changes their avatar, every duplicated copy must be updated, so the write path takes on fan-out and consistency work.","explanation":"Denormalization by duplicating hot fields to eliminate read-time joins"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-11","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A B2B analytics product's customer dashboards fire thousands of read queries per second against the primary database, which also handles a modest stream of data-ingest writes. Query tuning is done, the box is already large, and read load keeps growing with the customer count. What architecture change fits here, and how does traffic get divided?","answer":"Add read replicas. Writes continue to the primary, which replicates to followers, and the dashboard read traffic is load-balanced across the replicas — read capacity then scales roughly linearly by adding replicas without touching the write path.","explanation":"Read replicas: route reads to followers, writes to the primary"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-12","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A concert ticketing site serves seat-map reads from read replicas and processes purchases on the primary. Occasionally a user sees a seat as available, clicks it, and gets 'already sold' — support confirms those seats sold a few seconds before the user loaded the map. What is going wrong?","answer":"Replication lag. Replication to the replicas is asynchronous, so the seat map reflects the primary's state from a few seconds ago; reads from replicas are inherently slightly stale. The purchase path correctly rejects on the primary, but the display lies during the lag window.","explanation":"Replication lag produces stale reads"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-13","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"Tired of stale-read bug reports, an engineer at a job-listings site proposes reconfiguring all five read replicas to fully synchronous replication so every read is guaranteed fresh. What would this do to the write path, and why do read-replica setups accept lag instead?","answer":"Every write would have to wait for all five replicas to acknowledge before committing, multiplying write latency, and one slow or dead replica would stall writes entirely — write availability becomes hostage to replica health. That's why replication to read replicas is asynchronous by default, and staleness is handled at the read layer (for example, primary reads for the few paths that need freshness).","explanation":"Asynchronous replication is the default because synchronous replication ties write latency and availability to every replica"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-14","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A dating app user edits their profile bio, hits save, and the confirmation page shows the old bio; refreshing a minute later shows the new one. Writes go to the primary and profile reads go to replicas. What should the team change, and why?","answer":"Pin the author's reads to the primary for a short window after they write (or use session-consistent routing so post-write reads see the primary) — read-your-own-writes consistency. The post-save read raced replication and hit a replica that hadn't applied the update yet; other users can tolerate the lag, but an author seeing their own edit vanish reads as data loss.","explanation":"Read-your-own-writes: pin post-write reads to the primary"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-15","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A podcast platform's metadata database has grown to 12TB and is growing fast. The lead proposes 'just keep adding read replicas as we grow' as the long-term storage strategy. What is wrong with this plan?","answer":"Every replica stores a complete copy of all 12TB, so replicas multiply storage cost and do nothing about dataset growth — they only add read throughput. Scaling data volume requires partitioning the data (sharding), not copying it.","explanation":"Replicas duplicate the full dataset — they don't scale data volume"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-16","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"two-choice","question":"A gaming-stats service has a 300GB database that fits comfortably on one machine. Writes are light, but read traffic from player profile pages is overwhelming the database. Which approach fits better?","optionA":"Shard the database across multiple machines by player ID","optionB":"Add read replicas and distribute the profile reads across them","correct":"B","explanation":"The problem is read throughput, not data size or write volume; sharding would add cross-shard query and rebalancing complexity to solve a problem replicas handle simply."},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-17","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A recipe website added a Redis cache in front of its database. On each request the code checks Redis, and on a miss it queries the database and returns the result to the user. After a week the cache hit ratio is still near zero and DB load hasn't dropped. What is going wrong?","answer":"The miss path never writes the result back into Redis. Cache-aside requires the application to populate the cache after a miss (read DB, set key, return) — without that step nothing ever lands in the cache, so every request misses forever.","explanation":"Cache-aside mechanics: populate the cache on miss"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-18","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A sports app caches live game scores with a 30-minute TTL and gets complaints that scores are ancient during games. An engineer proposes deleting the cache entirely; another proposes a 2-second TTL. What trade-off are they actually negotiating, and what's the sensible framing?","answer":"TTL trades freshness against database load: long TTLs mean stale data, short TTLs mean more misses hitting the DB. The answer isn't cache-or-no-cache — it's picking a TTL that matches the data's tolerable staleness (a few seconds for live scores still absorbs most of the read traffic).","explanation":"TTL as a freshness-versus-database-load dial"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-19","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"An e-learning platform's cache shows a 90% hit ratio, but the database is nearing capacity as traffic grows, and a director argues the cache 'is already doing its job' so they should buy a bigger database. Why might improving the cache to 99% be the stronger move?","answer":"Database load is set by the miss rate: going from 90% to 99% hits cuts misses from 10% to 1% — a 10x reduction in DB read traffic, far more impact than an incremental hardware bump. Chasing the last percentage points of hit ratio (better keys, longer TTLs, warming) is leverage, not polish.","explanation":"Miss rate, not hit rate, determines residual database load"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-20","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"An online bookstore caches rendered product pages in Redis with a generous 24-hour TTL, yet the hit ratio is stuck around 55%, and most misses are for pages that were cached earlier that same day. Redis is running at its configured memory limit. What is actually limiting the hit ratio?","answer":"The cache is undersized: Redis at its memory ceiling evicts entries (typically LRU) long before their TTL expires, so the working set doesn't fit and recently cached pages get pushed out. The fix is capacity or economy — more cache memory, or smaller/fewer cached values — not longer TTLs, which are irrelevant once eviction rather than expiry is removing keys.","explanation":"Undersized caches evict entries before TTL expiry, capping the hit ratio"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-21","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"two-choice","question":"What is the primary reason a Redis cache serves a lookup orders of magnitude faster than a well-indexed relational database serving the same data?","optionA":"It answers from RAM with a simple key lookup, skipping disk I/O and SQL query processing entirely","optionB":"It uses a more efficient network protocol than databases use","correct":"A","explanation":"Memory access is orders of magnitude faster than disk, and there's no parsing, planning, or index traversal on the hot path."},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-22","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A meditation app hosts everything in a Virginia data center. Server-side p50 latency is 40ms, yet users in Sydney report images and audio taking 2+ seconds to load. The team has profiled the backend and found nothing to fix. What is going wrong, and what should they add?","answer":"The bottleneck is geography, not the servers — every asset pays multiple ~200ms round trips across the Pacific. Put the static assets on a CDN so they're cached at edge locations near Sydney; no backend optimization can beat the speed of light to Virginia.","explanation":"CDN/edge caching to beat geographic round-trip latency"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-23","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A video-streaming service, proud of its CDN win on thumbnails, also configures the CDN to cache the /api/continue-watching response. Soon users report seeing strangers' half-watched shows on their home screen. What is going wrong?","answer":"They edge-cached a personalized response: the CDN stored one user's continue-watching list and served it to everyone hitting that URL from the same edge. CDN caching fits content shared across users; per-user API responses must bypass the edge cache (or be keyed per user, which destroys the benefit anyway).","explanation":"Edge caching is for shared content, not personalized responses"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-24","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A social network runs a 20-node Redis cluster. When a celebrity posts, the app slows to a crawl; monitoring shows one Redis node at 100% CPU and network saturation while the other 19 sit nearly idle. What is going wrong?","answer":"A hot key. The celebrity post's cache key hashes to exactly one node, so millions of concurrent reads for that single key all land on that node — cluster capacity is irrelevant because one key's traffic can't spread across nodes on its own.","explanation":"Hot key: one popular key saturates the single node that owns it"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-25","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A live-video platform knows big streams will keep melting whichever cache node holds the popular stream's metadata key. The data is small and identical for every viewer. Name a concrete mitigation and how it spreads the load.","answer":"Write the value under N suffixed copies (stream:123#1 through #N) that hash to different nodes and have readers pick a suffix at random — the hot traffic spreads across N nodes. Alternatively (or additionally), cache the value in-process on each app server for a few seconds so most reads never reach Redis at all.","explanation":"Hot key mitigation: suffixed key copies and/or in-process caches"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-26","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A marketing-analytics SaaS caches an expensive account-overview aggregate with a 10-minute TTL. Like clockwork, every 10 minutes the database CPU spikes to 100% for about a minute and some requests time out, then everything calms down. What is going wrong?","answer":"A cache stampede (thundering herd): when the popular entry expires, every concurrent request misses simultaneously and each one runs the expensive query to rebuild the same value, briefly multiplying load on the DB by the full concurrency level.","explanation":"Cache stampede: synchronized misses on expiry of a popular entry"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-27","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A flight-search site has diagnosed that when a popular route's cached results expire, hundreds of workers simultaneously rerun the same expensive fare computation. What should the team change so an expiry no longer multiplies load, and how does it behave for users during the rebuild?","answer":"Make the rebuild single-flight: the first request to miss takes a lock and recomputes while everyone else either waits briefly or is served the previous stale value (stale-while-revalidate). One computation replaces hundreds, and users see at worst slightly outdated fares for a moment; jittering TTLs also prevents synchronized expiries.","explanation":"Stampede mitigation: single-flight rebuild with stale serving"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-28","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A flash-sale sneaker store caches per-item stock counts with a 5-minute TTL using cache-aside. During drops, buyers keep seeing items as in stock minutes after they sell out, causing failed checkouts and angry customers. What should the team change, and why?","answer":"Stop relying on TTL expiry for freshness: when stock changes, synchronously update the cached value (write-through) or delete the key so the next read repopulates from the DB (invalidate-on-write). TTL-based expiry guarantees a staleness window, which is unacceptable for data whose changes must be visible immediately.","explanation":"Write-through / invalidate-on-write for immediately visible updates"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-29","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A brokerage app shows users their buying power before placing trades. To relieve the primary database, an engineer proposes serving buying-power reads from read replicas with a Redis cache in front. Why should this proposal be rejected for this particular read?","answer":"Buying power gates real financial actions, so it needs strongly consistent reads from the primary — a stale cached or replica value could let a user place trades they can't cover. Read-scaling patterns all trade freshness for throughput, which disqualifies them for correctness-critical reads; save them for the app's tolerant reads instead.","explanation":"Strong-consistency reads must skip replicas and caches"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-30","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"open","question":"A pre-launch B2B compliance tool with 50 pilot users gets an architecture proposal: Redis cluster, three read replicas, a CDN, and sharding 'so we're ready to scale.' The current database serves under 5 QPS. What should the team do, and why?","answer":"Reject it and run a single well-indexed database. At 5 QPS none of that machinery is load-bearing — it adds cache-invalidation bugs, replication lag, and ops burden with zero benefit, and each layer can be added later when measurements demand it.","explanation":"Don't deploy read-scaling machinery at trivial scale"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-reads-31","topic":"patterns · scaling reads","type":"two-choice","question":"In a system design interview, proposing a cache in front of every read path by default is a safe way to show you know about scaling reads.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Reflexively adding caches signals pattern-matching, not judgment. Strong candidates first establish the workload numbers (read:write ratio, QPS, latency needs), start with in-database optimization, and justify each added layer while acknowledging its costs like invalidation and staleness."},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-01","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"TaskFlow, a project-management SaaS, sees its Postgres primary maxed out during business hours. The team adds three read replicas, but timeouts on task creation and updates don't improve at all. What is going wrong?","answer":"Replicas only offload reads — every write still funnels through the single primary. Fixing write capacity requires write-side techniques like sharding, queues, or batching, not more replicas.","explanation":"Read replicas and caches scale reads, not writes — writes still bottleneck on the primary"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-02","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery app's order-event writes time out every dinner rush. The team doubles its stateless API servers, then doubles them again, but write latency doesn't budge. Why isn't this working?","answer":"The bottleneck is the single database, which has hard physical limits on disk I/O, CPU, and network bandwidth. Adding app servers just aims more concurrent writes at the same saturated node; the database tier itself must be scaled.","explanation":"A single database has hard limits (disk I/O, CPU, network bandwidth); scaling the stateless app tier cannot fix it"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-03","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A fintech startup's ledger database runs on a mid-size cloud instance at about 80% write capacity, growing 5% per month. One engineer proposes a six-month project to shard it across ten nodes. What should the team do instead, and why?","answer":"Vertically scale first — move to a larger instance with faster NVMe storage and more memory. That buys substantial headroom immediately with zero architectural complexity; sharding is only justified once a genuinely large single node is near its ceiling.","explanation":"Vertical scaling first: exhaust bigger hardware before adding distributed complexity"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-04","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A messaging app stores chat history in MySQL and has hit a write-throughput wall: profiling shows most time going to random-I/O B-tree index updates on every insert. An engineer suggests moving message storage to Cassandra. Why would that raise write throughput, and at what cost?","answer":"Cassandra's LSM-tree engine buffers writes in memory and flushes immutable SSTables sequentially, converting random writes into sequential appends that disks handle far faster. The cost is on the read path — reads may consult multiple SSTables and background compaction consumes resources.","explanation":"Storage engine choice: LSM-tree engines (Cassandra) trade read performance for sequential-write throughput vs B-tree engines (MySQL/Postgres)"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-05","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"An ad-tech company lands a million click events per second into a modest Kafka cluster without trouble, yet the Postgres warehouse downstream chokes above roughly 20k inserts per second on similar hardware. Why can Kafka absorb write volumes the database can't?","answer":"Kafka only appends records sequentially to a log — no in-place updates, no B-tree index maintenance, no per-row transactional bookkeeping. Pure sequential I/O runs near raw disk throughput, so log-based systems ingest orders of magnitude more writes per node than a general-purpose database.","explanation":"Append-only logs (Kafka) reach extreme ingest rates via pure sequential I/O with no index or in-place-update overhead"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-06","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A server-monitoring SaaS inserts one relational-DB row per metric data point: timestamped, append-only, queried in recent time windows, and deleted after 30 days. Inserts are the bottleneck and the mass deletions cause table bloat. What storage change fits this workload, and why?","answer":"Move metrics to a time-series database. TSDBs are purpose-built for append-heavy timestamped ingestion — sequential writes, aggressive compression, and retention handled by dropping whole time chunks instead of expensive row-by-row deletes.","explanation":"Purpose-built time-series databases fit append-heavy timestamped ingestion with cheap retention"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-07","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing platform must ingest roughly 300k driver-location updates per second, but even the largest single database instance it benchmarked peaks far below that. What is the standard remedy, and what does it change about capacity?","answer":"Horizontally shard by a key such as driver_id so each database node receives only a fraction of the writes. Aggregate write capacity then scales roughly linearly with the number of shards instead of being capped by one machine's limits.","explanation":"Horizontal sharding: partition writes by key so write capacity scales with node count"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-08","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce company is sharding its orders table and is torn between order_id (perfectly uniform) and customer_id as the partition key. The most common query by far is 'fetch all orders for a given customer.' Which key is the better choice, and why?","answer":"customer_id — with millions of customers it still spreads write load evenly, and it keeps each customer's orders on one shard so the dominant query never fans out cluster-wide. A good partition key balances even distribution with keeping related data together.","explanation":"Partition key selection: distribute load evenly while co-locating related data for the dominant query"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-09","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A news publisher shards its article-analytics events by calendar date across eight nodes. Monitoring shows one node pinned at 100% while the other seven idle, and the overloaded node rotates at midnight. What is going wrong?","answer":"Date is a monotonic partition key: at any moment every incoming write targets 'today's' shard, so the cluster delivers single-node throughput no matter how many shards exist. Partition on a high-cardinality, evenly distributed key (like a hashed article or user ID) instead.","explanation":"Monotonic/time-based partition keys create hot shards — all current writes hit one node"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-10","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A social network keeps posts in one table: mostly-immutable content columns alongside a like_count column receiving millions of increments per hour, which drags down every write to the table. How should they restructure the data?","answer":"Vertically partition: pull the write-hot counter out into its own store — a separate table, database, or an in-memory store like Redis. The high-churn data stops contending with the cold content, and each store can be tuned and scaled for its own workload.","explanation":"Vertical partitioning: isolate write-hot columns/tables from cold data into separate stores"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-11","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A marketplace shards its database by seller_id and write throughput improves as hoped. But checkout orders that span two sellers can no longer be wrapped in one ACID transaction, and the analytics dashboard's cross-seller joins now have to query every shard and merge results in code. What did the team give up, and what is the lesson?","answer":"Sharding trades away the guarantees a single database provided for free: transactions, joins, and secondary-index queries that cross shard boundaries must now be rebuilt or approximated in the application. Shard only when write volume genuinely demands it, and choose a partition key that keeps the dominant transactional queries on one shard.","explanation":"Sharding trade-offs: cross-shard transactions, joins, and secondary-index queries become application problems once data is split"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-12","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A ticketing platform's database handles normal traffic easily, but the first two minutes of a big on-sale bring a 50x write spike that crashes it. Averaged over an hour, utilization stays under 20%. What should the team add, and why does it fit?","answer":"A write queue (Kafka, SQS) between the API and the database. The queue absorbs the two-minute burst while consumers drain it at the database's sustainable rate — exactly right for spiky traffic whose average load is modest.","explanation":"Write queues buffer short bursts, letting consumers drain at the database's sustainable rate"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-13","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"After overload incidents, an email-marketing platform put Kafka in front of its event database. Nothing crashes anymore, but consumer lag has grown continuously for three days and events now process hours late. What is the actual problem?","answer":"The average arrival rate exceeds downstream processing capacity, so the queue is just an ever-growing backlog. Queues only smooth temporary bursts — sustained overload requires more downstream throughput (sharding, batching) or shedding load.","explanation":"Queues don't fix sustained overload: if average arrival rate exceeds processing rate, backlog grows without bound"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-14","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A restaurant-review app moved review submissions onto a queue-backed pipeline. Users now complain that right after posting, their review seems to vanish — refreshing shows nothing for several seconds before it finally appears. What is the cause, and what is the lesson?","answer":"The API acknowledges when the review is enqueued, not when it's persisted, so there's a visibility gap before the data is readable. That read-your-write lag is the inherent price of async writes — mask it with optimistic UI rendering or keep truly critical writes synchronous.","explanation":"Async queued writes trade immediacy: ack-on-enqueue causes read-your-write visibility lag"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-15","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A stock brokerage's ingestion tier handles both customer order records and high-volume clickstream telemetry. During market-open surges the pipeline saturates and order writes start failing right alongside telemetry writes. What strategy should they implement?","answer":"Load shedding with priorities: under pressure, reject or defer the low-value telemetry so full capacity protects the critical order writes. Not all writes are equally important — deliberately degrading the unimportant ones beats failing uniformly.","explanation":"Load shedding: prioritize critical writes and reject or defer low-value ones under overload"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-16","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A mobile game uploads every single player-movement event — billions per day — into its analytics pipeline, and ingest costs are exploding. The product team only ever looks at aggregate heatmaps and trend charts. What is the fix?","answer":"Sample: persist only one in N movement events (or pre-aggregate on the client). Aggregate analytics remain statistically accurate without every raw event, so deliberately dropping redundant data is a legitimate write-reduction tool.","explanation":"Sampling: deliberately persist only a statistical fraction of high-volume telemetry"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-17","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A warehouse-robotics fleet of 10,000 robots each issues an individual INSERT per sensor reading every second. Profiling shows database load dominated by connection handling, per-transaction commit and fsync overhead rather than actual data volume. What should change?","answer":"Batch the writes: buffer readings and insert hundreds or thousands per multi-row statement or transaction. The fixed per-write costs — network round trips, commit/fsync, index touches — get amortized across the whole batch, often improving throughput by 10x or more.","explanation":"Batching amortizes fixed per-write overhead (round trips, transaction commits, fsync) across many records"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-18","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A web-analytics startup buffers pageview events in application memory and flushes them to the database every 10 seconds. After a server crash, customers notice a gap in their traffic graphs, and a PM asks whether batching was a mistake. What is the honest answer?","answer":"This is batching's inherent trade-off: events buffered but not yet flushed die with the process, and the flush interval defines both the loss window and the added latency. If the data can't be lossy, shrink the interval or stage events in a durable log like Kafka before batching.","explanation":"Batching trade-offs: in-memory buffer loss window on crash plus added flush latency"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-19","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"An electricity utility's five million smart meters each report usage every 10 seconds straight to one central database, which cannot keep up. The business only needs per-neighborhood and per-region totals at minute granularity. What architecture handles this?","answer":"Hierarchical aggregation: pre-aggregate at the edge or neighborhood tier, combine at a regional tier, and write only compact rollups to the central store. Each fan-in level cuts write volume by orders of magnitude, at the cost of extra moving parts and slight staleness.","explanation":"Hierarchical aggregation: multi-tier fan-in pre-aggregation slashes central write volume"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-20","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"In a mock system design interview for an ad-click aggregator handling 500k clicks per second, the candidate immediately starts drawing boxes and services. What step did they skip, and why does it matter?","answer":"Back-of-envelope math comparing the required write rate to single-node capacity — a single relational database sustains on the order of tens of thousands of simple writes per second. 500k/sec is 10-50x beyond one node, and that calculation is what justifies sharding, queues, and aggregation; without it the design is unmotivated.","explanation":"Interview trigger: do back-of-envelope math comparing required write rate to single-node capacity"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-21","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"Designing an internal HR tool that peaks around 100 writes per minute, a candidate proposes a 12-node Cassandra ring fed by Kafka. As the interviewer, what feedback do you give?","answer":"It's over-engineering: 100 writes per minute fits trivially in a single relational database, so distributed write machinery adds cost, complexity, and failure modes for nothing. Knowing when the pattern is NOT needed is itself a judgment signal interviewers grade.","explanation":"When NOT to use: modest write volumes fit one database — distributed write machinery is over-engineering"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-22","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A sneaker marketplace's flash drops keep failing: 50,000 buyers hit purchase on the same limited-edition shoe with 200 units in stock, causing oversells and deadlocks. The team plans to shard the inventory table across 10 nodes. Why won't that help?","answer":"This is a contention problem, not a throughput problem: every write targets the same inventory row, which lives on exactly one shard no matter how many exist. It needs concurrency-control techniques — atomic operations, locking, or serialized processing — which is the 'dealing with contention' pattern, not scaling writes.","explanation":"Scaling writes vs contention: many writers targeting one record is a concurrency problem sharding can't solve"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-23","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A URL shortener spreads links across 4 shards using hash(short_code) mod 4. When adding a fifth shard, the team discovers that the vast majority of existing keys now map to a different shard, forcing a massive data migration. What scheme avoids this?","answer":"Consistent hashing: nodes and keys map onto a hash ring, so adding a node relocates only roughly 1/N of the keys instead of the (N-1)/N that mod-N remaps. Mod-N routing hard-couples every key's placement to the node count, which is exactly what breaks during resharding.","explanation":"Resharding pain: hash-mod-N remaps most keys when adding nodes; consistent hashing moves only ~1/N"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-24","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A note-taking SaaS needs only 4 database servers today but expects heavy growth. How should they design their sharding scheme now so future scale-outs never require rehashing keys?","answer":"Pre-create a large fixed number of logical partitions (say 256) and map many to each physical node. Scaling out then means moving whole partitions to new machines — the key-to-partition mapping never changes, so there's no rehash and no per-key migration logic.","explanation":"Pre-provisioning many fixed logical partitions makes scale-out a partition move, not a rehash"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-25","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"A video platform shards view counters by video_id and has verified the hash distributes keys uniformly. Yet when one video goes viral with 800k view-writes per second, a single shard melts down while the others sit idle. Why didn't uniform sharding save them?","answer":"It's a hot key: all writes for a given key must land on that key's one shard, and this single key alone exceeds a node's write capacity. Key-based sharding balances load across keys — it can do nothing when one key is the entire problem.","explanation":"Hot key problem: one key's write volume exceeds a single shard's capacity despite uniform key hashing"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-26","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"To handle occasional hot counters, a live-streaming service appends a random suffix (1-16) to every viewer-count key, spreading each counter across 16 shards. Write hot spots are gone, but reads have become slower and costlier across the entire service. Why?","answer":"Splitting every key means every read — including the millions of cold counters — must fan out to all 16 sub-keys and sum them. The split-all-keys approach buys write headroom for the rare hot keys at a read-amplification cost paid by everyone.","explanation":"Split-all-keys mitigation: suffix every key across shards, paying read fan-out on every key including cold ones"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-27","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"open","question":"In a polling app, 99.9% of polls get modest vote traffic, but a few go mega-viral each week and overwhelm their shard. The team wants hot-key protection without slowing down reads for ordinary polls. Which splitting variant fits, and what does it require?","answer":"Dynamically split only detected hot keys: monitor per-key write rates, split a key across sub-keys once it crosses a threshold, and record it in a registry so readers know which keys need fan-out. The long tail keeps cheap single-key reads; the price is detection and bookkeeping machinery.","explanation":"Dynamic hot-key splitting: detect hot keys via monitoring and split only those, tracked in a registry"},
  {"id":"hi-scaling-writes-28","topic":"patterns · scaling writes","type":"two-choice","question":"Scaling writes is fundamentally a hardware problem: with a big enough single machine, architectural changes like sharding, queues, and batching are unnecessary at any scale.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"A single machine hits hard ceilings on disk I/O, CPU, and network bandwidth, so beyond that point only architectural techniques (sharding and partitioning, queues with load shedding, batching, and hierarchical aggregation) keep write throughput growing."},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-01","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce company's mobile app hardcodes the hostnames of twelve backend microservices. Every time the platform team splits a service or renames one, the mobile team must ship a new app release and wait for users to update. What architectural component fixes this, and what does it change?","answer":"Add an API gateway as the single public entry point. Clients call one stable address and the gateway routes internally, so the backend topology can change freely without any client ever knowing or needing a release.","explanation":"Single entry point hiding internal service topology from clients"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-02","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A project-management SaaS runs as one monolith at api.acme.com, and thousands of third-party integrations call it directly. The team wants to carve the monolith into microservices over the next year without breaking a single integration. How does an API gateway make this migration possible?","answer":"Put the gateway at api.acme.com and initially route everything to the monolith. As each capability is extracted, you change only the gateway's route table to point that path at the new service — the public API contract never moves, so integrations keep working throughout the migration.","explanation":"Gateway enables incremental monolith decomposition without breaking clients"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-03","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"An IoT platform ingests telemetry from millions of cheap sensors, and a firmware bug makes about 5% of devices send requests with missing headers and truncated JSON bodies. The ingestion services are burning significant CPU parsing and rejecting this garbage. Where should this junk be stopped, and why there?","answer":"At the API gateway's request-validation step: check URL validity, required headers, and body format before routing. Malformed requests get rejected at the edge so backend services never spend cycles on traffic that was doomed anyway.","explanation":"Request validation at the gateway rejects bad requests before backends"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-04","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A meme-sharing app accepts images as base64 fields inside JSON posts. A few users' scripts have started sending 300 MB payloads, and the upload service sometimes gets OOM-killed while buffering a request before its own size check can even run. Where should oversized requests be stopped?","answer":"At the gateway with request size validation middleware: enforce a maximum body size and reject oversized requests at the edge (e.g., with a 413). Backends then never have to buffer or parse a payload that was going to be rejected anyway.","explanation":"Request size validation middleware at the gateway"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-05","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A chat startup has nine microservices, and each one independently implements JWT verification. Two services have subtly broken expiry checks, and a third forgot to validate the signature at all, causing a security incident. What structural change should the team make?","answer":"Move authentication into the API gateway as middleware. Auth is a cross-cutting concern: verifying the JWT once at the edge gives every service consistent, correct enforcement and removes nine divergent implementations.","explanation":"Centralized authentication middleware instead of per-service duplication"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-06","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A gaming company's public API is being hammered by one partner's misconfigured client at 50x its quota, degrading several backend services at once. An engineer proposes adding rate-limit code to each affected service. What is the better placement, and why?","answer":"Enforce rate limiting at the API gateway. It is the single choke point all traffic passes through, so one limiter protects every backend uniformly and abusive clients are cut off before their requests consume any service capacity.","explanation":"Rate limiting enforced at the gateway choke point"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-07","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A fintech company's internal admin API must only be reachable from the corporate VPN's IP range, but the endpoints currently sit on the same public API as customer traffic. Compliance wants network-level restriction without building a separate deployment. How can the gateway satisfy this?","answer":"Use the gateway's IP allowlisting middleware: for admin routes, accept only requests originating from the VPN's IP range and reject everything else at the edge. No separate infrastructure needed.","explanation":"IP allowlisting/blocklisting middleware"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-08","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A ticketing platform manages TLS certificates separately in fifteen microservices, and last month three services broke because their certs expired on different schedules. What gateway capability eliminates this class of outage?","answer":"Terminate SSL/TLS at the API gateway. Certificates then live and renew in one place, encrypted client traffic is decrypted at the edge, and individual services stop carrying certificate-management burden.","explanation":"SSL/TLS termination at the gateway"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-09","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A SaaS analytics dashboard served from app.metrico.io calls its API at api.metrico.io, and browsers are blocking the requests with cross-origin errors. Each backend team has been asked to add the right response headers, and they keep getting them inconsistent. Where should this be solved?","answer":"Handle CORS in the API gateway's middleware. The gateway attaches the correct cross-origin headers (and answers preflight requests) uniformly for every route, so no individual service has to get it right.","explanation":"CORS handling as gateway middleware"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-10","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A marketplace with twenty microservices gets a partner complaint that \"the API was erroring last night.\" Engineers spend hours grepping twenty services' logs, each in a different format, trying to reconstruct which requests failed and where. What gateway middleware would have made this a five-minute lookup?","answer":"Traffic logging and monitoring at the gateway. Since every request passes through it, the gateway emits one uniform log stream and one set of metrics for all API traffic, giving a single place to see request rates, errors, and latencies across every service.","explanation":"Centralized traffic logging and monitoring middleware"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-11","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing platform routes /drivers/* to driver-service and /riders/* to rider-service through its gateway. The team is launching a new payments-service for /payments/* endpoints. What has to change for clients to reach it, and what stays the same?","answer":"Only the gateway's route table changes: add a rule mapping /payments/* to payments-service's address. Clients keep calling the same public host and need no changes at all.","explanation":"Path-prefix routing rules mapping URLs to services"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-12","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A social feed team wants to split reads from writes: GET /posts should hit a read-optimized service while POST /posts hits a separate write service. A junior engineer objects that this is impossible since both share the same URL path. Is it possible, and how?","answer":"Yes. Gateways route on more than the path — they can match on HTTP method, headers, and query parameters. A rule sending GET /posts and POST /posts to different backends is standard gateway configuration.","explanation":"Routing on HTTP method, headers, and query parameters, not just path"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-13","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"In a mock interview, a candidate introduces an API gateway and describes it entirely as \"the layer that does auth, rate limiting, and caching.\" The interviewer asks: \"If I stripped out all of that middleware, why would the gateway still need to exist?\" What answer is the interviewer looking for?","answer":"Routing. The gateway's core job — and the reason it exists at all — is mapping each incoming request to the correct backend service; middleware is a valuable add-on, and candidates commonly over-emphasize it while overlooking routing.","explanation":"Routing is the gateway's fundamental function; middleware is secondary"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-14","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A video platform's internal services all communicate over gRPC for performance, but its public API must serve plain HTTP/JSON to web and mobile clients. The team is debating adding an HTTP endpoint to every service. What is the cleaner approach?","answer":"Let the API gateway do protocol translation: clients send HTTP requests, and the gateway converts them into gRPC calls to the backends. Services stay gRPC-only internally while the public API remains plain HTTP.","explanation":"Protocol translation from client HTTP/JSON to internal gRPC"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-15","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A fitness app's backend returns responses in a compact internal protobuf-derived format with internal field names, but the mobile client's contract specifies clean JSON with public field names. Where should the conversion from internal shape to public shape happen?","answer":"In the gateway's response-transformation step on the way back to the client. The gateway reshapes the backend's internal output into the JSON format the client expects, keeping services free of client-specific formatting concerns.","explanation":"Response transformation into the client-facing contract"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-16","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"An online bookstore's /bestsellers endpoint returns the identical list to every user and gets millions of requests an hour during a promotion, all fanning through to the catalog service. The catalog database is straining. What gateway feature relieves this, and why is this endpoint a good fit?","answer":"Enable response caching at the gateway. This endpoint is ideal: it changes rarely and is not user-specific, so the gateway can serve the cached copy directly and the catalog service sees a tiny fraction of the traffic.","explanation":"Full response caching for hot non-personalized endpoints"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-17","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A telehealth portal enabled aggressive response caching at its gateway to cut latency. Within an hour, patients report seeing other patients' appointment lists on their own dashboard. What went wrong?","answer":"The gateway cached user-specific responses. One patient's /appointments payload was stored and then served to other users hitting the same route. Gateway caching must be restricted to responses that are identical for every caller — personalized data must never be cached this way.","explanation":"Never cache user-specific responses at the gateway"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-18","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A news site caches its headlines endpoint at the gateway with a 10-minute TTL. During breaking news, editors publish corrections that readers don't see for up to 10 minutes, which the newsroom finds unacceptable, but shortening the TTL to seconds would gut the cache's value. What should the team change?","answer":"Switch to event-based invalidation: when an editor publishes, the CMS fires an event that evicts the cached headlines entry immediately. Updates appear instantly while unchanged content stays cached until it actually changes — better on both freshness and hit rate than any TTL compromise.","explanation":"TTL versus event-based cache invalidation"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-19","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A travel-booking site's flight-details response combines rarely-changing data (aircraft, route, amenities, photos) with a live seat-availability count that must always be current. Full response caching is off the table because of the live count. Must the team forgo gateway caching entirely?","answer":"No — use partial caching. Cache the stable fragment (route, aircraft, amenities) and fetch only the volatile seat count live on each request, then compose the response. Most of the payload's cost is saved without serving stale availability.","explanation":"Partial caching of the stable fragment of a response"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-20","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A weather API runs twelve gateway instances, each holding its own in-memory response cache for the popular /forecast endpoints. The backend still sees heavy traffic because every instance must warm its own copy of each entry independently, and overall hit rates are poor. What storage change fixes this?","answer":"Back the gateway cache with a shared distributed store such as Redis instead of per-instance memory. An entry populated by any one instance then serves all twelve, so hit rates jump and the backend is warmed once per entry instead of twelve times; in-memory caching only suits a single instance or tolerably duplicated hot keys.","explanation":"Cache storage choice: per-instance in-memory versus shared distributed store like Redis"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-21","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery app expects 10x traffic during a Super Bowl promotion. An engineer worries that adding more gateway instances will break things because \"requests from the same user might hit different gateways,\" and proposes sticky sessions. What property of a well-built gateway makes this worry unfounded?","answer":"Gateways are stateless — no instance holds per-user session data, so any instance can serve any request identically. That is exactly what makes horizontal scaling trivial: just add instances behind the load balancer. Sticky sessions would only be needed if someone wrongly put state in the gateway.","explanation":"Statelessness makes gateway horizontal scaling safe"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-22","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A photo-sharing app runs six API gateway instances for capacity. During a design review, someone asks: \"The gateway routes traffic to backends, but what decides which of the six gateway instances a client's request lands on in the first place?\" What is the answer?","answer":"A separate load balancer (e.g., an ELB or NGINX tier) sits in front of the gateway fleet and spreads incoming client traffic across the instances. Client-to-gateway distribution is the external load balancer's job, distinct from anything the gateway itself does.","explanation":"An external load balancer distributes traffic across the gateway instances themselves"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-23","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A grocery-delivery platform's order-service runs eight replicas. An architect proposes inserting a dedicated load balancer between the API gateway and the order-service replicas so traffic is spread evenly. Is this layer necessary?","answer":"No. Load balancing across a backend service's replicas is a job the gateway itself handles when forwarding requests. Adding a separate internal LB layer for this duplicates functionality the gateway already provides.","explanation":"The gateway performs internal load balancing across backend replicas"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-24","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"During a design discussion at a logistics SaaS, a teammate says: \"We already run an ALB in front of our services, so we effectively have an API gateway.\" What is wrong with this claim?","answer":"A load balancer distributes identical traffic across identical copies of a service; an API gateway routes by request content (path, method, headers) to entirely different services and runs middleware like auth, rate limiting, and transformation. The ALB gives them none of that — the two components solve different problems and are commonly confused.","explanation":"API gateway vs load balancer are different components"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-25","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"two-choice","question":"True or false: In a system design interview, drawing a single combined box labeled \"API Gateway / Load Balancer\" at your system's entry point, instead of two separate boxes, is an acceptable simplification.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"A","explanation":"A single combined entry-point box is usually sufficient, and drawing out the full entry-path plumbing wastes time; you just need to be able to explain the two components' distinct roles if the interviewer probes."},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-26","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A social fitness app hosts its single gateway deployment in Virginia. Users in Singapore and Berlin complain that every API call feels sluggish even though the backends themselves are fast. What deployment change addresses this, and what new operational challenge does it introduce?","answer":"Deploy gateway instances regionally and use GeoDNS to send each user to the nearest region, cutting the long first-hop latency. The new challenge is keeping routing rules and middleware configuration synchronized across all regional deployments.","explanation":"Global distribution with regional gateways, GeoDNS, and config synchronization"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-27","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A three-person startup runs its entire backend on AWS Lambda and needs auth, throttling, and routing in front of it by next month. They are debating self-hosting Kong on EC2 versus using AWS API Gateway. Which fits better here, and what is the trade they are accepting?","answer":"AWS API Gateway: it integrates natively with Lambda, ships throttling and auth out of the box, and requires no infrastructure for a tiny team to operate. The trade is cost at scale and less control/customization than a self-hosted gateway.","explanation":"Managed gateway trade-off: integration speed versus cost and control"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-28","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"two-choice","question":"A team runs its entire stack on Google Cloud, its internal services speak gRPC natively, and it wants a managed gateway with first-class gRPC support and automatic OpenAPI documentation. Which managed offering is the natural fit?","optionA":"Google Cloud Endpoints","optionB":"AWS API Gateway","correct":"A","explanation":"It integrates tightly with GCP services and supports gRPC natively with automatic OpenAPI documentation; AWS API Gateway is built around the AWS ecosystem and would be a poor fit for an all-GCP, gRPC-first stack."},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-29","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"two-choice","question":"A platform team wants a self-hosted API gateway they fully control, built on the battle-tested NGINX core they already know, with a large plugin ecosystem for extending middleware. Which option matches these requirements?","optionA":"Kong","optionB":"AWS API Gateway","correct":"A","explanation":"It is the open-source, self-hostable gateway built on NGINX with an extensive plugin ecosystem; AWS API Gateway is a managed cloud service you neither host nor extend at that level."},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-30","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"two-choice","question":"A company's public API is GraphQL, it wants API analytics built into the gateway rather than bolted on, and it must run across multiple data centers. Which open-source gateway matches these needs most directly?","optionA":"Tyk","optionB":"Express Gateway","correct":"A","explanation":"It offers native GraphQL support, built-in API analytics, and multi-data-center capabilities; Express Gateway is a lightweight Node.js gateway without that feature set."},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-31","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"two-choice","question":"A small team whose entire backend is Node.js microservices wants a lightweight gateway their JavaScript developers can extend directly in the language they already use every day. Which open-source gateway fits best?","optionA":"Express Gateway","optionB":"Kong","correct":"A","explanation":"It is Node.js/JavaScript based and lightweight, designed specifically for Node microservice stacks; Kong is NGINX-based and heavier, so extending it pulls the team out of its language."},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-32","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A solo developer runs a habit-tracking web app: one monolithic backend, one web client, modest traffic. After reading about microservices best practices, they plan to spend a sprint putting an API gateway in front of it. Is this worthwhile?","answer":"No. A gateway earns its keep when it hides many services behind one entry point; with a single monolith and a single client type there is nothing to route or decouple, so it adds operational complexity and an extra hop for no benefit.","explanation":"Skip the gateway for a simple monolith with one client type"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-33","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"In a mock system design interview for a ride-sharing system, a candidate spends ten minutes enumerating the gateway's middleware stack, plugin choices, and caching knobs, then runs out of time before covering matching and location tracking. How should the candidate have handled the gateway?","answer":"State that an API gateway sits at the entry point handling routing and middleware, then move on. The gateway is a supporting component interviewers rarely probe; the scored substance is in the problem-specific design, and dwelling on the gateway reads as unfocused.","explanation":"Interview heuristic: mention the gateway briefly and move on"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-34","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"An insurance platform's team proposes implementing premium-discount calculation rules directly in their API gateway \"since every quote request passes through it anyway.\" Why is this a design mistake?","answer":"Gateways should stay thin: routing, middleware, and transformation only. Premium calculation is business logic and belongs in a backend service — embedding it in the gateway makes the gateway a hard-to-test, hard-to-version choke point and blurs the architecture's separation of concerns.","explanation":"Keep business logic out of the gateway"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-35","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"An analytics platform on Kubernetes autoscales its services, so pod IPs churn constantly. The gateway's route table points at addresses that were entered by hand, and users see intermittent 502s whenever pods get rescheduled. What integration fixes this?","answer":"Integrate the gateway with service discovery (a service registry). The gateway then resolves each route to the currently healthy backend instances dynamically instead of relying on stale hardcoded addresses.","explanation":"Service discovery integration for dynamic backend locations"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-36","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"In a document-collaboration SaaS, the PDF-export service occasionally deadlocks. When it does, users' export requests spin forever in the browser and gateway connections pile up until other routes degrade too. What gateway middleware limits the blast radius?","answer":"Request timeout handling: the gateway enforces a deadline on each backend call and returns an error to the client when it's exceeded. Hung backends then can't hold connections open indefinitely or drag down unrelated routes.","explanation":"Request timeout enforcement at the gateway"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-37","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A classifieds app popular in regions with slow 3G connections serves large JSON listing payloads, and page loads are painfully slow. Backend teams don't want to add compression code to every service. What is the low-effort fix at the gateway?","answer":"Enable response compression middleware at the gateway (e.g., gzip). Payloads shrink dramatically over the wire for every route at once, with zero changes to backend services.","explanation":"Response compression middleware"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-38","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"open","question":"A payments company must keep its public /v1 API running for existing merchants while newer merchants adopt the incompatible /v2, which is served by a rewritten backend. How does the gateway let both live side by side during the multi-year migration?","answer":"The gateway handles API versioning by routing on the version: /v1/* requests go to the legacy backend and /v2/* to the new one. Both versions share one public entry point, and v1 can be retired later purely by changing gateway routes.","explanation":"API versioning by routing versions to different backends"},
  {"id":"hi-api-gateway-39","topic":"key-technologies · api gateway","type":"two-choice","question":"True or false: In product-style system design interviews, you should include an API gateway as your system's entry point in nearly every design.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"A","explanation":"It is the standard first point of contact in almost all product design interviews; you draw it, note that it handles routing and cross-cutting middleware, and move on to the problem-specific parts."},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-01","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"An IoT platform ingests readings from thousands of device models: some report temperature and humidity, others report vibration, GPS, and battery, with new sensor types added monthly. In Postgres the readings table has become a wall of NULL columns and every new sensor type forces a schema migration. Why does Cassandra's data model fit this workload better?","answer":"Cassandra is a wide-column store: each row only stores the columns it actually has, so heterogeneous devices can write different column sets into one table with no NULL bloat. New sensor fields are just new columns on new rows — no migration of existing data.","explanation":"Wide-column data model allows sparse, variable columns per row"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-02","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"An architect studying Cassandra notices its design reads like a fusion of two famous distributed-systems papers: the ring topology, gossip, and tunable consistency echo one, while the wide-column tables, memtables, and SSTables echo the other. Which two systems is Cassandra descended from, and what did it inherit from each?","answer":"Amazon's Dynamo and Google's Bigtable. From Dynamo it takes the masterless distribution layer — consistent hashing on a ring, replication, gossip, and tunable consistency — and from Bigtable it takes the wide-column data model and the LSM-tree storage engine of memtables and SSTables.","explanation":"Cassandra combines Dynamo's masterless distribution with Bigtable's wide-column storage"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-03","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"One Cassandra cluster hosts two applications: a payments service whose data must survive a full datacenter outage, and an internal debug-logging service where the team would rather save disk than replicate heavily. A new engineer asks whether replication is a single cluster-wide setting or can differ between the two apps. What is the answer?","answer":"Replication is configured per keyspace — Cassandra's top-level container above tables, which also owns user-defined types. Put each app's tables in its own keyspace: the payments keyspace can use NetworkTopologyStrategy with replicas in multiple datacenters while the logging keyspace uses a low replication factor.","explanation":"Keyspaces are the top-level container where replication strategy and factor are configured"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-04","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A social app user edits their status message on their phone and, a second later, on their laptop. The two writes land on different replicas of a Cassandra cluster. After the replicas sync, every node deterministically shows the laptop's version, with no application code involved. What mechanism decided the winner?","answer":"Every column value in Cassandra carries a write timestamp, and conflicts are resolved last-write-wins per column. The laptop's write had the later timestamp, so during convergence all replicas kept it and discarded the phone's value.","explanation":"Per-column write timestamps drive last-write-wins conflict resolution"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-05","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A SaaS invoicing dashboard stores invoices in a table with PRIMARY KEY ((customer_id), invoice_id). A new 'all overdue invoices' admin screen runs a query (with ALLOW FILTERING) that filters only on status, and it takes tens of seconds while pegging every node in the cluster. What is going wrong?","answer":"The query omits the partition key, so the coordinator cannot route it to one replica set — it must fan out and scan every partition on every node. In Cassandra a query pattern that isn't served by a partition key needs its own table keyed for that query.","explanation":"Queries must include the partition key or they scatter across the whole cluster"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-06","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A comments service stores comments with PRIMARY KEY ((post_id), comment_id) and no ordering clause. To show the 20 newest comments, the app fetches the entire partition and sorts in application code, which is slow for popular posts. What should the team change, and why?","answer":"Declare comment_id as a clustering key with CLUSTERING ORDER BY (comment_id DESC). Cassandra then stores rows physically sorted newest-first inside the partition, so the newest 20 comments are a cheap LIMIT 20 read off the top with no app-side sort.","explanation":"Clustering keys pre-sort rows inside a partition to match read order"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-07","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A URL shortener team built their own sharding layer using hash(short_code) % 4 across four database nodes. When they added a fifth node, roughly 80% of keys mapped to a new node and the rebalancing storm caused an outage. How does Cassandra's placement scheme avoid this problem?","answer":"Cassandra uses consistent hashing: keys and nodes hash onto a ring, and a key belongs to the next node clockwise. Adding a node only takes over the ring range from its immediate neighbor, so only that slice of data moves instead of nearly everything remapping as with mod-N.","explanation":"Consistent hashing minimizes data movement compared to mod-N hashing"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-08","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"An ops team runs a Cassandra cluster mixing older 8-core machines with new 32-core machines. They want the big machines to carry proportionally more data, and they've also noticed that with one ring position per machine, some nodes own much larger ring ranges than others by luck of the hash. What Cassandra feature addresses both issues?","answer":"Virtual nodes. Each physical machine owns many small vnode positions scattered around the ring, which averages out range-size luck, and beefier machines can simply be assigned more vnodes so they hold proportionally more data.","explanation":"Virtual nodes (vnodes) smooth load and exploit heterogeneous hardware"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-09","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"two-choice","question":"Because replica placement walks clockwise around a ring of vnodes, two replicas of the same partition can end up on vnodes that belong to the same physical server.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"While walking the ring to pick replicas, Cassandra skips any vnode whose physical machine already holds a replica of that partition, so one machine failure can never take out multiple replicas of the same data."},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-10","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A payments company runs Cassandra with SimpleStrategy and replication factor 3. A single rack loses power, and although most of the cluster is fine, some partitions become completely unavailable because all three of their replicas lived in that rack. What should the team change, and why?","answer":"Switch the keyspace to NetworkTopologyStrategy, the production-recommended strategy. It is rack- and datacenter-aware, placing replicas across failure domains so no single rack or DC outage can hold every copy of a partition; SimpleStrategy just walks the ring with no topology awareness.","explanation":"NetworkTopologyStrategy spreads replicas across racks/datacenters; SimpleStrategy does not"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-11","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A gaming leaderboard writes scores to Cassandra with consistency level ALL on a replication factor of 3. During a routine rolling restart, score submissions start failing cluster-wide even though two of three replicas for each partition are healthy. What is going wrong, and what is the trade-off the team must make?","answer":"ALL requires every replica to acknowledge, so a single down replica fails the write — the team bought maximum consistency at the price of availability. They should lower the consistency level: QUORUM keeps strong read-after-write guarantees when paired with QUORUM reads, or ONE maximizes availability at the cost of consistency.","explanation":"Tunable consistency levels trade availability against consistency (ONE through ALL)"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-12","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A ticketing site on Cassandra (replication factor 3) writes purchases at consistency ONE and reads at ONE. Users report that immediately after buying, refreshing 'My Tickets' sometimes shows nothing, then the ticket appears a few seconds later. What configuration change fixes this, and why does it work?","answer":"Use QUORUM for both writes and reads. With RF=3, QUORUM is 2 nodes (a majority, n/2+1), so any read quorum must share at least one node with the write quorum (2+2 > 3), guaranteeing the read sees the just-written ticket; with ONE/ONE the read can land on a replica that hasn't applied the write yet.","explanation":"QUORUM writes plus QUORUM reads guarantee an overlapping replica (R+W > N)"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-13","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A fintech startup wants to move wallet balances to Cassandra for scale. A transfer must debit one user's row and credit another user's row, and either both changes happen or neither does. Can Cassandra deliver this requirement as stated?","answer":"No. Cassandra has no ACID multi-row transactions — atomicity and isolation apply only within a single row, so a two-row debit/credit can partially fail and leave money lost or duplicated. Transfers like this belong in a transactional database or must be redesigned around single-row operations.","explanation":"No multi-row transactions — atomicity and isolation exist only at the single-row level"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-14","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing team integrating Cassandra starts building a client-side routing library that computes which node 'owns' each key so requests always hit the right server, worried that hitting the wrong node will fail. Is this library necessary?","answer":"No. Every Cassandra node can act as coordinator for any query: it knows the full ring topology via gossip and forwards the request to the correct replicas at the requested consistency level. Clients can send any query to any node.","explanation":"Any node can act as query coordinator; there is no master to locate"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-15","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"An engineer joining a streaming-analytics team audits their Cassandra deployment and is confused to find no ZooKeeper, etcd, or any central coordination service — yet every node somehow knows the full cluster membership, which nodes are alive, and the current schema. How does this information spread?","answer":"Through the gossip protocol: nodes periodically exchange cluster state peer-to-peer with other nodes, so membership, liveness, and schema metadata propagate through the whole cluster without any central registry.","explanation":"Gossip protocol spreads membership, liveness, and schema peer-to-peer with no central registry"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-16","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"two-choice","question":"If a Cassandra node that was partitioned away for an hour rejoins and gossips its outdated view of the cluster, other nodes may regress to that stale state.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Gossiped state carries each node's generation number (bootstrap time) and version number (a logical clock), forming a vector clock. Receivers compare these and simply discard information older than what they already have, so stale gossip cannot roll back cluster state."},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-17","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"During a config cleanup, an SRE proposes deleting the seed-node list from a Cassandra deployment, arguing that since gossip partners are chosen randomly anyway, designated seeds are redundant. What risk is the SRE missing?","answer":"Without seeds, purely random gossip can let groups of nodes form isolated sub-clusters that never learn about each other, especially during bootstrap. Seed nodes are deliberately biased gossip targets — guaranteed hotspots everyone contacts — ensuring cluster state converges across all nodes.","explanation":"Seed nodes are guaranteed gossip hotspots that prevent isolated sub-clusters"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-18","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"During a network blip in a chat platform's Cassandra cluster, node A stops routing requests to node X while node B continues sending to X successfully. A junior engineer files a bug titled 'inconsistent failure state across cluster'. Is this a bug?","answer":"No — it's how Phi Accrual failure detection works. Each node independently judges every other node's availability from its own gossip observations, so two nodes can legitimately disagree about X. A convicted node is only routed around, never marked permanently dead unless an operator removes it.","explanation":"Phi Accrual failure detection is per-node, independent, and never permanently convicts a node"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-19","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"In a smart-meter ingestion system, one Cassandra replica goes down for ten minutes during a kernel patch. Writes keep succeeding, and when the node comes back the SRE prepares to run a manual repair — but the node has already caught up on the writes it missed. What mechanism did this, and would it still work after a multi-day outage?","answer":"Hinted handoff: the coordinator stored the down replica's writes as hints and replayed them automatically when gossip showed the node was back. It would not cover a multi-day outage — hints expire, so extended downtime requires an anti-entropy repair or rebuilding the node.","explanation":"Hinted handoff buffers writes for briefly-down replicas and replays them on recovery; hints expire"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-20","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A vehicle-telemetry platform must absorb roughly a million small appends per second. In load tests, Cassandra sustains far higher write throughput per node than their B-tree-based relational database. What about Cassandra's storage engine explains this?","answer":"Cassandra uses LSM trees: every write is an append to the commit log plus an in-memory memtable insert — pure sequential I/O with no read-modify-write of on-disk pages. B-trees must find and update pages in place, incurring random I/O per write. The trade-off is that reads may consult multiple structures, so LSM buys write speed at some read cost.","explanation":"LSM-tree storage favors write throughput over read speed versus B-trees"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-21","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"An engineer on an ad-clicks pipeline notices that Cassandra acknowledges writes once they're in the memtable, which is RAM. She worries that a node crashing before the memtable is flushed to disk would silently lose acknowledged clicks. Why is she wrong?","answer":"Before touching the memtable, every write is appended to the commit log on disk — a write-ahead log. After a crash, the node replays the commit log to rebuild the unflushed memtable state, so acknowledged writes survive; log entries are only discarded once the memtable is safely flushed to an SSTable.","explanation":"The commit log (write-ahead log) makes acknowledged writes durable before memtable flush"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-22","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A new hire debugging a product-catalog service watches Cassandra's data directory while updating the same row repeatedly. Existing data files are never modified; instead, new files keep appearing. He asks where the 'update in place' happens. What should you tell him?","answer":"Nowhere — SSTables are immutable. Every update is a brand-new entry written to the memtable and later flushed as part of a new SSTable; the row's current state is resolved at read time (and consolidated by compaction) by taking the newest entries across files.","explanation":"SSTables are immutable — updates are new entries in the memtable flushed as new files, never edits in place"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-23","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A gaming-inventory service has accumulated hundreds of SSTables per Cassandra node, yet point lookups by primary key remain fast. An engineer asks how Cassandra avoids opening every one of those files to find a key. What's the answer?","answer":"The read checks the memtable first for the freshest data, then consults a per-SSTable bloom filter that says definitively when a file does NOT contain the key, so almost all SSTables are skipped without disk I/O. The few candidate files are read newest-to-oldest, using their sorted order and byte-offset indexes to jump straight to the key.","explanation":"Read path checks memtable first, then uses bloom filters to skip SSTables, scanning newest to oldest"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-24","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"To cut storage costs, a chat platform bulk-deletes millions of old messages from Cassandra. The next morning, disk usage has gone up, not down. What is going on?","answer":"Deletes in Cassandra don't remove anything immediately — each delete writes a tombstone marker, which is additional data on top of the still-present old entries. Space is only reclaimed later when compaction merges SSTables and drops the tombstoned rows.","explanation":"Deletes are tombstone writes, so deleting data initially consumes more space"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-25","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"To save CPU, an ops team on a logistics platform throttles Cassandra compaction to nearly zero. Over the following weeks, disk usage climbs steadily and read latencies degrade even though write volume is unchanged. Why did disabling compaction cause this?","answer":"Compaction is what merges the ever-growing pile of immutable SSTables into fewer files, discarding superseded row versions and expired tombstones. Without it, reads must consult more and more SSTables per key (rising read amplification) and dead data is never reclaimed, so both latency and disk usage grow.","explanation":"Compaction merges SSTables to bound read amplification and purge tombstones/obsolete versions"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-26","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce team lifts their normalized relational schema — customers, orders, order_items in separate tables — straight into Cassandra. Now every product page and order-history screen requires stitching data from multiple tables in application code, and performance is poor. What core modeling mistake did they make?","answer":"They modeled entities instead of queries. Cassandra data modeling starts from each access pattern and builds a denormalized table that answers it in a single partition read — duplicating data across tables is expected. Normalized schemas assume joins and flexible queries, which Cassandra doesn't provide.","explanation":"Query-first data modeling: one denormalized table per access pattern, not normalized entities"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-27","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A live-streaming platform stores chat with PRIMARY KEY ((stream_channel_id), message_id). A handful of hugely popular channels have accumulated years of messages, their partitions have grown enormous, and reads on those channels are degrading. Ordinary channels are fine. What schema change fixes this, and why?","answer":"Add a time bucket to the partition key — PRIMARY KEY ((stream_channel_id, bucket), message_id) — where the bucket is a fixed window (e.g., 10 days). This caps every partition's size regardless of channel popularity, and since users overwhelmingly read recent messages, most reads still hit a single (current-bucket) partition. This is exactly how Discord solved the same problem.","explanation":"Bucketing adds a bounded dimension to the partition key to cap unbounded partition growth (Discord)"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-28","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A group-messaging app clusters messages within each conversation partition by a created_at timestamp column. Occasionally two messages sent in the same millisecond end up with one silently overwriting the other. What should the team use as the clustering key instead, and why?","answer":"A chronologically sortable unique ID like a Snowflake ID. It embeds time so clustering order still matches send order, but its uniqueness guarantees two same-millisecond messages get distinct keys instead of colliding into one row — a raw timestamp is not a unique identifier.","explanation":"Chronologically sortable unique IDs (Snowflake) avoid clustering-key collisions that timestamps cause"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-29","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A stadium-ticketing product stores seats with PRIMARY KEY ((event_id), seat_id). Big events have 60,000 seats in one partition, and the seat-map UI only ever displays one section at a time anyway. How should the schema change, and what principle does this illustrate?","answer":"Move to a composite partition key of ((event_id, section_id), seat_id), so each partition holds one section's seats. Partitions shrink to a healthy size and every UI request maps to exactly one partition read. The principle: choose partition granularity to mirror how the application actually accesses the data.","explanation":"Partition granularity should mirror the UI/access granularity (Ticketmaster event+section split)"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-30","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"That same ticketing product's venue-overview page shows, for every section, the number of remaining seats and the cheapest price. Computing this means aggregating over tens of thousands of seat rows on every page load, and Cassandra offers no cheap server-side aggregation. What should the team build?","answer":"A separate denormalized summary table (e.g., event_sections with num_tickets and price_floor per section) that the application updates whenever seats are sold or repriced. The overview page then becomes a trivial read of a few pre-aggregated rows — in Cassandra you precompute aggregates at write time rather than aggregating at read time.","explanation":"Precomputed denormalized summary tables replace expensive read-time aggregations"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-31","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"An online pharmacy keys its orders table by customer_id, which serves virtually all traffic. The support team occasionally needs to look up orders by pharmacy_branch, maybe a dozen times a day. Building and dual-writing a whole denormalized orders_by_branch table feels like overkill. What Cassandra feature fits this case?","answer":"A Storage Attached Index (SAI) — a secondary index on the pharmacy_branch column. It enables querying by a non-partition-key column without maintaining a duplicate table, which is the right trade for rare, low-volume access patterns; hot query paths should still get their own purpose-built tables.","explanation":"Storage Attached Indexes (SAI) serve infrequent secondary access patterns without a new table"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-32","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A social app maintains posts_by_user and posts_by_hashtag as two Cassandra tables, with application code writing every post to both. Occasional bugs and partial failures have left the tables subtly out of sync. What Cassandra feature removes this class of bug, and how?","answer":"Materialized views. You define the derivative table declaratively and Cassandra itself keeps it updated as the base table changes, eliminating the application-level dual-write where one leg can be forgotten or fail independently.","explanation":"Materialized views auto-maintain denormalized derivative tables, avoiding manual dual writes"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-33","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A knowledge-base SaaS stores articles in Cassandra and now needs keyword search with relevance ranking across article bodies. An engineer proposes doing it with Cassandra queries alone. Why won't that work, and what is the standard approach?","answer":"Cassandra has no full-text search — no relevance ranking or ad-hoc text matching, and queries are bound to key-based access paths. The standard approach is to pair it with a dedicated search engine like Elasticsearch or Apache Solr (e.g., via plugins such as the Stratio Lucene index) that indexes the text.","explanation":"Full-text search requires integrating an external search engine (Elasticsearch/Solr)"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-34","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A backend developer joins a team using Cassandra and is relieved to see CREATE TABLE, INSERT, and SELECT statements that look just like the SQL she already knows. Assuming full SQL support, she designs a reporting endpoint around a three-table JOIN with a GROUP BY. What is she about to discover?","answer":"CQL borrows SQL's syntax to feel familiar, but not its semantics: there are no joins, and queries must follow each table's primary-key structure rather than filtering arbitrarily. Her report needs a purpose-built precomputed table (or a different engine), not a JOIN.","explanation":"CQL borrows SQL syntax for familiarity but not SQL semantics — no joins or ad-hoc queries"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-35","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"An energy company is picking a database for smart-grid ingestion: millions of sensor appends per second, reads limited to a few well-known query patterns, and the system must keep accepting writes through node and network failures — slightly stale reads are acceptable. Between Cassandra and a single-leader relational database, which fits better and why?","answer":"Cassandra. The workload is exactly its sweet spot: append-heavy writes that its LSM engine absorbs, masterless replication that stays writable through failures, horizontal scaling for volume, and fixed access patterns that suit query-first modeling. A single-leader RDBMS bottlenecks writes on one node and sacrifices availability, buying consistency guarantees this system doesn't need.","explanation":"When to choose Cassandra: extreme write throughput, high availability, known access patterns"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-36","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A BI startup wants to build its analytics product on Cassandra 'for scale'. Analysts will compose arbitrary exploratory queries — filtering on any column, joining customers to events, running GROUP BY aggregations that nobody can predict in advance. What's the fundamental problem with this plan?","answer":"Cassandra requires access patterns to be known upfront and baked into table designs — it has no joins, no ad-hoc aggregations, and each new query shape effectively demands a new table. An exploratory analytics workload is the definitive Cassandra anti-pattern; they need an analytical/relational engine instead.","explanation":"When to avoid Cassandra: ad-hoc queries, joins, aggregations, strong consistency needs"},
  {"id":"hi-cassandra-37","topic":"key-technologies · cassandra","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing platform partitions driver GPS pings by city. The cluster as a whole is underutilized, yet the nodes holding the New York partition are constantly overloaded while nodes serving small towns idle. What is going wrong, and how should the key change?","answer":"Classic hot partition: city is a low-cardinality, heavily skewed partition key, so one giant city's traffic concentrates on a single partition's replicas no matter how many nodes exist. Repartition on a higher-cardinality key — driver_id, or a composite like (city, time bucket) — so load spreads across the ring.","explanation":"Low-cardinality or skewed partition keys create hot partitions that overload single nodes"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-01","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"An HR SaaS stores employee profiles in a DynamoDB table. Three different microservices write to it, and after a few months the team finds items with misspelled attribute names like 'salray', missing fields, and inconsistent date formats — yet DynamoDB accepted every write without complaint. Why did the database allow this, and where does the fix belong?","answer":"DynamoDB is schema-less: beyond the primary key it enforces no attribute structure, so any well-formed item is accepted. Uniformity must be enforced by application-level validation (or a shared write layer), not by the database.","explanation":"Schema-less model pushes validation to the application layer"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-02","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A news app stores each article as one DynamoDB item, embedding the article body plus its entire comment thread as a nested attribute. Popular articles with thousands of comments suddenly start failing on writes with validation errors. What is going wrong?","answer":"The items are blowing past DynamoDB's 400KB per-item limit, which counts all attributes. Comments must be split into their own items (e.g., article_id as partition key, comment id as sort key) instead of being embedded.","explanation":"400KB maximum item size"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-03","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A ticketing platform keys its orders table on the event date (partition key = 'sale_date'). On the day a superstar tour goes on sale, writes throttle badly even though the table has enormous provisioned capacity. What is the design flaw?","answer":"Every order that day hashes to the same partition key, so all traffic lands on one partition — a hot partition — while the rest of the table's capacity sits idle. The partition key must spread load evenly, e.g., key on event_id or order_id rather than the date.","explanation":"Partition key choice and hot partitions"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-04","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A fitness app needs one query pattern above all: 'fetch user X's workouts between March 1 and March 31, newest first.' The current table uses a random workout_id as the only primary key, forcing expensive full-table operations. How should the table's primary key be designed?","answer":"Use a composite primary key: user_id as the partition key and the workout timestamp/date as the sort key. All of a user's workouts then live together ordered by date, so the date-range request becomes a single efficient Query with a sort-key range condition.","explanation":"Sort key enables range queries within a partition"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-05","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A team-chat product uses (channel_id, millisecond_timestamp) as its composite key for messages. Occasionally when two members post at nearly the same instant, one message silently disappears. What is happening and what should the sort key be instead?","answer":"Two messages with the same partition key and identical timestamp share a primary key, so the second write overwrites the first. Use a sortable unique ID — UUIDv7, ULID, or a Snowflake ID — which preserves time ordering while guaranteeing uniqueness.","explanation":"Sortable unique IDs beat raw timestamps as sort keys"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-06","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"two-choice","question":"DynamoDB hashes the partition key to pick which partition an item lives on. Inside a single partition, how are the items sharing that partition key organized so sort-key range queries stay fast?","optionA":"In a B-tree ordered by sort key","optionB":"In a hash index keyed on the sort key","correct":"A","explanation":"Hashing the sort key would scatter adjacent values and make range scans impossible; hashing is used only across partitions for placement."},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-07","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce marketplace's orders table uses buyer_id as partition key and order_date as sort key. The seller-tools team now needs 'all orders for a given seller_id' and is currently running full-table Scans with filters. What should they add, and why does it solve the problem?","answer":"Add a Global Secondary Index with seller_id as its partition key (order_date as sort key). A GSI is essentially a separately partitioned copy of the data keyed differently, turning the seller lookup into a cheap Query instead of a Scan.","explanation":"GSI adds an alternate partition-key access pattern"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-08","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A payments app writes a completed payment to its base table, then immediately queries a GSI (keyed by merchant_id) to render the merchant's confirmation screen. A small percentage of the time the fresh payment is missing from the result. What is going wrong?","answer":"GSIs are replicated asynchronously from the base table and support only eventually consistent reads — strong consistency on a GSI is impossible. The read-after-write confirmation must query the base table (optionally with a strong read) instead of the GSI.","explanation":"GSIs are asynchronously updated and only eventually consistent"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-09","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A social-bookmarking service runs a provisioned-capacity table with a GSI keyed by tag. During a traffic surge the team cranks up the base table's write capacity, yet base-table writes keep throttling, and the metrics point at the index rather than the table. Why can a GSI choke writes to the base table, and would an LSI behave the same way?","answer":"A GSI has its own separately provisioned RCU/WCU, and every base-table write must also propagate to the index — if the GSI's write capacity is exhausted, base-table writes get throttled no matter how much the table itself has. An LSI would not: it has no separate budget and shares the base table's capacity.","explanation":"GSIs have separate provisioned throughput; LSIs share the base table's capacity"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-10","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A project-management SaaS has a year-old production table keyed on (project_id, task_id) and wants to add a Local Secondary Index sorting tasks by due_date. Some large enterprise projects hold tens of gigabytes of tasks. Why is this plan doubly broken?","answer":"LSIs can only be created when the table is created — they cannot be added later — and each partition key value in an LSI-bearing table is capped at 10GB, which the big projects would exceed. They should add a GSI (addable anytime, no 10GB cap) or migrate to a new table.","explanation":"LSI must be defined at table creation and caps 10GB per partition key"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-11","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing app stores trips keyed by (driver_id, trip_id) and needs an alternate view of a driver's trips sorted by fare amount. Regulations require that this view be strongly consistent — a just-completed trip must appear immediately. Should the team use a GSI or an LSI, and why?","answer":"An LSI: it keeps the same partition key (driver_id) with a different sort key (fare) and is updated synchronously with the base table, so it supports strongly consistent reads. A GSI cannot — it is eventually consistent only.","explanation":"Choose LSI over GSI when the index needs strong consistency"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-12","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery startup's 'my orders' page is implemented with a Scan carrying a FilterExpression on customer_id. As the table grows to millions of orders, the page takes many seconds and the read bill soars, even though each customer has only a handful of orders. What should the team change?","answer":"Replace the Scan with a Query on the customer_id key. Scan reads every item in the table (and bills for all of it) before filtering, while Query reads only the items matching the key condition.","explanation":"Query reads only matching items; Scan reads everything"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-13","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"An ed-tech platform stores 300KB course items and fetches only the title and instructor fields using a ProjectionExpression, expecting the read cost to drop to a fraction of an RCU per fetch. The bill doesn't move at all. Why?","answer":"ProjectionExpression only trims what is sent over the network — DynamoDB still reads the full item from storage and charges RCUs based on the entire item's size. To actually cut cost, the large attributes must live in separate items.","explanation":"ProjectionExpression does not reduce RCU charges"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-14","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A local-business review app must choose between (a) one item per business embedding all its reviews, or (b) a businesses table plus a separate reviews table keyed by business_id. The dominant traffic is map views that need only each business's name and address. Which schema fits better, and why?","answer":"Option (b). Since read cost scales with total item size, embedding reviews makes every name-and-address lookup pay to read a huge item; splitting reviews out keeps the hot business items tiny and cheap, and reviews are fetched only when actually needed.","explanation":"Normalize bulky attributes into separate items to keep hot reads small"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-15","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"two-choice","question":"Because DynamoDB supports PartiQL, teams can use it to run relational-style joins and ad-hoc aggregations just like a SQL database.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"PartiQL is only SQL-flavored syntax translated into the same underlying key-based operations; it adds no new query capability, and a PartiQL statement without a key condition still executes as a full scan."},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-16","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A hotel-booking team believes they must configure their DynamoDB table as either 'strongly consistent' or 'eventually consistent' globally. Their booking-confirmation flow needs read-after-write guarantees, but their high-traffic browse pages need the cheapest, fastest reads. What are they misunderstanding?","answer":"Consistency in DynamoDB is a per-request choice, not a table setting: pass ConsistentRead=true on the booking-path reads and leave browse reads on the default eventual consistency. One table serves both needs.","explanation":"Consistency is chosen per request, not per table"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-17","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"After a minor bug, a logistics company's engineers set ConsistentRead=true on every read in their package-tracking service 'just to be safe.' Their read bill doubles and p99 read latency worsens. Why did both happen?","answer":"Strongly consistent reads cost 1 RCU per 4KB versus 0.5 RCU eventually consistent — exactly double — and every one must be served by the partition's leader replica rather than any of the three, adding latency and concentrating load. Strong reads belong only on paths that truly need read-after-write.","explanation":"Strong reads cost double RCU and route to the leader"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-18","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A catalog service stores 9KB product items and budgets its strongly consistent fetches at 1 RCU each, reasoning 'one read, one unit.' Measured consumption comes in at three times the estimate. What billing detail did the engineer miss?","answer":"RCUs are billed per 4KB of item size, rounded up — not per request. A strongly consistent read of a 9KB item consumes ceil(9/4) = 3 RCU (1.5 RCU if eventually consistent), so large items multiply read cost even when fetched one at a time.","explanation":"Read billing rounds item size up to 4KB blocks"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-19","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"On a social network, a user edits their bio, the write returns success, and an immediate page refresh briefly shows the old bio before correcting itself seconds later. Nothing is cached in the app tier. What is the mechanical explanation?","answer":"The default eventually consistent read can be served by any of the partition's three replicas; the write was acknowledged after a quorum, so one follower may not have applied it yet, and the refresh happened to hit that lagging follower. A strongly consistent read (leader-only) would have returned the new bio.","explanation":"Why eventually consistent reads return stale data (replica lag)"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-20","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A fintech wallet must atomically debit one user's balance item and credit another's — possibly in different tables — with no state where only one side applied. An engineer objects that they chose a NoSQL store and atomic multi-item operations are off the table. Is the objection valid?","answer":"No. DynamoDB's TransactWriteItems provides ACID transactions with serializable isolation spanning up to 100 items across multiple tables — the debit and credit either both commit or both fail.","explanation":"ACID transactions across tables up to 100 items"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-21","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"During an AWS availability-zone outage, a SaaS invoicing product's DynamoDB writes keep succeeding with zero intervention from the team. Walk through the replication mechanics that make this possible.","answer":"Each partition is a three-replica group (one leader, two followers) spread across three AZs and coordinated with Multi-Paxos. The leader writes a WAL entry and acknowledges the write once a 2-of-3 quorum has persisted it — so losing one AZ's replica still leaves a quorum, and writes proceed.","explanation":"Write path: WAL replicated to a 2-of-3 quorum across AZs via Multi-Paxos"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-22","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A smart-home IoT platform's device-events table grows 10x in a year and traffic quadruples, yet nobody on the team has ever resharded, rebalanced, or touched partitioning. Why has nothing fallen over?","answer":"DynamoDB splits and redistributes partitions automatically when one approaches its size or throughput limits, coordinated by its partition metadata service. Sharding is fully managed — there is no manual resharding operation to perform.","explanation":"Automatic partition splitting on size or throughput"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-23","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A multiplayer gaming company runs its player-inventory table in us-east-1. Players in Singapore and Frankfurt complain about sluggish inventory operations — every read and write crosses the ocean. What DynamoDB feature addresses this, and what does it do?","answer":"Global Tables: they replicate the table across chosen AWS regions in near real time, so each region's players read and write a local replica at local latency instead of a single distant region.","explanation":"Global Tables for multi-region local latency"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-24","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A healthcare SaaS is going through a compliance audit requiring encryption of data at rest and in transit. An engineer budgets a two-week sprint to 'add encryption to our DynamoDB tables.' What should the tech lead tell them?","answer":"There is nothing to build: DynamoDB encrypts all data at rest by default and every API call already goes over TLS. The remaining work is access control — scoping IAM policies and, if needed, VPC endpoints — not encryption.","explanation":"Encryption at rest and TLS are on by default"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-25","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A B2B analytics SaaS has flat, highly predictable DynamoDB traffic around the clock, yet its bill is painfully high. The table was set up on on-demand billing 'to keep things simple.' What should the team change, and what is the trade-off?","answer":"Switch to provisioned capacity: for steady, predictable workloads, paying for reserved RCUs/WCUs per hour is far cheaper than on-demand's per-request premium. The trade-off is that provisioned capacity can throttle or sit wasted if traffic deviates from the forecast — which their flat load makes unlikely.","explanation":"On-demand vs provisioned capacity billing"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-26","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"An agricultural IoT fleet writes 100-byte sensor readings individually to DynamoDB. The engineer estimated write costs by dividing total bytes written by 1KB per WCU, but the actual bill comes in roughly ten times higher. Where did the estimate go wrong?","answer":"Every write consumes at least 1 WCU — write size is rounded up to the nearest 1KB — so each 100-byte write costs a full WCU, not a tenth. Batching around ten readings into one ~1KB item would bring cost in line with the estimate.","explanation":"Minimum 1 WCU per write regardless of size"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-27","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A live TV voting app increments a single 'global_tally' item for each vote. The table is provisioned for 50,000 WCU, yet during the finale, writes throttle at roughly 1,000 per second. Why doesn't the huge table-level capacity help?","answer":"Table capacity is spread across partitions, but a single partition (and thus a single key) is hard-capped at 1,000 WCU (and 3,000 RCU). One item can never absorb more than that; the counter must be sharded across many keys (e.g., global_tally#1..N) and summed on read.","explanation":"Per-partition throughput caps (3,000 RCU / 1,000 WCU)"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-28","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A video platform proposes writing every single view event — peaking at around ten million writes per second — straight into DynamoDB, arguing 'it scales infinitely, so why not.' Technically it can scale to this. What is the counterargument the team is missing?","answer":"Cost. At 1,000 WCU per partition, ten million writes per second needs roughly 10,000 partitions and works out to about $156,000 per day even on provisioned capacity — per-write pricing makes DynamoDB a poor fit for firehose-style write volumes. Aggregate/batch events before persisting, or land raw events in a cheaper store.","explanation":"Extreme write volume makes DynamoDB's pricing prohibitive"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-29","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A sneaker-drop retailer's product-detail items get read millions of times per second during releases, and single-digit-millisecond DynamoDB reads are still too slow and expensive. They want caching without hand-rolling Redis invalidation logic. What does DynamoDB offer, and what does adoption actually require?","answer":"DAX, DynamoDB's managed in-memory read-through/write-through cache, serves repeated reads in microseconds and keeps itself populated. It is not transparent, though: the application must swap the standard DynamoDB client for the API-compatible DAX client SDK.","explanation":"DAX: read/write-through cache adopted via client swap, for microsecond reads"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-30","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A grocery chain adopted DAX for its price-lookup service. A nightly repricing job writes directly to DynamoDB using the regular SDK, and every morning stores report stale prices for a while. What is the root cause?","answer":"DAX only invalidates its cache for writes that flow through DAX itself; the batch job's direct-to-DynamoDB writes bypass it, leaving cached entries stale until they expire. Route the job's writes through the DAX client (or accept/tune the cache TTL).","explanation":"DAX staleness when writes bypass the cache"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-31","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A betting platform puts DAX in front of its odds table expecting a near-100% cache hit rate. But for regulatory reasons every read sets ConsistentRead=true, and after launch the cache hit rate is essentially zero — every read still lands on DynamoDB at full latency and cost. Why is DAX doing nothing for them?","answer":"DAX never caches strongly consistent reads: a ConsistentRead=true request is passed straight through to DynamoDB and its result is not stored in the cache. DAX only accelerates eventually consistent read traffic, so this workload gains nothing from it.","explanation":"DAX passes strongly consistent reads through uncached"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-32","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A job-listings site keeps postings in DynamoDB and wants full-text search in Elasticsearch. The first proposal is to have the API write to both stores on every change, but the team worries about the two drifting apart when one write fails. What is the cleaner DynamoDB-native pattern?","answer":"Enable DynamoDB Streams on the table and have a consumer (typically a Lambda) apply each insert/update/delete to Elasticsearch. The stream is change-data-capture from the source of truth, so the index converges without fragile dual-write logic.","explanation":"Streams as CDC to sync a search index"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-33","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"An ad-tech data team designs a pipeline: DynamoDB Streams feeding Kinesis Data Firehose directly, which then loads events into S3 and Redshift for analytics. The design review rejects it. What is wrong with the plan?","answer":"Firehose cannot consume DynamoDB Streams directly. They need an intermediary — enable Kinesis Data Streams on the table (or put a Lambda between the DynamoDB stream and Firehose) — and then Firehose can deliver to S3/Redshift.","explanation":"Firehose cannot read DynamoDB Streams directly"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-34","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"A CRM-style SaaS on DynamoDB is up to a dozen GSIs, and each new feature request — 'filter accounts by owner and region', 'join contacts to open deals' — spawns another index and application-side join code. What is this pattern telling the team?","answer":"Constantly piling on secondary indexes to emulate joins and ad-hoc filters is the classic sign the access patterns are relational, not key-value. A relational database like PostgreSQL would serve these query patterns natively and is likely the better fit.","explanation":"Many GSIs/LSIs signal the data model is really relational"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-35","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"open","question":"An insurance enterprise with a strict multi-cloud, no-proprietary-dependencies mandate is evaluating DynamoDB for a new claims system that otherwise fits its access patterns perfectly. What consideration should drive the decision here?","answer":"Vendor lock-in: DynamoDB is AWS-proprietary with no portable equivalent, so it violates the neutrality mandate no matter how well it fits technically. An open-source alternative (e.g., Cassandra for key-value scale, or PostgreSQL) is the appropriate choice under that constraint.","explanation":"Vendor lock-in as a selection criterion"},
  {"id":"hi-dynamodb-36","topic":"key-technologies · dynamodb","type":"two-choice","question":"In a system design interview, which justification for choosing DynamoDB signals the stronger candidate?","optionA":"\"I'm picking NoSQL because SQL databases can't handle this scale.\"","optionB":"\"My access pattern is simple key-based lookups at high write volume, and I want managed multi-AZ durability plus built-in CDC via Streams.\"","correct":"B","explanation":"Blanket 'NoSQL for scale' claims read as inexperience, since well-designed relational databases scale extremely well; strong candidates tie the choice to specific features solving their specific problem."},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-01","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce site implements product search as SQL `WHERE description LIKE '%mug%'` over 40 million rows, and p99 latency is 8 seconds. The team is evaluating Elasticsearch and asks what it would actually do differently under the hood. What data structure makes the difference, and how does it work?","answer":"The inverted index: at index time every document is broken into terms, and the index maps each term to the list of documents containing it. A query becomes a cheap lookup of the term's posting list (plus list intersections) instead of scanning every row's text.","explanation":"Inverted index fundamentals"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-02","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A backend team coming from Postgres is planning its first Elasticsearch deployment for a podcast app and keeps asking where the tables, rows, and schema live. Translating their relational vocabulary, what are the three core Elasticsearch constructs they will work with, and what does each hold?","answer":"An index is the table-like collection you search against, each document in it is a JSON object playing the role of a row, and the mapping is the index's schema declaring which fields exist, their types, and how they are indexed. Searches run against an index and return matching documents.","explanation":"Documents, indices, and mappings as the core data model"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-03","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A CRM SaaS pushes its full 45-field contact object into Elasticsearch, though the search UI only ever queries name and company. The cluster's memory and index size are far larger than the data volume seems to justify. What should the team change?","answer":"Map and index only the fields users actually search — the other 43 fields are paying indexing and memory overhead for nothing. Keep the full record in the primary database and fetch it by ID after the search returns matches.","explanation":"Mapping only searchable fields"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-04","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A ticketing platform mapped its `status` field as `text`. Filtering for status \"on hold\" also returns tickets in other statuses, and a status breakdown aggregation produces buckets for the individual words \"on\" and \"hold\". What is the mapping mistake?","answer":"Exact-value fields like status codes should be `keyword`, which stores the whole value untokenized. `text` runs the value through an analyzer that tokenizes it, which is right for full-text prose but wrong for exact matching and aggregations.","explanation":"text vs keyword field types"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-05","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"two-choice","question":"When Elasticsearch indexes a `text` field, it stores the entire field value as a single entry in the inverted index.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"The analyzer tokenizes the text into individual terms, and each term becomes its own key in the inverted index pointing to the documents that contain it. That is what makes word-level full-text lookup possible."},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-06","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A recipe app's search for \"baking\" misses recipes that only contain the words \"baked\" or \"bakes\", so users think content is missing. The documents are indexed with a bare-bones analyzer. What analysis feature fixes this, and how?","answer":"Stemming: it reduces words to a common root form (bake) at both index and query time, so morphological variants of the same word match each other.","explanation":"Stemming"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-07","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A workplace chat app has a people-search box. Typing \"Jhon Smith\" returns zero results even though John Smith exists, and support tickets about it keep coming in. What capability should the team turn on, and what makes it work?","answer":"Fuzzy matching: the query tolerates a small edit distance (character insertions, deletions, swaps) between the typed term and indexed terms, so common misspellings still match.","explanation":"Fuzzy matching via edit distance"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-08","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"During a flash sale, two backend services both read a product document from Elasticsearch, modify different fields, and write it back with a full PUT. One service's change silently disappears. Without adding locks, how should the writes be made safe?","answer":"Use optimistic concurrency: send the version the writer read (the document's `_version` / sequence metadata) with the update, so a write against a stale version fails with a conflict instead of clobbering. The loser re-reads and retries.","explanation":"Optimistic concurrency via document versions"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-09","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A logistics app stores shipment documents with 30 fields in Elasticsearch. To change just a shipment's `eta`, the client currently GETs the whole document, edits it in memory, and PUTs it back, occasionally clobbering a concurrent carrier-status change. What API removes the round trip?","answer":"POST to the document's `_update` endpoint with only the changed fields: Elasticsearch merges the partial change into the latest version of the document server-side, so the client neither fetches the full document nor overwrites concurrent edits to other fields. Internally the document is still reindexed as a new version.","explanation":"Partial updates via the _update endpoint"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-10","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"An IoT platform stores a status document per device in Elasticsearch and rewrites each one every two seconds across 500,000 devices. Indexing throughput collapses and disk I/O is constantly saturated. Why is Elasticsearch struggling with this workload?","answer":"Elasticsearch never updates documents in place — every update soft-deletes the old document and indexes a new one, and the garbage only disappears at segment merge. Constant per-document churn generates enormous merge overhead, so rapidly mutating state is the wrong workload for it.","explanation":"Cost of frequent document updates"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-11","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A used-car marketplace needs one search where the listing text must match the requested model AND the price must fall under a limit, while listings mentioning \"one owner\" should rank higher without being required. How is this expressed in a single Elasticsearch query?","answer":"With a `bool` query: put the text match and the price `range` condition in `must` clauses, which every result has to satisfy, and the \"one owner\" match in a `should` clause, which boosts the relevance score when it hits without excluding documents that lack it.","explanation":"Bool queries for combining conditions"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-12","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A BI dashboard lets analysts jump to any page of query results. Requesting page 1,100 with `from=11000, size=10` fails outright, and pages in the hundreds are painfully slow. What is going on?","answer":"With from/size, every shard must fetch and sort the full `from + size` top documents for each request, so cost grows with depth, and Elasticsearch caps from+size at 10,000 by default. Deep random-access pagination is fundamentally expensive in this model.","explanation":"from/size deep pagination penalty"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-13","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A marketing platform runs a nightly export that walks through 5 million matching contact documents in sorted order. from/size dies partway through. What pagination approach fits, and what does the client give up?","answer":"Use `search_after`: sort on a deterministic key (with `_id` as tiebreaker) and pass the last document's sort values to fetch the next page, so each page starts where the previous ended at constant cost. The trade-off is client-held state and no jumping to arbitrary pages.","explanation":"search_after pagination"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-14","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A job board paginates search results with search_after while new job posts are being indexed continuously. Users report seeing the same job on two consecutive pages and other jobs never appearing at all. What should the team add?","answer":"Open a Point in Time (PIT) and paginate against it: the PIT pins a consistent snapshot of the index, so live writes can't shift results between pages. Close it when the walk finishes, since keeping the snapshot has overhead.","explanation":"Point-in-Time consistent pagination"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-15","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A food delivery app stores each restaurant's delivery area, which is an irregular polygon drawn by the restaurant owner. The team mapped it as a `geo_point` and can't answer \"which restaurants deliver to this address\". What is the right mapping?","answer":"`geo_point` only represents a single lat/lon point; delivery areas are polygons and need `geo_shape`. With geo_shape the query becomes a containment check of the user's location against each restaurant's polygon.","explanation":"geo_shape for polygon zones"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-16","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"An EV-charging app needs \"show chargers within 5 km of the driver\" over a few million charger documents, each with a fixed lat/lon. How should the field be mapped and queried in Elasticsearch?","answer":"Map the location as `geo_point` and run a `geo_distance` query with the radius and the driver's coordinates. Elasticsearch's spatial index structures (geohash/BKD-based) make the radius lookup efficient.","explanation":"geo_distance radius queries"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-17","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A marketplace wants search results ordered by each member's personalized price — list price times a per-request discount factor that isn't stored in the document. How can Elasticsearch sort on that, and what's the catch?","answer":"Use a script-based sort (a Painless script computes the value per document at query time). The catch is cost: scripts run for every candidate document, so it's slower than sorting on an indexed field — precompute the value into the document if this is a hot path.","explanation":"Script-based sorting"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-18","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A vacation-rental site embeds an array of review objects in each listing document. Searching for listings with a review that has rating >= 4 AND mentions \"clean\" returns a listing whose only 5-star review says nothing of the sort — the word \"clean\" appears in a different 1-star review. Why, and what's the fix?","answer":"Default object mapping flattens arrays, so query conditions can match across different review objects. Map reviews as `nested` and use a `nested` query, which applies all conditions within a single review object.","explanation":"Nested field type and nested queries"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-19","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A news site's search for \"budget referendum\" ranks a short article using the rare word \"referendum\" once above a long article using \"budget\" twenty times. The PM asks if ranking is broken. What is actually determining the order?","answer":"Nothing is broken — without an explicit sort, Elasticsearch orders by `_score`, a TF-IDF/BM25-style relevance score. Rare terms carry high inverse-document-frequency weight, so a match on \"referendum\" contributes far more than repeats of a common word.","explanation":"Relevance scoring with _score"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-20","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A mobile gaming company disabled doc values on the `score` field to save disk space. Leaderboard queries that sort by score and aggregations like average-score-per-region immediately broke or became unusably slow. Why did that field setting matter so much?","answer":"Doc values are a columnar, per-field layout of every document's value in a segment, and they're what sorting and aggregations read. The inverted index only answers term-to-document lookups; without doc values ES has no efficient way to get each document's field value.","explanation":"Doc values for sorting and aggregations"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-21","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A video platform's metadata index has grown to 4 TB, larger than any single node's storage, and indexing plus query load exceed one machine's capacity. What Elasticsearch mechanism addresses this, and what is each unit under the hood?","answer":"Split the index into multiple shards, which Elasticsearch distributes across data nodes — that's how both data and load scale horizontally. Each shard is a self-contained Lucene index.","explanation":"Shards for horizontal partitioning"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-22","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A social bookmarking site expects a 10x read spike during a press launch; write volume stays flat, and the team also worries about losing a data node mid-launch. What single Elasticsearch knob addresses both concerns?","answer":"Increase the replica count. Replicas serve reads, so throughput scales roughly with the number of copies (a shard doing X TPS gives ~X*Y TPS with Y copies), and each replica is a redundant copy that survives a node failure.","explanation":"Replicas for throughput and availability"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-23","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"While sizing a cluster, a fintech team is surprised that vendors recommend small, cheap instances for the dedicated master nodes but big machines for data nodes. Why can master nodes be small?","answer":"Masters only manage cluster state — node membership, index creation/deletion, shard allocation — and one elected active master does this while other master-eligible nodes stand by. They don't store data or serve queries, so they need little hardware.","explanation":"Master node role"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-24","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"An analytics SaaS runs huge aggregation queries. Data nodes suffer GC pauses not from searching their own shards but from merging partial results arriving from every other shard. How can the cluster topology fix this?","answer":"Add dedicated coordinating nodes as the client entry point: they parse and plan the query, scatter it to the relevant shards, and gather/merge the partial results. That offloads the memory-heavy merge work from the data nodes.","explanation":"Dedicated coordinating nodes"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-25","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A security team ships raw firewall logs to Elasticsearch and needs each event enriched before indexing — GeoIP lookup on the source address, timestamp parsing, and noisy fields dropped. They are about to build a standalone preprocessing service. What built-in cluster feature already does this, and where should it run?","answer":"Ingest pipelines: chains of processors that transform and prepare documents on their way into the index, executed on ingest nodes. Dedicating ingest nodes keeps the transformation work off the data nodes so heavy enrichment doesn't degrade search.","explanation":"Ingest nodes and pipelines"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-26","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"An observability platform retains 180 days of logs in Elasticsearch, but 95% of queries touch the last 7 days. Storing everything on NVMe-backed nodes is blowing the budget. How should the cluster be restructured?","answer":"Use tiered data nodes: keep recent, hot indices on fast hot-tier hardware and roll aging indices to warm/cold/frozen nodes on progressively cheaper storage. Query likelihood by age is exactly what the tiering model is for.","explanation":"Hot/warm/cold data tiers"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-27","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A search engineer instrumenting disk activity notices Elasticsearch never modifies an existing index file — bulk indexing only ever buffers documents and writes brand-new files. Is this an implementation quirk or a deliberate design, and why?","answer":"Deliberate: documents are batched and flushed as new immutable Lucene segments, never edited in place. Immutability gives fast sequential writes, lock-free concurrent reads, aggressive caching, better compression, and simpler recovery.","explanation":"Immutable segments and append-only writes"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-28","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"To satisfy a data-deletion request, a fintech app deletes thousands of user documents from Elasticsearch. Queries no longer return them, but disk usage barely moves for days. Compliance asks why. What is happening?","answer":"Deletes are soft: the segment just records the document IDs as deleted and pretends they don't exist at query time, while the bytes stay on disk. Space is physically reclaimed only when segment merges rewrite the data — force a merge if hard removal is required.","explanation":"Tombstone deletes and delayed space reclamation"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-29","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"After a nightly bulk load, a travel-search index accumulates hundreds of small segments and query latency creeps up; hours later the cluster shows heavy background disk I/O and latency recovers on its own. Explain both observations.","answer":"Every query must consult each segment, so a proliferation of small segments slows search. The background I/O is segment merging: Elasticsearch rewrites many small segments into fewer large ones, purging soft-deleted documents along the way, which restores performance.","explanation":"Segment merging"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-30","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"An encyclopedia search for \"the meteorite\" returns almost instantly even though \"the\" appears in essentially every document. An engineer expected the intersection of both terms' posting lists to be dominated by the huge \"the\" list. What is the engine doing?","answer":"The query planner uses term statistics to order execution: it starts from the rarest term (\"meteorite\") so the candidate set is tiny before it checks the common term. Execution order can change query cost by orders of magnitude, which is why the planner, not the query author, picks it.","explanation":"Query planner term-order optimization"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-31","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"On a social app, a user creates a post and immediately searches for it; it doesn't appear for a second or two, and the PM files a bug. Search is backed by Elasticsearch. What should the engineering team tell the PM?","answer":"It's expected behavior: Elasticsearch is near-real-time — documents become searchable only after the index refreshes, and results are eventually consistent and can be stale. For read-your-own-writes flows, serve the user's own content from the primary datastore, not the search index.","explanation":"Near-real-time search and staleness"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-32","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A startup wants to simplify its stack by making Elasticsearch the only datastore for orders and payments — \"it stores JSON and queries fast, why add Postgres?\". What's the blunt answer?","answer":"Don't: Elasticsearch is a search engine first, without the transactional and durability guarantees a system of record needs, and its update model is expensive. Keep an authoritative database and treat Elasticsearch as a derived, rebuildable search index.","explanation":"Not a system of record"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-33","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"An online pharmacy keeps its catalog in Postgres and dual-writes every change to both Postgres and Elasticsearch from application code. Over months, search results increasingly show prices and stock that don't match the database. What should the architecture look like instead?","answer":"Drop the dual-writes and stream changes from the authoritative store into Elasticsearch via Change Data Capture (or a reliable queue). Dual-writes fail independently, and that sync drift is the classic Elasticsearch bug; CDC gives one ordered source of truth for the index.","explanation":"CDC synchronization and drift"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-34","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"An adtech platform indexes clickstream events into Elasticsearch for its dashboard. During big campaigns, event volume spikes 20x, ingestion falls behind, and the cluster starts rejecting writes. Without massively overprovisioning, what should the team add?","answer":"Put a buffer — a queue like Kafka — between producers and Elasticsearch so indexing consumes at a sustainable rate and spikes are absorbed upstream. Elasticsearch is built for read-heavy workloads; write-heavy ingestion needs smoothing in front of it.","explanation":"Write buffering for write-heavy ingestion"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-35","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A 30-person company wants to stand up a three-node Elasticsearch cluster to search its internal wiki of about 20,000 pages that change rarely. Is this justified?","answer":"No — well under ~100k mostly-static documents, plenty of simpler and faster options exist, starting with the search capability of the database already holding the pages. A dedicated cluster adds ops burden for no real gain at this size.","explanation":"Small-dataset threshold"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-36","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing company has normalized rider, driver, and trip tables and wants trip search in Elasticsearch to filter by driver name and rider city. The team is looking for Elasticsearch's join feature. What should they do instead?","answer":"There is no real join — Elasticsearch isn't relational. Denormalize: embed the searchable driver and rider fields into each trip document at index time, accept the duplication, and design for answering a search in one query.","explanation":"Denormalization for search"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-37","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"open","question":"In a system design for a helpdesk SaaS, search over articles is a minor feature and the design already includes Postgres. The candidate reflexively adds an Elasticsearch cluster plus a sync pipeline. What would a stronger answer do?","answer":"Use Postgres's built-in full-text search with a GIN index and skip the extra system entirely — leveraging the existing database shrinks the design's footprint and removes a whole sync problem. Reach for Elasticsearch only when search requirements outgrow that.","explanation":"Postgres full-text as an alternative"},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-38","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"two-choice","question":"In a system design interview, describing Elasticsearch as simply \"a search index\" with no further detail is considered adequate at every seniority level.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"That level of detail passes for mid-level candidates, but senior candidates are expected to explain the underlying mechanics, like how inverted indexes work and how the cluster scales."},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-39","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"two-choice","question":"Beyond Elasticsearch specifically, what is the main payoff of building a storage engine on immutable data files?","optionA":"Data that never changes can be cached, compressed, and read concurrently without locks, at the cost of periodic merges and temporary extra storage","optionB":"Total disk usage stays lower because no piece of data ever has to be written more than once","correct":"A","explanation":"Immutability actually increases storage temporarily (old versions linger until merge), so its wins are caching, concurrency, compression, and recovery, not disk savings."},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-40","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"two-choice","question":"Elasticsearch splits query handling (coordinating nodes) from data storage (data nodes). What general design principle does this illustrate?","optionA":"Separating query execution from storage lets each concern be optimized and scaled independently","optionB":"Routing all queries through one node type guarantees every query sees a consistent snapshot across shards","correct":"A","explanation":"The split is about specialization and independent scaling, not consistency; results remain eventually consistent regardless."},
  {"id":"hi-elasticsearch-41","topic":"key-technologies · elasticsearch","type":"two-choice","question":"Under the hood, Lucene uses skip lists to intersect posting lists and finite state transducers for its term dictionary instead of one general-purpose structure everywhere. What design lesson does this illustrate?","optionA":"A data structure tailored to one specific access pattern can outperform a general-purpose structure by orders of magnitude","optionB":"Standardizing every component on a single general-purpose structure like a B-tree keeps a system simpler and just as fast","correct":"A","explanation":"Elasticsearch's speed comes from stacking purpose-built structures (inverted index, doc values, skip lists, FSTs), each optimized for exactly one job."},
  {"id":"hi-flink-01","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce company's finance team wants revenue-per-category numbers delivered every morning at 8am. An engineer proposes standing up a Flink cluster to compute the figures continuously off the order event stream. What should the team push back on, and why?","answer":"There is no real-time requirement — a nightly batch job over the order data is simpler and cheaper to build and operate. Stream processing is hard and expensive to get right, so it should be reserved for problems that genuinely need low-latency results.","explanation":"Validate the real-time requirement before choosing stream processing"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-02","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A logistics company reads package-scan events from Kafka, attaches a warehouse name from a static lookup, and writes each row to Postgres. An architect suggests migrating this to Flink for its fault-tolerance guarantees. Is Flink warranted here, and why or why not?","answer":"No — every event is processed independently with no accumulated state, so a plain Kafka consumer already handles this well. Flink earns its operational complexity when you need managed state (windows, counts, pattern tracking), which is the part of streaming that is genuinely hard.","explanation":"Stateless pipelines don't need Flink; managed state is what justifies it"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-03","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A gaming studio hand-rolled a Kafka consumer that keeps per-player score aggregates in memory for a live leaderboard. When the service crashes, they restart and replay the topic from the beginning to rebuild the aggregates, which now takes four hours. What is the underlying problem, and how does Flink address it?","answer":"In-memory state with no snapshots means every failure forces a replay of all history. Flink periodically checkpoints operator state to durable storage, so on failure it restores the last checkpoint and rewinds the source only to that point — recovery takes seconds to minutes instead of hours.","explanation":"Crash recovery: snapshots avoid full-topic replays of in-memory state"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-04","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A chat platform's home-grown stream processor tracks per-channel message rates. When traffic doubled they added consumer instances, but after the group rebalanced many channels' counters reset or double-counted, because the instance newly responsible for a channel had none of its history. What does this illustrate?","answer":"Scaling a stateful consumer means each key's state must move to whichever worker now owns that key — a failure-prone dance when hand-rolled. Flink manages keyed state itself and redistributes it correctly when parallelism changes, which is a core reason to use it over DIY consumers.","explanation":"Scaling stateful processing requires redistributing keyed state"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-05","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A fitness wearable app aggregates step counts per hour. Watches buffer data offline and sync when they reconnect, sometimes hours later. Reconnect bursts inflate the current hour's totals while the hours the steps actually happened stay undercounted. What should the team change?","answer":"They are aggregating on processing time (when events arrive) instead of event time (when the steps occurred). Switching the job to event-time semantics using the timestamps carried on the events puts late-synced data into the correct hourly windows.","explanation":"Event time vs processing time"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-06","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"An industrial IoT platform computes 5-minute aggregates over sensor readings that arrive with variable network delay and out of order. An engineer asks: if events can always show up late, how can the job ever safely declare a 5-minute window finished? What Flink mechanism answers this?","answer":"Watermarks. A watermark with timestamp T is the job's assertion that no events earlier than T are still expected, so a window can fire once the watermark passes its end. It turns the fuzzy question of data completeness into an explicit, tunable signal flowing through the stream.","explanation":"Watermarks as the completeness signal that lets windows fire"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-07","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing company's demand heat map uses event-time windows with the watermark configured to tolerate 10 minutes of out-of-order arrival. Ops complains that every zone's numbers appear roughly 10 minutes after each window ends. What is the connection, and what trade-off is involved in fixing it?","answer":"Windows cannot fire until the watermark — which trails event time by the 10-minute bound — passes their end, so the tolerance directly becomes output delay. Tightening the bound cuts latency but causes more genuinely late events to miss their windows: you are trading completeness for freshness.","explanation":"Watermark bound trade-off: latency vs completeness"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-08","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A fintech company bills customers on API-call counts computed in hourly event-time windows. Auditors find undercharges: events that arrived after the watermark had passed were silently discarded. Without hugely delaying results, how should the team handle these stragglers?","answer":"Stop dropping them silently — configure allowed lateness so windows accept stragglers and emit corrected results, and/or route late events to a side output for reconciliation. That keeps results timely while keeping billing complete.","explanation":"Handling late events: allowed lateness and side outputs instead of drops"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-09","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A SaaS observability product shows API error counts for each discrete minute — 12:00 to 12:01, 12:01 to 12:02, and so on — with every event counted in exactly one bucket. Which window type fits, and what defines it?","answer":"Tumbling windows: fixed-size, back-to-back windows that never overlap, so each event lands in exactly one. It is the default choice for per-minute or per-hour style metrics.","explanation":"Tumbling windows for fixed non-overlapping buckets"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-10","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A music streaming service wants a 'hot right now' chart: play counts over the trailing 60 minutes, refreshed every 5 minutes. One window type covers this directly. Which one, and why?","answer":"A sliding window with size 60 minutes and slide 5 minutes. The windows overlap, so each play contributes to multiple windows, producing a rolling last-hour view that updates far more often than once per hour.","explanation":"Sliding windows for rolling views refreshed more often than the window length"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-11","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A travel booking site wants to analyze browsing visits, where a visit ends once a user goes quiet for 30 minutes and visits vary wildly in length. Their fixed-size windows keep chopping single visits into fragments. What should they use instead?","answer":"Session windows, which group a key's events by an inactivity gap rather than a fixed duration. The window closes only after 30 quiet minutes, so each variable-length visit becomes exactly one window.","explanation":"Session windows defined by an inactivity gap"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-12","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A trading analytics platform wants to emit an updated per-symbol summary after every 100 trades of that symbol, however long those trades take to accumulate — liquid symbols would update every few seconds, thinly traded ones maybe once an hour. Time-based windows keep firing either too often or with nothing new. What windowing approach fits?","answer":"A global window per key with a count-based trigger (Flink's count windows are exactly this). A global window never closes on its own — the custom trigger you attach entirely determines when it fires, which is how you window by element count or other non-time conditions.","explanation":"Global/count windows: firing driven by a custom trigger instead of time"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-13","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A news publisher's Flink job counts views per article, but per-article totals come out fragmented and wrong. Investigation shows each parallel subtask holds its own partial count for the same article, because events were spread round-robin across subtasks. What is missing from the job?","answer":"A keyBy on article ID. Keying the stream hashes each event's key so all events for an article reach the same subtask, which owns that key's state — without it no single instance holds the authoritative count.","explanation":"keyBy routes all of a key's events to the one subtask owning its state"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-14","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A social feed's Flink job keeps a 'seen post IDs' set per user to dedupe events and never removes anything. Months later, checkpoints take many minutes and keep growing. What is the pitfall and the fix?","answer":"Unbounded keyed state — every user's set grows forever, and checkpoint size and duration grow with it. Attach a TTL (or explicit timer-based cleanup) so entries expire once they can no longer affect results.","explanation":"State TTL: bounding state growth to keep checkpoints healthy"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-15","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"two-choice","question":"In a Flink cluster, which component decides where a job's parallel tasks run and coordinates the periodic checkpoints?","optionA":"The JobManager — the coordinator that schedules tasks onto workers and triggers and tracks checkpoints","optionB":"The TaskManagers — each worker schedules its own tasks and snapshots its state on its own timer","correct":"A","explanation":"Flink is master/worker: TaskManagers execute tasks and hold state, while the JobManager orchestrates scheduling, checkpoint coordination, and recovery."},
  {"id":"hi-flink-16","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A video platform submits a Flink job with parallelism 16 to a cluster of two TaskManagers with four task slots each. The job sits pending and never fully starts. What is wrong?","answer":"The cluster only exposes 8 task slots, and each parallel subtask pipeline needs a slot — parallelism 16 cannot be scheduled onto 8 slots. Add TaskManagers or slots, or lower the job's parallelism.","explanation":"Task slots bound schedulable parallelism"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-17","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"An ad platform's Flink job reads from a Kafka topic with 8 partitions. Ingestion is the bottleneck, so the team raises the job's parallelism from 8 to 32 — but read throughput does not move, and 24 of the source subtasks sit idle. Why?","answer":"A Kafka partition can be consumed by at most one source subtask, so effective source parallelism is capped at the topic's partition count — the extra 24 subtasks have no partitions to read. To ingest faster they must add partitions to the topic; extra Flink parallelism only helps operators downstream of the source.","explanation":"Source parallelism is capped by the Kafka topic's partition count"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-18","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A telecom analytics job holds several hundred gigabytes of per-device keyed state. With the in-memory heap state backend, TaskManagers keep dying with out-of-memory errors. What should the team switch to, and what does it cost?","answer":"The RocksDB state backend, which keeps state on local disk so it can far exceed RAM. The price is that every state access pays serialization and deserialization, so per-record reads and writes are slower than the heap backend.","explanation":"State backend choice: heap vs RocksDB trade-off"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-19","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"two-choice","question":"How does Flink capture a consistent snapshot of a running job's distributed state without halting processing?","optionA":"It injects checkpoint barriers into the streams; as a barrier passes each operator, that operator snapshots its state, producing a globally consistent cut","optionB":"It briefly pauses all operators cluster-wide, copies all state at once, then resumes processing","correct":"A","explanation":"This Chandy-Lamport-style technique keeps data flowing while aligning every operator's snapshot to the same point in the stream."},
  {"id":"hi-flink-20","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A ticketing platform writes Flink checkpoints to each TaskManager's local disk to keep them fast. A host is lost to hardware failure, and the job cannot restore that machine's share of the state. What did they get wrong?","answer":"Checkpoints exist precisely to survive machine loss, so they must be written to durable storage independent of the workers, such as S3 or HDFS. Checkpoints kept only on a worker's local disk die with that worker.","explanation":"Checkpoints belong in durable shared storage, not worker-local disk"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-21","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A CRM SaaS feeds Flink directly from partner webhooks pushed over HTTP, processed immediately with nothing retained. After a crash the job restores its checkpoint, but results are permanently missing the events from the minutes before the failure. Why, and what does Flink's recovery model require of a source?","answer":"Recovery is restore-then-rewind: Flink resets the source to the positions recorded in the checkpoint and re-reads from there. A push-only source retains nothing to re-read, so events between the checkpoint and the crash are simply gone — sources must be replayable, e.g., land the webhooks in Kafka and consume from there.","explanation":"Recovery rewinds the source, so sources must be replayable"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-22","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A podcast analytics pipeline runs Flink with exactly-once checkpointing and increments play counts in Postgres from the job. After a TaskManager failure and recovery, several shows' counts exceed the true number of plays. How did this happen, and what is the fix?","answer":"Checkpointing makes Flink's internal state exactly-once, but recovery replays a span of input and the job re-emits writes for it — a blindly incrementing sink applies them twice. End-to-end exactly-once requires the sink to cooperate: idempotent writes (e.g., upsert the window's final total keyed by window) or a transactional two-phase-commit sink.","explanation":"End-to-end exactly-once needs idempotent or transactional sinks"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-23","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"To minimize overhead, an e-learning platform sets its Flink job to checkpoint once per hour. The job is cheap in steady state, but a midday failure forces reprocessing of nearly an hour of events while dashboards go stale. What trade-off is being tuned here?","answer":"Checkpoint interval trades continuous overhead against recovery cost: frequent checkpoints tax throughput and IO but bound replay to a short span, while rare checkpoints are cheap in steady state but make each failure expensive. Pick an interval matched to how much reprocessing lag you can tolerate — typically seconds to a few minutes, not hours.","explanation":"Checkpoint interval trade-off: overhead vs recovery time"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-24","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A payments company must deploy a new version of its Flink fraud job, which holds months of accumulated per-card state. Engineers fear a redeploy means either abandoning that state or replaying months of Kafka to rebuild it. What Flink feature handles this, and how does it differ from ordinary checkpoints?","answer":"Take a savepoint — a deliberately triggered, durable snapshot of the job's state — stop the job, and start the new version from that savepoint (which is also how you change a job's parallelism). Checkpoints are automatic, framework-owned snapshots for crash recovery; savepoints serve planned operations like upgrades, migrations, and rescaling.","explanation":"Savepoints: operator-triggered snapshots for planned upgrades and rescaling"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-25","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A product analytics tool's Flink job enriches events and writes them to Elasticsearch. During an ES slowdown the whole pipeline's throughput drops and Kafka consumer lag climbs, yet no data is lost. What Flink behavior explains this?","answer":"Backpressure: when the sink cannot keep up its input buffers fill, and the slowdown propagates upstream operator by operator until the source itself reads more slowly. Flink throttles rather than sheds data, so the real fix is scaling or batching the slow sink.","explanation":"Backpressure propagates upstream instead of dropping data"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-26","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A URL shortener's click-analytics Flink job calls a shared Redis on every event to read, increment, and write per-link counters. Throughput plateaus far below expectations and the Redis round trip dominates processing time. What design change does Flink push you toward?","answer":"Hold the counters in Flink's own keyed state, local to the operator, and only sink periodic results downstream. State co-located with compute replaces a network round trip per event with a local access, and checkpointing keeps that local state fault tolerant — a central lesson of Flink's design.","explanation":"Co-locate state with compute instead of per-event remote store calls"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-27","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A card issuer wants to flag 'card testing': several sub-dollar charges on one card followed by a large charge within ten minutes. Which Flink building blocks implement this detection, and how do they fit together?","answer":"keyBy(cardId) so all of a card's events reach one subtask; keyed state to remember the recent small-charge pattern; and event-time timers in a process function to enforce the ten-minute bound and clear state when it lapses. Keyed state plus timers is the canonical Flink setup for temporal pattern detection.","explanation":"Fraud/pattern detection built from keyBy, keyed state, and timers"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-28","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A food delivery company wants a live ops dashboard of orders per city over the last few minutes. Prototype one scanned raw Kafka events per page load and was hopeless; prototype two hit the OLTP database with GROUP BY and buckled under refresh traffic. What is the standard Flink-based shape for this?","answer":"A Flink job consumes the order stream, keys by city, aggregates in short tumbling windows, and sinks results into a fast store like Redis; the dashboard just reads the precomputed values. The aggregation happens once in the stream, not once per viewer per refresh.","explanation":"Dashboard pattern: windowed aggregates sunk to a fast serving store"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-29","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"An ad-tech startup must show advertisers click counts per ad no more than a minute stale, at roughly 100k clicks per second. One camp wants to insert every click into the analytics database and aggregate with queries on demand; the other wants a streaming job. Which approach fits, and why?","answer":"Streaming aggregation with Flink: window clicks per ad in near real time and store only the aggregates. Inserting and repeatedly re-aggregating 100k rows per second on demand crushes the database and still lags the freshness target — high-volume, low-latency aggregation is exactly the problem shape Flink exists for.","explanation":"When Flink fits in interviews: high-volume low-latency aggregation the DB can't do"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-30","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"two-choice","question":"In Flink's model, a bounded dataset is just a stream that happens to end, so the same dataflow concepts cover both batch and streaming jobs.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"A","explanation":"Flink treats batch as a special case of streaming over finite input, which is why one engine and one programming model serve both instead of batch and streaming being separate systems."},
  {"id":"hi-flink-31","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"two-choice","question":"A gaming company needs cheat alerts within about one second of the triggering event. Choosing between Flink and classic Spark Streaming, which fits, and why?","optionA":"Flink — it processes events one at a time as they arrive, so per-event latency can be well under a second","optionB":"Spark Streaming — it groups events into micro-batches, and each batch amortizes overhead for lower latency","correct":"A","explanation":"Micro-batching forces each event to wait for its batch to be assembled and scheduled, imposing a latency floor that true per-event streaming avoids."},
  {"id":"hi-flink-32","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"open","question":"A five-person startup already runs Java services against Kafka and needs modest per-key stateful aggregation. They are weighing embedding Kafka Streams into an existing service against standing up a Flink cluster. What is the core operational difference between the two, and when does Flink earn its keep?","answer":"Kafka Streams is a library that runs inside your own application processes with no separate cluster to operate, while Flink is a standalone distributed engine with JobManager/TaskManager infrastructure you must deploy and run. Flink earns that cost when you need its heavier machinery — very large state, rich event-time and windowing features, connectors beyond Kafka — which a small team with modest needs may not.","explanation":"Flink vs Kafka Streams: standalone cluster engine vs embedded library"},
  {"id":"hi-flink-33","topic":"key-technologies · flink","type":"two-choice","question":"A Flink program defines a dataflow graph running from sources through operators to sinks; when submitted, the JobManager splits it into parallel subtasks and schedules them into TaskManager slots.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"A","explanation":"You author the graph (e.g., with the DataStream API), and the cluster's coordinator maps its parallel instances onto worker slots for execution; that authored-graph-to-scheduled-tasks flow is how every Flink job runs."},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-01","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing app publishes trip lifecycle events (requested, driver_assigned, started, completed) to a Kafka topic with no message key. The billing service occasionally processes a trip's \"completed\" event before its \"started\" event and computes nonsense fares. What is going wrong, and what is the fix?","answer":"Kafka only guarantees ordering within a single partition, and keyless messages are spread across partitions, so one trip's events interleave out of order. Key every event by trip ID so all of a trip's events hash to the same partition and arrive in order.","explanation":"Per-key ordering is guaranteed only within a single partition"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-02","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A game studio's telemetry topic has 4 partitions and consumer lag keeps growing. The team scales its consumer group from 4 instances to 12, but lag does not improve at all. Why not?","answer":"Each partition is assigned to exactly one consumer in a group, so at most 4 consumers can do work while the other 8 sit idle. Partition count is the ceiling on group parallelism; they must add partitions, not consumers.","explanation":"Consumer-group parallelism is capped by partition count (extra consumers idle)"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-03","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce company's orders topic has 3 partitions. Ahead of Black Friday, ops grows the Kafka cluster from 3 brokers to 9, but topic throughput does not budge. What did they miss?","answer":"A topic's throughput is carried by its partition leaders, and with only 3 partitions at most 3 brokers can lead them — the new brokers sit idle for this topic. Adding brokers is useless without enough partitions to spread across them.","explanation":"Adding brokers only helps if the topic has enough partitions to spread"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-04","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"An ad-analytics pipeline keys click events by ad ID. When one ad goes viral, a single partition's lag explodes and its broker pegs at 100% while the rest of the cluster idles. What failure mode is this, and why does it happen?","answer":"A hot partition: hashing the key routes every click for that ad to one partition, so a skewed key distribution concentrates the entire traffic spike on a single partition and broker.","explanation":"Hot partitions caused by skewed partition keys"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-05","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A social platform keys hashtag-usage events by hashtag. To defuse a trending-hashtag hot partition, an engineer appends a random 0-9 suffix to each key. The per-hashtag counts on the dashboard immediately drop to roughly a tenth of reality. What must the team change downstream?","answer":"The salt split each hashtag across ten sub-keys and partitions, so the aggregator must strip the salt and sum counts across all salted variants. Salting always buys load spreading at the cost of an extra de-salt/re-aggregation step.","explanation":"Random salting of keys and the de-salt/re-aggregate cost downstream"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-06","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"An IoT platform keys machine sensor readings by factory ID, and one giant factory overloads its partition. Ordering only matters per individual machine, never across a whole factory. What key change fixes the skew without losing the ordering they need?","answer":"Switch to a compound key like factoryId:machineId. It spreads the big factory's traffic across many partitions while every reading from a given machine still hashes to one partition, preserving per-machine order.","explanation":"Compound keys spread load while preserving the ordering that actually matters"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-07","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A music-streaming service keys play-log events by user ID purely out of habit, and a few power listeners create partition skew. No consumer depends on the order of these events. What is the simplest fix?","answer":"Drop the key entirely: the default sticky partitioner spreads keyless messages roughly evenly across partitions. This sacrifices per-key ordering, which is exactly the guarantee nobody here needs.","explanation":"Keyless sticky partitioning when ordering is irrelevant"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-08","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A ticketing site runs a flash sale. Producers write purchase-intent events far faster than consumers can drain them, and lag grows until confirmations arrive uselessly late. Beyond adding consumers, what mechanism keeps lag bounded at the source?","answer":"Backpressure: the producer side monitors consumer/partition lag and throttles or pauses publishing once lag crosses a threshold, trading producer throughput for bounded end-to-end latency.","explanation":"Producer backpressure driven by consumer lag"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-09","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A fintech firm produces ledger entries with acks=1. During a broker failure, several entries the producer had received success acknowledgments for are simply missing from the topic. How did acknowledged writes get lost?","answer":"With acks=1 only the partition leader confirms the write; the leader died before its followers replicated those entries, and a follower missing them was promoted. Durable writes need acks=all with replication factor >=3.","explanation":"acks=1 data-loss window on leader failure"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-10","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A delivery-tracking app streams courier GPS pings every two seconds. A lost ping is irrelevant because a fresh one replaces it almost immediately, but the live map must feel real-time. Which producer acks setting fits, and why?","answer":"acks=0, fire-and-forget: it minimizes producer latency and overhead, and the workload explicitly tolerates occasional loss. Paying the round-trip cost of acks=all buys durability this data does not need.","explanation":"Choosing acks=0 for loss-tolerant, latency-sensitive workloads"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-11","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"To cut disk costs, a logistics startup runs its shipment-events topic with replication factor 1. A broker's disk dies and weeks of not-yet-consumed shipment events are permanently gone. What principle did they violate?","answer":"With RF=1 there are no follower replicas, so any single broker failure destroys that partition's data. Production topics need replication factor >=3 (a leader plus two followers), accepting 3x storage for durability.","explanation":"Replication factor >=3 for durability; RF=1 loses data on broker failure"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-12","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"In a chat backend, the Kafka broker hosting several partition leaders crashes. Consumers see a brief pause, then delivery resumes with no data loss and no operator action. What mechanism made that happen?","answer":"The cluster controller detected the dead broker and promoted in-sync replica (ISR) followers on surviving brokers to be the new partition leaders. Because ISR followers had fully replicated the leader's log, failover was automatic and lossless.","explanation":"Cluster controller promotes in-sync replica (ISR) followers on leader failure"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-13","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A notifications service consumes events and sends push notifications, committing offsets only after each batch finishes. After a consumer crash, some users receive the same notification twice. Is this a Kafka bug, and what should the team do?","answer":"Not a bug — it is Kafka's at-least-once guarantee: the crash landed after sending but before the offset commit, so those messages were redelivered. The fix is idempotent processing, such as deduplicating on a notification ID.","explanation":"At-least-once redelivery and the need for idempotent consumers"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-14","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A SaaS analytics consumer commits offsets the moment it fetches a batch, then processes it. After a crash during a deploy, the nightly numbers show a permanent gap: those events were never processed and never redelivered. Why?","answer":"Committing before processing marks unprocessed messages as done, so a crash mid-batch skips them forever — effectively at-most-once delivery. Commit after processing completes, accepting possible reprocessing instead of silent loss.","explanation":"Committing offsets before processing silently loses messages (at-most-once)"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-15","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A payments team enables aggressive producer retries to survive a flaky network, and duplicate payment events start appearing in the topic itself — consumers never crashed. What setting is missing?","answer":"The idempotent producer. Without it, a retry after a lost acknowledgment writes the same message twice; with idempotence on, the broker recognizes and discards the duplicate, making retries safe.","explanation":"Idempotent producer prevents duplicates from producer retries"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-16","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery order consumer hits a malformed \"poison\" message that throws on every attempt. The consumer retries it in place and the whole partition backs up behind it. Kafka offers no built-in remedy — what should the team build?","answer":"A retry pipeline: publish failing messages to a dedicated retry topic consumed with exponential backoff, and after N failed attempts route them to a dead-letter queue for offline inspection. The main partition keeps flowing past the poison message.","explanation":"Retry topic + dead-letter queue pattern for failed consumer processing"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-17","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"two-choice","question":"A team is building a background job queue for a web crawler. They want per-message retries with backoff and a dead-letter queue, with zero custom infrastructure to maintain. Which technology fits better?","optionA":"Kafka","optionB":"AWS SQS","correct":"B","explanation":"It ships consumer retries and dead-letter queues out of the box, whereas Kafka makes you build retry topics and DLQ plumbing yourself."},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-18","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A video platform writes 50 MB raw video segments directly into Kafka messages. Brokers hit memory pressure and latency degrades for every topic on the cluster. What is the right architecture?","answer":"Kafka messages should stay around 1 MB or smaller. Store the video segments in object storage like S3 and publish only a small pointer/metadata message to Kafka that consumers use to fetch the blob.","explanation":"Large-message anti-pattern: keep messages ~1MB, put blobs in object storage with a pointer"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-19","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"In a capacity-planning review, an engineer proposes a single Kafka broker to ingest a news site's clickstream, projected at 4 million events per second. What does back-of-envelope math say?","answer":"Rough rules of thumb are about 1 million messages/second and around 1 TB of practical storage per broker, so one broker is roughly 4x undersized before replication is even counted. This needs a multi-broker cluster with enough partitions to spread the load.","explanation":"Rules of thumb: ~1M messages/sec and ~1TB storage per broker"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-20","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A growth team plans to backfill a new metric by replaying the last 60 days of clickstream events from Kafka, but the consumer finds only about one week of history. What happened?","answer":"Kafka's default retention.ms keeps data for 7 days, after which segments are deleted. Deep replays require raising retention ahead of time or replaying from an external archive — the data is already gone.","explanation":"Default 7-day retention (retention.ms) limits replay depth"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-21","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A healthcare SaaS must retain audit events for seven years. An engineer proposes simply setting the audit topic's retention to seven years. What is the better pattern, and why?","answer":"Keep Kafka retention short and continuously drain the topic into cheap long-term storage such as a data warehouse or object store. Replicated broker disks make Kafka an expensive, awkward archive — it is a transport and buffer, not a system of record.","explanation":"Archive to warehouse/object storage instead of multi-year Kafka retention"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-22","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A feature-flag service streams every flag change through Kafka. New consumers only need the current value of each flag, but time-based retention either deletes flags that never change or hoards endless stale updates. What retention mode fits?","answer":"Log compaction: Kafka keeps the latest message per key indefinitely and discards superseded values, turning the topic into a compact changelog that new consumers replay to rebuild current state.","explanation":"Log compaction keeps the latest value per key (changelog topics)"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-23","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A live-streaming platform must deliver every chat comment to three separate services — moderation, on-screen display, and analytics — each needing its own complete copy of the stream at its own pace. How should consumption be organized?","answer":"Put each service in its own consumer group. Every group independently receives all messages and tracks its own offsets — Kafka acting as pub/sub fan-out rather than a work queue.","explanation":"Multiple consumer groups each get a full copy (pub/sub fan-out)"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-24","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A photo-sharing app generates thumbnails synchronously inside the upload HTTP request, and uploads time out whenever traffic spikes. How specifically does inserting Kafka fix this?","answer":"It makes the work asynchronous: the upload service writes a job message and returns immediately, while thumbnail workers consume at their own rate. The topic absorbs bursts and lets producers and consumers scale and fail independently.","explanation":"Kafka as an async buffer decoupling producers from consumers"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-25","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce team migrating off webhooks worries that Kafka will flood their slow search indexer the way peak-hour webhook bursts used to crash it. Is the worry valid?","answer":"No — Kafka consumers pull. The indexer fetches batches at whatever rate it can handle while unread messages accumulate safely in the durable log, so producers cannot overwhelm it the way push delivery did.","explanation":"Pull-based consumption means slow consumers cannot be overwhelmed"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-26","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A SaaS company's 40-instance consumer group deploys new code one instance at a time, and every single restart stalls the entire group's consumption for several seconds. Nothing is crashing. What causes the stalls?","answer":"Each consumer joining or leaving triggers a group rebalance that reassigns partitions and briefly pauses everyone — a stop-the-world event. A one-by-one rolling restart fires 40 rebalances back to back; tune rebalance/session settings or restart in fewer waves.","explanation":"Consumer-group rebalancing pauses consumption (stop-the-world)"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-27","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A telemetry producer sends every metric as its own individual request to Kafka. Per-message network and protocol overhead dominates, and throughput plateaus far below what the hardware can do. What is the standard first fix?","answer":"Batch the sends: accumulating many messages per request amortizes the fixed per-request costs across hundreds of messages. Producer batching is the basic Kafka throughput lever before anything fancier.","explanation":"Producer batching amortizes per-message network/protocol overhead"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-28","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A gaming company ships verbose JSON event logs from regional data centers to a central Kafka cluster over an expensive, bandwidth-capped WAN link. The link is saturated while broker and producer CPUs sit mostly idle. What producer setting helps, and what does it cost?","answer":"Enable producer compression (GZIP, Snappy, or LZ4): repetitive JSON compresses dramatically and relieves the WAN. The price is CPU spent compressing and decompressing — a clear win when bandwidth, not CPU, is the bottleneck.","explanation":"Compression (GZIP/Snappy/LZ4) trades CPU for bandwidth and storage"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-29","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"two-choice","question":"A Kafka pipeline running with default settings delivers each message to a consumer exactly once.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"The default guarantee is at-least-once: crashes between processing and offset commit cause redelivery. Exactly-once requires transactional producers/consumers plus idempotent processing, none of which is on by default."},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-30","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A logistics company publishes truck-fleet telemetry and warehouse-robot events into one shared Kafka topic. The warehouse-automation service reads and discards the majority of what it fetches, wasting compute and cluttering its code with filters. What structural feature of Kafka addresses this?","answer":"Separate topics per event category. Topics are Kafka's logical segregation mechanism: each consumer subscribes only to the streams it actually cares about instead of filtering out another team's data.","explanation":"Separate topics provide logical segregation so consumers only read relevant data"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-31","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"An ETL consumer feeding a B2B dashboard is restarted every night by its orchestrator. An engineer worries each restart will reprocess the entire topic from the beginning. What Kafka mechanism makes that fear unfounded?","answer":"Committed offsets: the consumer periodically commits its position back to Kafka, and on restart it reads its last committed offset and resumes exactly from there rather than from the start of the log.","explanation":"Committed offsets let a restarted consumer resume from its last position"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-32","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"two-choice","question":"Kafka sustains extremely high write throughput even on ordinary spinning disks. What is the primary reason?","optionA":"It keeps all recent messages in RAM and flushes to disk lazily","optionB":"It only appends to the end of each partition's log, so disk I/O is sequential and avoids seeks","correct":"B","explanation":"Seek time is the bottleneck append-only design eliminates; Kafka's speed does not depend on holding data in RAM."},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-33","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A chat platform keys messages by conversation ID and later doubles the topic's partition count for capacity. Immediately afterwards, new messages in existing conversations appear on different partitions than their history, breaking per-conversation ordering. Why?","answer":"Partition choice is hash(key) % num_partitions, so changing the partition count remaps many keys to new partitions, splitting a conversation's stream across two partitions. This is why partition counts should be provisioned for future scale up front.","explanation":"partition = hash(key) % num_partitions, so changing partition count remaps keys"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-34","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A two-engineer fintech startup needs Kafka's durability and ordering for payment events but has nobody to provision, tune, and page for broker clusters. What should they choose, and why?","answer":"A managed service like Confluent Cloud or AWS MSK, which handles provisioning, scaling, and broker operations. Self-hosting Kafka is a real operational burden a two-person team should not absorb — and saying so is the expected interview answer.","explanation":"Managed Kafka (Confluent Cloud / AWS MSK) offloads cluster operations"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-35","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"To \"decouple services,\" a team routes price-quote lookups through Kafka: the web frontend produces a request event, then blocks waiting for the pricing service's response event before rendering the page. Latency is terrible and the code is contorted. What is the actual mistake?","answer":"Using Kafka for a synchronous request-response interaction. Kafka is built for asynchronous, decoupled flows; a blocking lookup in the request path should be a direct REST or gRPC call.","explanation":"Kafka is wrong for synchronous request-response paths"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-36","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A metrics-dashboard consumer polls Kafka in a tight loop, receives only a handful of messages per fetch, and burns CPU on request overhead — even though a few extra seconds of delay would be perfectly acceptable. Which consumer settings address this?","answer":"Raise fetch.min.bytes and fetch.max.wait.ms so the broker holds each fetch until a meaningful batch accumulates. That trades a little latency for far fewer, larger fetches and better consumer throughput.","explanation":"Consumer fetch tuning (fetch.min.bytes / fetch.max.wait.ms) trades latency for throughput"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-37","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A platform team wants every Kafka event to carry a trace ID and schema version for its observability tooling, but the payload schemas are owned by other teams and must not change. Where in the Kafka message does this metadata belong?","answer":"In message headers — key-value metadata pairs that travel with the message without touching the payload. A Kafka message has four optional fields (key, value, timestamp, headers), and headers exist precisely for cross-cutting metadata like trace IDs.","explanation":"Message anatomy: four optional fields (key, value, timestamp, headers); headers carry cross-cutting metadata"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-38","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"In a system design interview, a candidate spends most of their fault-tolerance time on a contingency plan for the entire Kafka cluster disappearing, and barely touches consumer crash handling. Why is this emphasis backwards?","answer":"Kafka is 'always available, sometimes consistent': with partitions replicated across brokers, total cluster loss is not a realistic failure mode worth designing around. The realistic failures are consumer crashes, so offset management, redelivery, and rebalancing deserve the attention.","explanation":"'Always available, sometimes consistent': design for consumer failures, not total Kafka loss"},
  {"id":"hi-kafka-39","topic":"key-technologies · kafka","type":"open","question":"A recommendations team ships a bug that mis-scores three hours of user events before anyone notices. Every one of those events was already consumed during the window. Can the team reprocess them, and how?","answer":"Yes — consuming from Kafka never deletes messages; the log is retained regardless of reads, unlike a traditional queue. They fix the consumer and reset the group's offsets to just before the bad deploy, replaying the retained history (it is well within the 7-day default retention).","explanation":"Consuming does not delete messages; resetting offsets replays retained history"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-01","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A photo-sharing startup stores every uploaded image as a binary column in its Postgres database. Storage costs are climbing fast, backups take hours, and image reads are competing with transactional queries. What should the team change, and why?","answer":"Move the image bytes to object storage (e.g., S3) and keep only metadata plus a URL pointer in Postgres. Object storage is an order of magnitude cheaper per GB and built for large binaries, while the database stays small, fast, and queryable.","explanation":"Store blob bytes in object storage with metadata pointers in the core database"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-02","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A video platform routes every user upload through its application servers, which then write the file to S3. During peak hours the app servers max out on bandwidth and CPU even though the rest of the system is idle. How should the upload path be redesigned?","answer":"Have the server generate a time-limited presigned URL and let the client upload directly to S3. The app servers only mint the URL and record metadata, so file bytes never flow through them and the bottleneck disappears.","explanation":"Presigned URLs let clients upload directly to blob storage, bypassing app servers"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-03","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A fintech app stores customers' account statements in a private storage bucket. An engineer suggests making the bucket public so the web app can link to files directly, since proxying downloads through the backend is slow. What is the right approach instead?","answer":"Generate short-lived presigned download URLs per request. The client still downloads directly from storage, but access is temporary and scoped to one object, so private documents are never publicly exposed.","explanation":"Time-limited presigned URLs secure private file downloads"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-04","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A dashcam company's mobile app uploads 2 GB video files over flaky LTE connections. When a connection drops at 90%, the entire upload restarts from zero, and users are abandoning the app. What mechanism fixes this?","answer":"Use multipart (chunked) upload: split the file into pieces, upload them independently, and retry or resume only the missing chunks after a failure. Chunks can also upload in parallel, which speeds up the transfer.","explanation":"Multipart/chunked upload gives resume-on-failure and parallelism for large files"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-05","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A team building a document-sharing product on S3 plans a sprint to copy every uploaded file to three self-managed VMs 'so we don't lose customer data if S3 fails'. Is this sprint worth it, and why?","answer":"No. Managed object storage already replicates and erasure-codes data internally for roughly eleven nines of durability, far beyond what a hand-rolled VM copy scheme achieves. The custom layer adds cost and failure modes without adding safety.","explanation":"Managed object storage already provides ~11 nines durability — don't build your own replication"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-06","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A podcast app stores episode audio in an S3 bucket in Oregon and hands listeners presigned URLs pointing straight at the bucket. US listeners are happy, but users in Europe and Asia report slow starts and constant buffering on popular shows. What should be added to the delivery path?","answer":"Put a CDN in front of the blob storage bucket and serve downloads through it. Edges near each listener cache popular episodes, so most requests are served locally instead of crossing the ocean to the origin — the standard pattern is metadata in the database, bytes in blob storage, delivery via CDN.","explanation":"Serving blob downloads through a CDN caches popular files at edges near users"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-07","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A ticketing site implements event search with SQL: WHERE title LIKE '%springsteen%' over 40 million rows. Searches take 8+ seconds and pin the database CPU. Why is this slow, and what class of system fixes it?","answer":"A leading-wildcard LIKE can't use a B-tree index, so every query scans the table. A search-optimized database builds an inverted index mapping each term to the documents containing it, turning search into a fast lookup instead of a scan.","explanation":"Inverted indexes turn full-text search from table scans into term lookups"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-08","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A job board builds its own mini search engine and inserts each full job description into the index as one atomic string. A search for 'python' returns nothing unless a description is literally just the word 'python', even though thousands of postings mention it. What processing step did the team skip?","answer":"Tokenization: breaking each document — and each query — into individual words before indexing. The inverted index maps individual terms to the documents containing them, so without splitting text into tokens there are no term-level entries for a query word to match.","explanation":"Tokenization splits text into individual terms before inverted indexing"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-09","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A recipe app's new search engine indexes exact words. Users searching 'baking' get no results for recipes titled 'Baked Ziti' or 'Easy Bakes', even though they clearly match. What feature of text analysis is missing?","answer":"Stemming. Reducing words to their root form ('baking', 'baked', 'bakes' → 'bake') at both index time and query time makes all variants land on the same inverted-index entry, so they match each other.","explanation":"Stemming normalizes word variants to a root form so they match"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-10","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A restaurant discovery app finds that a large share of mobile searches return zero results — queries like 'restarant' or 'sushu' fail even though matching entries exist. What capability should the search system add, and how does it work?","answer":"Fuzzy search based on edit distance: the engine matches terms within a small number of character insertions, deletions, or substitutions, so 'restarant' still finds 'restaurant'. A tolerance of 1–2 edits catches most typos without flooding results.","explanation":"Fuzzy search tolerates typos via edit distance"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-11","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"two-choice","question":"An e-commerce team is building an order-status page where customers paste their exact order ID. One engineer wants to stand up an Elasticsearch cluster for it; another wants a B-tree index on order_id in the existing database. Which is right?","optionA":"Add a B-tree index on order_id in the existing database","optionB":"Stand up an Elasticsearch cluster for order lookups","correct":"A","explanation":"Search-optimized databases earn their operational cost for full-text relevance search, not exact-match key lookups a normal index already serves in milliseconds."},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-12","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A niche blogging platform runs entirely on one Postgres instance, holds about 150,000 posts, and serves a few thousand searches a day. For its new search box, the team is planning to deploy and operate a three-node Elasticsearch cluster. What lighter option should they weigh first, and when would the cluster be justified?","answer":"Use Postgres's built-in full-text search (tsvector columns with a GIN index), which provides tokenization, stemming, and ranking at this scale with zero new infrastructure. A dedicated search engine earns its operational burden at much larger scale or when advanced relevance and fuzzy features become the product.","explanation":"Postgres built-in full-text search can defer a dedicated search engine at moderate scale"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-13","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"On a marketplace, sellers create a listing and immediately search for it to confirm it's live. For a few seconds the search returns nothing, even though the listing row is already in the primary database, and support keeps getting 'my listing is broken' tickets. What is going on?","answer":"The search index is populated asynchronously from the primary database, so it's eventually consistent — new documents take seconds to become searchable. Nothing is broken; either set expectations in the UI or serve the seller's own listings from the primary database.","explanation":"Search indexes are updated asynchronously, so new data is briefly unsearchable"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-14","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A chat product keeps long-lived WebSocket connections from every client. The team is choosing a load balancer and defaults to the same L7 HTTP balancer they use for their REST API. Which layer fits better here, and why?","answer":"An L4 load balancer. It balances at the TCP connection level, cheaply maintaining each persistent connection pinned to one backend, whereas L7 balancers are built around inspecting and routing individual HTTP requests. For plain REST traffic, L7 remains the right default.","explanation":"L4 load balancers suit persistent connections like WebSockets; L7 suits HTTP routing"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-15","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"two-choice","question":"In a system design interview, a candidate is diagramming a microservices design. How should they handle load balancers on the whiteboard?","optionA":"Draw a load balancer box in front of every horizontally scaled service","optionB":"State that scaled services sit behind load balancing and only draw one where the routing is interesting","correct":"B","explanation":"Load balancing is assumed wherever a service is horizontally scaled, and cluttering the diagram with repeated LB boxes wastes time without adding signal."},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-16","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A flash-sale e-commerce site sees order submissions spike to 10x normal for about three minutes when a drop goes live. The fulfillment service processes at a fixed rate, so during spikes it rejects orders and customers rage-post screenshots. What should sit between order submission and fulfillment, and why?","answer":"A message queue. It absorbs the short burst by holding submitted orders while workers drain them at their steady rate, so spike traffic waits a bit instead of being dropped. This works precisely because the overload is brief and average load is within capacity.","explanation":"Queues buffer bursty traffic so spikes wait instead of being dropped"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-17","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A SaaS analytics product lets users export dashboards as PDFs. Generation takes about 60 seconds, HTTP requests time out at 30, and frustrated users hammer the button, spawning duplicate renders. How should the team restructure this feature?","answer":"Make it asynchronous: the API enqueues an export job and immediately returns a job ID, worker processes consume the queue and render the PDF, and the client polls or gets notified when it's ready. Long-running work never belongs inside a synchronous request.","explanation":"Queues move long-running work off the synchronous request path"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-18","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A payments team must return an authorization decision to the point-of-sale terminal within 300 ms. An engineer proposes putting a message queue between the API and the authorization service 'for resilience'. Why is this the wrong place for a queue?","answer":"The caller needs a synchronous answer under a tight latency budget, and a queue adds unpredictable queuing delay with no way to wait for the response inline. Queues fit async work; a latency-sensitive request/response path should call the service directly.","explanation":"Queues are wrong for latency-sensitive synchronous request/response flows"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-19","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A telemetry ingest pipeline receives a steady 500 events/sec all day, but its workers process only 200/sec. The team added a queue in front, yet the queue depth grows without bound and events are now processed hours late. Why didn't the queue help, and what actually fixes this?","answer":"A queue only smooths temporary bursts; with sustained input above processing capacity the backlog grows forever, just delaying the same overload. The real fixes are scaling out workers to match the input rate or applying backpressure to reject or slow producers.","explanation":"A queue cannot fix chronic under-capacity — backpressure or more workers can"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-20","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing app publishes trip lifecycle events (requested, driver_assigned, started, completed) to a partitioned queue with random partition assignment. Consumers sometimes see 'completed' before 'started' for the same trip and corrupt the trip state machine. What should change?","answer":"Use the trip ID as the partition key. All events for one trip then land on the same partition and are consumed in order, while different trips still spread across partitions for parallelism. Ordering is only guaranteed within a partition, never across them.","explanation":"Partition keys give per-key message ordering while allowing parallelism"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-21","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A subscription service sends billing charges via a queue. After a worker crashed mid-task, the message was redelivered and one customer was charged twice. The queue is working as documented. What must the team change on the consumer side?","answer":"Make the consumer idempotent — for example, record a unique charge ID and skip messages already processed. Queues typically guarantee at-least-once delivery, so duplicates after crashes are expected and must be absorbed by the consumer, not prevented by the queue.","explanation":"At-least-once delivery means consumers must be idempotent against duplicates"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-22","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"An IoT platform's ingestion worker keeps crashing on one malformed device message. The queue redelivers it, the worker crashes again, and this loop has blocked the partition and flooded the logs for hours. What mechanism is missing?","answer":"A dead letter queue: after a bounded number of failed attempts, the poison message is moved to a separate DLQ instead of retrying forever. That unblocks the pipeline and preserves the bad message for alerting, inspection, and possible replay after a fix.","explanation":"Dead letter queues quarantine poison messages after max retries"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-23","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A social feed pipeline partitions post-engagement events by author user ID. Whenever a celebrity posts, one consumer falls hours behind while the others sit idle, even though total throughput is well within cluster capacity. What is going wrong?","answer":"The partition key is skewed: one celebrity's ID funnels a huge share of traffic to a single hot partition, and per-partition ordering means only one consumer can drain it. Choose a higher-cardinality or compound key (e.g., author ID + shard suffix) to spread the load.","explanation":"A skewed partition key creates a hot partition that lags"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-24","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"An analytics team discovers a bug that corrupted a week of computed metrics and wants to recompute them from the raw click events. But the events flowed through a queue that deletes each message once acknowledged, so there is nothing to reprocess. What infrastructure choice would have made this recovery possible?","answer":"A log-based stream (e.g., Kafka-style) instead of a destructive queue. Streams retain events for a configured window and let consumers rewind to any offset, so the week of clicks could simply be replayed through the fixed pipeline.","explanation":"Streams differ from queues by retention, replay, and multiple consumers"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-25","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A digital wallet must prove to auditors exactly how every balance was reached and be able to reconstruct any account's balance as of any past date. Storing only the current balance per account can't do either. What pattern fits, and how does it work?","answer":"Event sourcing: persist every deposit, withdrawal, and transfer as an immutable, append-only event, and derive the current balance by folding over the events. The log itself is the audit trail, and replaying it up to any timestamp reconstructs historical state.","explanation":"Event sourcing stores an immutable event log and derives state from it"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-26","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A fraud-detection service and an analytics warehouse loader both read the same order-events stream. Each sees only about half the events because messages one service consumes never reach the other. What is misconfigured?","answer":"Both services are in the same consumer group, so the stream's partitions are split between them as if they were one horizontally scaled app. Put each service in its own consumer group; every group then receives the full stream and tracks its own offsets independently.","explanation":"Separate consumer groups each get the full stream with independent offsets"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-27","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"two-choice","question":"An ops dashboard must report the count of checkout errors in each discrete 5-minute bucket (12:00–12:05, 12:05–12:10, ...), with every event counted in exactly one bucket. Which stream windowing type is correct?","optionA":"Tumbling windows","optionB":"Sliding windows","correct":"A","explanation":"They are fixed-size and non-overlapping, so each event falls into exactly one bucket; sliding windows overlap, so events would be counted in multiple windows."},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-28","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A gaming company runs its event stream with a single copy of each partition to save disk. A broker's machine dies, and every event on its partitions is permanently lost, breaking downstream leaderboards. What configuration prevents a repeat, and what does it cost?","answer":"Set a replication factor of 3 so each partition's events live on multiple brokers, and a replica takes over as leader when one fails. The price is extra storage and slightly higher write latency while replicas acknowledge — the standard trade for fault tolerance.","explanation":"Stream partition replication tolerates broker failure"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-29","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A concert ticketing app wraps seat reservation in a language-level mutex, and it worked in staging. In production, with twelve app servers behind a load balancer, two users on different servers still buy the same seat. Why does the mutex fail, and what is the fix?","answer":"An in-process mutex only serializes threads inside one server; requests landing on different servers never contend on it. Cross-server mutual exclusion needs a distributed lock in a shared store (e.g., Redis or ZooKeeper) keyed on the seat, held with a TTL during checkout.","explanation":"In-process locks don't span servers — cross-server mutual exclusion needs a distributed lock"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-30","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing matcher takes a distributed lock on a driver while offering them a trip. One matcher process crashed while holding a lock, and that driver has now been invisible to dispatch for six hours. What is missing from the lock design?","answer":"An expiry (TTL) on the lock. Since a crashed holder can never release it, the lock must auto-expire — long enough to cover the offer flow, short enough that a crash frees the driver quickly. Choosing that TTL is the core trade-off of distributed locking.","explanation":"Lock expiry (TTL) prevents a crashed holder from locking a resource forever"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-31","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A team implements a distributed lock in Redis as two steps: GET the key to check it's absent, then SET it to claim it. Under load, two schedulers routinely both believe they hold the lock. What is wrong with this implementation?","answer":"Check-then-set is a race: both processes can GET 'absent' before either SETs. Acquisition must be one atomic operation — SET with NX (set only if not exists) plus an expiry — so the store itself guarantees exactly one winner.","explanation":"Lock acquisition must be a single atomic operation, not check-then-set"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-32","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A banking backend locks both accounts before executing a transfer. When user A pays user B at the same moment B pays A, both transfers hang forever, each holding one account lock and waiting for the other. What design rule was violated?","answer":"Locks must be acquired in a consistent global order — e.g., always lock the lower account ID first. With a fixed ordering, the circular wait that causes deadlock cannot form; timeouts on acquisition are a backstop, not the primary fix.","explanation":"Acquiring multiple locks in a consistent global order prevents deadlock"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-33","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"An auction platform guards bid placement with a single global 'bids' lock to keep updates safe. It works, but during busy evenings all auctions slow to a crawl because bids on unrelated items queue behind each other. What should change?","answer":"Narrow the lock granularity: lock per auction item, not globally. Bids on different items then proceed in parallel while bids on the same item still serialize — coarse locks are simpler but throttle throughput far more than necessary.","explanation":"Fine-grained locks preserve concurrency; one coarse lock serializes everything"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-34","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"To decrement inventory safely, an e-commerce team acquires a Redis distributed lock, runs a single UPDATE stock SET count = count - 1 WHERE id = ? AND count > 0 in Postgres, then releases the lock. Is the lock earning its keep here?","answer":"No. That conditional UPDATE is already atomic inside the database's transaction machinery, so the distributed lock adds latency and a failure dependency for nothing. Distributed locks are for exclusivity that spans multiple requests or extended time (like holding a cart item), not single-statement updates.","explanation":"Single-statement atomic updates belong in a database transaction, not a distributed lock"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-35","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A news site's cache uses LRU and normally hits well: a small set of trending articles serves most traffic. Every night a partner's crawler reads the entire archive once, flushing the trending articles out of the cache, and morning hit rates crater. Which eviction policy fits better, and why?","answer":"LFU (least frequently used). The nightly scan touches each archive article exactly once, so under LFU those one-off entries are evicted first while the frequently read trending set survives. LRU keys purely on recency, which is exactly what the scan exploits.","explanation":"LFU beats LRU when a stable hot set gets polluted by one-off scans"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-36","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"An online store caches product pages with a 1-hour TTL. Merchandisers complain that after they correct a price, some customers see the old price for up to an hour, occasionally forcing the store to honor a wrong price. What should the team change about invalidation?","answer":"TTL-only invalidation guarantees staleness up to the full TTL after any change. Invalidate (or update) the cache entry explicitly when the price write happens — event-driven invalidation — keeping TTL only as a safety net; alternatively shorten the TTL and accept more origin load.","explanation":"TTL-only cache invalidation serves stale data until expiry; event-driven invalidation keeps it fresh"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-37","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"An IoT fleet reports device counters at 50,000 writes/sec. The current write-through cache forwards every write to the database synchronously, and database write latency now throttles the whole ingest path. Losing a few seconds of counter updates in a crash is acceptable. Which write strategy fits, and what is the trade?","answer":"Write-back: write to the cache and return immediately, flushing to the database asynchronously in batches. Writes get fast and the database sees smoothed, batched load, in exchange for a small window of data that can be lost if the cache dies before flushing — acceptable here.","explanation":"Write-back caching absorbs heavy write rates at the cost of a data-loss window"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-38","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A catalog service uses write-around caching. After a bulk overnight import of 200,000 updated products, the morning's first page loads are noticeably slow, then everything speeds up. The team suspects a regression. What is actually happening?","answer":"This is write-around working as designed: writes go straight to the database and bypass the cache, so the first read of each updated product is a cache miss that repopulates the entry. If the morning slowdown matters, pre-warm the cache for hot products after the import.","explanation":"Write-around caching makes writes fast but the first subsequent read a miss"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-39","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A banking dashboard caches account balances using write-around, so after each transaction the cached balance is stale until it expires or is re-read from the database — and users sometimes see an old balance right after a transfer. Write volume is modest, but reads must always reflect the latest write. Which cache write strategy fits, and what does it cost?","answer":"Write-through: every write updates the cache and the database together, so reads always see fresh data and the cache never drifts from the store of record. The cost is slower writes, since each one performs two synchronous updates — an easy trade at modest write volume.","explanation":"Write-through caching keeps cache and database consistent at the cost of slower writes"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-40","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A chat backend writes every message into Redis 'to speed things up', but analysis shows messages are almost never read back through the cache — clients hold recent history locally. Meanwhile the cache is evicting constantly and hit rate is near zero. What should the team do?","answer":"Stop caching the messages. Caching pays off for hot, repeatedly read data; a write-heavy stream that's rarely re-read just burns memory and eviction churn for no hit-rate benefit. Write messages to the store of record and cache only genuinely hot reads.","explanation":"Write-heavy, rarely-reread data is a poor caching candidate"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-41","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A game studio serves patch assets from a storage bucket in Virginia through a CDN. Players report that the very first download in a city each morning is slow, but every download after that in the same city is fast. Explain the pattern.","answer":"Each CDN edge keeps its own regional cache, and entries expire by TTL or get evicted during the quiet overnight hours. The first request of the day therefore misses — the edge fetches from the Virginia origin and caches the asset — and every later request in that city is served locally. A cold miss followed by fast hits, per edge, is normal CDN behavior, not a fault.","explanation":"CDN edges cache per region: first request misses to origin, later ones hit"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-42","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A gaming platform's global leaderboard API returns the same JSON to every user and recomputes every 60 seconds, yet millions of clients poll it and the origin fleet is drowning. The team assumes CDNs are only for images and JS files. What should they do?","answer":"Cache the API response at the CDN with a TTL of up to 60 seconds. Since the payload is identical for all users and staleness within a minute is acceptable, the vast majority of requests get absorbed at the edge and the origin serves roughly one fetch per edge per TTL.","explanation":"CDNs can cache identical-for-everyone API responses, not just static assets"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-43","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"To cut latency, a streaming service put its /homepage endpoint — which returns each user's personalized recommendations — behind CDN caching keyed on the URL. Users immediately began seeing strangers' watch histories. What went wrong, and what is the rule here?","answer":"The edge cached one user's personalized response and served it to everyone hitting the same URL. Per-user dynamic content must bypass the CDN (or be keyed per user, which destroys hit rates anyway); edge caching is for responses that are safely identical across users.","explanation":"Per-user personalized responses must not be cached at shared CDN edges"},
  {"id":"hi-key-technologies-overview-44","topic":"key-technologies · key technologies overview","type":"open","question":"A web team deploys a critical JavaScript fix, but many users keep receiving the old bundle for hours because the CDN caches app.js with a long TTL, and manual purges are slow and error-prone. What release practice eliminates this problem?","answer":"Fingerprint the assets: serve app.<contenthash>.js so every build gets a new URL, and have the (short-TTL) HTML reference it. New deploys are picked up instantly because the new URL is never cached yet, old versions expire naturally, and long TTLs stay safe — no purging needed.","explanation":"Content-hashed/versioned asset URLs sidestep CDN invalidation after deploys"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-01","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"two-choice","question":"In a system design interview, the strongest way to justify picking PostgreSQL is to open with a detailed walkthrough of MVCC and write-ahead-log internals.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Interviewers mainly want to know whether the database fits your data relationships, consistency needs, and scale. Lead with capabilities and trade-offs; bring up internals only when they explain a specific limit you are designing around."},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-02","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A startup is building an invoicing SaaS: customers, invoices, line items, credit notes, and payments all reference each other, and an invoice total must always equal the sum of its line items. Traffic is a few hundred requests per second. One engineer pushes for a document database 'for flexibility'; another wants PostgreSQL. Which fits better, and why?","answer":"PostgreSQL. The data is inherently relational with cross-entity invariants, which foreign keys, joins, and ACID transactions enforce directly, while a document store would push that integrity work into application code. The scale is trivially within a single Postgres node.","explanation":"PostgreSQL as the default for relational data with integrity needs"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-03","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A hotel-booking app runs queries like 'find reservations with check_in between two dates, sorted by check_in'. The query is slow, and a junior engineer claims they need to research some exotic index type because 'regular indexes only do exact matches'. What should the team actually do?","answer":"Just create a default B-tree index on check_in. B-trees are not limited to exact matches — they serve range predicates and can return rows already in sorted order, which covers this query entirely.","explanation":"B-tree default index handles equality, range, and ordering"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-04","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"In a chat application, the messages table declares channel_id as a foreign key to channels. Loading a channel's messages does a full sequential scan of millions of rows, and the team is confused because 'the foreign key is right there'. What is going wrong?","answer":"PostgreSQL creates indexes automatically only for primary key and unique constraints — declaring a foreign key does not index the referencing column. They need to explicitly create an index on messages(channel_id).","explanation":"Foreign key columns are not automatically indexed"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-05","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing service has an index on trips(driver_id, started_at) that makes per-driver history queries fast. A new operations dashboard queries all trips across every driver started in the last hour, and it is dog slow despite 'started_at being indexed'. Why doesn't the existing index help?","answer":"A composite B-tree index is ordered by its leading column first, so queries that don't constrain driver_id can't use an index on (driver_id, started_at) effectively. The dashboard needs an index that leads with started_at.","explanation":"Multi-column index leftmost-prefix rule"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-06","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A smart-home platform's sensor_readings table has accumulated nine indexes as different teams added ones for their own reports. Ingestion throughput has steadily dropped even though hardware hasn't changed. What is the likely cause and the fix?","answer":"Every insert must update all nine indexes, so index maintenance is now dominating the write path. Audit index usage, drop the unused ones, and keep only indexes that earn their write cost.","explanation":"Over-indexing degrades write throughput"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-07","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce catalog page runs 'SELECT id, name FROM products WHERE category_id = ?' thousands of times per second. There is already an index on category_id, but EXPLAIN shows the query still fetching every matching row from the table heap to get the name. How can the team make this query served from the index alone?","answer":"Create a covering index that carries every selected column — for example on (category_id) INCLUDE (id, name) — so Postgres can do an index-only scan and skip the heap fetches. Note that Postgres secondary indexes do not implicitly contain the primary key, so id must be included explicitly.","explanation":"Covering indexes and index-only scans"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-08","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A SaaS platform's jobs table holds 200 million rows, 99% of them with status 'completed'. Workers constantly poll for status = 'pending' rows. A full index on status is huge, mostly filled with completed rows nobody queries. What indexing approach fits better?","answer":"A partial index: CREATE INDEX ... WHERE status = 'pending'. It only contains the tiny hot subset, so it stays small and fast, and completed rows never pay index maintenance for it.","explanation":"Partial indexes for a hot subset of rows"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-09","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A recipe-sharing site with three million recipes wants keyword search over titles and instructions. The team is about to stand up an Elasticsearch cluster plus a sync pipeline from their PostgreSQL database. What should they consider first, and why?","answer":"Use Postgres's built-in full-text search: a tsvector column with a GIN index. At this scale it handles keyword search well and avoids running and keeping a second search system consistent with the source data. Reach for Elasticsearch only when relevance features or scale genuinely outgrow it.","explanation":"Postgres full-text search before reaching for Elasticsearch"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-10","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A gaming platform stores per-game player settings whose shape varies wildly between games and changes every release. The team proposes adding MongoDB alongside their existing PostgreSQL database just to hold these settings. What is the simpler alternative?","answer":"Store the settings in a JSONB column on the existing players/games tables, adding a GIN index if they need to query inside it. That keeps flexible-schema data next to the relational data without operating a second database.","explanation":"JSONB for semi-structured data in a relational schema"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-11","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A CRM's contact search uses ILIKE '%smit%' so reps can find 'Smith' with partial or slightly misspelled input. With two million contacts each search takes seconds, and a plain B-tree index on name didn't help at all. What should the team use?","answer":"The pg_trgm extension with a trigram GIN index. B-trees can't serve ILIKE patterns with a leading wildcard, but trigram indexes accelerate substring and similarity matching directly in Postgres.","explanation":"pg_trgm for fuzzy and substring matching"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-12","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery app answers 'restaurants within 3 km of the user' by scanning latitude/longitude columns with hand-rolled bounding-box math over B-tree indexes. Queries are slow and the code is error-prone near the poles and the antimeridian. What is the right tool here?","answer":"PostGIS with a GiST spatial index. It gives correct distance and radius semantics and index-accelerated proximity queries, replacing the fragile manual geometry.","explanation":"PostGIS/GiST for geospatial queries"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-13","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A B2B analytics dashboard query is fast in staging but takes 20 seconds in production. An engineer wants to start adding indexes based on gut feel until it improves. What should happen first instead?","answer":"Run EXPLAIN ANALYZE against the production query to see the actual plan — whether it's a sequential scan, a bad join order, or wildly wrong row estimates. Diagnose from the real plan, then change exactly what it shows is wrong.","explanation":"EXPLAIN ANALYZE before guessing at fixes"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-14","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A social app's home feed runs a query joining posts, users, follows, media, and like_counts — five tables — on every single feed load. At tens of thousands of requests per second the joins dominate database CPU, even though every join column is properly indexed. What is the standard way out?","answer":"Cut the join work off the hot path: denormalize (e.g., copy author name and avatar onto the post row) or precompute the feed, so the hot read becomes a simple indexed lookup. Simple indexed reads scale to tens of thousands per second per node, but multi-way joins do real per-query work and cap out far lower — indexes alone can't fix that.","explanation":"Join-heavy hot paths: denormalize or precompute"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-15","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A news site serving about 2,000 mostly-read requests per second is planning a sharded PostgreSQL deployment 'because relational databases don't scale'. Is the sharding justified?","answer":"No. A single well-tuned Postgres instance handles tens of thousands of simple reads per second, so 2,000 rps is comfortably within one node — and a cache or read replicas cover growth long before sharding is needed. Sharding now adds huge complexity for zero benefit.","explanation":"Single-node read capacity — sharding modest read load is premature"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-16","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A fintech ledger service commits every entry as its own transaction. Throughput plateaus at a few thousand writes per second while CPU sits mostly idle and the team is baffled that a beefier instance didn't help. What is the bottleneck?","answer":"Each commit forces the write-ahead log to be flushed to disk, so throughput is capped by how many synchronous disk flushes per second the storage can do — not CPU. One-commit-per-row designs hit this wall regardless of instance size.","explanation":"Write throughput bounded by per-commit WAL flushes"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-17","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A payments API tells clients 'payment recorded' only after PostgreSQL confirms COMMIT. Moments later the server loses power — before the background writer has flushed the modified table pages to disk. On restart, are those acknowledged payments gone?","answer":"No. Postgres flushes each transaction's WAL record to disk before acknowledging the commit, so crash recovery replays the WAL on restart and reconstructs every acknowledged change even though the table pages were stale. That WAL-before-acknowledge rule is how ACID durability is implemented.","explanation":"ACID durability: WAL flushed before commit is acknowledged"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-18","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A product-analytics pipeline inserts 20,000 events per second into PostgreSQL, one INSERT and one commit per event, and can't keep up. Events can tolerate a second or two of buffering. What change gives the biggest win?","answer":"Batch: buffer events and write hundreds or thousands of rows per transaction (multi-row INSERT or COPY). Amortizing the per-commit WAL flush across a batch multiplies effective write throughput by orders of magnitude.","explanation":"Batching rows per transaction to amortize commit cost"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-19","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A URL-shortener runs on serverless functions, each opening its own PostgreSQL connection. During traffic spikes the database throws 'sorry, too many clients already' and its memory usage balloons even though the queries themselves are tiny. What is going on and what fixes it?","answer":"Postgres spawns an OS process per connection, so thousands of short-lived serverless connections exhaust max_connections and memory. Put a connection pooler like PgBouncer in front so many clients multiplex over a small set of real connections.","explanation":"Process-per-connection model and connection pooling"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-20","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A marketplace on a mid-size PostgreSQL instance is nearing CPU and I/O limits. Half the team wants to implement application-level sharding now; the other half wants to move to a much larger instance. Which is the right next step, and why?","answer":"Scale vertically first. A bigger instance requires no application changes, and modern hardware pushes a single node very far, while sharding permanently complicates queries, transactions, and operations. Shard only when vertical scaling is genuinely exhausted.","explanation":"Vertical scaling before sharding"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-21","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A fleet-telemetry table has grown to billions of rows. Queries only ever touch the last 7 days, yet they keep slowing down, and the monthly job that DELETEs data older than a year runs for hours and bloats the table. What structural change helps both problems?","answer":"Partition the table by time range. Queries then prune to only the recent partitions, and retention becomes dropping old partitions — an instant metadata operation instead of a massive DELETE.","explanation":"Table partitioning for very large time-series tables"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-22","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A payments platform needs ACID transactions, but sustained write volume has grown past what one PostgreSQL primary can absorb even after batching, tuning, and a maxed-out instance. Read replicas are already in place. What is the remaining scaling path?","answer":"Shard the data across multiple primaries — application-level sharding by a key like account_id, or an extension like Citus. Replicas can't help because they don't take writes; only splitting the write load across nodes raises the ceiling.","explanation":"Sharding when write volume exceeds one primary"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-23","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A B2B dashboard product added three read replicas hoping to fix both slow report queries and slow bulk imports. Reports got faster, but import (write) latency didn't move at all. Why?","answer":"Replicas only serve reads; every write still goes through the single primary. Adding replicas offloads read traffic but does nothing for write throughput — if anything, more replicas add replication work for the primary.","explanation":"Read replicas scale reads, not writes"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-24","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"On a community forum, users edit their profile bio, the save succeeds, but refreshing the page shows the old bio for a few seconds before the new one appears. Reads are load-balanced across replicas. What is happening and what is the standard fix?","answer":"The write hit the primary but the follow-up read hit an asynchronously replicating replica that hadn't applied it yet — classic replication lag. Route a user's reads of their own recently written data to the primary (or pin the session) to guarantee read-your-own-writes.","explanation":"Async replication lag breaks read-your-own-writes"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-25","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A stock-brokerage wants a guarantee that no acknowledged transaction can ever be lost, so they are enabling synchronous replication to a standby. What cost should they expect to pay for this guarantee?","answer":"Every commit now waits for the standby to confirm before acknowledging, so write latency rises and the primary's availability is coupled to the standby's health. That latency and coupling is the price of zero-loss durability versus async replication.","explanation":"Synchronous replication trades latency for zero loss"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-26","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce site's PostgreSQL primary crashed and an asynchronous replica was automatically promoted. Afterward, support finds a handful of orders that customers received confirmation emails for but that don't exist in the database. How did this happen?","answer":"With async replication the primary acknowledges commits before replicas receive them, so the promoted replica was missing the last moments of writes. Those acknowledged-but-unreplicated orders were lost in the failover — the inherent risk of async replication.","explanation":"Failover onto an async replica can lose acknowledged commits"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-27","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A ticketing site's checkout runs two separate statements: insert the order row, then decrement the seat-inventory row. A server crash between them left inventory decremented with no corresponding order, and a seat effectively vanished. What should the team change?","answer":"Wrap both statements in a single transaction so they commit atomically — either the order exists and inventory is decremented, or neither happened. This all-or-nothing guarantee is exactly what ACID atomicity is for.","explanation":"Transactions make multi-row operations atomic"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-28","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"In a multiplayer game, two concurrent requests each read a player's gold balance of 100, add a 50 reward, and write back 150. The player earned 100 gold but ended with 150. The database is at PostgreSQL's default isolation level. What is going wrong?","answer":"A lost update: both transactions did read-modify-write on the same row, and READ COMMITTED — the default — does nothing to stop the second write from overwriting the first. The fix is row locking, an atomic 'SET gold = gold + 50' update, or optimistic versioning.","explanation":"READ COMMITTED does not prevent lost updates"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-29","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A courier-dispatch system keeps assigning the same courier to two orders at once: two dispatch workers read the courier row as 'available' at the same time and both claim them. Contention on individual courier rows is frequent. What mechanism prevents the double-assignment?","answer":"SELECT ... FOR UPDATE when reading the courier row. The first transaction locks the row, the second blocks until it commits and then sees the courier is taken — pessimistic locking, which is the right call when conflicts on a row are common.","explanation":"Pessimistic locking with SELECT ... FOR UPDATE"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-30","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A CMS lets editors open an article, edit for many minutes, and save. Conflicts are rare, and holding a database row lock for a whole editing session is off the table. How should the system detect conflicting saves?","answer":"Optimistic concurrency: store a version number, and save with UPDATE ... WHERE id = ? AND version = ?, incrementing it. If zero rows update, someone saved first — surface the conflict to the editor. No locks are held during think-time, which fits rare conflicts.","explanation":"Optimistic concurrency control with a version column"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-31","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A bank moved its transfer logic to SERIALIZABLE isolation for correctness. Now logs show intermittent 'could not serialize access' (40001) errors and the on-call treats each one as a bug to be eliminated. Are these errors a defect?","answer":"No — they are how SERIALIZABLE works. Postgres aborts transactions whose interleaving can't be made equivalent to a serial order, and clients are expected to catch serialization failures and retry the transaction. The missing piece is a retry loop, not a bug fix.","explanation":"SERIALIZABLE isolation requires client retry logic"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-32","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A social feed updates a post's like_count by reading the row with a lock, adding one, and writing it back. When a post goes viral, like requests pile up and latency spikes on just that post. What is wrong and how should likes be counted at this scale?","answer":"Every like serializes on one hot row, so lock waits stack up. Drop the locked read-modify-write for a single atomic UPDATE ... SET like_count = like_count + 1, and at true viral scale batch or aggregate likes asynchronously — a like counter can be eventually consistent.","explanation":"Hot-row counter contention"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-33","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"A collaboration app expands from the US into Europe and Asia and wants every region to accept writes locally with low latency. The plan is to keep the single PostgreSQL primary in Virginia and add read replicas in each region. What is wrong with this plan for writes?","answer":"Postgres is single-primary: the regional replicas can serve local reads, but every write still crosses the ocean to Virginia, so region-local write latency is impossible. Active-active writes require sharding data by region so each region's primary owns its users, or a multi-master store like DynamoDB Global Tables — vanilla Postgres replication won't provide it.","explanation":"Single-primary Postgres cannot serve multi-region active-active writes"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-34","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"open","question":"An industrial-IoT platform must ingest one million sensor readings per second, written once, looked up only by device ID and time, with no joins, no transactions, and relaxed consistency. Is PostgreSQL the right primary store here?","answer":"No — this is the workload where Postgres stops being the default. Extreme-volume simple writes with key-based access and no relational or transactional needs fit a horizontally scalable write-optimized store like Cassandra or DynamoDB; Postgres would demand heavy sharding while providing benefits this workload never uses.","explanation":"When a write-optimized NoSQL store fits better than Postgres"},
  {"id":"hi-postgres-35","topic":"key-technologies · postgres","type":"two-choice","question":"When proposing a database in a system design interview, you should deliver a thorough SQL-versus-NoSQL pros-and-cons comparison before naming your choice.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"The generic SQL-vs-NoSQL debate is a well-known interview pothole. State your choice and justify it with specific strengths tied to the requirements, e.g. 'Postgres, because ACID transactions protect these invariants at our scale.'"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-01","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"two-choice","question":"In Redis, every value stored at a key is a plain string; richer structures like rankings, queues, or logs must be assembled client-side by the application.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Redis is a data structure store: the value at a key can natively be a hash, list, set, sorted set, stream, or geospatial index, each with its own command family. That is precisely what makes leaderboards, queues, and logs one-command operations rather than client-side constructions."},
  {"id":"hi-redis-02","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce engineer runs an expensive debugging command that scans every key on the production Redis cache in the middle of the day. Every product-page request stalls for the full duration, even ones touching completely unrelated keys. Why?","answer":"Redis executes commands sequentially on a single thread, so one long-running command blocks every command queued behind it regardless of which keys they touch. That design removes the need for locks, but it means expensive full-keyspace operations must never run against a busy production instance.","explanation":"Single-threaded sequential execution: one slow command blocks everything"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-03","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A media startup expects a peak of 40,000 vote increments per second during a live TV event and plans a 15-node Redis Cluster because that is what their Postgres capacity math suggested. What does a single Redis node actually absorb, and what does that imply for the plan?","answer":"A single Redis node handles on the order of 100k writes per second, executing each command in microseconds with sub-millisecond network reads, so 40k increments per second fits on one node with headroom. Size Redis by its own performance envelope, not relational-database math — cluster only when data size or traffic genuinely exceeds one node.","explanation":"Performance envelope: ~100k writes/sec per node with microsecond command execution — size Redis by its own numbers"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-04","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A social app renders a 50-post feed by issuing one Redis GET per post. A code reviewer flags this as a fatal N+1 problem, the same way it would be against a SQL database. Is the reviewer right, and what would you do?","answer":"Not fatal — Redis commands cost microseconds server-side, so an N+1 pattern that would sink a SQL design is survivable here. Still, collapse the 50 GETs into a single round trip with MGET or pipelining to remove the per-request network overhead.","explanation":"N+1 reads are survivable on Redis; MGET/pipelining collapses round trips"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-05","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A fintech startup stores customer ledger balances only in Redis, with a replica attached for high availability. During a routine failover the replica is promoted, and several transactions the API had already confirmed to customers are simply gone. What went wrong?","answer":"Redis replication is asynchronous: the primary acknowledges writes before the replica has copied them, so promoting the replica discards the newest acknowledged writes. Redis must never be the system of record for money — the ledger belongs in a durable database, with Redis in front only as a cache.","explanation":"Async replication drops acknowledged writes on failover — not a system of record"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-06","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"An ops dashboard reads metrics from Redis replicas to offload the primary and occasionally shows counts a few hundred milliseconds behind what the primary holds. The team is debating whether this is a bug. What is your read?","answer":"Expected behavior, not a bug: replication is asynchronous, so replica reads always lag by the replication delay. That bounded staleness is exactly the price of scaling reads onto replicas, and for a metrics dashboard it is a perfectly good trade.","explanation":"Replica reads trade replication-lag staleness for read scaling"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-07","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A SaaS analytics dashboard grows its Redis Cluster from 6 to 9 nodes. Immediately afterward, application logs fill with MOVED responses, though every request succeeds on retry. What is happening?","answer":"Resharding migrated some of the 16,384 hash slots to the new nodes, so clients holding the old slot-to-node map are asking the wrong node. Redis never forwards the request — it replies MOVED with the correct node's address, and the client refreshes its cached map. This is expected, self-healing behavior during a rebalance.","explanation":"Cluster resharding: MOVED redirects refresh the client-cached slot map; no server-side forwarding"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-08","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A mobile game running Redis Cluster stores each player's inventory at player:42:inventory and wallet at player:42:wallet, updating both in one MULTI transaction on every purchase. In production the transaction fails with a cross-slot error even though both keys belong to the same player. What should the team change, and why?","answer":"Rename the keys with a hash tag — {player:42}:inventory and {player:42}:wallet — so only the text in braces is hashed and both keys land in the same slot on the same node. Cluster mode requires all keys in a multi-key command or transaction to live on one node.","explanation":"Cross-slot errors in cluster mode and hash tags for key co-location"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-09","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A team migrating a multi-tenant app onto Redis Cluster asks where to configure the query planner that will route and spread their lookups across the nodes. What do they need to understand about how Redis scales?","answer":"There is no query planner or scatter-gather router — the client hashes each key name to a slot and talks directly to the owning node. Your key naming scheme is your distribution and access strategy, so keys must be designed upfront to match the exact lookups the application will make; that is how you scale Redis.","explanation":"Key structure design is the scaling strategy — there is no query planner or scatter-gather router"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-10","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A note-taking app persists its Redis instance with RDB snapshots taken every five minutes. After a crash, users lose everything written in the last four minutes and the team is surprised. What should they understand about their persistence choice?","answer":"RDB is periodic snapshotting: a crash loses every write since the last snapshot, which here can be up to five minutes. If they need a tighter loss window they should switch to AOF, which narrows the exposure to about one second of writes — and if even that is unacceptable, Redis is the wrong primary store.","explanation":"RDB snapshots lose everything since the last snapshot"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-11","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"An IoT platform buffers sensor readings in Redis with AOF persistence enabled and assumes this means zero data loss. After a host crash, roughly the last second of acknowledged writes is missing. Why?","answer":"AOF logs every write but by default fsyncs to disk only once per second, so a crash loses up to a second of writes Redis already acknowledged. Fsync-on-every-write exists but almost nobody uses it because it destroys the speed that justified Redis in the first place.","explanation":"AOF default fsync-per-second loses up to ~1s of acknowledged writes"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-12","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"two-choice","question":"A team loves the Redis API but requires that every acknowledged write survive a node loss. Which managed option is the more appropriate choice?","optionA":"Standard Redis with AOF configured to fsync every second","optionB":"A durability-focused Redis-compatible service such as AWS MemoryDB","correct":"B","explanation":"It gives up a little speed for real disk-based durability of acknowledged writes, whereas AOF with per-second fsync still loses up to a second of acknowledged writes on a crash."},
  {"id":"hi-redis-13","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce site caches product pages in Redis with 10-minute TTLs. Under heavy load Redis begins rejecting writes with out-of-memory errors, even though keys are expiring constantly. What should the team change?","answer":"Out of the box Redis rejects writes once memory is full — TTLs control staleness, not memory pressure. Configure an eviction policy such as allkeys-lru so Redis discards least-recently-used keys to make room, which is exactly the behavior a cache wants.","explanation":"TTL handles staleness, not memory pressure — an eviction policy like allkeys-lru must be configured"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-14","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"two-choice","question":"When configured with allkeys-lru, Redis tracks the exact recency order of every key and always evicts the strictly least-recently-used one.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Redis approximates LRU by sampling a handful of candidate keys and evicting the best candidate, trading precision for speed. For caching workloads the approximation is entirely sufficient."},
  {"id":"hi-redis-15","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing dispatcher uses Redis keys as zone locks, acquired with a bare SETNX and no expiry. A worker crashes while holding a zone's lock, and dispatch for that zone freezes indefinitely until someone manually deletes the key. What was missing from the design?","answer":"An expiry. Locks should be acquired with SET key token NX EX <seconds> so that if the holder crashes, the lock self-expires and another worker can take over instead of deadlocking the zone forever.","explanation":"Lock acquisition needs NX plus EX so crashed holders self-release"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-16","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A ticketing site guards seat holds with Redis locks. A worker's task overran its lock TTL, so a second worker acquired the lock; when the first worker finally finished, it ran DEL on the lock key, and a third worker instantly grabbed the same seat. How should lock release work to prevent this?","answer":"Store a random unique token as the lock's value and release with a Lua script that deletes the key only if the stored token matches yours. Because Lua runs atomically on the single thread, this check-and-delete can never free a lock that now belongs to another worker.","explanation":"Safe lock release: random token plus atomic Lua check-and-delete"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-17","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A payments service holds a 30-second Redis lock while reconciling an account. One process hits a long GC pause, its lock expires, a second process starts reconciling, and then the first process wakes up and writes anyway, corrupting the account. The team asks how to make the Redis lock airtight. What do you tell them?","answer":"You cannot from Redis alone — a paused or slow client can always act after its lock has expired, and Redis has no built-in fencing tokens (monotonically increasing grant numbers the storage layer checks) to reject the stale writer. Enforce the invariant where the data lives or use a consensus system, and treat Redis locks as an efficiency optimization, not a correctness guarantee.","explanation":"Paused clients acting after lock expiry; no fencing tokens — locks are efficiency, not correctness"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-18","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A booking service acquires a Redis lock on a single primary; milliseconds later that primary dies and its replica is promoted, after which a second client is granted the very same lock and both clients proceed. Why did the lock protocol fail, and what does Redlock do differently?","answer":"Replication is asynchronous, so the promoted replica had never heard of the lock and granted it again. Redlock counters this by acquiring the lock on a majority of independent Redis nodes so no single node failure forgets it — though it remains controversial, since a paused client can still act after expiry without fencing tokens.","explanation":"Single-primary locks vanish on failover; Redlock acquires a majority of independent nodes"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-19","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"two-choice","question":"Two app servers must never concurrently update the same order row in the team's Postgres database. Which approach fits better?","optionA":"Acquire a Redis distributed lock around the update","optionB":"Use the database's own row lock (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE) or a conditional UPDATE ... WHERE version = X","correct":"B","explanation":"The invariant lives in the database, which can enforce it transactionally with no extra infrastructure and none of the lock-expiry hazards a Redis lock introduces."},
  {"id":"hi-redis-20","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A gaming studio's SQL-backed leaderboard is buckling: score updates arrive far faster than the database can absorb them, and top-100 queries are slow. What Redis-based design fixes this, and why does it fit?","answer":"A sorted set: ZADD writes or re-scores a player in logarithmic time, and top-N reads are cheap rank-range queries served in microseconds. A single Redis node absorbs on the order of 100k writes per second, which is exactly the write-heavy ranked workload SQL indexes struggle with.","explanation":"Leaderboards on sorted sets: ZADD writes and log-time top-N reads"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-21","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A social platform keeps a per-hashtag sorted set of post scores that grows without bound, and memory usage keeps climbing — yet the product only ever displays each hashtag's top 10 posts. What is the standard fix?","answer":"Trim after writes with ZREMRANGEBYRANK using negative rank indexes (for example, removing ranks 0 through -11) so each set retains only its top entries. Since negative indexes count down from the highest rank, this deletes the low-scoring tail and keeps memory bounded.","explanation":"Trimming sorted sets with ZREMRANGEBYRANK and negative ranks to cap memory"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-22","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A developer-tools API enforces 1,000 requests per hour by INCRing a per-client counter and calling EXPIRE 3600 on every single request. Heavy users complain their quota never resets as long as they keep making calls. What is going wrong?","answer":"Refreshing EXPIRE on every request pushes the reset time forward each call, so under steady traffic the key never expires and the counter never resets. The expiry must be set only when INCR returns 1 — the first request of a fresh window — so the window boundary stays fixed.","explanation":"Fixed-window bug: EXPIRE on every request means the window never resets"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-23","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A SaaS rate limiter issues INCR and then a separate EXPIRE command from the application. After a crash between the two calls, some counter keys now exist with no TTL at all and permanently block those users. What is the fix?","answer":"Make the two steps atomic by bundling INCR and EXPIRE in one Lua script — Redis's single thread executes the script as an indivisible unit, so no crash between the two commands can leave a counter without its expiry.","explanation":"INCR+EXPIRE must be atomic via Lua or crashes leave TTL-less counters"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-24","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A fintech API caps each client at 100 requests per minute using per-minute counter keys, yet monitoring shows nearly 200 requests slipping through within a few seconds straddling each minute mark. What is going on, and what design closes the gap?","answer":"Classic fixed-window boundary effect: a client can spend its full quota at the end of one window and again at the start of the next, doubling the burst rate. Switch to a sliding window — a per-client sorted set of request timestamps, with ZREMRANGEBYSCORE to drop old entries, ZCARD to count, and ZADD to record, run together as one atomic Lua script.","explanation":"Fixed-window boundary bursts; sliding window via sorted set of timestamps"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-25","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery app needs to find every courier within 2 km of a restaurant, with courier positions refreshing every few seconds. Which Redis capability serves this, and how is it used?","answer":"Redis geospatial commands: GEOADD records each courier's longitude and latitude, and GEOSEARCH with FROMLONLAT and BYRADIUS returns everyone within 2 km. Both updates and radius queries are cheap enough to keep pace with positions refreshing every few seconds.","explanation":"Proximity search with GEOADD and GEOSEARCH"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-26","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"two-choice","question":"Redis GEOSEARCH answers a radius query in two passes: it first collects candidate members from grid-aligned geohash boxes in the underlying sorted set, then filters those candidates down to the exact radius.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"A","explanation":"Redis stores positions as geohashes in a sorted set, and because geohash boxes are grid-aligned squares that only approximate a circle, it gathers candidates from the bounding boxes and then distance-filters them, giving O(N + log M) complexity where N is the candidates in the boxes and M the true matches."},
  {"id":"hi-redis-27","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A video platform needs to farm transcoding jobs out to a fleet of workers so that each job is handled by exactly one worker, and jobs held by a crashed worker are not lost. Which Redis feature fits, and what are its moving parts?","answer":"Redis Streams with a consumer group: producers XADD jobs to the append-only stream, workers XREADGROUP so each entry is delivered to only one consumer in the group, and workers acknowledge entries on completion. Unacknowledged entries remain tracked in the pending list, from which another worker can XCLAIM them if the original holder dies.","explanation":"Streams consumer groups distribute work: XADD, XREADGROUP, acknowledgements, pending-entry recovery"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-28","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A notifications service processes a Redis stream with a consumer group. One worker occasionally stalls on a slow downstream call, and afterward users report receiving the same push notification twice. Explain the duplication and the required design response.","answer":"While the worker stalled, its pending entry's idle time grew until another worker XCLAIMed and reprocessed it — then the original worker also completed. Redis fundamentally cannot distinguish a slow worker from a dead one, so occasional double-processing is inevitable and every handler must be idempotent (for example, deduplicating on a notification ID before sending).","explanation":"XCLAIM cannot tell slow from dead workers — processing must be idempotent"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-29","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"An analytics company ingests clickstream events consumed by three independent teams — one in real time, one in nightly batches, and one backfilling ML models over the previous 30 days. They are choosing between Redis Streams and Kafka. Which fits better here, and why?","answer":"Kafka — this workload needs long retention, durable ordered storage, and independent replay by multiple consumers, which is exactly Kafka's job. Redis Stream entries are only as durable as Redis's persistence settings and fit modest queues and background-job distribution, not month-long replayable logs.","explanation":"Streams vs Kafka: retention, replay, and multiple independent consumers"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-30","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A chat app delivers messages through Redis Pub/Sub. Users on flaky mobile connections complain that messages sent during a brief disconnect never arrive, even after they reconnect. Why, and what is the fix?","answer":"Redis Pub/Sub is at-most-once with zero persistence: a subscriber offline at publish time misses the message permanently. Back the live Pub/Sub path with durable storage — Redis Streams or a message broker — so reconnecting clients can catch up on what they missed.","explanation":"Pub/Sub at-most-once delivery: offline subscribers miss messages forever"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-31","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"two-choice","question":"A Redis Pub/Sub deployment with millions of channels requires millions of client connections, since each subscribed channel needs its own connection.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Connections are pooled per node, not per channel: one connection multiplexes subscriptions to many channels, and channels themselves are free (they exist merely by being named and vanish when the last subscriber leaves). Millions of channels do not imply millions of connections."},
  {"id":"hi-redis-32","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A live-sports platform runs Pub/Sub on a classic Redis Cluster and keeps adding nodes to raise message throughput, but capacity never improves. Why not, and what changed in Redis 7?","answer":"Classic cluster Pub/Sub broadcasts every published message to every node, so adding nodes adds broadcast work instead of capacity. Redis 7's sharded Pub/Sub (SPUBLISH/SSUBSCRIBE) routes each channel to the one node owning its slot, so throughput finally scales with cluster size.","explanation":"Classic cluster Pub/Sub broadcasts to all nodes; Redis 7 sharded Pub/Sub scales"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-33","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A chat startup worries Redis Pub/Sub won't scale, so they plan to store each channel's subscriber server addresses in Redis keys, look them up on every publish, and open connections to push messages to those servers directly. Evaluate this plan.","answer":"It is an anti-pattern that rebuilds Pub/Sub badly: it adds an extra network hop and fresh TCP connections per publish where native Pub/Sub holds persistent per-node pooled connections, and it needs heartbeat/TTL bookkeeping to track which servers are still alive, which Pub/Sub's automatic channel cleanup gives for free. If the use case looks like Pub/Sub, use Pub/Sub.","explanation":"Rolling your own Pub/Sub over Redis lookups is an anti-pattern"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-34","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"During a flash sale, exactly one node of a retailer's 100-node Redis cluster pegs its CPU and starts timing out while the other 99 nodes sit nearly idle. Total traffic is well within the cluster's aggregate capacity. What is the diagnosis?","answer":"A hot key: the sale item's key hashes to a single slot owned by that one node, which is absorbing traffic comparable to the rest of the cluster combined. Aggregate cluster capacity is irrelevant — a single key's load always lands on one node unless you deliberately spread it.","explanation":"Hot key diagnosis: one key's traffic overwhelms one node regardless of cluster size"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-35","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"To relieve a read-hot product key, a retailer writes identical data to product:9:1 through product:9:10 and has readers pick a random suffix. What does this buy them, and what does it cost?","answer":"The ten suffixed keys hash to different slots on different nodes, so read load spreads roughly tenfold across the cluster. The costs: every write must fan out to all ten copies, and clients must know which keys are duplicated — either designated upfront or detected at runtime — which is real operational complexity.","explanation":"Hot-key mitigation: key copies with random suffixes and their costs"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-36","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A social network sees one viral post's metadata key dominating Redis traffic. An engineer proposes a tiny in-process cache on each app server holding just that key with a 2-second TTL. What is the trade-off of this mitigation?","answer":"The near cache absorbs most reads before they ever reach Redis, killing the hot spot, but every server may now serve data up to 2 seconds stale. That is acceptable precisely because the TTL is short and the cached key set is narrow — with long TTLs or broad key sets the staleness would become a correctness problem.","explanation":"Hot-key mitigation: client-side near cache with short-TTL staleness trade-off"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-37","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A news site adds read replicas to relieve a hot article-counter key, but the primary owning that slot stays just as overloaded. Why might the replicas not be helping?","answer":"Two reasons: cluster clients read from primaries by default, so replicas absorb nothing unless clients are explicitly configured for replica reads; and if the key is hot because of writes (it is a counter being incremented), replicas cannot help at all — every write still lands on the one primary that owns the slot.","explanation":"Replica limits for hot keys: explicit replica reads required; useless for write-hot keys"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-38","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"two-choice","question":"In a system design interview you have just routed a popular item's reads through Redis. Regarding hot keys, which behavior scores better with the interviewer?","optionA":"Proactively point out which keys could become hot and sketch the mitigation upfront","optionB":"Keep the design simple and address hot keys only if the interviewer raises the concern","correct":"A","explanation":"Spotting the hot-key failure mode unprompted and designing around it is a strong positive signal, while waiting to be asked reads as not knowing the failure mode exists."},
  {"id":"hi-redis-39","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A media company wants to load its 40 TB video-metadata archive — most of it rarely accessed — entirely into Redis 'for speed'. What is wrong with this plan?","answer":"Redis keeps everything in RAM, the most expensive place to store data, and its speed comes entirely from that fact — 40 TB of mostly-cold data in memory is economically absurd. Keep the archive in a disk-backed database or object store and cache only the hot working set in Redis.","explanation":"When not to use Redis: working set that cannot economically fit in RAM"},
  {"id":"hi-redis-40","topic":"key-technologies · redis","type":"open","question":"A B2B SaaS team stores customer records as Redis hashes and now needs ad-hoc reports that join customers to invoices with filters across several fields. They ask how to express these queries in Redis. What do you advise?","answer":"Don't — Redis has no joins and no flexible cross-key querying, and in cluster mode multi-key operations are restricted to a single slot anyway. Relational, ad-hoc query workloads belong in a real database; keep Redis for the fast, targeted key lookups it is built for.","explanation":"When not to use Redis: no joins or flexible cross-key querying"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-01","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"PingPal, a chat startup, ran happily on one server: every message was delivered in-process because that single server held every user's connection. After scaling to three servers behind a load balancer, messages routinely go undelivered whenever the sender and recipient landed on different servers. What class of problem has the team hit, and what kind of component solves it?","answer":"This is a distributed coordination problem: no server knows which peer holds a given user's connection anymore. They need a consistent shared registry — a coordination service like ZooKeeper — mapping each user's session to its server so any server can route messages correctly.","explanation":"Recognizing a distributed coordination problem (shared registry of which server owns what)"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-02","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"Dashly, an analytics SaaS, started storing whole dashboard-template blobs — some several megabytes — inside ZooKeeper znodes 'since it's already replicated.' Writes for the biggest templates fail outright, and the smaller ones have ballooned memory usage on every ensemble node. What is wrong with this design?","answer":"ZNodes are capped at roughly 1 MB and the entire tree is held in memory on every server — ZooKeeper is a small-metadata store, not a blob store. Put the templates in object storage and keep only a pointer and version number in the znode.","explanation":"ZNode payload limit (~1MB) and metadata-only hierarchical data model"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-03","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"SensorGrid registers each field gateway by creating a persistent znode in ZooKeeper at startup. The ops dashboard, which lists those znodes, still shows gateways that burned out weeks ago. What should the team change, and why?","answer":"Register with ephemeral znodes instead. An ephemeral node is tied to the creating client's session and is deleted automatically when that session dies, so crashed gateways drop out of the registry on their own instead of lingering forever.","explanation":"Ephemeral znodes: session-bound lifecycle as automatic failure detection"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-04","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"PayFlow's settlement workers each create a znode under /workers using names generated client-side. Occasionally two workers pick the same name, one create fails, and the retry logic is a mess. The team also wants a stable ordering of workers for fair task handoff. Which znode feature fixes both issues?","answer":"Sequential znodes. ZooKeeper appends a monotonically increasing counter to the name it assigns, so every create is guaranteed unique and the counters give a natural total order across workers.","explanation":"Sequential znodes: server-assigned unique, monotonically ordered names"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-05","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"Two admin tools at TixHub edit the same /config/pricing znode: each reads the JSON blob, changes one field, and writes the whole thing back. Occasionally one tool's change silently vanishes seconds after being saved, even though both writes reported success. Which ZooKeeper feature prevents this lost-update race?","answer":"Version-conditioned writes: every znode carries a version number that increments on each update, and setData can demand the exact version the writer last read, failing if anyone wrote in between. The loser re-reads and retries instead of blindly overwriting — optimistic concurrency control built into the data model.","explanation":"ZNode version numbers and conditional writes (optimistic concurrency control)"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-06","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"SeatRush runs a 6-node ZooKeeper ensemble, reasoning that six servers means they can afford to lose three. During a rack failure that took out three nodes at once, the entire ensemble stopped serving. What did they get wrong?","answer":"Quorum is a strict majority: 6 nodes need 4 alive, so a 6-node ensemble tolerates only 2 failures — the same as a 5-node one. That's why ensembles use odd sizes; surviving three simultaneous losses requires 7 nodes.","explanation":"Quorum math and odd-sized ensembles (majority rule, fault tolerance counts)"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-07","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"A game studio grew its ZooKeeper ensemble from 5 voting servers to 11 to 'add capacity' for matchmaking metadata. Read latency stayed fine, but write latency got noticeably worse. Why?","answer":"Every write must be acknowledged by a majority of voters, so more voting members means more machines must coordinate on each commit — writes get slower, not faster. Keep the voting set at 3-5 and scale reads by other means.","explanation":"Write latency grows as the voting ensemble grows — keep 3-5 voters"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-08","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"TeamBoard serves users on three continents and wants low-latency ZooKeeper reads in every region, but adding regional servers as voters would drag each write through cross-continent quorum round trips. What ZooKeeper server role solves this?","answer":"Observers. They replicate state and serve reads locally but don't vote, so they add regional read capacity without enlarging the write quorum or adding WAN latency to commits.","explanation":"Observer role: read scaling without enlarging the write quorum"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-09","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"Streamz monitors its 5-node ZooKeeper ensemble and sees one node with far higher CPU and network usage than the other four, even though client connections are evenly load-balanced across all five. Is something broken?","answer":"No — that node is the leader. Every write from any client is forwarded to the single leader, which proposes and orders it, while followers mainly serve reads. The leader naturally runs hotter.","explanation":"All writes are funneled through and ordered by the single leader"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-10","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"An API-gateway team caches per-tenant rate limits from a znode and sets a watch to refresh the cache. When an admin updates a tenant's limit twice within a second, gateways sometimes see only the final value and never the middle one. What watch behavior explains this?","answer":"ZooKeeper watches are one-shot: after a watch fires you must re-read and re-register, and changes that happen in that gap are not individually delivered. Watches guarantee you learn the latest state, not that you observe every intermediate update.","explanation":"Watches are one-shot triggers that deliver latest state, not every intermediate change"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-11","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"ShopNest's 800 storefront servers each poll ZooKeeper every 2 seconds to check whether a feature flag changed. The ensemble is drowning in read traffic, and flag flips still take up to 2 seconds to reach servers. What is the idiomatic fix?","answer":"Replace polling with watches: each server reads the flag once and registers a watch, and ZooKeeper pushes a notification the moment the znode changes. Propagation becomes near-instant and the constant polling load disappears.","explanation":"Push-based watches vs client polling for config propagation"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-12","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"RideLoop's API layer keeps a static file listing its dispatch-service IPs. When a dispatch instance crashes, requests keep hitting the dead IP until an engineer edits the file. How would ZooKeeper-based service discovery fix this?","answer":"Each dispatch instance registers an ephemeral znode under a path like /services/dispatch, and clients list the children and set a watch. When an instance crashes its session lapses, its znode vanishes, and all watchers are notified — the registry stays current with zero manual edits.","explanation":"Service discovery via ephemeral registrations plus child watches (auto-deregistration)"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-13","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"CronCloud runs three replicas of its job scheduler for resilience, but exactly one may fire jobs at any moment; lately all three occasionally run the same job. Describe the standard ZooKeeper recipe that gives them a single active scheduler with automatic failover.","answer":"Each replica creates an ephemeral sequential znode under an election path; the replica holding the lowest sequence number is the leader. If the leader dies, its session expires, its znode disappears, and the next-lowest replica takes over automatically.","explanation":"Leader election recipe: ephemeral-sequential znodes, lowest sequence wins"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-14","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"StreamForge has 400 transcoding workers contending for a ZooKeeper-based lock. Every time the lock is released, all 400 workers wake simultaneously and slam the ensemble with requests, causing latency spikes. What is this called, and how does the standard recipe avoid it?","answer":"That's the herd (thundering herd) effect. In the correct recipe each waiter watches only the znode with the next-lower sequence number — its immediate predecessor — so a release wakes exactly one client instead of all of them.","explanation":"Herd effect avoidance: each waiter watches only its immediate predecessor"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-15","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"FreightIQ guards its nightly billing job with a Redis lock carrying a 10-minute TTL. On heavy nights the job runs longer than the TTL, the lock silently expires mid-job, and a second runner starts. How does a ZooKeeper ephemeral-node lock avoid this specific failure?","answer":"A ZooKeeper lock is held as long as the holder's session stays alive via heartbeats — there is no fixed expiry to outlive. The lock releases when the holder actually disconnects or crashes, not when an arbitrary timer fires while the work is still running.","explanation":"Crash-safe locks: session-tied ephemeral release vs fixed TTL leases"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-16","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"A core-banking team asks: if ZooKeeper acknowledges our write and the leader machine dies a millisecond later, can that write vanish when a new leader takes over? What does ZAB guarantee here?","answer":"No. ZAB acknowledges a write only after a majority of the ensemble has logged the proposal, and the new leader is elected from that majority with the most complete history — so every acknowledged write survives leader failure.","explanation":"ZAB quorum-acknowledged commits: acked writes survive leader failure"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-17","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"QuantDesk's risk service writes an updated position limit to ZooKeeper; milliseconds later a separate trading service reads the same znode and gets the old limit, with no errors anywhere. What is going on?","answer":"Reads are answered locally by whichever server the client is connected to, which may lag the leader slightly — ZooKeeper guarantees linearizable writes but only sequentially consistent reads. A briefly stale read from a follower is expected behavior, not a bug.","explanation":"Linearizable writes but sequentially consistent (possibly stale) follower reads"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-18","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"FeatureFlagCo makes two ZooKeeper writes in order: first it disables flag A, then it enables flag B, because B is only safe once A is off. An engineer worries that app servers reading from different followers might observe B enabled while A is still on — a combination that never existed at the leader. Can that happen?","answer":"No. ZAB stamps every committed write with a monotonically increasing zxid and every server applies writes in that single global order, so any server that shows B's change has already applied A's. A lagging replica can show an older prefix of history, but never a reordered or mixed one.","explanation":"Global total order of updates: every client observes writes in the same zxid sequence"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-19","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"CartLock's inventory reconciler must, at one critical step, read the absolute latest value of a znode that other services write — a slightly stale follower read would corrupt the reconciliation. What does ZooKeeper offer for this?","answer":"Call sync() on the path before reading. sync() forces the server the client is connected to to catch up with the leader, so the read that follows reflects every write committed before the sync.","explanation":"sync() to force a connected server to catch up before a read"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-20","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"SearchLite's Java coordinator keeps losing its ZooKeeper leadership during 12-second full-GC pauses even though the process is perfectly healthy, triggering pointless failovers. What is happening, and what is the trade-off in fixing it?","answer":"The GC pause stops heartbeats longer than the session timeout, so ZooKeeper expires the session and deletes the ephemeral leader node. Raising the timeout stops the false failovers but slows detection of genuinely dead nodes — session timeout tuning is exactly this trade-off.","explanation":"Session timeout tuning trade-off: false expirations (GC pauses) vs slow crash detection"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-21","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"During routine patching, HuddleChat's ops team restarts ZooKeeper servers one at a time. A new engineer expects users' presence entries (ephemeral znodes) to flap whenever the server a client is connected to restarts, but they never do. Why not?","answer":"Sessions belong to the ensemble, not to one server. When a client's server goes down, the client reconnects to another ensemble member within the session timeout, the session continues uninterrupted, and its ephemeral nodes are untouched.","explanation":"Sessions are ensemble-wide and survive failover to another server"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-22","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"A full power failure took down every node of BuildHub's ZooKeeper ensemble at once, yet the cluster came back with all znode data intact — surprising an engineer who had read that ZooKeeper 'keeps everything in memory.' How is that possible?","answer":"The in-memory tree is backed by a write-ahead transaction log fsynced before each commit is acknowledged, plus periodic snapshots. On restart each server loads the latest snapshot and replays the log, rebuilding the exact state.","explanation":"Durability mechanics: in-memory tree backed by transaction log (WAL) and snapshots"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-23","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"An SRE at Snackly, a food-delivery platform, kills one ZooKeeper node as a chaos experiment. Reads continue uninterrupted, but every write fails for about two seconds before things recover on their own. What happened?","answer":"The killed node was the leader. Writes paused while the remaining majority ran ZAB leader election and synced with the new leader — a brief, expected write-unavailability window — while followers kept serving reads throughout.","explanation":"Brief write-unavailability window during ZAB leader re-election, reads continue"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-24","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"OpsPanel runs a 5-node ensemble split 3-2 across two data centers. When the inter-DC link fails, applications in the smaller DC lose all ZooKeeper service even though both local ZooKeeper servers are perfectly healthy. Is this a bug?","answer":"No — it's the design. Only the partition holding a majority (the 3-node side) may keep operating; the 2-node minority deliberately refuses service so two independent leaders can never coexist. ZooKeeper chooses consistency over availability, and that refusal is precisely what prevents split-brain.","explanation":"Majority-partition rule: CP behavior, minority side goes dark to prevent split-brain"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-25","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"A messaging platform upgrading to a current Kafka version has budgeted for a new dedicated ZooKeeper ensemble because 'Kafka needs ZooKeeper.' What should they know before spending the money?","answer":"Modern Kafka replaced ZooKeeper with KRaft, an embedded Raft quorum run by the brokers/controllers themselves, so no external ensemble is needed. It reflects a broader trend of systems internalizing consensus rather than depending on ZooKeeper.","explanation":"Kafka's KRaft migration and the trend toward embedded consensus"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-26","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"two-choice","question":"True or False: Now that Kafka has moved to KRaft, no widely used open-source system still depends on ZooKeeper, so studying it has no practical payoff.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":". ZooKeeper remains central to the Apache ecosystem — HBase, SolrCloud, and Hadoop's HA components still rely on it — and its recipes teach coordination concepts that transfer directly to every modern alternative."},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-27","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"A platform team running everything on Kubernetes needs leader election and shared config for a new custom controller, and proposes standing up a three-node ZooKeeper ensemble for it. What is the leaner alternative, and why?","answer":"Use etcd — the Raft-based coordination store already powering the Kubernetes control plane, with leases and watches as its coordination primitives (and Kubernetes exposes a Lease-based election API on top). A separate ZooKeeper ensemble adds operational burden for capabilities the platform already provides.","explanation":"etcd as the coordination layer when already on Kubernetes (Raft, leases, watches)"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-28","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"two-choice","question":"A polyglot microservices company mainly needs service discovery with active health checking — instances should drop from the registry when they fail HTTP health probes, and services should locate each other via DNS. Which tool fits better out of the box?","optionA":"Consul","optionB":"ZooKeeper","correct":"A","explanation":"It is purpose-built for service discovery with built-in application-level health checks and DNS/HTTP interfaces, whereas ZooKeeper offers only low-level primitives you must assemble yourself and can detect liveness only via session loss, not failing health probes."},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-29","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"open","question":"A startup built its work queue on ZooKeeper: producers create sequential znodes for each task and consumers claim and delete them. At a few thousand tasks per second the ensemble is melting down. Why is this the wrong tool?","answer":"ZooKeeper is a control-plane metadata store: every write is a quorum commit through a single leader, all data lives in RAM, and heavy znode-and-watch churn overwhelms it. High-throughput task flow belongs in a real queue like Kafka or SQS — use ZooKeeper to coordinate the consumers, not to carry the tasks.","explanation":"Anti-pattern: using ZooKeeper as a message queue or high-throughput data plane"},
  {"id":"hi-zookeeper-30","topic":"key-technologies · zookeeper","type":"two-choice","question":"In a system design interview, your design needs exactly one active job scheduler with automatic failover. Which answer plays better with the interviewer?","optionA":"Sketch a custom leader-election protocol built on database heartbeat rows and timestamps","optionB":"Delegate to an off-the-shelf coordination service like ZooKeeper or etcd and spend the time on your core design","correct":"B","explanation":"Hand-rolling consensus is subtly error-prone and a known red flag; interviewers want you to recognize coordination as a solved problem and reach for a battle-tested tool."},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-01","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A photo-sharing app must avoid re-showing any of its 10 billion posts to users who already saw them. Each of 400 million users has a 'seen set' of post IDs in Redis, and memory costs are exploding. An engineer proposes simply adding more Redis shards. What class of solution should the team consider instead, and what do they give up?","answer":"Replace each exact seen-set with a probabilistic structure like a per-user Bloom filter, which shrinks memory by orders of magnitude. The cost is a small, tunable error rate — occasionally a never-seen post is wrongly treated as seen. Sharding just spreads the same enormous footprint across more machines without shrinking it.","explanation":"When specialized big-data structures are warranted: trading exactness for sublinear memory"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-02","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"In a mock system design interview for a URL shortener expected to hold about 100,000 links, the candidate proposes a Bloom filter to check whether a short code is already taken. The interviewer frowns. Why does this proposal hurt the candidate?","answer":"At 100k entries, an exact hash table or a unique database index is trivially small and gives exact answers. Reaching for a probabilistic structure when a plain lookup suffices signals over-engineering and poor judgment about scale — exactly the red flag interviewers watch for.","explanation":"Over-engineering red flag: probabilistic structure where an exact hash table suffices"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-03","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"two-choice","question":"For most system design interview candidates, studying probabilistic data structures like Bloom filters and HyperLogLog should come before mastering caching, load balancing, and partitioning.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"The fundamentals (caching, load balancing, partitioning) appear in nearly every interview and give far higher return; specialized structures are an advanced layer that only helps once the core is solid."},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-04","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"An email marketing platform built a Bloom filter over its suppression list using 7 hash functions. A junior engineer wrote the lookup to report 'suppressed' if ANY of the 7 computed bit positions is set. Soon nearly every send is being blocked. What is the bug?","answer":"A Bloom filter membership test must require ALL k bits to be set; if even one is 0 the element is definitely absent. Testing 'any bit set' turns almost every query into a hit once the array has a moderate number of 1s, exploding the false positive rate.","explanation":"Bloom filter query mechanics: membership requires ALL k bits set"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-05","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A ticketing company loads a Bloom filter of already-redeemed ticket codes onto offline gate scanners. Occasionally a valid, never-scanned ticket is rejected at the gate. The fraud team asks: can the reverse happen — a truly redeemed ticket sailing through as unused?","answer":"Not for any code that was actually in the filter: Bloom filters have no false negatives, so every inserted (redeemed) code will always be flagged. The rejections of valid tickets are false positives — the one-sided error Bloom filters accept in exchange for tiny memory.","explanation":"Bloom filter guarantee asymmetry: false positives possible, false negatives impossible"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-06","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A news aggregator deduplicates 1 billion article URLs with a Bloom filter and needs roughly a 1% false positive rate. To save memory, an engineer allocates a 1-billion-bit array — one bit per URL. Will this hit the target, and what allocation is actually needed?","answer":"No — at 1 bit per element the array becomes almost entirely 1s and the false positive rate approaches useless levels. A ~1% FPR needs on the order of 10 bits per element with around 7 hash functions, so roughly 10 billion bits (~1.25 GB), which is still tiny versus storing a billion URLs.","explanation":"Bloom filter sizing rule of thumb: ~10 bits per element for ~1% FPR"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-07","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A fintech risk team sized a Bloom filter for 100 million device fingerprints. Two years later it has absorbed 800 million, and the 'device seen before' check now fires for nearly every brand-new device. What happened, and what is the fix?","answer":"The filter is saturated: far past its design capacity, most bits are set, so almost any query looks like a hit. Bloom filters cannot grow in place — the team must rebuild a larger filter sized for current volume (or adopt a scalable/layered filter scheme that adds capacity in tiers).","explanation":"Bloom filter saturation: FPR degrades past design capacity, no in-place growth"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-08","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A chat app keeps a Bloom filter of usernames each user has blocked. When someone unblocks a user, the code zeroes out the bits that username hashed to. Users now report that other, unrelated people they blocked can suddenly message them. Why, and what should the team use instead?","answer":"Bits in a Bloom filter are shared across elements, so clearing one element's bits silently deletes others and creates false negatives. Standard Bloom filters do not support deletion — use a counting Bloom filter (increment/decrement small counters) or periodically rebuild the filter from the source of truth.","explanation":"Bloom filter deletion limitation: shared bits, counting variant"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-09","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A startup's web crawler tracks visited URLs to avoid refetching. Storing every URL in a database set is dominating storage, but the team hesitates to use a Bloom filter because 'we might skip a page we never actually crawled.' Is that trade acceptable here, and why?","answer":"Yes. A ~1% false positive rate means skipping a tiny fraction of never-crawled pages, which is a negligible, recoverable loss at web scale, while memory drops from full URL strings to a few bits per URL. Crucially there are no false negatives, so the crawler never wastes work refetching a page it already has.","explanation":"Bloom filter use case: web-crawl dedup where false positives are acceptable"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-10","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A CDN operator finds that about half the objects in its edge caches were requested exactly once and never again, and these one-off objects keep evicting genuinely popular content. What admission technique should they adopt, and how does a Bloom filter enable it?","answer":"Cache only on the second request: record every requested URL in a Bloom filter, and admit an object to the cache only if the filter already contains it (proving a prior request). One-hit wonders never get cached, so eviction pressure on hot content drops dramatically at the cost of a few filter bits per URL.","explanation":"Bloom filter use case: one-hit-wonder cache admission (cache on second request)"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-11","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"An IoT time-series database stores data in immutable sorted files on disk (LSM-style). Point lookups for keys that do not exist are painfully slow because every file must be checked. How do production LSM engines make these negative lookups cheap?","answer":"Each on-disk file carries a small in-memory Bloom filter of its keys; a lookup consults the filter first and skips any file that reports 'definitely not present.' Only the false-positive fraction of lookups incurs a wasted disk read, turning most misses into pure memory operations.","explanation":"Bloom filter use case: skipping pointless storage reads in LSM engines"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-12","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"An ad-tech pipeline tracks impression counts per campaign in a count-min sketch with 5 hash rows. When reading a count, an engineer takes the MAXIMUM of the 5 counters 'to be safe,' and the reported numbers run wildly high. What should the read do instead, and why?","answer":"Read the MINIMUM across the rows — that is the 'min' in count-min. Every counter can only be inflated by hash collisions, never deflated, so the smallest of the d counters is the tightest estimate; taking the max deliberately picks the most collision-polluted value.","explanation":"Count-min sketch query mechanics: take the minimum across rows"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-13","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A music streaming service uses a count-min sketch of play counts to trigger royalty payouts once a track passes 10,000 plays. Legal asks: could an artist whose track genuinely crossed 10,000 plays ever be missed by this check?","answer":"No — count-min sketch error is strictly one-sided: collisions only add to counters, so a true count is never underestimated and every genuine threshold-crosser will register as crossed. The actual risk runs the other way: some tracks below 10,000 may appear over it and trigger early payouts.","explanation":"Count-min sketch one-sided error: never underestimates"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-14","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A mobile game store estimates item purchase frequencies with a count-min sketch and finds every estimate biased noticeably high. The team doubles the number of hash rows (depth), but the inflation barely changes. Which dimension should they have grown, and what does each dimension actually control?","answer":"They should widen the sketch: the expected overestimate scales with total stream volume divided by the width, so more columns means fewer collisions per counter. Depth only shrinks the probability of getting an unusually bad estimate — it cannot reduce the systematic collision bias that width controls.","explanation":"Count-min sketch tuning: width controls error magnitude, depth controls error probability"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-15","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A social platform wants a live 'top 10 trending hashtags' widget and has a count-min sketch over the hashtag stream. An engineer discovers there is no way to ask the sketch 'which hashtags are biggest.' What is missing from the design?","answer":"A count-min sketch only answers point queries for keys you already name — it cannot enumerate its contents. The standard pattern pairs it with a small min-heap: as each hashtag streams in, query its estimated count and update the heap of the current top-K candidates, so the heap holds the answer at all times.","explanation":"Count-min sketch cannot enumerate keys: pair with a heap for top-K"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-16","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce team reuses its count-min sketch of product views to build a report covering every product, including obscure ones viewed a handful of times. The rare products show absurdly inflated counts relative to reality. Why is the sketch trustworthy for bestsellers but not for these items?","answer":"The sketch's error is an absolute amount proportional to total stream size, not relative to each item's count. That noise floor is negligible against a bestseller's millions of views but can dwarf a true count of five, so count-min sketches are only reliable for heavy hitters — never for the long tail.","explanation":"Count-min sketch pitfall: absolute error swamps long-tail items"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-17","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A SaaS platform's in-memory cache must decide, on every eviction, whether the incoming key is likely more valuable than the victim it would displace — but keeping an exact access counter for every key in the huge keyspace is off the table. What compact design solves this?","answer":"Keep a count-min sketch of recent access frequencies over the whole request stream (the TinyLFU approach). On admission, compare the sketch's frequency estimate for the incoming key against the victim's and only admit if the newcomer is likelier to be popular — near-LFU quality decisions with a few megabytes of counters.","explanation":"Count-min sketch use case: frequency-based cache admission (TinyLFU-style)"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-18","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A web analytics SaaS shows 'unique visitors today' for each of its customers' 50 million pages. Storing a set of visitor IDs per page to dedupe repeat views is consuming terabytes. What structure fits, and what does the memory-accuracy trade look like?","answer":"A HyperLogLog per page: each one occupies a fixed few kilobytes yet estimates distinct visitors within roughly 1-2% error even into the billions. The trade is exactness for a fixed tiny footprint — perfect for dashboard counts where 2% error is invisible.","explanation":"HyperLogLog purpose: distinct counts without storing the set"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-19","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A data engineer at a video platform is skeptical: 'How can a 12KB structure count a billion unique viewers? It can't possibly remember who it saw.' What is the core statistical intuition that makes this work?","answer":"It doesn't remember anyone — it hashes each ID to a uniform random value and tracks the rarest bit pattern observed, like the longest run of leading zeros. Seeing n leading zeros is a 1-in-2^n event, so the rarest pattern witnessed implies roughly how many distinct values must have been hashed.","explanation":"HyperLogLog intuition: rare leading-zero patterns imply high cardinality"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-20","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"An engineer implements a naive cardinality counter that tracks only the single maximum leading-zero run over the whole stream. The estimates swing wildly — one dataset reads 4x too high after a single 'lucky' hash. How does HyperLogLog fix this instability?","answer":"One lucky hash dominates a single-register estimate. HyperLogLog splits the stream into many registers (using a hash prefix to pick the register) and combines their observations with a harmonic mean, which suppresses outlier registers — averaging thousands of independent estimates drives the error down to a percent or two.","explanation":"HyperLogLog variance reduction: registers plus harmonic mean"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-21","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"two-choice","question":"A HyperLogLog's memory footprint grows in proportion to the number of distinct elements it has counted.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"The register array is fixed-size regardless of cardinality; Redis's implementation uses about 12KB to count billions of distinct items with under 1% standard error."},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-22","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing app counts unique riders per city. Requests land on 40 API servers, so the team funnels every ride event through a queue to one central HyperLogLog — and the queue is now a bottleneck. How should they restructure the counting, and what property of HLL makes it safe?","answer":"Each server keeps its own local HLL and periodically ships it for merging. HLL union is lossless: taking the per-register maximum of two sketches yields exactly the sketch you'd get from one stream, so the same rider hitting different servers is still counted once. The central chokepoint disappears entirely.","explanation":"HyperLogLog mergeability: lossless union via per-register max"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-23","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"An API platform bills customers by the number of unique devices their campaigns reached, computed with HyperLogLog. A large customer audits their invoice and finds the count off by about 1.5%. Was HLL the right tool here?","answer":"No. HLL's built-in 1-2% error is fine for dashboards but indefensible when money changes hands — invoices must be exact and auditable. Billable metrics need exact counting (e.g., deduplicated records in a database), with HLL reserved for the analytics views.","explanation":"HyperLogLog pitfall: approximate counts unacceptable for billing"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-24","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A fintech's login API is being credential-stuffed from botnets. The security team wants an alert whenever an endpoint is hit by an abnormally high number of DISTINCT IP addresses within 5 minutes, but storing per-endpoint IP lists at their traffic volume is too expensive. What is the lightweight design?","answer":"Keep a HyperLogLog per endpoint per 5-minute window and alert when its cardinality estimate spikes above baseline. Distinct-IP counts cost a few KB per endpoint no matter the traffic, and a botnet's signature is precisely a surge in distinct sources — approximate counts are plenty for anomaly detection.","explanation":"HyperLogLog security use case: distinct-source cardinality spikes"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-25","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A travel-booking site is about to add a Redis cache in front of its hotel-details service. They know the request rate but have no idea how much memory to provision. How can a HyperLogLog run against production traffic answer the sizing question?","answer":"Stream the requested keys through an HLL (per hour or per day) to estimate the working set — how many distinct keys are actually touched in a window. Cache capacity should be sized to that distinct-key count times average object size, not to raw QPS, and HLL measures it for kilobytes of overhead.","explanation":"HyperLogLog cache sizing use case: working-set estimation"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-26","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A platform team computes exact p99 latency by logging every request duration to disk and sorting the full day's samples in a nightly batch job. Storage is ballooning and engineers only see yesterday's numbers. What should replace this, and what is the trade?","answer":"A streaming histogram or quantile sketch: it ingests each sample into a bounded-size structure and answers p50/p95/p99 continuously in real time. The trade is a small bounded error on the percentile values in exchange for constant memory instead of storing every sample.","explanation":"Approximate quantiles purpose: exact percentiles require storing all samples"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-27","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"An IoT platform histograms message-processing latency using 100 fixed buckets of 10ms each, spanning 0 to 1000ms. Most messages complete in under 5ms — so p50 just reads '0-10ms' — while rare 30-second stalls all pile into a single overflow bucket. What is the structural problem with this histogram?","answer":"Fixed-width buckets allocate resolution uniformly, but latency is heavily skewed: nearly all the mass lands in one bucket (no precision where it matters) while the interesting tail lies beyond the range entirely. Skewed or wide-ranging data needs exponential or adaptive bucket boundaries instead.","explanation":"Fixed-width buckets weakness: wasted resolution on skewed distributions"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-28","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"An observability vendor must report latency percentiles within a few percent of the true value whether the measurement is 1 millisecond or 30 seconds. Why do exponentially-spaced bucket boundaries (each boundary a constant multiple of the last) meet this requirement where fixed-width buckets cannot?","answer":"With exponential boundaries every bucket spans the same ratio, so the worst-case error is a constant percentage of the value at every scale — a few percent at 1ms and a few percent at 30s alike. Fixed-width buckets give absolute error, which is uselessly coarse at the low end or requires an absurd bucket count to cover the range.","explanation":"Exponential buckets: constant relative error across orders of magnitude"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-29","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A developer-tools company lets customers record arbitrary custom metrics — one metric might range 0-1, another 0-10 billion. Every preconfigured bucket layout they ship ends up wrong for someone. What histogram approach removes the need to know the distribution up front, and what is its cost?","answer":"A dynamic/adaptive histogram (t-digest style): it adjusts its bucket boundaries from the data itself, merging and splitting so precision concentrates where the mass and the tails actually are. The cost is extra implementation complexity and somewhat less predictable error bounds than fixed schemes.","explanation":"Dynamic histograms: bucket boundaries adapt to observed data"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-30","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A video streaming service runs 20 API servers, each reporting its own p99 latency. The dashboard displays the average of the 20 p99 values. During an incident where one server degrades badly, the graph barely moves. What is mathematically wrong with this dashboard, and what is the correct aggregation?","answer":"Percentiles are not averageable — the mean of per-server p99s is not the fleet's p99, and a single sick server is diluted 20-to-1. Each server must export its underlying histogram or quantile sketch; the aggregator merges those (histograms with identical bucket boundaries merge by summing counts) and computes p99 on the combined distribution.","explanation":"Percentiles cannot be averaged across servers: merge histograms instead"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-31","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A payments API has an SLO stating '99% of requests complete within 200ms.' An SRE sets up alerting on average latency crossing 200ms, reasoning it's simpler. Weeks later customers report widespread slow requests while no alert ever fired. Why did the alert design fail?","answer":"The SLO is a percentile guarantee, and the mean says nothing about it — average latency can sit at 50ms while 5% of requests take 2 seconds, blowing the SLO with no alert. Compliance must be measured by tracking p99 directly via a quantile sketch or histogram and alerting on that.","explanation":"SLO monitoring: percentile-defined objectives require percentile tracking"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-32","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A food delivery app A/B tests a rewritten checkout flow. Mean completion time is identical in both variants, so the team declares it neutral and ships variant B — then support tickets about checkout hangs spike. What did the analysis miss?","answer":"They compared only the means, which can match while the distributions differ wildly — B likely regressed the tail (p95/p99), hanging for a minority of users while being marginally faster for the rest. Experiment analysis should compare percentile profiles per variant, which quantile sketches make cheap.","explanation":"A/B testing: compare distributions and tails, not just means"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-33","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A multiplayer game backend autoscales when mean request latency crosses a threshold. Players consistently report lag spikes several minutes before scaling ever triggers. Why is the mean a lagging signal here, and what should drive scaling instead?","answer":"Under rising load, queuing hurts the slowest requests first: p95/p99 degrade sharply while the mean stays diluted by the fast majority. By the time the mean moves, users have been suffering for minutes. Scale on tail percentiles — they are the leading indicator of saturation.","explanation":"Autoscaling: tail latency leads mean latency as a saturation signal"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-34","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A checkout service sits behind a load balancer that spreads requests round-robin across 12 identical app servers and health-checks each with a simple ping. One server's failing disk makes 5% of its requests take 8 seconds, yet its pings succeed and its average latency looks normal, so it keeps receiving full traffic. What should the load balancer track per backend to catch this, and why does its current signal miss it?","answer":"Track each backend's latency distribution — a per-backend histogram or quantile sketch — and drain or route around any instance whose p95/p99 diverges from the fleet. Pings and the mean both stay healthy because 95% of requests are fine; only tail percentiles expose an instance that is slow for a meaningful minority of its traffic.","explanation":"Load balancing on tail latency: per-backend histograms expose degraded instances that averages hide"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-35","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"In a system design interview about a video platform, a candidate is asked to count unique video viewers and immediately answers 'HyperLogLog' with no other discussion. The interviewer keeps probing skeptically. What stronger narrative should the candidate have used?","answer":"Start with the exact approach — a deduplicating set per video — then do the memory math showing it collapses at billions of viewer-video pairs, confirm that small error is tolerable for a view counter, and only then introduce HLL as the justified trade. Naming the structure without the reasoning reads as buzzword recall, not judgment.","explanation":"Interview narrative: exact solution first, then justify the sketch"},
  {"id":"hi-data-structures-for-big-data-36","topic":"advanced-topics · data structures for big data","type":"open","question":"A search team needs to report how many DISTINCT queries were issued today. One engineer proposes a count-min sketch since 'it's the counting sketch.' Why is that the wrong tool, and what is the right way to pick among these structures?","answer":"Count-min answers 'how many times did THIS item occur,' not 'how many different items occurred' — distinct counting is HyperLogLog's job. Pick by question shape: membership tests want a Bloom filter, per-item frequency wants count-min, distinct counts want HLL, and percentiles want a quantile sketch.","explanation":"Matching the structure to the question: membership vs frequency vs cardinality vs quantiles"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-01","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A real-estate site runs 'homes within 2 km of this point' queries against Postgres. An engineer adds a B-tree index on the latitude column, but the query is barely faster and still touches hundreds of thousands of rows. What is going wrong?","answer":"The latitude index only narrows results to a horizontal strip circling the entire globe — longitude is completely unconstrained. The database must still compute distance for every row in that strip, so a 1-D index cannot answer a 2-D closeness question.","explanation":"Single-column lat/lon index only narrows to a 1-D strip"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-02","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery startup 'fixes' its slow nearby-restaurant query by replacing the latitude index with a composite index on (latitude, longitude). Latency barely improves in dense cities. Why didn't the composite index solve it?","answer":"A composite index still sorts primarily by its first column, so it only narrows the latitude range; within that strip the longitude values for nearby points are scattered throughout the index. One-dimensional sort order cannot preserve two-dimensional closeness, so the DB still distance-checks a huge candidate set.","explanation":"Composite (lat, lon) index still sorts on one dimension first"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-03","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A fitness app shows 'runners within 5 km'. The backend maps the user to a grid cell, fetches everyone indexed in matching cells, and returns them directly. Users complain some listed runners are 7 km away. What step did the team skip?","answer":"Post-filtering. A spatial index only turns 'check every row' into 'check a small candidate set' — cells are coarse, so matches can lie outside the radius. The team must compute exact distance on the candidates and drop those beyond 5 km.","explanation":"Spatial indexes yield candidate sets that must be post-filtered by exact distance"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-04","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A mapping SaaS stores points of interest: millions packed into Manhattan, almost none across rural Nevada. The team debates a fixed uniform grid versus a quadtree for their in-memory index. Which fits this data better, and why?","answer":"The quadtree. It recursively splits a region into four quadrants only when it holds too many points, so Manhattan gets deep fine-grained subdivision while empty Nevada stays as a few coarse cells — a uniform grid would either overload city cells or waste huge numbers of empty ones.","explanation":"Quadtree density-adaptive subdivision vs a uniform grid"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-05","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"An MMO game uses an in-memory quadtree to find players near each other. Ops notices p99 lookup latency in the crowded capital-city zone is several times worse than in sparse wilderness zones, with identical hardware. What explains the gap?","answer":"Dense regions force many more recursive splits, so the tree is much deeper there and lookups traverse more levels. Quadtree latency is inherently location-dependent: it tracks local data density, so the busiest regions are the slowest.","explanation":"Quadtree query latency varies with local data density"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-06","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A check-in app's quadtree keeps producing sibling leaves where one holds thousands of points and the other three are nearly empty, even after repeated splits over a clustered downtown area. Why does this happen, and which tree family avoids it?","answer":"Quadtrees always split a cell at its geometric midpoint, ignoring where the data actually sits, so a cluster near one corner keeps landing in a single quadrant. k-d trees avoid this by splitting at the data median, which halves the points at every level.","explanation":"Quadtrees split at the geometric midpoint, not the data median"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-07","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A telematics team's quadtree outgrew RAM, so they serialized it to SSD and page in nodes on demand. Query throughput collapses, with the profiler showing the time going to storage reads. What is the fundamental problem?","answer":"A quadtree is a pointer-chasing structure whose nodes scatter across pages, so every level of descent risks a random disk read. Quadtrees belong in memory (map tiles, game engines); on disk you want a page-oriented structure like an R-tree or BKD tree.","explanation":"Pointer-based quadtrees perform poorly on disk"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-08","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A warehouse-robotics team needs an in-memory index for nearest-neighbor lookups over robot positions that cluster heavily around loading docks. They want predictable O(log n) lookup depth no matter how skewed the clustering gets. Should they pick a quadtree or a k-d tree, and why?","answer":"A k-d tree. It alternates split dimensions (x, y, x, y...) and cuts at the data median each time, so the tree stays balanced at O(log n) depth even under extreme clustering — unlike a quadtree, whose depth blows up wherever data is dense.","explanation":"k-d trees stay balanced via alternating-dimension median splits regardless of skew"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-09","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce team already runs Elasticsearch for product search and adds a 'stores near you' feature with a geo_point field. It performs well over their nightly-reindexed store catalog with no spatial database involved. What index structure is doing the geo work, and why does it suit this workload?","answer":"Elasticsearch indexes geo_point fields with BKD trees — disk-optimized block k-d trees that pack points into page-sized blocks and are built in batches. Store locations rarely change and are reindexed in bulk, which matches BKD's write-once, batch-built design perfectly.","explanation":"Elasticsearch geo_point is backed by BKD trees"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-10","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A scooter-share company pushes every scooter's GPS position into an Elasticsearch geo_point index every three seconds so riders can search nearby scooters. Indexing costs balloon and segment merges never stop. What is the mismatch?","answer":"BKD-backed geo indexes are essentially write-once and built from batches; constant per-scooter updates force Elasticsearch into perpetual segment rewriting and merging. Live, fast-moving positions belong in an encoded-cell column (H3/geohash) on a store built for cheap scalar updates.","explanation":"Write-once BKD indexes are wrong for high-churn live locations"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-11","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A logistics platform must index millions of irregular road segments and delivery-zone polygons — not points — and an engineer asks how any tree could organize shapes of wildly different sizes and forms. What is the R-tree's answer to this?","answer":"Wrap every shape in its minimum bounding rectangle, then group nearby rectangles into progressively larger enclosing rectangles, forming a balanced tree. Queries compare simple rectangles at every level and only touch the real geometry at the leaves.","explanation":"R-trees index arbitrary geometry via minimum bounding rectangles"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-12","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A GIS SaaS on PostGIS sees region queries slow down as data grows; tracing shows each query descending many subtrees instead of one clean path. The bounding rectangles in the index overlap heavily. Why does overlap cause this, and what variant addresses it?","answer":"When bounding rectangles overlap, a query region can fall inside several siblings at once, so the search must descend every matching branch. R*-trees use smarter insertion heuristics that minimize overlap, keeping most queries on few paths.","explanation":"MBR overlap forces multi-branch descent; R*-tree heuristics reduce it"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-13","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A fleet-tracking startup stores live truck positions as PostGIS geometries with a GiST index and updates each of 80,000 trucks every five seconds. Write throughput tanks and vacuum can't keep up. What is going wrong?","answer":"Every position change is an R-tree index update that can split and rebalance rectangle pages, so constant movement turns the index into a write bottleneck. R-trees are built for geometry that mostly sits still; fast-moving points should be snapped to encoded cells instead.","explanation":"R-tree updates trigger splits/rebalancing, making moving objects expensive"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-14","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A team building an offline navigation device must index a large, mostly static set of map polygons on flash storage with very little RAM, using an embedded database like SQLite. Quadtree or R-tree — which fits, and why?","answer":"The R-tree. It was designed for disk: page-sized nodes, balanced depth, and B-tree-style page splits mean a lookup costs a handful of page reads, which is why SQLite, PostGIS, and Oracle Spatial all ship R-tree-family indexes. A pointer-based quadtree would thrash storage with random reads.","explanation":"R-trees are disk-native: balanced depth and page-sized nodes"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-15","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A fintech's ATM-locator runs on a managed MySQL where installing spatial extensions is not allowed. The team assumes nearby-search is impossible without PostGIS. What approach lets them ship anyway?","answer":"Encode each ATM's lat/lon into a single sortable cell ID (geohash, S2, or H3) stored in a normal indexed column. Nearby-search becomes an ordinary index lookup over a set of cell values plus an exact-distance post-filter — no spatial extension required.","explanation":"Encoded keys let an ordinary B-tree index answer proximity queries"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-16","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"In a design review, a senior engineer objects to a geohash proposal: the team already established that one-dimensional B-tree order cannot preserve two-dimensional closeness, yet geohash sorts every location into a single ordered key and range-scans it. How is the contradiction resolved?","answer":"Geohash interleaves the latitude and longitude bits, tracing a space-filling curve — a single line threaded through 2-D space so points close on the map usually stay close along the line. Unlike a lat-then-lon sort, a few short range scans over that ordering cover a neighborhood, and the ring-plus-post-filter step patches the places where the curve jumps.","explanation":"Space-filling curves let 1-D range scans approximate 2-D closeness"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-17","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A 'people nearby' feature in a chat app stores each user's geohash string and finds neighbors with WHERE geohash LIKE 'gbsuv%'. A new engineer asks why matching a string prefix has anything to do with physical distance. What is the explanation?","answer":"Geohash is hierarchical: each added character subdivides the parent cell, so two points share a prefix only if they landed in the same cell at every level the prefix covers. A prefix match therefore means both points sit inside the same bounded region, making prefix scans a cell-containment query.","explanation":"Geohash shared prefixes indicate same-cell containment at every covered level"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-18","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"To widen a too-narrow search, an engineer truncates the query geohash from 6 characters to 5 and is startled that the query cell's area jumps by more than an order of magnitude rather than roughly doubling. What does a single geohash character actually encode?","answer":"Each character is 5 bits selecting one of 32 sub-cells of its parent, so geohash is a base-32 recursive grid. Dropping one character steps up a whole level, merging 32 finer cells into one — which is also why the encoding packs efficiently into integers, like Redis's 52-bit representation.","explanation":"Each geohash character encodes 5 bits, subdividing its parent cell into 32"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-19","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A ticketing app searches for venues within roughly 5 km of the user and stores geohashes at 9 characters, querying by exact 9-character cell values. Searches are slow because the app must enumerate enormous numbers of cells. What should the team change?","answer":"Query at a precision matched to the radius: about 5 geohash characters gives ~5 km cells, so a handful of cells covers the search area — and the stored 9-character hashes still match those coarser cells by prefix. Nine characters is ~5 m precision, absurdly fine for a 5 km radius, forcing the app to enumerate millions of tiny cells.","explanation":"Choosing geohash query precision to match the search radius"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-20","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"Two users of a dating app live directly across the street from each other, well inside the app's 1 km match radius, yet the app never surfaces them to each other. The backend looks up matches by the user's own geohash cell. What is going wrong?","answer":"They straddle a geohash cell boundary. Points meters apart on opposite sides of a boundary can have completely different prefixes, so a naive query against only the user's own cell misses genuinely close neighbors.","explanation":"Geohash boundary problem: adjacent points across a cell edge share no prefix"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-21","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A travel app's coffee-shop finder misses shops just across cell boundaries. An engineer proposes fixing it by switching to a finer geohash precision so the cells are smaller. Why won't that work, and what is the standard production fix?","answer":"Smaller cells still have boundaries — the miss just moves closer in. The standard fix is the 3x3 ring query: fetch the query cell plus its eight neighbors, then post-filter candidates by exact distance.","explanation":"The 3x3 ring query is the fix for boundary misses, not smaller cells"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-22","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A courier platform uses Redis GEOADD and GEOSEARCH for live courier positions. During a design review, someone claims Redis must maintain a special spatial tree internally to make this work. Is that right — what does Redis actually do?","answer":"No special structure. GEOADD interleaves latitude and longitude into a 52-bit geohash integer and stores it as a sorted-set score; geo queries are just sorted-set range scans (ZRANGEBYSCORE-style) over geohash ranges, plus distance post-filtering.","explanation":"Redis geo = interleaved 52-bit geohash scored in a sorted set"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-23","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A maritime-tracking product works fine in the Mediterranean but behaves oddly for vessels near Svalbard: cells at the same precision cover wildly different areas and neighbor lookups act strangely. What property of geohash causes this?","answer":"Geohash treats the earth as a flat lat/lon rectangle, but a degree of longitude spans ~111 km at the equator and shrinks toward zero at the poles, so high-latitude cells become badly distorted slivers. For accurate global coverage, a spherical scheme like S2 is the right tool.","explanation":"Geohash's flat-earth projection distorts cells toward the poles"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-24","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A flight-tracking service needs cells of roughly consistent area everywhere on earth, including routes that cross the ±180° antimeridian and pass near the poles — places where their geohash prototype broke. Which encoding fits, and what makes it work?","answer":"S2. It projects the globe onto a cube's six faces, producing roughly equal-area cells everywhere and treating the surface spherically, so the antimeridian wrap and polar regions are handled correctly instead of being edge cases of a flat grid.","explanation":"S2 projects the sphere onto six cube faces for equal-area, antimeridian-safe cells"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-25","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A mobility-analytics team stores one 64-bit S2 cell ID per trip event (this is also what MongoDB's 2dsphere builds on). Product now wants roll-ups at city, district, and neighborhood granularity, and an engineer proposes adding three more columns, one per zoom level. Is that necessary?","answer":"No. S2 cell IDs are hierarchical: truncating the 64-bit ID yields the ancestor cell at a coarser level, so one stored ID supports aggregation at any resolution — just truncate to the target level at query time.","explanation":"Hierarchical 64-bit S2 IDs: truncation yields parent cells"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-26","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery company builds demand heat maps on a square grid and smooths each cell with its neighbors. The maps look directionally distorted because diagonal neighbors sit about 1.4x farther away than edge neighbors. Why does switching to H3 fix this?","answer":"Square cells have eight neighbors at two different distances, so uniform smoothing over them is geometrically uneven. H3's hexagons have exactly six neighbors, all equidistant, which makes ring-based smoothing and analytics uniform in every direction.","explanation":"Hexagons have six equidistant neighbors; squares have eight at two distances"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-27","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"An IoT team migrates from geohash to H3 and keeps their old query style: SELECT ... WHERE cell BETWEEN :low AND :high to grab a neighborhood of cells. Results are geographically wrong. What did they misunderstand about H3?","answer":"H3 does not use a space-filling curve, so numerically adjacent IDs are not geographically adjacent — prefix and range tricks from geohash don't transfer. Instead, use H3's cheap grid functions to compute the explicit ring of neighbor cells and query WHERE cell IN (that list).","explanation":"H3 has no space-filling curve — use grid math, not ID range scans"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-28","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing startup must match riders to nearby drivers while ingesting millions of driver location updates per second, without exotic database features. Sketch the standard cell-based dispatch design they should use.","answer":"Snap each driver to a fine H3 cell (~200 m), so every location update is a single indexed-integer column write. On a rider request, compute the rider's cell plus surrounding rings, run WHERE h3_cell IN (those IDs) as a plain index lookup, then post-filter candidates by exact distance — the design Uber built H3 for.","explanation":"The H3 dispatch pattern: snap to fine cells, integer writes, ring IN-list, post-filter"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-29","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A govtech SaaS stores parcels and zoning polygons and must answer 'which district contains this point' and 'which parcels intersect this proposed corridor'. One engineer wants to avoid PostGIS by tagging everything with H3 cells. Which approach fits, and why?","answer":"PostGIS with its R-tree-style GiST index. The workload is true geometry — containment and intersection over polygons — which a spatial tree answers natively, while cell IDs only crudely approximate shapes and would produce wrong or expensive answers at polygon edges.","explanation":"Decision rule: polygon/containment workloads belong on a spatial tree (PostGIS)"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-30","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"A pet-tracker company has two million GPS collars each reporting every ten seconds, and the app's only spatial query is 'pets near me right now'. The team debates PostGIS geometries versus a plain indexed H3-cell column. Which fits this workload better, and why?","answer":"The encoded-cell column. Each collar ping becomes a cheap single-integer update on an ordinary index — sustainable at millions of writes per second — whereas the workload has no polygon queries to justify a spatial tree and its per-update rebalancing cost.","explanation":"Decision rule: constantly moving points belong on encoded cells"},
  {"id":"hi-proximity-search-31","topic":"advanced-topics · proximity search","type":"open","question":"In a mock system design interview for a nearby-search feature, a candidate immediately answers 'just use H3' and moves on. The interviewer scores the answer poorly despite H3 being a reasonable choice. What did the candidate fail to demonstrate?","answer":"The reasoning: why ordinary 1-D indexes fail on 2-D data, that any spatial index only produces a candidate set needing exact-distance post-filtering, and the trade-off between spatial trees (geometry) and encoded cells (moving points). Interviewers reward that derivation far more than name-dropping a library.","explanation":"Interview tip: derive the reasoning before naming a technology"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-01","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A smart-thermostat startup is debating whether its telemetry justifies specialized storage. Devices report a temperature reading every 30 seconds, readings are never edited after they arrive, and the customer dashboard almost exclusively charts averages over the last few hours. Which properties of this workload make it a textbook time-series fit?","answer":"It has the defining traits: writes are appends that arrive in time order, existing points are never updated, and reads aggregate over recent time windows rather than fetching individual records. Every TSDB optimization — sequential appends, time partitioning, delta compression, downsampling — assumes exactly this shape.","explanation":"Recognizing a time-series workload: append-only time-ordered writes, no updates, recent-window aggregate reads"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-02","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing platform tracks driver telemetry by keeping one row per driver-metric pair and running an UPDATE with the newest value every five seconds across 400,000 drivers. Disk utilization is pinned at 100% while actual throughput is a small fraction of what the hardware's sequential write specs suggest. What is going wrong?","answer":"Update-in-place forces random I/O: every write must locate the row on disk, read it, modify it, and write it back, and disks handle orders of magnitude fewer random operations than sequential ones — a spinning disk manages roughly 100-200 random operations per second versus tens of thousands sequential. Appending new points to the end of a file instead would use the disk's full sequential throughput.","explanation":"Update-in-place requires random read-modify-write I/O"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-03","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"An e-commerce company logs order-processing metrics into a Postgres table with B-tree indexes on timestamp, host, and metric_name. Inserts were fast at launch, but after months of growth insert throughput has fallen sharply, even though rows are small and arrive in time order. Why is ingestion slowing down?","answer":"Every insert must also update all three B-tree indexes, whose pages sit at scattered disk locations, and that maintenance cost grows as the indexes grow. The heap insert isn't the bottleneck — the index upkeep is.","explanation":"Secondary B-tree index maintenance degrades insert throughput"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-04","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A logistics firm stores GPS pings as rows of (timestamp, vehicle_name, metric_name, value). The real information per ping is roughly a timestamp plus a float — about 16 bytes — yet each stored row consumes 60-100 bytes, and storage bills run several times the forecast. Where is the space going, and how does a time-series engine avoid it?","answer":"Each row repeats the full vehicle and metric identifier strings. A time-series engine factors those identifiers into a series key stored once, keeping only compressed timestamps and values in the data blocks.","explanation":"Row stores repeat metadata per row; TSDBs store the series key once"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-05","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A SaaS analytics vendor enforces 30-day retention with a nightly DELETE ... WHERE ts < now() - 30 days on a giant metrics table. The job runs for hours, thrashes the indexes, and the table keeps growing anyway. What should the team change, and why?","answer":"Partition the data by time and expire old data by dropping whole partitions. Dropping a partition is a near-instant file-level operation, while row-level DELETEs must scan, update every index, and suffer write amplification.","explanation":"Retention: expensive row DELETEs vs dropping whole time partitions"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-06","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A chat-infrastructure team is building a custom LSM-style store. A developer proposes making the memtable an unsorted append-only array, arguing that keeping it sorted wastes CPU on the write path. What breaks downstream if they do this?","answer":"Flushes would produce unsorted files, ruining binary-search point lookups, efficient range scans, and merge-sort-based compaction. A sorted memtable (red-black tree or skip list) makes the flush a fast sequential write of an already-sorted SSTable.","explanation":"Memtables are kept sorted so flushes yield sorted, mergeable SSTables"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-07","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"An ad-tech pipeline discovers that a batch of impression metrics written an hour ago is wrong. An engineer proposes opening the on-disk SSTable and patching the bad values in place. Why is that not how the engine works, and what is the correct route?","answer":"SSTables are immutable — that is what keeps writes sequential and files safe for concurrent readers and compaction. Corrections are written as new entries (or tombstones for deletes), and background compaction merges them so the newest version wins.","explanation":"SSTables are immutable; changes arrive as new entries reconciled by compaction"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-08","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"To save CPU, a video-streaming ops team disables background compaction on their metrics store. Ingest keeps working, but over the following weeks read latency climbs steadily and disk usage grows faster than the ingest rate explains. What is happening?","answer":"Without compaction, small SSTables pile up, so every read must consult more and more files, and duplicate entries and tombstones are never purged. Compaction is the background merge that keeps file count and dead data bounded without blocking ingestion.","explanation":"Compaction merges SSTables, removes duplicates/tombstones, and bounds read fan-out"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-09","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"two-choice","question":"LSM trees deliver their high write throughput without giving anything up in return.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"The costs are write amplification (the same data is rewritten repeatedly during compaction) and read overhead, since a lookup may have to check the memtable plus several SSTables."},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-10","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A payments-monitoring service accepts writes into an in-memory buffer and only flushes to disk files every few minutes. The node crashes between flushes, yet after restart no acknowledged data points are missing. Which mechanism made that possible, and how?","answer":"The write-ahead log: every point is appended to the WAL on disk before being acknowledged, so on restart the engine replays the WAL to rebuild the buffer contents that never made it into an SSTable.","explanation":"WAL provides crash durability for unflushed in-memory data"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-11","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A weather platform stores barometric pressure as whole pascals — values near 101,325 that drift by only a few units between samples. An engineer switches from storing each raw 4-byte reading to storing the difference from the previous reading, encoded as a variable-length integer, and files shrink several-fold. Why does this combination work?","answer":"Adjacent readings differ by tiny amounts, so the deltas are small integers, and varint encoding stores a small number in a single byte where the raw value needed several. Neither trick alone suffices: deltas in fixed-width slots save nothing, and varints on the large raw values stay multi-byte.","explanation":"Delta encoding plus varint shrinks slowly-changing integer values"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-12","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"An uptime-monitoring agent reports a heartbeat exactly every 10 seconds. The storage engine records the timestamps of millions of these heartbeats using almost no space. What encoding achieves this, and why does it work here?","answer":"Delta-of-delta encoding: the gaps between timestamps are all 10, so the differences between successive gaps are all zero, which compresses to essentially nothing. Facebook's Gorilla paper showed this averages as low as 1 bit per timestamp for regular data.","explanation":"Delta-of-delta encoding makes regular timestamps nearly free"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-13","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A fitness wearable streams heart-rate values that change gradually. Its storage engine XORs each float with the previous value before writing. Why would XOR-ing consecutive measurements help compression?","answer":"Consecutive similar floats share the sign, exponent, and most mantissa bits, so their XOR is mostly zero bits, which run-length encodes down to just the few meaningful bits — about 1.37 bytes per 8-byte value on typical time-series data.","explanation":"XOR compression exploits bit-similarity of consecutive floats"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-14","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A metrics engine buffers points from thousands of servers, and instead of flushing them as one big time-ordered stream, it groups points by series key and writes each series into its own block. A new engineer argues a single time-sorted file would be simpler. What would that simplification cost?","answer":"Delta-of-delta and XOR encodings exploit the similarity between consecutive points of the same series; interleaving thousands of unrelated series makes adjacent values arbitrary, gutting the compression. Per-series blocks also keep each series contiguous, so a query reads only the blocks for the series it needs.","explanation":"Per-series columnar block layout is what makes delta and XOR encodings effective"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-15","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A ticketing platform stores purchase events in a time-series engine: timestamps arrive in irregular bursts and the recorded values (seat prices) jump around arbitrarily. The team is disappointed that compression is barely better than a plain row store. Why did the advertised compression not materialize?","answer":"Delta-of-delta and XOR compression bank on regular timestamps and small value deltas. Irregular event times and erratic values produce large noisy deltas, so the specialized encodings have nothing to exploit.","explanation":"Compression gains depend on regular timestamps and smooth values"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-16","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A food-delivery company sharded its metrics files by hash of metric name, so every incoming batch touches nearly every file. After switching to partitioning by time window, ingest concentrates on a single active file and throughput improves. What property of time partitioning explains this?","answer":"Write locality: incoming data always belongs to the current time partition, so ingest appends to one hot partition instead of scattering writes across many files.","explanation":"Time partitioning gives write locality — all ingest hits the current partition"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-17","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A social-feed analytics dashboard mostly runs 'last hour' queries. After moving the metrics store to daily time partitions, those queries stop scanning 90 days of data and touch only the newest partition. What is the engine doing?","answer":"Partition pruning: each partition covers a known time window, so the planner intersects the query's time range with partition boundaries and skips every partition that cannot contain matching data.","explanation":"Query-time partition pruning by time range"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-18","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A B2B monitoring SaaS is choosing a partition size. One engineer wants per-minute partitions for maximum pruning; another wants per-year partitions to keep the file count down. Typical queries span a few hours and retention is 90 days. What is wrong with each extreme?","answer":"Per-minute partitions force a few-hour query to open hundreds of files and drown in partition metadata; per-year partitions destroy pruning and make 90-day retention impossible to enforce by dropping partitions. A middle size like daily matches both the query span and the retention granularity.","explanation":"Partition sizing trade-off between file count and pruning/retention granularity"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-19","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"two-choice","question":"An SSTable's Bloom filter can occasionally cause a query to miss data that really is present in the file.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"Bloom filters never produce false negatives, so a 'definitely not here' answer is always safe to act on. The only error mode is a false positive: an occasional wasted disk read when the filter says 'maybe' but the key is absent."},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-20","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A hotel-booking company's metrics store accumulates dozens of SSTables per partition, and each series lookup was issuing a disk read against nearly every file. The team adds a small in-memory structure alongside each SSTable, and lookups now read only one or two files. What did they add, and how does it help?","answer":"A Bloom filter per SSTable. Before reading a file the engine asks the filter whether the key might be present; a 'definitely not' answer skips the file with zero I/O, collapsing dozens of potential reads into one or two.","explanation":"Per-SSTable Bloom filters skip disk reads on lookups"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-21","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"two-choice","question":"Roughly how much memory does a well-tuned Bloom filter need per key to keep its false-positive rate around 1%?","optionA":"About 10 bits per key","optionB":"About 10 bytes per key","correct":"A","explanation":"That budget is the standard tuning point that yields roughly a 1% false-positive rate."},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-22","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"An energy-grid operator must keep a year of meter data, but dashboards need fine detail only for recent incidents; beyond a week, analysts look at broad trends. Storing everything at 10-second resolution for a year is blowing the storage budget. What scheme should they adopt?","answer":"Tiered retention: keep raw 10-second data for a short recent window, then roll older data up to 1-minute, 5-minute, and eventually 1-hour averages as it ages. Trends survive at a tiny fraction of the storage — an hourly rollup is hundreds of times smaller than raw.","explanation":"Tiered retention via downsampling (raw recent data, coarser rollups with age)"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-23","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"An uptime-monitoring vendor's rollup job stores only the average latency per day. When a customer requests a monthly average, engineering averages the 30 daily averages — and the result is wrong whenever days have different sample counts. Peak-latency queries are impossible entirely. What should the rollups have stored?","answer":"Per-interval sum, count, min, and max. Sums and counts recombine into correct averages over any window, and min/max preserve the extremes that a bare average destroys.","explanation":"Rollups should precompute recombinable aggregates (sum, count, min, max), not just averages"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-24","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A team configured their metrics store to keep only 1-hour averages for anything older than 30 days. Two months later, an incident review needs to examine a 30-second CPU spike from that period — and it is simply not there. What limitation did they hit?","answer":"Downsampling is a one-way trade of precision for storage: once raw points are rolled up and discarded, sub-interval detail is gone forever. A 30-second spike disappears inside an hourly average.","explanation":"Downsampling permanently trades precision on old data"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-25","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"In a system design interview, the interviewer states that metrics must be kept at 10-second resolution for one full year. The candidate silently designs storage for exactly that. What stronger move did the candidate miss?","answer":"Push back on the requirement: propose full resolution for a recent window and coarser rollups for older data, since year-old 10-second detail is rarely useful. Even if the interviewer declines, questioning rigid requirements is the kind of judgment that marks a staff-level candidate.","explanation":"Interview move: challenge rigid full-resolution retention requirements with rollups"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-26","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A smart-factory dashboard queries 'show intervals where vibration exceeded 90'. The storage engine answers after decompressing only a handful of data blocks, even though the partition holds thousands. What allowed it to ignore the rest?","answer":"Each block carries metadata such as its min/max values and timestamps; any block whose max is below 90 cannot contain a match and is pruned without ever being read or decompressed. It stacks on top of time-based partition pruning as another filtering layer.","explanation":"Block-level min/max metadata prunes blocks without reading them"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-27","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A smart-home platform models temperature readings with room as a field and the temperature value as a tag. Filtering 'all readings for the kitchen' is slow, and the series count grows with every distinct temperature ever recorded. What did they get backwards?","answer":"They inverted tags and fields. Tags are indexed metadata you filter by (room); fields are the measured values (temperature). With temperature as a tag, every distinct reading spawns a new series, while the attribute they actually filter on has no index.","explanation":"Tags are indexed filter metadata; fields are the unindexed measured values"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-28","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"An APM vendor's store held 50,000 series (1,000 hosts times 50 metrics). After adding one new tag, env, with five possible values, the series count jumped to roughly 250,000. Why did a single tag multiply the total?","answer":"Each unique combination of tag values defines its own series, so the series count is the product of tag cardinalities: 1,000 hosts times 50 metrics times 5 envs. Adding a tag multiplies the count, it does not add to it.","explanation":"A series is one unique combination of measurement and tag values"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-29","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A CDN monitoring query filters on region='eu' AND tier='edge'. The engine identifies exactly which series match before touching disk at all. What structure makes this possible, and how is the AND evaluated?","answer":"An in-memory inverted index — the same structure that powers Elasticsearch, but mapping tag values to series instead of words to documents. The engine fetches the series list for each predicate and intersects them, so only the surviving series' blocks are ever read from disk.","explanation":"Tag index is an in-memory inverted index; multi-tag queries intersect series lists"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-30","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A live-ops gaming dashboard queries the last five minutes of match metrics and gets complete results, even though the storage engine has not flushed anything to disk in ten minutes. Where did the recent points come from?","answer":"Queries merge data from the in-memory buffer with the on-disk files. The newest points are served straight from the memtable; only older time ranges require reading disk.","explanation":"Reads merge the in-memory buffer with on-disk files"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-31","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A climate-research portal computes the yearly mean of a sensor that produced hundreds of millions of points, on a query node with a few gigabytes of RAM, without running out of memory. How can the engine do this?","answer":"It aggregates in streaming fashion: as blocks are read it maintains running state (a sum and a count for a mean) and discards the points, so memory use stays constant no matter how much data the query spans.","explanation":"Aggregations are computed streaming, not by materializing all points"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-32","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"Profiling a time-series storage engine shows that fetching one series from a data file costs about two disk seeks — whether the file holds a megabyte or fifty gigabytes. What about the file's layout produces this constant cost?","answer":"The file contains compressed per-series data blocks plus an index mapping each series key to its block offsets. A lookup is one seek into the index and one seek to the data block, independent of file size.","explanation":"Data-block-plus-index file layout bounds a series lookup to about two seeks"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-33","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"An observability platform runs 'average CPU for prod hosts in us-west over the last hour' against a store holding 90 days of data and thousands of series, yet the engine answers after reading just two compressed blocks. Trace the successive steps that eliminated nearly everything else before any data was read.","answer":"The time filter first prunes to the partitions overlapping the last hour; the in-memory tag index then intersects the region and env series lists down to a couple of matching series; the file index maps those series to specific block offsets, and only those blocks are decompressed and fed through a streaming average. Each layer discards data without reading it, so query cost tracks the result size rather than the dataset size.","explanation":"Query execution composes pruning layers: time partitions, then tag index, then block offsets, then streaming aggregation"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-34","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"An observability vendor benchmarks the same filtered-average query on Postgres (with proper B-tree indexes on the filter columns) and on their time-series engine. Postgres finds the matching row IDs quickly but still loses badly. Where does Postgres spend its time?","answer":"Fetching each matching row from scattered heap pages — thousands of random reads — and then extracting the value column row by row before averaging. The time-series engine reads each matching series' points from contiguous compressed blocks, so the same data arrives in a few sequential reads.","explanation":"Series locality on disk beats Postgres's scattered heap fetches even with indexes"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-35","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A social app adds user_id — about 10 million distinct users — as a tag on its engagement metrics. Within days the database's memory footprint explodes and nodes start getting OOM-killed. What is going wrong?","answer":"Cardinality explosion: every unique tag combination becomes a series with its own entry in the in-memory series index, and 10 million users multiplied across metrics and other tags yields billions of potential series. The index no longer fits in memory and queries crawl.","explanation":"High-cardinality tags explode series count and the in-memory series index"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-36","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"After backing out its user_id tag, an API-gateway SaaS still needs to record the request ID alongside each latency measurement, and support occasionally looks one up. Where should request_id live in the data model, and what does that placement cost?","answer":"Store it as a field. Fields are written but not indexed, so ingest stays cheap and the series count stays bounded — but looking up a specific request ID means scanning data, because you give up the tag index's fast filtering for that value.","explanation":"Unbounded identifiers belong in fields — writable but not fast to filter on"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-37","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A music-streaming service wants an hourly 'top 100 most-played songs' chart and proposes a time-series database with song_id as a tag, one series per song. Why is this the wrong tool?","answer":"Top-K requires sorting and aggregating across a huge number of series, which TSDBs are not designed to do — they optimize reading a small set of series at a time — and millions of song IDs as tag values is itself a cardinality explosion. Having time-stamped data does not mean you need a time-series database.","explanation":"TSDBs are a poor fit for Top-K ranking across enormous numbers of series"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-38","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"In an interview, a candidate is asked to design the metrics feature for a small SaaS product — a few hundred writes per second — and immediately proposes InfluxDB, then cannot answer follow-up questions about how it works. What should they have done instead?","answer":"Start with a general-purpose database — Postgres comfortably handles that write rate — and reach for a specialized TSDB only when a genuine bottleneck appears. Falling back to technology you actually understand beats winging one you cannot explain.","explanation":"Stretch general-purpose databases first; only adopt a TSDB at a real bottleneck"},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-39","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"two-choice","question":"A time-series database will be roughly 10-100x faster than a general-purpose database on any dataset that includes a timestamp column.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"B","explanation":"The 10-100x gains apply only to the target workload where the underlying assumptions hold: bounded tag cardinality, mostly regular timestamps, and small deltas between values. When those assumptions break, a TSDB can actually perform worse than Postgres."},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-40","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"two-choice","question":"Which of these is a database purpose-built for time-series workloads, rather than a general-purpose store that happens to use an LSM storage engine?","optionA":"Prometheus","optionB":"Cassandra","correct":"A","explanation":"Like InfluxDB and TimescaleDB, it is built specifically for time-series metrics, whereas Cassandra is a general-purpose wide-column store that uses LSM trees under the hood."},
  {"id":"hi-time-series-databases-41","topic":"advanced-topics · time series databases","type":"open","question":"A mid-size company runs its whole product on Postgres and has deep operational expertise in it, but metrics ingest is starting to strain the main tables. The team wants time partitioning, compression, and retention policies without adopting and learning an entirely unfamiliar database. Which option fits, and why?","answer":"TimescaleDB — it is implemented as a Postgres extension, so its hypertables add automatic time partitioning, columnar compression, and retention policies while keeping standard SQL, drivers, and the team's existing operational tooling. For a Postgres shop it is the lowest-risk path to time-series performance.","explanation":"TimescaleDB brings time-series behavior to Postgres as an extension"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-01","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A home-goods e-commerce site gets complaints that searching \"warm coat for winter\" returns zero results, even though the catalog has dozens of insulated parkas — none of which contain the words \"warm\" or \"winter\" in their titles. The search stack is a standard inverted-index keyword engine. What capability is missing, and how does it work?","answer":"They need semantic (vector) search: an embedding model maps queries and product text into a shared vector space where meaning-similar text lands close together, so \"warm coat\" retrieves parkas despite zero token overlap. Keyword search can only match literal terms, which is exactly the failure here.","explanation":"Semantic search vs keyword search — embeddings match meaning, not tokens"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-02","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A grocery-delivery app builds a \"frequently bought together\" widget by embedding product descriptions with a general-purpose text embedding model and retrieving nearest neighbors. The widget keeps recommending five nearly identical brands of ketchup next to ketchup, never hot dogs or buns. What is going wrong?","answer":"The embedding model defines the notion of similarity, and a generic text model encodes semantic sameness — so ketchup's neighbors are other ketchups. Complementary-purchase recommendations need embeddings trained on behavioral signals like co-purchase data, not description text.","explanation":"Embedding model choice defines what 'similar' means"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-03","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A stock-photo marketplace wants customers to type \"lonely astronaut on a red desert\" and get matching images. Its 40 million photos have only sparse, inconsistent human-written tags, and captioning them all is out of budget. What retrieval design solves this, and what property of the model makes it possible?","answer":"Embed every photo with a multimodal model such as CLIP, embed the typed query with that same model's text encoder, and run ANN over the image vectors. CLIP-style models are trained so images and their descriptions land in one shared vector space, which is what lets a text vector be compared directly against image vectors with no tags or captions involved.","explanation":"Multimodal models put text and images in one shared space, enabling cross-modal retrieval"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-04","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A legal-tech startup indexing 200 million contract clauses chose 3072-dimensional embeddings \"to maximize quality.\" Their memory bill is enormous and query latency is disappointing, and an A/B test shows retrieval quality barely differs from a 768-dimensional model. What should the team change, and why?","answer":"Drop to the lower-dimensional embeddings: memory and per-comparison compute scale linearly with dimensions, so 3072 dims costs ~4x a 768-dim setup for marginal quality gain at their task. Dimension count is a cost knob, not a free quality upgrade.","explanation":"Dimensionality drives cost linearly — bigger embeddings are not automatically better"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-05","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A travel-booking site embeds hotel reviews with a model whose documentation says it was trained for cosine similarity. An engineer instead configures the index for raw Euclidean distance over unnormalized vectors. Relevance drops noticeably, and ranking now seems to track how long a review is rather than what it says. What is going wrong?","answer":"Metric mismatch: the model encodes similarity as the angle between vectors, but unnormalized L2 distance also folds in vector magnitude, which correlates with incidental properties like text length — so length leaks into the ranking. Use the metric the model was trained for, or normalize the vectors to unit length first (after which L2 and cosine rank identically).","explanation":"Similarity metric must match the embedding model's training"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-06","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"An ad-tech team wants to switch their similarity computation from cosine to plain dot product because it skips two magnitude calculations per comparison. All their vectors are normalized to unit length at ingestion. The PM worries results will change. Will they, and why?","answer":"No — cosine similarity is the dot product divided by the two magnitudes, and on unit vectors both magnitudes are 1, so the scores and rankings are identical. The switch is a pure performance win here.","explanation":"On unit-normalized vectors, cosine and dot product rank identically"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-07","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A news aggregator computes \"related articles\" by loading all 50 million article vectors and computing the distance from the query vector to every single one on each request. Queries take several seconds and CPU cost is climbing with the corpus. What is the fundamental problem?","answer":"They are doing exact brute-force kNN, which is O(n * d) per query — cost grows linearly with the corpus, so it can never be fast at 50M vectors. The fix is an approximate nearest neighbor index (e.g., HNSW), which answers in milliseconds by not comparing against everything.","explanation":"Exact kNN is a brute-force linear scan and does not scale"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-08","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"An engineer at an IoT analytics company tries to speed up 768-dimensional similarity search by building a k-d tree over the vectors, reasoning that spatial trees make 2D geo-queries fast. Benchmarks show the tree performs no better than scanning everything. Why did this fail?","answer":"The curse of dimensionality: in hundreds of dimensions, tree partitioning can prune almost nothing, so k-d trees and similar exact spatial indexes degrade to near-linear scans. High-dimensional search requires approximate structures (graphs, clustering, hashing) that give up exactness for speed.","explanation":"Curse of dimensionality defeats tree/spatial indexes"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-09","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"After a streaming service migrates from brute-force search to an ANN index, QA files a bug: for certain queries, a title known to be the closest match no longer appears in the top 10, though latency dropped from 2s to 8ms. Is this a bug, and what should the team do?","answer":"It is not a bug — ANN indexes intentionally trade a few percent of recall for orders-of-magnitude speedups, so occasionally missing a true neighbor is expected behavior. The team should measure recall against a brute-force baseline and tune search parameters until it meets their quality bar.","explanation":"ANN misses are by design — recall is the quality metric, not a bug count"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-10","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A messaging platform is adding semantic search over messages. Requirements: high recall, low-millisecond latency, a continuous stream of new messages that must become searchable without batch rebuilds, and RAM is not a constraint. Which index family fits, and what makes it work?","answer":"HNSW: it builds a multi-layer navigable graph where queries greedily descend from a sparse top layer to dense lower layers, giving the best recall/latency trade-off available, and it accepts incremental inserts. It is the default choice when memory allows.","explanation":"HNSW is the default index: layered graph, greedy search, best recall/latency, incremental inserts"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-11","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"An observability SaaS moves its trace-similarity index to HNSW. The raw 384-dimensional vectors total about 40GB, but the serving process settles at roughly 52GB resident, and an engineer opens a ticket for a suspected memory leak. What is the more likely explanation?","answer":"No leak — HNSW stores a neighbor list of up to M links per node per layer alongside every vector, and the whole structure has to sit in RAM to be fast. That overhead is a fixed number of bytes per vector, so it is a large relative surcharge for short vectors like these and a smaller one for 1536-dim vectors; either way, budget above raw vector size.","explanation":"HNSW graph links add fixed per-node RAM on top of the raw vectors"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-12","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A gaming platform's \"players like you\" feature runs on HNSW with 90% measured recall; the team wants 97%+ and can tolerate somewhat higher latency, but absolutely cannot afford to rebuild the index. What should they change?","answer":"Raise the query-time search breadth parameter (efSearch): it widens the candidate list explored per query, buying recall with latency and requiring no rebuild. Construction-time parameters like M would improve the graph but force a rebuild.","explanation":"efSearch is the query-time recall knob — no rebuild required"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-13","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A biotech search team has pushed efSearch until query latency hits their 40ms budget, and recall has plateaued at 93% — below the 98% their scientists need. They do have a maintenance window and can rebuild the index offline. Which knobs come next, and what do they cost?","answer":"Rebuild with larger construction parameters: M (links kept per node) and efConstruction (candidates considered while inserting) produce a denser, better-connected graph, which raises the recall ceiling that efSearch is bumping against. The price is more RAM per vector for the extra links and substantially longer build time — and unlike efSearch, neither can be changed without reindexing.","explanation":"HNSW build parameters (M, efConstruction) set the achievable recall ceiling and require a rebuild"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-14","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A fintech firm indexes compliance documents with IVF using 1024 clusters and probes only the single nearest cluster per query. Latency is superb but users report obviously relevant documents missing from results. What is the cheapest fix?","answer":"Increase nprobe so each query searches several nearby clusters instead of one: true neighbors often sit in adjacent clusters, especially for queries near cluster boundaries. It directly trades latency for recall with no reindexing.","explanation":"IVF nprobe controls how many clusters are searched — the recall/latency knob"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-15","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A job board trained its IVF index on resumes from 2024. A year of hiring-trend shifts later — new job categories, new skill vocabulary — recall has slowly degraded even though nprobe was never changed. What is going wrong?","answer":"The k-means centroids were fit to the old data distribution; as new resumes drift away from those centroids, vectors land in poorly-fitting clusters and queries probe the wrong partitions. IVF needs periodic re-clustering/rebuilds as the corpus evolves.","explanation":"IVF centroids go stale under data drift and need periodic retraining"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-16","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A stock-photo marketplace must index 500 million image vectors. Constraints: a hard memory budget far below what HNSW would need, the catalog is reindexed in a nightly batch anyway, and a small recall sacrifice is acceptable. Which indexing approach fits better, and why?","answer":"IVF, typically combined with product quantization (IVF-PQ): it has a far smaller memory footprint than HNSW and its main weaknesses — batch-oriented builds and slightly lower recall — are explicitly acceptable here. HNSW's advantages (top recall, incremental inserts) are exactly the things this team doesn't need.","explanation":"Index selection: IVF+PQ for huge memory-constrained batch-rebuilt corpora vs HNSW"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-17","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"An email-security company inherits a near-duplicate phishing detector built years ago on locality-sensitive hashing. It's fast, but it misses too many variants that hash into different buckets. They want materially better recall at similar latency. What should they migrate to, and why?","answer":"Migrate to a graph index like HNSW: LSH's random-projection bucketing inherently drops neighbors that land in unprobed buckets, and modern graph indexes dominate it on the recall/latency frontier. That's why LSH is rarely the default in current vector databases.","explanation":"LSH: sub-linear bucket hashing but lower recall — superseded by graph indexes"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-18","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A podcast app uses Annoy for episode similarity. Creators complain that episodes uploaded during the day never show up in \"similar episodes\" until the next morning, when a scheduled job runs. Why is this happening?","answer":"Annoy builds a static forest of random-projection trees that is read-only once constructed — there are no incremental inserts, so new episodes are invisible until the nightly full rebuild. If same-day freshness matters, they need an index supporting live inserts, like HNSW.","explanation":"Annoy is a static build-once index — new data requires a full rebuild"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-19","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A media-monitoring platform applies product quantization to its 300 million 1024-dimensional vectors, cutting memory from roughly 1.2TB of float32 down to well under 100GB so it finally fits in RAM. But measured recall drops from 96% to 88%, and buying more RAM is off the table. What can they do without giving up quantization?","answer":"Over-fetch and rescore: have the quantized index return a wide candidate set (say top-200 instead of top-10), then re-rank just those candidates against their full-precision vectors held on SSD. Quantization only has to be accurate enough to pull true neighbors into the candidate list, and exact distances on a short list recover most of the lost recall for a small, bounded cost.","explanation":"Quantization trades recall for memory; over-fetch and rescore with full-precision vectors to win it back"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-20","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A news platform's vector database spreads 1.2 billion article vectors across 40 nodes because no single machine can hold them. A new engineer is confused: if no node has the full index, how can any single \"top 10 related articles\" query possibly be answered correctly? How does the query actually execute?","answer":"Scatter-gather: the router fans the query out to every shard, each shard runs ANN over its local slice and returns its own top-k, and a coordinator merges the partial lists by score into the global top 10. Nearest neighbors have no partition key, so you cannot route a query to one shard the way you would with a user-id-sharded SQL database — the exception is when a filter like tenant or namespace already scopes the query to one partition.","explanation":"Distributed vector serving: shard the corpus, scatter-gather and merge partial top-k results"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-21","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A ticketing platform finds events by vibe (\"chill outdoor jazz\") using vector search for the top 10, then filters those 10 down to \"this weekend in Austin.\" Users in Austin often see only 1-2 results even though dozens of matching events exist. What is going wrong?","answer":"Classic post-filtering failure: the ANN search picks its top 10 from the whole corpus before the location/date filter runs, and the selective filter discards most of them. Fix by filtering during/before the vector search or by over-fetching (retrieve top-200, then filter down).","explanation":"Post-filtering returns fewer than k results under selective filters"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-22","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A multi-tenant B2B search product applies a tenant_id filter inside its ANN index. For its smallest tenants — each owning ~0.05% of the corpus — filtered queries are slower and lower-recall than for big tenants, which seems backwards to the team. What should they change?","answer":"When a filter matches a tiny slice of the corpus, ANN traversal falls apart — the graph walk keeps hitting excluded nodes. Below a selectivity threshold, just brute-force scan the tenant's own vectors: a few thousand exact comparisons is faster and gives perfect recall, and good engines switch strategies automatically.","explanation":"Highly selective filters break ANN efficiency — brute-force the filtered subset instead"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-23","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A hardware retailer replaced keyword search with pure vector search. Semantic queries improved, but contractors typing exact part numbers like \"BRK-4472\" now get visually similar but wrong breakers instead of the exact item. What should the team build, and why?","answer":"Hybrid search: run BM25/keyword retrieval and vector retrieval in parallel and merge the lists (e.g., reciprocal rank fusion). Embeddings blur rare exact tokens like part numbers — precisely the queries lexical search handles perfectly — so you need both signals.","explanation":"Hybrid search (BM25 + vector with rank fusion) rescues exact tokens like SKUs"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-24","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A social feed app deletes millions of vectors as posts are removed, but the vector index's memory footprint never shrinks and query latency is slowly creeping up. The delete API returns success every time. What is going on?","answer":"Graph indexes handle deletes as soft tombstones — the nodes are marked dead but stay in the structure, still consuming memory and being traversed. Reclaiming space and speed requires periodic index rebuilds or compaction, which the team isn't running.","explanation":"Deletes are tombstones — indexes need periodic rebuild/compaction"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-25","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"After a real-estate site added semantic listing search, the create-listing endpoint's p99 jumped from 120ms to over 2 seconds. Tracing shows the handler calls a hosted embedding API and writes to the vector index before responding to the agent. What should the team change?","answer":"Move embedding generation off the request path: enqueue the listing and let an async worker call the embedding model and update the index. Writes return fast, and the trade — a listing becomes searchable seconds later instead of instantly — is almost always acceptable.","explanation":"Embedding generation belongs off the synchronous write path"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-26","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A 6-person startup with 2 million product vectors and all data already in Postgres is debating deploying a managed dedicated vector database cluster \"to do it properly.\" What is the pragmatic recommendation, and why?","answer":"Use pgvector inside the Postgres they already run: at 2M vectors (far below the ~10M rough threshold), an extension delivers fine performance while avoiding new infrastructure, a second system to operate, and a data-sync problem. Reach for purpose-built vector DBs only when scale or QPS demands it.","explanation":"Rule of thumb: below ~10M vectors, use a vector extension on your existing DB (pgvector)"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-27","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A video platform's recommendation retrieval has grown to 800 million vectors, 30k queries per second, and rich metadata filters on every query. Their single-node pgvector instance is saturated despite aggressive tuning. What kind of move is justified now, and what does it buy them?","answer":"This is the profile where a purpose-built distributed vector database (Milvus, Pinecone, Qdrant, etc.) earns its complexity: sharding and replication across nodes, high-QPS serving, and filter-aware ANN at scale. The earlier \"just use your existing DB\" rule stops applying past roughly the 100M-vector / high-throughput mark.","explanation":"Purpose-built distributed vector DBs are justified at 100M+ scale, high QPS, heavy filtering"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-28","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A team prototypes similarity search with FAISS inside a single Python process, loading vectors from a file at startup, and it works beautifully. Productionizing it, they discover they need per-user metadata filters, live inserts and deletes, durability across restarts, and several replicas behind a load balancer. What is the gap they've hit?","answer":"FAISS is an ANN index library, not a database — it gives you the search structure and nothing else: no durable storage, no CRUD or metadata filtering, no replication, sharding, backups, or network API. Those operational layers are exactly what a vector database or a vector extension wraps around the same algorithms, so they either adopt one or build that layer themselves.","explanation":"An ANN library (FAISS) is only the index — a vector database adds persistence, CRUD, filtering, and replication"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-29","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"During capacity planning, a knowledge-base team must size memory for 10 million documents embedded at 1536 dimensions in float32, served from an HNSW index. Roughly how much RAM should they budget, and how is that derived?","answer":"Raw vectors are 1536 x 4 bytes = ~6KB each, so 10M x 6KB = ~60GB. Add roughly 20-50% on top for HNSW's neighbor lists, ids, stored metadata, and rebuild headroom — budget on the order of 75-90GB. Dimensions x 4 bytes x vector count is the back-of-envelope every interview expects.","explanation":"Capacity math: dims x 4 bytes per float32 vector, plus index and operational overhead"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-30","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"two-choice","question":"A properly tuned in-memory ANN index such as HNSW can typically answer a similarity query over tens of millions of vectors in single-digit to low-tens-of-milliseconds.","optionA":"True","optionB":"False","correct":"A","explanation":"Because ANN visits only a tiny, well-chosen fraction of the corpus, millisecond-scale queries at that size are the norm; it's brute-force scanning that takes hundreds of milliseconds to seconds at the same scale."},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-31","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A ride-hailing company's support team upgrades to a newer embedding model and points the query path at it immediately, while the stored knowledge-base vectors were produced by the old model. Search results instantly become near-random garbage. What happened, and what does a correct migration look like?","answer":"Vectors from different embedding models live in unrelated spaces — comparing a v2 query vector against v1 document vectors is meaningless, even at matching dimensions. A correct migration re-embeds the entire corpus with the new model into a new index and cuts queries over atomically (blue/green), keeping query and document models in lockstep.","explanation":"Changing embedding models requires re-embedding the entire corpus — mixed-model vectors are incomparable"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-32","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A marketplace dual-writes product data to Postgres and a vector database from application code. Months later, users report that products deleted from the catalog still appear in semantic search, and some new products never appear. What should the team change architecturally?","answer":"Stop treating the vector DB as a co-equal store: keep Postgres as the source of truth and feed the vector index from it via CDC or an outbox/queue, so every insert, update, and delete propagates reliably. The index should be derived, rebuildable data — dual-writes from app code will always drift.","explanation":"The vector index is derived data — primary DB stays source of truth, synced via CDC"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-33","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"Enthused by their new vector stack, a banking app's team routes all lookups through it — including retrieving a customer by their exact account number. Occasionally the wrong customer record comes back. Why is this the wrong design?","answer":"ANN is approximate by construction — it can and will occasionally return a near neighbor instead of the exact item, which is catastrophic when correctness is binary. Exact-key lookups belong in a traditional index (hash/B-tree); vector search is only for fuzzy, similarity-shaped queries.","explanation":"ANN is the wrong tool for exact-match lookups"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-34","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A company's internal chatbot confidently invents HR policies because the LLM has never seen the private wiki. The team wants answers grounded in the actual documents without retraining the model. What architecture should they build, end to end?","answer":"Retrieval-augmented generation: split the wiki into chunks, embed each chunk, and store vectors in a vector DB; at question time, embed the query, retrieve the top-k most similar chunks, and inject them into the LLM prompt as context. The model answers from retrieved facts instead of guessing.","explanation":"RAG architecture: chunk, embed, retrieve top-k, ground the LLM"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-35","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A support team's RAG bot embeds each entire 30-page product manual as one vector. Retrieval usually surfaces the right manual, but answers stay vague and the LLM's context window is stuffed with dozens of irrelevant pages per question. What is the design flaw?","answer":"Chunking granularity: a whole-document embedding averages many unrelated topics into one diluted vector and forces the entire manual into the prompt. Split documents into passage-sized chunks (a few hundred tokens, often overlapping) so retrieval pinpoints just the relevant passages — while noting that chunks too small lose surrounding context, making chunk size a tuning trade-off.","explanation":"RAG chunking granularity: whole-document embeddings dilute meaning — retrieve passage-sized chunks"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-36","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"An insurance company's RAG assistant always retrieves the top 5 help-center chunks and hands them to the LLM. When a customer asks something the documentation never covers — \"do you insure pet grooming vans?\" — the bot confidently fabricates an answer from unrelated billing and claims pages. What is the retrieval-layer fix?","answer":"Vector search always returns k neighbors regardless of whether anything is actually similar; there is no built-in concept of \"no match.\" Enforce a minimum similarity score, drop chunks that fall below it, and when nothing clears the bar pass no context so the model can say it doesn't know or hand off to a human.","explanation":"ANN always returns k neighbors even when nothing is relevant — enforce a similarity threshold"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-37","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A photo-sharing app bans an image, but users re-upload it slightly cropped, resized, or re-compressed, and the MD5-hash blocklist catches none of the variants. What approach actually solves this, and why does hashing fail?","answer":"Embed images with a vision model (CLIP-style) and check uploads against banned-image vectors via similarity search — visually near-identical images land close in embedding space regardless of crops or compression. Cryptographic hashes flip completely on a single changed pixel, so they only ever catch byte-exact copies.","explanation":"Image embeddings enable near-duplicate detection where file hashes fail"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-38","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A short-video app wants a personalized \"for you\" rail that must be assembled in under 50ms. It already has an embedding for every one of its 30 million videos, but nothing that represents a user. How does it generate candidates for a specific viewer?","answer":"Build a user vector on the fly by aggregating the embeddings of the items they engaged with — typically a recency- and engagement-weighted average of recently watched videos — and use that as the ANN query against the video index. Because users and items then live in the same space, \"more of what you like\" becomes an ordinary nearest-neighbor lookup that returns in milliseconds.","explanation":"Recommendation retrieval: aggregate a user's item embeddings into a query vector, then run ANN"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-39","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"A music-streaming team has a highly accurate but slow neural model that scores how well a track fits a listener — about 10ms per track, against a 50-million-track catalog. They want its quality in a real-time recommendation row. How should they architect this?","answer":"Two stages: use fast ANN vector search to pull a few hundred candidate tracks in milliseconds, then run the expensive model only on that short list to re-rank it. Cheap-retrieve-then-precise-rerank is the standard pattern for putting slow, high-quality models behind real-time latency budgets.","explanation":"Two-stage retrieval: cheap ANN candidate generation, then heavyweight re-ranking"},
  {"id":"hi-vector-databases-40","topic":"advanced-topics · vector databases","type":"open","question":"In a system design interview for a marketplace, the interviewer asks for three features: \"search listings by meaning, not just keywords,\" \"show similar listings on each listing page,\" and \"catch re-uploads of banned photos.\" A candidate starts designing three unrelated subsystems. What insight is the candidate missing?","answer":"All three are the same problem — nearest-neighbor search over embeddings — differing only in what gets embedded (query text, listing content, images), so one vector-search system serves all of them. Recognizing that semantic search, recommendations, RAG, and duplicate detection are all vector-search-shaped, then defending the simplest sufficient deployment, is exactly what the interview rewards.","explanation":"Interview framing: semantic search, recommendations, and dedup all reduce to the same nearest-neighbor primitive"}
]);
